globalThis.EXAM4_BANK = {"schema_version":1,"id":"spark-exam4-20261025-r1","title":"Spark 再編模試A〜D・模試4","description":"模試4の原文155問を保持し、代表91問と自作89問で重複を避けた再編模試A〜D（計180問）を構成。","exams":[{"id":"EX4-S1","title":"原文セット 1","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-001","number":1,"stem":"A developer is building a Spark application that executes several DataFrame transformations and actions. The developer wants to ensure optimal performance by managing the SparkSession efficiently during the execution of the application.\n\nWhat is the best way to manage the SparkSession within the Spark application?","options":["A. Stop the SparkSession and start a new one after each action is performed.","B. Do not use SparkSession and instead depend only on SparkContext.","C. Instantiate a new SparkSession before every transformation.","D. Create one SparkSession and reuse it throughout the entire application."],"correct":3,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"session-lifecycle","concept":"session-lifecycle","concept_label":"session-lifecycle","explanation_ja":"SparkSessionは通常アプリ内で再利用する。getOrCreateは毎回別セッションを作る命令ではない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. Create one SparkSession and reuse it throughout the entire application.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark applications, the SparkSession is the unified entry point used to interact with Spark functionality such as DataFrames, SQL, and structured data processing. It internally manages and wraps the SparkContext, SQLContext, and HiveContext, making it the central interface for all Spark operations.\n\nFrom a performance and resource-management perspective, a Spark application should typically create a single SparkSession instance and reuse it across all transformations and actions. Creating multiple sessions unnecessarily can introduce overhead and may lead to inefficient resource utilization.\n\nKey foundational concept:\n\nSpark follows a lazy evaluation model for transformations. Transformations build a logical execution plan that is only executed when an action is triggered. Because of this design, repeatedly creating or stopping SparkSession instances during transformations or actions would interrupt execution planning and create unnecessary overhead.\n\nTypical Spark application structure:\n\n- \nInitialize SparkSession once at the start of the application.\n\n- \nPerform all DataFrame transformations and actions using this session.\n\n- \nStop the session once the application finishes.\n\nThis pattern ensures efficient resource use, consistent configuration, and optimal performance.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. Stop the SparkSession and start a new one after each action is performed.\nThis approach is inefficient because creating and stopping a SparkSession is an expensive operation. Restarting the session repeatedly would introduce unnecessary overhead and significantly degrade performance. Spark applications are designed to maintain a single active session during execution.\n\nB. Do not use SparkSession and instead depend only on SparkContext.\nSparkSession is the recommended entry point for Spark 2.x and later. It integrates Spark SQL, DataFrames, and structured APIs. Using only SparkContext is outdated for modern Spark applications, especially when working with DataFrames.\n\nC. Instantiate a new SparkSession before every transformation.\nTransformations in Spark are lightweight logical operations that contribute to the execution plan. Creating a new SparkSession for each transformation would be extremely inefficient and could disrupt the logical plan construction and execution optimization performed by Spark’s Catalyst optimizer.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSparkSession replaced older context objects (SQLContext, HiveContext) starting with Spark 2.0 and is designed to be the single shared entry point for the lifetime of a Spark application. Reusing the same session ensures consistent configuration, efficient resource management, and proper coordination with the underlying SparkContext.","source_option_explanations":["A. Stop the SparkSession and start a new one after each action is performed.\nThis approach is inefficient because creating and stopping a SparkSession is an expensive operation. Restarting the session repeatedly would introduce unnecessary overhead and significantly degrade performance. Spark applications are designed to maintain a single active session during execution.","B. Do not use SparkSession and instead depend only on SparkContext.\nSparkSession is the recommended entry point for Spark 2.x and later. It integrates Spark SQL, DataFrames, and structured APIs. Using only SparkContext is outdated for modern Spark applications, especially when working with DataFrames.","C. Instantiate a new SparkSession before every transformation.\nTransformations in Spark are lightweight logical operations that contribute to the execution plan. Creating a new SparkSession for each transformation would be extremely inefficient and could disrupt the logical plan construction and execution optimization performed by Spark’s Catalyst optimizer.","Correct Answer: D. Create one SparkSession and reuse it throughout the entire application.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark applications, the SparkSession is the unified entry point used to interact with Spark functionality such as DataFrames, SQL, and structured data processing. It internally manages and wraps the SparkContext, SQLContext, and HiveContext, making it the central interface for all Spark operations.\n\nFrom a performance and resource-management perspective, a Spark application should typically create a single SparkSession instance and reuse it across all transformations and actions. Creating multiple sessions unnecessarily can introduce overhead and may lead to inefficient resource utilization."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"595296d88854770b484d35be019d72bd4eeb53db7d2e58d51491bca1371109f4","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"session-reuse","decision_id":"session-reuse"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-002","number":2,"stem":"A data engineer is working with a DataFrame named num_df defined as follows:\n\n```\nnum_df = spark.range(5).toDF(\"num\")\n```\n\nThe engineer has also defined the following Python UDF:\n\n```\ndef cubefunc(val):\n \n    return val ** 3\n```\n\nWhich of the following code snippets correctly registers this function as a Spark SQL UDF and applies it to the DataFrame `num_df`?","options":["A.spark.udf.register(\"cubeudf\", cubefunc, IntegerType())\nnum_df.selectExpr(\"cubeudf(num)\")","B.cubeudf = udf(cubefunc)\nnum_df.select(cubeudf(col(\"num\")))","C.spark.udf.register(\"cubeudf\", cubefunc, DoubleType())\nnum_df.selectExpr(\"cubeudf(num)\")","D.cubeudf = udf(cubefunc)\nnum_df.selectExpr(\"cubeudf(num)\")"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"sql-udf-registration","concept":"sql-udf-registration","concept_label":"sql-udf-registration","explanation_ja":"Python関数をSQL名としてregisterし、selectExprでその登録名を呼ぶ。戻り値と宣言したSpark型を一致させる。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-functions-udf-scalar.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, when a function needs to be used inside Spark SQL expressions (for example with `selectExpr()` or SQL queries), it must first be registered as a SQL UDF using `spark.udf.register()`.\n\nThe correct approach is:\n\n- \nRegister the Python function with a name.\n\n- \nSpecify the return type.\n\n- \nCall the function inside a SQL expression.\n\nOption A does exactly this:\n\n```\nspark.udf.register(\"cubeudf\", cubefunc, IntegerType())\nnum_df.selectExpr(\"cubeudf(num)\")\n```\n\nExecution flow:\n\n- \n`spark.udf.register()` registers the Python function as a Spark SQL UDF named `\"cubeudf\"`.\n\n- \nThe return type `IntegerType()` is declared so Spark knows how to serialize the output.\n\n- \n`selectExpr(\"cubeudf(num)\")` calls the UDF using SQL syntax.\n\nSince `num_df` is created with:\n\n```\nnum_df = spark.range(5).toDF(\"num\")\n```\n\nthe values in `num` are `0,1,2,3,4`. The result of the UDF would produce the cubes of those numbers.\n\nImportant conceptual point:\n\nSpark supports two main ways of using UDFs:\n\n- \nSQL UDF registration\n\n- \nUsed with SQL strings or `selectExpr()`\n\n- \nRequires `spark.udf.register()`\n\n- \nDataFrame API UDF\n\n- \nUsed with `select()` and column expressions\n\n- \nCreated with `udf()` and applied directly\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-functions-udf-scalar.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-functions-udf-scalar.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nB.\n\n```\ncubeudf = udf(cubefunc)\nnum_df.select(cubeudf(col(\"num\")))\n```\n\nThis code does work for DataFrame API usage, but it does not register the function as a Spark SQL UDF. The question specifically asks to register the function as a Spark SQL UDF. Therefore this option does not satisfy the requirement.\n\nC.\n\n```\nspark.udf.register(\"cubeudf\", cubefunc, DoubleType())\nnum_df.selectExpr(\"cubeudf(num)\")\n```\n\nWhile this would technically run, the return type is incorrect. The function returns an integer (`val ** 3`). Declaring `DoubleType()` is inconsistent with the function output and is therefore not the correct registration.\n\nD.\n\n```\ncubeudf = udf(cubefunc)\nnum_df.selectExpr(\"cubeudf(num)\")\n```\n\nThis fails because `cubeudf` is not registered in the Spark SQL catalog. `selectExpr()` evaluates SQL expressions, and SQL cannot resolve `cubeudf` unless it has been registered using `spark.udf.register()`.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nA Spark SQL UDF must be registered in the SparkSession catalog before it can be referenced in SQL expressions or `selectExpr()`. In contrast, DataFrame API UDFs created with `udf()` can only be used with column expressions inside DataFrame transformations.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, when a function needs to be used inside Spark SQL expressions (for example with `selectExpr()` or SQL queries), it must first be registered as a SQL UDF using `spark.udf.register()`.\n\nThe correct approach is:\n\n- \nRegister the Python function with a name.\n\n- \nSpecify the return type.\n\n- \nCall the function inside a SQL expression.\n\nOption A does exactly this:\n\n```\nspark.udf.register(\"cubeudf\", cubefunc, IntegerType())\nnum_df.selectExpr(\"cubeudf(num)\")\n```\n\nExecution flow:\n\n- \n`spark.udf.register()` registers the Python function as a Spark SQL UDF named `\"cubeudf\"`.\n\n- \nThe return type `IntegerType()` is declared so Spark knows how to serialize the output.\n\n- \n`selectExpr(\"cubeudf(num)\")` calls the UDF using SQL syntax.\n\nSince `num_df` is created with:\n\n```\nnum_df = spark.range(5).toDF(\"num\")\n```\n\nthe values in `num` are `0,1,2,3,4`. The result of the UDF would produce the cubes of those numbers.\n\nImportant conceptual point:\n\nSpark supports two main ways of using UDFs:\n\n- \nSQL UDF registration\n\n- \nUsed with SQL strings or `selectExpr()`\n\n- \nRequires `spark.udf.register()`\n\n- \nDataFrame API UDF\n\n- \nUsed with `select()` and column expressions\n\n- \nCreated with `udf()` and applied directly","B.\n\n```\ncubeudf = udf(cubefunc)\nnum_df.select(cubeudf(col(\"num\")))\n```\n\nThis code does work for DataFrame API usage, but it does not register the function as a Spark SQL UDF. The question specifically asks to register the function as a Spark SQL UDF. Therefore this option does not satisfy the requirement.","C.\n\n```\nspark.udf.register(\"cubeudf\", cubefunc, DoubleType())\nnum_df.selectExpr(\"cubeudf(num)\")\n```\n\nWhile this would technically run, the return type is incorrect. The function returns an integer (`val ** 3`). Declaring `DoubleType()` is inconsistent with the function output and is therefore not the correct registration.","D.\n\n```\ncubeudf = udf(cubefunc)\nnum_df.selectExpr(\"cubeudf(num)\")\n```\n\nThis fails because `cubeudf` is not registered in the Spark SQL catalog. `selectExpr()` evaluates SQL expressions, and SQL cannot resolve `cubeudf` unless it has been registered using `spark.udf.register()`."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-functions-udf-scalar.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"196a03678d8c03cf849452553c6e12acb1c534a65aa2e7863d3e635f9f49f681","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"sql-udf-register","decision_id":"sql-udf-register-name"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-003","number":3,"stem":"Which code snippet should be used to view the schema of a Parquet dataset stored at the location events_parquet?","options":["A. spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM \" + events_parquet).printSchema()","B. spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM parquet.`\" + events_parquet + \"`\").printSchema()","C. spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM \" + events_parquet).show()","D. spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM parquet.`\" + events_parquet + \"`\").show()"],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"file-sql-schema","concept":"file-sql-schema","concept_label":"file-sql-schema","explanation_ja":"SQLでファイルを直接読むときは、formatとバッククォートで囲んだパスを指定する。printSchemaはスキーマ表示。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B.\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark allows querying files directly using data source tables in SQL syntax. For Parquet files, Spark supports the syntax:\n\n```\nSELECT * FROM parquet.`path`\n```\n\nThis tells Spark to treat the specified file path as a Parquet data source and read it directly.\n\nTo inspect the schema of the dataset, the result of the query can be converted into a DataFrame and the method `printSchema()` can be called.\n\nThe correct code is:\n\n```\nspark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM parquet.`events_parquet`\").printSchema()\n```\n\nThis query:\n\n- \nReads the Parquet dataset located at `events_parquet`.\n\n- \nReturns a DataFrame.\n\n- \nCalls `printSchema()` to display the schema.\n\nThis method is commonly used in Spark SQL when working with external files without registering them as tables.\n\nDocumentation reference:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA.\n`spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM \" + events_parquet).printSchema()`\n\nThis assumes `events_parquet` is a registered table or view in the Spark catalog. If it is simply a file path, Spark SQL cannot resolve it as a table name and the query will fail.\n\nC.\n`spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM \" + events_parquet).show()`\n\nThis has the same issue as option A: Spark expects a table name, not a file path. Additionally, `show()` displays data rows, not the schema.\n\nD.\n`spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM parquet.\" + events_parquet + \"\").show()`\n\nAlthough it attempts to use the Parquet data source syntax, it calls `show()`, which displays the data contents rather than the schema. The question specifically asks how to view the schema, which requires `printSchema()`.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark SQL allows querying files directly through data source syntax such as:\n\n- \n`parquet.\\`path``\n\n- \n`json.\\`path``\n\n- \n`csv.\\`path``\n\nThis feature enables Spark to treat external storage files as temporary queryable datasets without registering them as tables in the metastore.","source_option_explanations":["A.`spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM \" + events_parquet).printSchema()`\n\nThis assumes `events_parquet` is a registered table or view in the Spark catalog. If it is simply a file path, Spark SQL cannot resolve it as a table name and the query will fail.","Correct Answer: B.\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark allows querying files directly using data source tables in SQL syntax. For Parquet files, Spark supports the syntax:\n\n```\nSELECT * FROM parquet.`path`\n```\n\nThis tells Spark to treat the specified file path as a Parquet data source and read it directly.\n\nTo inspect the schema of the dataset, the result of the query can be converted into a DataFrame and the method `printSchema()` can be called.\n\nThe correct code is:\n\n```\nspark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM parquet.`events_parquet`\").printSchema()\n```\n\nThis query:\n\n- \nReads the Parquet dataset located at `events_parquet`.\n\n- \nReturns a DataFrame.\n\n- \nCalls `printSchema()` to display the schema.\n\nThis method is commonly used in Spark SQL when working with external files without registering them as tables.","C.`spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM \" + events_parquet).show()`\n\nThis has the same issue as option A: Spark expects a table name, not a file path. Additionally, `show()` displays data rows, not the schema.","D.`spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM parquet.\" + events_parquet + \"\").show()`\n\nAlthough it attempts to use the Parquet data source syntax, it calls `show()`, which displays the data contents rather than the schema. The question specifically asks how to view the schema, which requires `printSchema()`."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"d4331ae3a1585dde26a23aa92321d6ebdca47c7bffd77e2d71f977c00b72c008","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"sql-file-format-path-schema","decision_id":"sql-file-format-path-schema"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-004","number":4,"stem":"A data scientist is working with a large dataset in Apache Spark and wants to run SQL queries on a DataFrame. Because the dataset contains sensitive information, access should be limited only to the current Spark session.\n\nWhich method should the data scientist use to create a view from the DataFrame that can be queried with SQL syntax and also avoid conflicts with any previously created view with the same name?","options":["A.df.createTempView(\"my_view\")","B.df.registerTempTable(\"my_view\")","C.df.createGlobalTempView(\"my_view\")","D.df.createOrReplaceTempView(\"my_view\")"],"correct":3,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"temp-view-scope","concept":"temp-view-scope","concept_label":"temp-view-scope","explanation_ja":"セッション内の一時viewを同名で置き換えるにはcreateOrReplaceTempView。viewはアクセス制御の代わりにはならない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html#running-sql-queries-programmatically","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. df.createOrReplaceTempView(\"my_view\")\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, a temporary view allows a DataFrame to be queried using Spark SQL syntax within the current Spark session. Local temporary views are session-scoped, meaning they are only accessible within the SparkSession in which they are created. This behavior is appropriate when dealing with sensitive datasets that should not be visible outside the current session.\n\nThe method `createOrReplaceTempView()` creates a temporary view tied to the current SparkSession and automatically replaces any existing view with the same name. This prevents failures caused by duplicate view names and ensures the code runs reliably even if the view was previously created.\n\nExample usage:\n\n```\ndf.createOrReplaceTempView(\"my_view\")\nspark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM my_view\")\n```\n\nThis satisfies both requirements in the question:\n\n- \nThe view is accessible only in the current Spark session.\n\n- \nAny existing view with the same name is replaced, avoiding conflicts.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html#running-sql-queries-programmatically](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html#running-sql-queries-programmatically)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA. `df.createTempView(\"my_view\")`\nThis creates a session-scoped temporary view, but it throws an exception if a view with the same name already exists. Since the question requires avoiding conflicts with existing views, this method is not the best option.\n\nB. `df.registerTempTable(\"my_view\")`\nThis method is deprecated and replaced by `createOrReplaceTempView()` in modern Spark versions. Using deprecated APIs is not recommended.\n\nC. `df.createGlobalTempView(\"my_view\")`\nGlobal temporary views are accessible across multiple Spark sessions within the same application through the `global_temp` database (e.g., `SELECT * FROM global_temp.my_view`). This violates the requirement that access should be limited to the current session.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark supports two major types of views for SQL queries on DataFrames:\n\nLocal temporary views\n\n- \nVisible only within the current SparkSession\n\n- \nAutomatically dropped when the session ends\n\n- \nCreated using `createOrReplaceTempView()`\n\nGlobal temporary views\n\n- \nShared across all SparkSessions within the same application\n\n- \nStored under the `global_temp` database\n\nWhen working with sensitive data or session-specific analysis, local temporary views are the recommended approach.","source_option_explanations":["A. `df.createTempView(\"my_view\")`\nThis creates a session-scoped temporary view, but it throws an exception if a view with the same name already exists. Since the question requires avoiding conflicts with existing views, this method is not the best option.","B. `df.registerTempTable(\"my_view\")`\nThis method is deprecated and replaced by `createOrReplaceTempView()` in modern Spark versions. Using deprecated APIs is not recommended.","C. `df.createGlobalTempView(\"my_view\")`\nGlobal temporary views are accessible across multiple Spark sessions within the same application through the `global_temp` database (e.g., `SELECT * FROM global_temp.my_view`). This violates the requirement that access should be limited to the current session.","Correct Answer: D. df.createOrReplaceTempView(\"my_view\")\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, a temporary view allows a DataFrame to be queried using Spark SQL syntax within the current Spark session. Local temporary views are session-scoped, meaning they are only accessible within the SparkSession in which they are created. This behavior is appropriate when dealing with sensitive datasets that should not be visible outside the current session.\n\nThe method `createOrReplaceTempView()` creates a temporary view tied to the current SparkSession and automatically replaces any existing view with the same name. This prevents failures caused by duplicate view names and ensures the code runs reliably even if the view was previously created.\n\nExample usage:\n\n```\ndf.createOrReplaceTempView(\"my_view\")\nspark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM my_view\")\n```\n\nThis satisfies both requirements in the question:\n\n- \nThe view is accessible only in the current Spark session.\n\n- \nAny existing view with the same name is replaced, avoiding conflicts."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html#running-sql-queries-programmatically"],"images":[],"source_hash":"82ff35ea4abfb7833363ad64187dc790e8971a2c02c4a37170201b57194c7ae1","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"temp-view-replace","decision_id":"session-temp-view-replace-api"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-005","number":5,"stem":"A data engineer is developing a Spark job that needs to join multiple DataFrames. Consider the following code snippet where several joins are performed:\n\n```\ndata1 = [Row(id=1, value=\"A\")]\ndf1 = spark.createDataFrame(data1)\n \ndata2 = [Row(id=1, description=\"Desc1\")]\ndf2 = spark.createDataFrame(data2)\n \ndata3 = [Row(id=1, details=\"Details1\")]\ndf3 = spark.createDataFrame(data3)\n \njoined_df = df1.join(broadcast(df2), df1.id == df2.id, \"inner\") \\\n .join(broadcast(df3), df2.id == df3.id, \"inner\")\n```\n\nWhat will be the result when this code is executed?","options":["A.The code will fail because Spark allows only one broadcast join in a single query.","B.The code will fail because the second join condition (df2.id == df3.id) is incorrect; it should instead be (df1.id == df3.id).","C.The code will execute successfully and perform two broadcast joins: first joining df1 with df2, and then joining the result with df3.","D.The code will throw an error because the broadcast function is used improperly; df2 and df3 must be broadcast separately before executing the joins."],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"broadcast-chain","concept":"broadcast-chain","concept_label":"broadcast-chain","explanation_ja":"小さい側を個別にbroadcastしたjoinは複数つなげられる。join条件の列参照を区別する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C. The code will execute successfully and perform two broadcast joins: first joining df1 with df2, and then joining the result with df3.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, a broadcast join is an optimization where a small DataFrame is replicated to all worker nodes so that the larger DataFrame does not need to be shuffled across the cluster. This reduces network I/O and significantly improves performance for joins involving small lookup datasets.\n\nIn the provided code:\n\n- \n`df1` is joined with `df2` using `broadcast(df2)`\n\n- \nThe result of that join is then joined with `df3` using `broadcast(df3)`\n\nSpark allows multiple broadcast joins within the same query plan. Each broadcast hint instructs Spark to replicate the specified DataFrame to executors before performing the join.\n\nExecution flow:\n\n- \nSpark broadcasts `df2` to executors and performs the first inner join between `df1` and `df2`.\n\n- \nThe resulting DataFrame is then joined with `df3`.\n\n- \nBecause `df3` is also wrapped with `broadcast()`, Spark performs another broadcast join.\n\nSince all three DataFrames contain a row with `id = 1`, the final result will contain one row combining all columns:\n\n```\nid | value | description | details\n1  | A     | Desc1       | Details1\n```\n\nBroadcast joins can be applied multiple times in a query when joining several small lookup tables.\n\nDocumentation reference:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. The code will fail because Spark allows only one broadcast join in a single query.\nThis is incorrect. Spark can apply multiple broadcast joins in the same query plan. Each join operation can independently use a broadcast hint if the optimizer determines it is beneficial.\n\nB. The code will fail because the second join condition (df2.id == df3.id) is incorrect; it should instead be (df1.id == df3.id).\nThis is not true. After the first join, the resulting DataFrame contains columns from both df1 and df2, so referencing `df2.id` in the second join condition is valid. Spark resolves columns from the joined logical plan.\n\nD. The code will throw an error because the broadcast function is used improperly; df2 and df3 must be broadcast separately before executing the joins.\nThis is incorrect because `broadcast()` is designed to be used directly within the join statement as a join hint. There is no requirement to pre-broadcast a DataFrame before using it in a join.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nA broadcast join is most effective when one side of the join is small enough to fit in executor memory. Instead of shuffling both datasets, Spark sends the small dataset to all executors and performs the join locally, avoiding expensive shuffle operations and improving performance in distributed environments.","source_option_explanations":["A. The code will fail because Spark allows only one broadcast join in a single query.\nThis is incorrect. Spark can apply multiple broadcast joins in the same query plan. Each join operation can independently use a broadcast hint if the optimizer determines it is beneficial.","B. The code will fail because the second join condition (df2.id == df3.id) is incorrect; it should instead be (df1.id == df3.id).\nThis is not true. After the first join, the resulting DataFrame contains columns from both df1 and df2, so referencing `df2.id` in the second join condition is valid. Spark resolves columns from the joined logical plan.","Correct Answer: C. The code will execute successfully and perform two broadcast joins: first joining df1 with df2, and then joining the result with df3.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, a broadcast join is an optimization where a small DataFrame is replicated to all worker nodes so that the larger DataFrame does not need to be shuffled across the cluster. This reduces network I/O and significantly improves performance for joins involving small lookup datasets.\n\nIn the provided code:\n\n- \n`df1` is joined with `df2` using `broadcast(df2)`\n\n- \nThe result of that join is then joined with `df3` using `broadcast(df3)`\n\nSpark allows multiple broadcast joins within the same query plan. Each broadcast hint instructs Spark to replicate the specified DataFrame to executors before performing the join.\n\nExecution flow:\n\n- \nSpark broadcasts `df2` to executors and performs the first inner join between `df1` and `df2`.\n\n- \nThe resulting DataFrame is then joined with `df3`.\n\n- \nBecause `df3` is also wrapped with `broadcast()`, Spark performs another broadcast join.\n\nSince all three DataFrames contain a row with `id = 1`, the final result will contain one row combining all columns:\n\n```\nid | value | description | details\n1  | A     | Desc1       | Details1\n```\n\nBroadcast joins can be applied multiple times in a query when joining several small lookup tables.","D. The code will throw an error because the broadcast function is used improperly; df2 and df3 must be broadcast separately before executing the joins.\nThis is incorrect because `broadcast()` is designed to be used directly within the join statement as a join hint. There is no requirement to pre-broadcast a DataFrame before using it in a join."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries"],"images":[],"source_hash":"1922a2b2d21f7164e3e4209031f0d96ac7361880a6e57139c8521568f0959eb9","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"chain-multiple-broadcast-joins","decision_id":"chain-multiple-broadcast-joins"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-006","number":6,"stem":"A data engineer is running a Spark job that reads data from a large text file, filters out invalid records, and then performs a grouping operation based on a particular column. After the results are written to a table, the engineer wants to analyze how Spark executed the job in order to improve performance.\n\nWhich sequence best describes how Spark processes these operations?","options":["A. Spark loads the entire text file into memory first, then applies the filter, and finally performs the grouping operation on the specified column.","B. Spark reads the text file, executes the filter as an action, and then performs the grouping as a transformation.","C. Spark reads the text file lazily, treats the filter and grouping as a single transformation, and executes the entire sequence only when an action is triggered.","D.Spark reads the text file lazily, applies the filter as a narrow transformation, and then performs the grouping on the specified column as a wide transformation that triggers a shuffle."],"correct":3,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"execution-shuffle","concept":"execution-shuffle","concept_label":"execution-shuffle","explanation_ja":"filterはnarrow、groupByの集計は通常shuffleを伴う。変換は実行計画を構築し、書込み等のactionで動く。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations-and-actions","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. Spark reads the text file lazily, applies the filter as a narrow transformation, and then performs the grouping on the specified column as a wide transformation that triggers a shuffle.\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark follows a lazy evaluation model. Transformations such as `filter()` or `groupBy()` do not execute immediately. Instead, Spark builds a logical execution plan (DAG) and executes the plan only when an action is triggered, such as writing results to a table.\n\nExecution behavior in the given scenario:\n\n- \nReading the text file\n\n- \nSpark reads the data lazily. No computation occurs yet; Spark simply creates a logical representation of the dataset.\n\n- \nFiltering invalid records\n\n- \nA `filter()` operation is a narrow transformation because each output partition depends only on a single input partition.\n\n- \nNarrow transformations do not require data movement between partitions.\n\n- \nGrouping by a column\n\n- \nA `groupBy()` operation is a wide transformation.\n\n- \nIt requires data to be reshuffled across partitions so that rows with the same key are grouped together.\n\n- \nExecution triggered by an action\n\n- \nWhen the results are written to a table, Spark executes the full DAG of transformations.\n\nThis sequence accurately reflects Spark's internal processing and explains why grouping operations often involve a shuffle stage, which can impact performance.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations-and-actions](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations-and-actions)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. Spark loads the entire text file into memory first, then applies the filter, and finally performs the grouping operation.\nThis is incorrect because Spark does not load the entire dataset into memory first. Spark processes data partition by partition in a distributed manner and uses lazy evaluation to avoid unnecessary computation.\n\nB. Spark reads the text file, executes the filter as an action, and then performs the grouping as a transformation.\nThis is incorrect because filter is a transformation, not an action. Actions are operations like `collect()`, `count()`, or writing results to storage.\n\nC. Spark reads the text file lazily, treats the filter and grouping as a single transformation, and executes the entire sequence only when an action is triggered.\nAlthough Spark executes transformations lazily, filter and grouping are different types of transformations. Spark specifically distinguishes between narrow and wide transformations, and grouping triggers a shuffle stage. Therefore they are not treated as a single transformation.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark transformations are categorized into two main types:\n\nNarrow transformations\n\n- \nData remains within the same partition\n\n- \nNo shuffle required\n\n- \nExamples: `map()`, `filter()`, `select()`\n\nWide transformations\n\n- \nRequire data movement across partitions\n\n- \nTrigger shuffle operations\n\n- \nExamples: `groupByKey()`, `groupBy()`, `reduceByKey()`, `join()`\n\nUnderstanding the difference between narrow and wide transformations is critical for optimizing Spark performance because shuffle operations are the most expensive operations in Spark jobs.","source_option_explanations":["A. Spark loads the entire text file into memory first, then applies the filter, and finally performs the grouping operation.\nThis is incorrect because Spark does not load the entire dataset into memory first. Spark processes data partition by partition in a distributed manner and uses lazy evaluation to avoid unnecessary computation.","B. Spark reads the text file, executes the filter as an action, and then performs the grouping as a transformation.\nThis is incorrect because filter is a transformation, not an action. Actions are operations like `collect()`, `count()`, or writing results to storage.","C. Spark reads the text file lazily, treats the filter and grouping as a single transformation, and executes the entire sequence only when an action is triggered.\nAlthough Spark executes transformations lazily, filter and grouping are different types of transformations. Spark specifically distinguishes between narrow and wide transformations, and grouping triggers a shuffle stage. Therefore they are not treated as a single transformation.","Correct Answer: D. Spark reads the text file lazily, applies the filter as a narrow transformation, and then performs the grouping on the specified column as a wide transformation that triggers a shuffle.\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark follows a lazy evaluation model. Transformations such as `filter()` or `groupBy()` do not execute immediately. Instead, Spark builds a logical execution plan (DAG) and executes the plan only when an action is triggered, such as writing results to a table.\n\nExecution behavior in the given scenario:\n\n- \nReading the text file\n\n- \nSpark reads the data lazily. No computation occurs yet; Spark simply creates a logical representation of the dataset.\n\n- \nFiltering invalid records\n\n- \nA `filter()` operation is a narrow transformation because each output partition depends only on a single input partition.\n\n- \nNarrow transformations do not require data movement between partitions.\n\n- \nGrouping by a column\n\n- \nA `groupBy()` operation is a wide transformation.\n\n- \nIt requires data to be reshuffled across partitions so that rows with the same key are grouped together.\n\n- \nExecution triggered by an action\n\n- \nWhen the results are written to a table, Spark executes the full DAG of transformations."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations-and-actions"],"images":[],"source_hash":"16177ad46befce0f6d127f89491f9761dbbdb48451e7cca64f43cd220c86460e","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"narrow-wide-shuffle","decision_id":"narrow-wide-shuffle"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-007","number":7,"stem":"A data scientist at a large e-commerce company must process and analyze 2 TB of daily customer transaction data. The organization plans to enable real-time fraud detection and personalized product recommendations. Currently, the data is processed using a traditional relational database system that is struggling to keep up with the growing data volume and processing speed requirements.\n\nWhich Apache Spark feature most effectively solves the challenge described in this scenario?","options":["A. The ability to efficiently process small datasets.","B. Support for SQL queries on structured datasets.","C. In-memory computation combined with parallel processing capabilities.","D.Built-in machine learning libraries."],"correct":2,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"spark-fit","concept":"spark-fit","concept_label":"spark-fit","explanation_ja":"大規模データを複数マシンで並列処理できる点が中心。in-memory処理はデータ全体が必ずメモリに載る保証ではない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C. In-memory computation combined with parallel processing capabilities.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe main challenge described in the scenario is:\n\n- \nProcessing 2 TB of daily transaction data\n\n- \nEnabling real-time fraud detection\n\n- \nSupporting fast analytics for recommendations\n\n- \nOvercoming limitations of a traditional relational database\n\nApache Spark addresses this problem primarily through in-memory distributed computation combined with parallel processing across a cluster.\n\nSpark processes data by dividing it into partitions distributed across multiple nodes in a cluster. Each node executes tasks in parallel, allowing very large datasets to be processed much faster than traditional single-node systems. Additionally, Spark can cache intermediate results in memory, reducing disk I/O and significantly improving performance for iterative workloads such as analytics and machine learning.\n\nThis architecture makes Spark highly suitable for:\n\n- \nLarge-scale data processing\n\n- \nReal-time analytics\n\n- \nIterative computations used in recommendation systems and fraud detection\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. The ability to efficiently process small datasets.\nSpark is optimized for large-scale distributed processing, not specifically for small datasets. The scenario involves 2 TB of daily data, which requires distributed computing capabilities.\n\nB. Support for SQL queries on structured datasets.\nSpark SQL allows querying structured data using SQL syntax, but SQL support alone does not address the performance and scalability challenges of processing massive datasets in real time.\n\nD. Built-in machine learning libraries.\nSpark MLlib provides machine learning capabilities, but the core problem described is handling large-scale data processing and speed requirements, which is solved by Spark’s distributed and in-memory architecture rather than its ML libraries.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nApache Spark’s performance advantage comes from two key architectural features:\n\n- \nIn-memory computation\n\n- \nFrequently used data can be cached in RAM\n\n- \nReduces expensive disk reads and writes\n\n- \nDistributed parallel processing\n\n- \nData is partitioned across many nodes\n\n- \nTasks execute simultaneously across executors\n\nThese features allow Spark to process terabytes or petabytes of data efficiently, making it ideal for large-scale analytics workloads.","source_option_explanations":["A. The ability to efficiently process small datasets.\nSpark is optimized for large-scale distributed processing, not specifically for small datasets. The scenario involves 2 TB of daily data, which requires distributed computing capabilities.","B. Support for SQL queries on structured datasets.\nSpark SQL allows querying structured data using SQL syntax, but SQL support alone does not address the performance and scalability challenges of processing massive datasets in real time.","Correct Answer: C. In-memory computation combined with parallel processing capabilities.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe main challenge described in the scenario is:\n\n- \nProcessing 2 TB of daily transaction data\n\n- \nEnabling real-time fraud detection\n\n- \nSupporting fast analytics for recommendations\n\n- \nOvercoming limitations of a traditional relational database\n\nApache Spark addresses this problem primarily through in-memory distributed computation combined with parallel processing across a cluster.\n\nSpark processes data by dividing it into partitions distributed across multiple nodes in a cluster. Each node executes tasks in parallel, allowing very large datasets to be processed much faster than traditional single-node systems. Additionally, Spark can cache intermediate results in memory, reducing disk I/O and significantly improving performance for iterative workloads such as analytics and machine learning.","D. Built-in machine learning libraries.\nSpark MLlib provides machine learning capabilities, but the core problem described is handling large-scale data processing and speed requirements, which is solved by Spark’s distributed and in-memory architecture rather than its ML libraries."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"94616bfb23e09fd1ccf3c884edd1529f59703bdcc66888abaf7fe547fc9f2bdc","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"distributed-large-data-fit","decision_id":"distributed-large-data-fit"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-008","number":8,"stem":"What is the primary benefit of partitioning data when storing or persisting tables?","options":["A.It automatically removes unused partitions to improve storage efficiency.","B.It compresses the stored data to reduce disk usage.","C.It improves performance by allowing queries to read only the relevant subset of data from specific partitions.","D.It guarantees that all data is loaded into memory at once to speed up query execution."],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"storage-partition","concept":"storage-partition","concept_label":"storage-partition","explanation_ja":"partition列の条件で不要なディレクトリを読まないpartition pruningが狙い。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html#partition-discovery","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C. It improves performance by allowing queries to read only the relevant subset of data from specific partitions.\n\nExplanation:\n\nPartitioning is a common optimization technique in Apache Spark and distributed data systems where a table is physically organized into separate directories based on column values (for example, partitioning by `date`, `country`, or `region`). Each partition contains only the rows associated with a specific value of the partition column.\n\nThe primary advantage is partition pruning. When a query filters on the partition column, Spark can skip reading irrelevant partitions and access only the data that matches the filter condition. This significantly reduces disk I/O and speeds up query execution.\n\nExample concept:\n\nIf a table is partitioned by `year`:\n\n```\n/events/year=2023/\n/events/year=2024/\n/events/year=2025/\n```\n\nA query like:\n\n```\nSELECT * FROM events WHERE year = 2025\n```\n\nwill only read the directory `year=2025` instead of scanning the entire dataset. This leads to faster queries and reduced resource consumption.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html#partition-discovery](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html#partition-discovery)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. It automatically removes unused partitions to improve storage efficiency.\nPartitioning does not automatically delete unused partitions. Partition management (adding or removing partitions) must be handled explicitly using table operations or data lifecycle processes.\n\nB. It compresses the stored data to reduce disk usage.\nCompression is controlled by file formats and compression codecs (such as Snappy, Gzip, or ZSTD), not by partitioning. Partitioning organizes data but does not compress it.\n\nD. It guarantees that all data is loaded into memory at once to speed up query execution.\nSpark does not load all data into memory at once. Instead, it processes data partition by partition across the cluster. Loading entire datasets into memory would not scale for large distributed datasets.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nPartitioning works together with predicate pushdown and partition pruning to reduce the amount of data scanned during queries. When queries filter on partition columns, Spark avoids scanning unnecessary files, which greatly improves performance in large data lakes and warehouse environments.","source_option_explanations":["A. It automatically removes unused partitions to improve storage efficiency.\nPartitioning does not automatically delete unused partitions. Partition management (adding or removing partitions) must be handled explicitly using table operations or data lifecycle processes.","B. It compresses the stored data to reduce disk usage.\nCompression is controlled by file formats and compression codecs (such as Snappy, Gzip, or ZSTD), not by partitioning. Partitioning organizes data but does not compress it.","Correct Answer: C. It improves performance by allowing queries to read only the relevant subset of data from specific partitions.\n\nExplanation:\n\nPartitioning is a common optimization technique in Apache Spark and distributed data systems where a table is physically organized into separate directories based on column values (for example, partitioning by `date`, `country`, or `region`). Each partition contains only the rows associated with a specific value of the partition column.\n\nThe primary advantage is partition pruning. When a query filters on the partition column, Spark can skip reading irrelevant partitions and access only the data that matches the filter condition. This significantly reduces disk I/O and speeds up query execution.\n\nExample concept:\n\nIf a table is partitioned by `year`:\n\n```\n/events/year=2023/\n/events/year=2024/\n/events/year=2025/\n```\n\nA query like:\n\n```\nSELECT * FROM events WHERE year = 2025\n```\n\nwill only read the directory `year=2025` instead of scanning the entire dataset. This leads to faster queries and reduced resource consumption.","D. It guarantees that all data is loaded into memory at once to speed up query execution.\nSpark does not load all data into memory at once. Instead, it processes data partition by partition across the cluster. Loading entire datasets into memory would not scale for large distributed datasets."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html#partition-discovery"],"images":[],"source_hash":"93d7d94b8bf9c91f0a7cf7bc480735a98c88f7c3b49835856d11d6317a45c29b","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"partition-pruning-layout","decision_id":"partition-pruning-layout"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-009","number":9,"stem":"An application architect is evaluating Spark Connect as a potential solution to modernize the Spark applications currently used within their organization.\n\nWhich requirement would prevent the organization from adopting Spark Connect?","options":["A.Debuggability: The capability to perform interactive debugging directly within the application code.","B.Stability: The ability to isolate application code and its dependencies from other applications and from the Spark driver.","C.Complete Spark API support: The requirement to migrate all existing code to Spark Connect without any modifications, including support for the RDD APIs.","D.Upgradability: The ability to upgrade Spark applications independently of the Spark driver."],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark Connect","topic":"connect-api-limits","concept":"connect-api-limits","concept_label":"connect-api-limits","explanation_ja":"Spark ConnectではRDDやSparkContextへそのまま依存するコードを移行できない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C. Complete Spark API support: The requirement to migrate all existing code to Spark Connect without any modifications, including support for the RDD APIs.\n\nExplanation:\n\nSpark Connect is a client-server architecture introduced in Apache Spark that allows applications to connect remotely to a Spark cluster using a decoupled client interface. The client builds logical plans and sends them to the Spark server for execution.\n\nHowever, Spark Connect currently focuses on DataFrame and SQL APIs and does not support the low-level RDD API. Because of this limitation, applications that rely heavily on RDD-based operations cannot be migrated without code changes.\n\nIf an organization requires complete Spark API compatibility, including RDD APIs, Spark Connect would not meet that requirement and would therefore block adoption.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. Debuggability: The capability to perform interactive debugging directly within the application code.\nSpark Connect actually improves debugging capabilities because application code runs in the client environment rather than inside the Spark driver. Developers can use standard debugging tools in their development environment.\n\nB. Stability: The ability to isolate application code and its dependencies from other applications and from the Spark driver.\nThis is one of the primary benefits of Spark Connect. By separating client code from the driver, dependency conflicts are reduced and applications are better isolated.\n\nD. Upgradability: The ability to upgrade Spark applications independently of the Spark driver.\nSpark Connect is specifically designed to decouple the client and server, which enables applications to be upgraded independently of the Spark runtime, improving maintainability.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark Connect introduces a client-server model where:\n\n- \nThe client builds logical query plans using the DataFrame API.\n\n- \nThe Spark server executes those plans.\n\n- \nClient applications run independently of the driver process.\n\nThis architecture improves isolation, debugging, and upgrade flexibility, but currently supports DataFrame and SQL APIs only, not RDD APIs.","source_option_explanations":["A. Debuggability: The capability to perform interactive debugging directly within the application code.\nSpark Connect actually improves debugging capabilities because application code runs in the client environment rather than inside the Spark driver. Developers can use standard debugging tools in their development environment.","B. Stability: The ability to isolate application code and its dependencies from other applications and from the Spark driver.\nThis is one of the primary benefits of Spark Connect. By separating client code from the driver, dependency conflicts are reduced and applications are better isolated.","Correct Answer: C. Complete Spark API support: The requirement to migrate all existing code to Spark Connect without any modifications, including support for the RDD APIs.\n\nExplanation:\n\nSpark Connect is a client-server architecture introduced in Apache Spark that allows applications to connect remotely to a Spark cluster using a decoupled client interface. The client builds logical plans and sends them to the Spark server for execution.\n\nHowever, Spark Connect currently focuses on DataFrame and SQL APIs and does not support the low-level RDD API. Because of this limitation, applications that rely heavily on RDD-based operations cannot be migrated without code changes.\n\nIf an organization requires complete Spark API compatibility, including RDD APIs, Spark Connect would not meet that requirement and would therefore block adoption.","D. Upgradability: The ability to upgrade Spark applications independently of the Spark driver.\nSpark Connect is specifically designed to decouple the client and server, which enables applications to be upgraded independently of the Spark runtime, improving maintainability."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"10c35166bd09257efeabaeef405ee5c067de5a63a8fca2155309021a5f265468","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"connect-unsupported-rdd-context","decision_id":"connect-unsupported-rdd-context"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-010","number":10,"stem":"In the Apache Spark architecture, which components are responsible for executing tasks once they are assigned?","options":["A. CPU Cores","B. Driver Nodes","C. Worker Nodes","D. Executors"],"correct":3,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"component-roles","concept":"component-roles","concept_label":"component-roles","explanation_ja":"taskを実際に処理するプロセスがexecutor。driverは計画・スケジュールを担当する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. Executors\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, once a job is submitted, the Driver program creates a logical execution plan and divides the work into tasks. These tasks are then scheduled on executors, which are the processes responsible for actually running the computations on worker machines.\n\nExecutors run inside worker nodes and perform the following responsibilities:\n\n- \nExecute the tasks assigned by the driver\n\n- \nStore data in memory or disk for caching\n\n- \nReturn the computation results back to the driver\n\nExecution flow in Spark:\n\n- \nThe Driver converts the application into stages and tasks.\n\n- \nThe Cluster Manager allocates resources on worker nodes.\n\n- \nExecutors are launched on worker nodes.\n\n- \nExecutors run the tasks in parallel using CPU cores.\n\nThus, the actual execution of tasks is performed by executors, making them the correct answer.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. CPU Cores\nCPU cores provide the parallel processing capacity inside executors, but they do not independently manage or execute Spark tasks. Tasks are executed by executors using their available CPU cores, not by cores as standalone components in the architecture.\n\nB. Driver Nodes\nThe driver is responsible for coordinating the application. It creates the DAG, schedules tasks, and communicates with executors, but it does not execute distributed tasks across the cluster.\n\nC. Worker Nodes\nWorker nodes are the machines that host executors, but they do not directly execute tasks. The actual execution is performed by the executor processes running inside those worker nodes.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark follows a Driver–Executor architecture:\n\n- \nDriver: orchestrates the application and schedules tasks\n\n- \nExecutors: distributed processes that execute tasks\n\n- \nWorker nodes: machines that host executors\n\n- \nCPU cores: allow executors to run multiple tasks in parallel\n\nTherefore, executors are the components responsible for executing tasks in Apache Spark.","source_option_explanations":["A. CPU Cores\nCPU cores provide the parallel processing capacity inside executors, but they do not independently manage or execute Spark tasks. Tasks are executed by executors using their available CPU cores, not by cores as standalone components in the architecture.","B. Driver Nodes\nThe driver is responsible for coordinating the application. It creates the DAG, schedules tasks, and communicates with executors, but it does not execute distributed tasks across the cluster.","C. Worker Nodes\nWorker nodes are the machines that host executors, but they do not directly execute tasks. The actual execution is performed by the executor processes running inside those worker nodes.","Correct Answer: D. Executors\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, once a job is submitted, the Driver program creates a logical execution plan and divides the work into tasks. These tasks are then scheduled on executors, which are the processes responsible for actually running the computations on worker machines.\n\nExecutors run inside worker nodes and perform the following responsibilities:\n\n- \nExecute the tasks assigned by the driver\n\n- \nStore data in memory or disk for caching\n\n- \nReturn the computation results back to the driver\n\nExecution flow in Spark:\n\n- \nThe Driver converts the application into stages and tasks.\n\n- \nThe Cluster Manager allocates resources on worker nodes.\n\n- \nExecutors are launched on worker nodes.\n\n- \nExecutors run the tasks in parallel using CPU cores.\n\nThus, the actual execution of tasks is performed by executors, making them the correct answer."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"38ed096d5fc2321ce94cdb95f59372d944abc8b6b1cb74b327834fa0a152c37f","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"executor-executes-tasks","decision_id":"executor-executes-tasks"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-011","number":11,"stem":"A data engineer needs to append all rows from one table to another. However, one of the columns present in the first table does not exist in the second table. When running the code below, the following error occurs:\n\nAnalysisException: [NUM_COLUMNS_MISMATCH] UNION can only be performed on inputs with the same number of columns.\n\nThe current implementation is:\n\n`anz_df = au_df.union(nz_df)`\n\nThe DataFrame au_df contains one additional column that is missing in nz_df, although the remaining columns share the same names and data types.\n\nWhat change should the data engineer make so that the DataFrames can be successfully combined?","options":["A.anz_df = au_df.unionByName(nz_df, allowMissingColumns=False)","B.anz_df = au_df.unionAll(nz_df)","C.anz_df = au_df.unionByName(nz_df, allowMissingColumns=True)","D.anz_df = au_df.join(nz_df, how='full_outer')"],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"union-schema","concept":"union-schema","concept_label":"union-schema","explanation_ja":"列名で揃え、片側にない列をNULLで補うunionByNameのallowMissingColumns=Trueを使う。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.unionByName.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C. anz_df = au_df.unionByName(nz_df, allowMissingColumns=True)\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, the standard `union()` operation requires both DataFrames to have the same number of columns and the same schema order. If one DataFrame contains an additional column, Spark throws the error:\n\n`AnalysisException: [NUM_COLUMNS_MISMATCH] UNION can only be performed on inputs with the same number of columns.`\n\nSpark provides a more flexible method called `unionByName()`, which aligns columns based on column names rather than column positions. When the parameter `allowMissingColumns=True` is used, Spark automatically handles schema differences by inserting NULL values for columns that are missing in one of the DataFrames.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\nanz_df = au_df.unionByName(nz_df, allowMissingColumns=True)\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nColumns with the same names are matched together.\n\n- \nIf a column exists in `au_df` but not in `nz_df`, Spark adds that column to `nz_df` with NULL values.\n\n- \nThe resulting DataFrame contains the union of both schemas.\n\nThis allows the append operation to succeed even when schemas differ.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.unionByName.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.unionByName.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. `anz_df = au_df.unionByName(nz_df, allowMissingColumns=False)`\nThis still requires both DataFrames to contain the exact same set of columns. Since `au_df` has an extra column, Spark will still throw a schema mismatch error.\n\nB. `anz_df = au_df.unionAll(nz_df)`\n`unionAll()` is deprecated and behaves similarly to `union()`, meaning the schemas must still match exactly. It will still fail due to the column mismatch.\n\nD. `anz_df = au_df.join(nz_df, how='full_outer')`\nA join combines rows based on matching column values (keys). The requirement here is to append rows from one table to another, which is a vertical operation. Using a join would produce an entirely different result and is not appropriate.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark supports two main ways to combine DataFrames vertically:\n\n- \nunion()\n\n- \nRequires identical schemas\n\n- \nMatches columns by position\n\n- \nunionByName()\n\n- \nMatches columns by name\n\n- \nCan optionally handle missing columns using `allowMissingColumns=True`\n\nWhen dealing with evolving schemas or datasets with slightly different structures, `unionByName()` with `allowMissingColumns=True` is the recommended approach.","source_option_explanations":["A. `anz_df = au_df.unionByName(nz_df, allowMissingColumns=False)`\nThis still requires both DataFrames to contain the exact same set of columns. Since `au_df` has an extra column, Spark will still throw a schema mismatch error.","B. `anz_df = au_df.unionAll(nz_df)`\n`unionAll()` is deprecated and behaves similarly to `union()`, meaning the schemas must still match exactly. It will still fail due to the column mismatch.","Correct Answer: C. anz_df = au_df.unionByName(nz_df, allowMissingColumns=True)\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, the standard `union()` operation requires both DataFrames to have the same number of columns and the same schema order. If one DataFrame contains an additional column, Spark throws the error:\n\n`AnalysisException: [NUM_COLUMNS_MISMATCH] UNION can only be performed on inputs with the same number of columns.`\n\nSpark provides a more flexible method called `unionByName()`, which aligns columns based on column names rather than column positions. When the parameter `allowMissingColumns=True` is used, Spark automatically handles schema differences by inserting NULL values for columns that are missing in one of the DataFrames.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\nanz_df = au_df.unionByName(nz_df, allowMissingColumns=True)\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nColumns with the same names are matched together.\n\n- \nIf a column exists in `au_df` but not in `nz_df`, Spark adds that column to `nz_df` with NULL values.\n\n- \nThe resulting DataFrame contains the union of both schemas.","D. `anz_df = au_df.join(nz_df, how='full_outer')`\nA join combines rows based on matching column values (keys). The requirement here is to append rows from one table to another, which is a vertical operation. Using a join would produce an entirely different result and is not appropriate."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.unionByName.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"d3d8712345f2eebef94382b2243e76833281be6a19b3eda91f88557ca17e1ceb","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"union-missing-column-null-fill","decision_id":"union-missing-column-null-fill"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-012","number":12,"stem":"A data engineer is analyzing a Spark cluster that shows underutilization during scheduled batch processing jobs. Upon reviewing the Spark logs, the engineer observes that executors are being marked as lost after network heartbeat timeouts. The resource warnings have already been resolved, and healthy executors are known to respond after the current network timeout.\n\nWhich action should the engineer take to address the underutilization problem?","options":["A.Increase the executor memory allocation in the Spark configuration.","B. Adjust `spark.network.timeout` to tolerate the diagnosed network/heartbeat delay before healthy executors are treated as lost.","C. Increase the number of executor instances so the cluster can process more tasks concurrently.","D. Decrease the size of the data partitions to improve task scheduling efficiency."],"correct":1,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"timeout-utilization","concept":"timeout-utilization","concept_label":"timeout-utilization","explanation_ja":"network.timeoutをタスクの最大実行時間と混同しない。実際のネットワーク遅延・heartbeatの原因を診断する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/configuration.html#networking"],"images":[],"source_hash":"f11944b9c20fd41a70dac60683c95483b2a3c3ca5a54cca3ccb6249355e230bd","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"network.timeoutは個々のタスクの実行時間制限ではなくネットワーク応答のタイムアウト。診断条件を明記し誤った説明を修正。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html"],"original_stem":"A data engineer is analyzing a Spark cluster that shows underutilization during scheduled batch processing jobs. Upon reviewing the Spark logs, the engineer observes that several tasks are being terminated due to timeout errors, and multiple warnings indicate insufficient resources.\n\nWhich action should the engineer take to address the underutilization problem?","original_options":["A.Increase the executor memory allocation in the Spark configuration.","B. Adjust the `spark.network.timeout` setting to give tasks more time to complete before they are terminated.","C. Increase the number of executor instances so the cluster can process more tasks concurrently.","D. Decrease the size of the data partitions to improve task scheduling efficiency."],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"heartbeat-network-timeout","decision_id":"heartbeat-network-timeout"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-013","number":13,"stem":"A data analyst is working with a DataFrame named employees_df. They need to create a new column called tax, calculated as 10% of the salary, and remove the age column since it is no longer required.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly adds the tax column and drops the age column from the DataFrame?","options":["A. employees_df = employees_df.withColumn(\"tax\", employees_df.salary * 10).dropField(\"age\")","B. employees_df = employees_df.withColumn(\"tax\", employees_df.salary * 0.1).drop(\"age\")","C. employees_df = employees_df.withColumn(\"tax\", lit(0.1) * col(\"salary\")).dropField(\"age\")","D. employees_df = employees_df.withColumn(\"tax\", employees_df.salary * 10).drop(\"age\")"],"correct":1,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"column-edit","concept":"column-edit","concept_label":"column-edit","explanation_ja":"withColumnでsalary×0.1を追加し、dropで不要列を落とす。dropFieldというDataFrameメソッドではない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B. employees_df = employees_df.withColumn(\"tax\", employees_df.salary * 0.1).drop(\"age\")\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark DataFrame transformations:\n\n- \n`withColumn()` is used to create or replace a column.\n\n- \n`drop()` is used to remove a column from the DataFrame.\n\nTo compute 10% tax, the correct expression is:\n\n```\nsalary * 0.1\n```\n\nThe correct implementation is therefore:\n\n```\nemployees_df = employees_df.withColumn(\"tax\", employees_df.salary * 0.1).drop(\"age\")\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \n`withColumn(\"tax\", employees_df.salary * 0.1)`\n\n- \nCreates a new column `tax`\n\n- \nComputes 10% of the `salary` column\n\n- \n`.drop(\"age\")`\n\n- \nRemoves the unnecessary `age` column from the DataFrame\n\nSpark DataFrames are immutable, meaning every transformation returns a new DataFrame, which is why the result must be reassigned to `employees_df`.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA.\n`employees_df.salary * 10` calculates 1000% of salary, not 10%.\nAdditionally, `dropField()` is not a valid method for removing a DataFrame column.\n\nC.\nWhile `lit(0.1) * col(\"salary\")` could technically work, the code still uses `dropField()`, which is not a valid DataFrame API method.\n\nD.\nThis option correctly drops the column using `drop(\"age\")`, but the tax calculation is incorrect because `salary * 10` represents 10 times the salary, not 10% of salary.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nTwo commonly used DataFrame column operations in Spark:\n\n- \nwithColumn()\n\n- \nAdds or modifies a column\n\n- \nUsed for derived columns and calculations\n\n- \ndrop()\n\n- \nRemoves one or more columns from a DataFrame\n\nBecause Spark transformations are immutable and lazy, these operations create a new logical plan that is executed only when an action is triggered.","source_option_explanations":["A.\n`employees_df.salary * 10` calculates 1000% of salary, not 10%.\nAdditionally, `dropField()` is not a valid method for removing a DataFrame column.","Correct Answer: B. employees_df = employees_df.withColumn(\"tax\", employees_df.salary * 0.1).drop(\"age\")\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark DataFrame transformations:\n\n- \n`withColumn()` is used to create or replace a column.\n\n- \n`drop()` is used to remove a column from the DataFrame.\n\nTo compute 10% tax, the correct expression is:\n\n```\nsalary * 0.1\n```\n\nThe correct implementation is therefore:\n\n```\nemployees_df = employees_df.withColumn(\"tax\", employees_df.salary * 0.1).drop(\"age\")\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \n`withColumn(\"tax\", employees_df.salary * 0.1)`\n\n- \nCreates a new column `tax`\n\n- \nComputes 10% of the `salary` column\n\n- \n`.drop(\"age\")`\n\n- \nRemoves the unnecessary `age` column from the DataFrame","C.\nWhile `lit(0.1) * col(\"salary\")` could technically work, the code still uses `dropField()`, which is not a valid DataFrame API method.","D.\nThis option correctly drops the column using `drop(\"age\")`, but the tax calculation is incorrect because `salary * 10` represents 10 times the salary, not 10% of salary."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"6b8395415dfc6c3c662f6b77b543603cbb572973528005a9a020b6e4ffc4e49f","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"column-removal","decision_id":"computed-column-and-drop"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-014","number":14,"stem":"The data engineering team created a pipeline that extracts data from a transaction system. The transaction system has timestamps stored in UTC so the data engineers ingested the timestamps in the raw DataFrame in UTC. The data engineers must now transform the transaction_datetime to the “America/New_York” timezone to create reports accurately.\n\nWhich code should be used to convert the transaction_datetime?","options":["A. raw.withColumn( \"transaction_datetime\", from_utc_timestamp (col (\"transaction_datetime\"), \"America/New_York\"), )","B. raw.withColumn( \"transaction_datetime\", date_format (col (\"transaction_datetime\"), \"America/New_York\") )","C. raw.withColumn( \"transaction_datetime\", to_utc_timestamp (col (\"transaction_datetime\"), \"America/New_York\"), )","D. raw.withColumn( \"transaction_datetime\", from_utc_timestamp (col (\"transaction_datetime\")), )"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"timezone","concept":"timezone","concept_label":"timezone","explanation_ja":"UTCから別timezoneへの時刻表現変換はfrom_utc_timestamp。session timezoneや入力型も確認する。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/sql/index.html#from_utc_timestamp","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe timestamps in the dataset are stored in UTC, and the requirement is to convert them to the America/New_York timezone. In Apache Spark, the correct function for converting a timestamp from UTC to another timezone is:\n\n`from_utc_timestamp()`\n\nThis function interprets the input timestamp as UTC and converts it to the specified timezone.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\nraw.withColumn(\n    \"transaction_datetime\",\n    from_utc_timestamp(col(\"transaction_datetime\"), \"America/New_York\")\n)\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nThe column `transaction_datetime` is assumed to be in UTC.\n\n- \n`from_utc_timestamp()` converts it to the target timezone.\n\n- \nThe result reflects the correct local time in America/New_York.\n\nThis function is commonly used in ETL pipelines when data is stored in UTC but reporting requires regional time zones.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/sql/index.html#from_utc_timestamp](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/sql/index.html#from_utc_timestamp)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nB.\n`date_format(col(\"transaction_datetime\"), \"America/New_York\")`\n\n`date_format()` formats timestamps into string representations based on a date pattern, not time zones. The second argument should be a format pattern like `\"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss\"`, not a timezone.\n\nC.\n`to_utc_timestamp(col(\"transaction_datetime\"), \"America/New_York\")`\n\n`to_utc_timestamp()` performs the opposite operation. It converts a timestamp from a specified timezone into UTC, whereas the requirement is to convert from UTC to New York time.\n\nD.\n`from_utc_timestamp(col(\"transaction_datetime\"))`\n\nThis is incorrect because `from_utc_timestamp()` requires two arguments: the timestamp column and the target timezone. Without specifying the timezone, the function call is invalid.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark provides two complementary timezone conversion functions:\n\n- \nfrom_utc_timestamp(timestamp, timezone)\nConverts a UTC timestamp to the specified timezone.\n\n- \nto_utc_timestamp(timestamp, timezone)\nConverts a timestamp in a specified timezone into UTC.\n\nBest practice in distributed data systems is to store timestamps in UTC and convert to local time only when required for reporting or analytics.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe timestamps in the dataset are stored in UTC, and the requirement is to convert them to the America/New_York timezone. In Apache Spark, the correct function for converting a timestamp from UTC to another timezone is:\n\n`from_utc_timestamp()`\n\nThis function interprets the input timestamp as UTC and converts it to the specified timezone.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\nraw.withColumn(\n    \"transaction_datetime\",\n    from_utc_timestamp(col(\"transaction_datetime\"), \"America/New_York\")\n)\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nThe column `transaction_datetime` is assumed to be in UTC.\n\n- \n`from_utc_timestamp()` converts it to the target timezone.\n\n- \nThe result reflects the correct local time in America/New_York.","B.\n`date_format(col(\"transaction_datetime\"), \"America/New_York\")`\n\n`date_format()` formats timestamps into string representations based on a date pattern, not time zones. The second argument should be a format pattern like `\"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss\"`, not a timezone.","C.\n`to_utc_timestamp(col(\"transaction_datetime\"), \"America/New_York\")`\n\n`to_utc_timestamp()` performs the opposite operation. It converts a timestamp from a specified timezone into UTC, whereas the requirement is to convert from UTC to New York time.","D.\n`from_utc_timestamp(col(\"transaction_datetime\"))`\n\nThis is incorrect because `from_utc_timestamp()` requires two arguments: the timestamp column and the target timezone. Without specifying the timezone, the function call is invalid."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/sql/index.html#from_utc_timestamp"],"images":[],"source_hash":"0738fb5a245eb64678c226952e55fdc7111457bfb262c11423bd2887b49935e4","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"utc-to-local-timestamp","decision_id":"utc-to-local-timestamp"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-015","number":15,"stem":"A data scientist is analyzing a very large dataset that cannot fit into the memory of a single machine. Instead of using traditional single-machine programming approaches such as standard Python scripts, the scientist is considering Apache Spark to process the data.\n\nWhich two advantages does Apache Spark provide over a typical single-machine programming approach in this situation? (Choose two.)","options":["A. It removes the need to write code by automatically managing all data processing operations.","B. It provides built-in fault tolerance, enabling the system to recover automatically if a node fails during computation.","C. It processes data exclusively from disk storage, minimizing the reliance on memory.","D. It can distribute computation across a cluster of machines, allowing horizontal scalability.","E.It requires specialized hardware to operate and therefore cannot run on commodity hardware clusters."],"correct":1,"correct_many":[1,3],"domain":"Architecture","topic":"spark-fit","concept":"spark-fit","concept_label":"spark-fit","explanation_ja":"分散処理による水平拡張と障害回復が利点。自動でコードが不要になるわけではない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#resilient-distributed-datasets-rdds","source_explanation":"Correct Answers: B and D\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe scenario involves processing a very large dataset that cannot fit into the memory of a single machine. Apache Spark is designed for distributed data processing, which allows workloads to be executed across multiple machines in a cluster.\n\nTwo key advantages Spark provides in this situation are fault tolerance and distributed computation.\n\nB. It provides built-in fault tolerance, enabling the system to recover automatically if a node fails during computation.\n\nSpark provides fault tolerance through its lineage-based execution model. Spark tracks the sequence of transformations used to build datasets (RDD lineage). If a node fails and some partitions are lost, Spark can recompute only the missing partitions instead of restarting the entire job.\n\nThis mechanism allows long-running distributed computations to continue reliably even when individual nodes fail.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#resilient-distributed-datasets-rdds](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#resilient-distributed-datasets-rdds)\n\nD. It can distribute computation across a cluster of machines, allowing horizontal scalability.\n\nSpark distributes data into partitions across multiple nodes in a cluster. Each node processes a portion of the dataset in parallel. This enables horizontal scalability, meaning performance improves as more machines are added.\n\nFor datasets larger than the memory of a single machine, distributed computation allows Spark to process terabytes or petabytes of data efficiently.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA. It removes the need to write code by automatically managing all data processing operations.\nSpark does not eliminate the need to write code. Developers must still implement transformations, queries, or machine learning pipelines using Spark APIs.\n\nC. It processes data exclusively from disk storage, minimizing the reliance on memory.\nSpark’s key performance advantage is actually the opposite: it performs in-memory computation whenever possible to accelerate processing.\n\nE. It requires specialized hardware to operate and therefore cannot run on commodity hardware clusters.\nSpark is specifically designed to run on commodity hardware clusters and does not require specialized hardware.","source_option_explanations":["A. It removes the need to write code by automatically managing all data processing operations.\nSpark does not eliminate the need to write code. Developers must still implement transformations, queries, or machine learning pipelines using Spark APIs.","B. It provides built-in fault tolerance, enabling the system to recover automatically if a node fails during computation.\n\nSpark provides fault tolerance through its lineage-based execution model. Spark tracks the sequence of transformations used to build datasets (RDD lineage). If a node fails and some partitions are lost, Spark can recompute only the missing partitions instead of restarting the entire job.\n\nThis mechanism allows long-running distributed computations to continue reliably even when individual nodes fail.","C. It processes data exclusively from disk storage, minimizing the reliance on memory.\nSpark’s key performance advantage is actually the opposite: it performs in-memory computation whenever possible to accelerate processing.","D. It can distribute computation across a cluster of machines, allowing horizontal scalability.\n\nSpark distributes data into partitions across multiple nodes in a cluster. Each node processes a portion of the dataset in parallel. This enables horizontal scalability, meaning performance improves as more machines are added.\n\nFor datasets larger than the memory of a single machine, distributed computation allows Spark to process terabytes or petabytes of data efficiently.","E. It requires specialized hardware to operate and therefore cannot run on commodity hardware clusters.\nSpark is specifically designed to run on commodity hardware clusters and does not require specialized hardware."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#resilient-distributed-datasets-rdds","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"b11c3f4bf2d2be4bf4d949ee9e84a2904fa83ebe33c3edca35a82757b3e03182","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"distributed-large-data-fit","decision_id":"distributed-large-data-fit"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-016","number":16,"stem":"How does the Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) represent the execution hierarchy in Apache Spark?","options":["A. It represents the execution of multiple jobs, where each job operates independently without stages or tasks.","B. It models the execution of a job as a sequence of dependent stages, where each stage is divided into multiple parallel tasks.","C. It represents Spark tasks as separate jobs, with each job consisting of multiple applications.","D. It represents only the final tasks in execution without considering stages or jobs."],"correct":1,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"execution-hierarchy","concept":"execution-hierarchy","concept_label":"execution-hierarchy","explanation_ja":"jobはstageに分かれ、stage内で各partitionのtaskを並列実行する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B. It models the execution of a job as a sequence of dependent stages, where each stage is divided into multiple parallel tasks.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, the Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) represents the logical execution plan of a Spark application. It captures the sequence of transformations and the dependencies between them. When an action is triggered, Spark uses the DAG to determine how the job should be executed.\n\nThe execution hierarchy in Spark follows this structure:\n\n- \nApplication\n\n- \nJob\n\n- \nStage\n\n- \nTask\n\nExecution flow:\n\n- \nWhen an action (such as `collect()`, `count()`, or writing data) is triggered, Spark creates a job.\n\n- \nThe DAG scheduler analyzes transformation dependencies and splits the job into stages.\n\n- \nEach stage contains multiple tasks, which are executed in parallel across cluster executors.\n\nStages are determined primarily by shuffle boundaries:\n\n- \nNarrow transformations remain in the same stage.\n\n- \nWide transformations (such as `groupBy`, `join`, `reduceByKey`) create a new stage due to shuffle.\n\nExample hierarchy:\n\n```\nJob\n ├── Stage 1\n │    ├── Task 1\n │    ├── Task 2\n │    └── Task 3\n └── Stage 2\n      ├── Task 1\n      ├── Task 2\n      └── Task 3\n```\n\nThis structure allows Spark to efficiently schedule tasks and execute them in parallel across the cluster.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. It represents the execution of multiple jobs, where each job operates independently without stages or tasks.\nThis is incorrect because Spark jobs are internally divided into stages and tasks based on transformation dependencies.\n\nC. It represents Spark tasks as separate jobs, with each job consisting of multiple applications.\nThis reverses the hierarchy. In Spark, applications contain jobs, and jobs contain stages and tasks.\n\nD. It represents only the final tasks in execution without considering stages or jobs.\nThe DAG describes the entire execution plan, including transformations, stage boundaries, and task parallelism, not just the final tasks.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark’s DAG scheduler determines how transformations are executed by analyzing data dependencies. It groups transformations into stages separated by shuffle operations and schedules tasks within those stages to run in parallel across executors.","source_option_explanations":["A. It represents the execution of multiple jobs, where each job operates independently without stages or tasks.\nThis is incorrect because Spark jobs are internally divided into stages and tasks based on transformation dependencies.","Correct Answer: B. It models the execution of a job as a sequence of dependent stages, where each stage is divided into multiple parallel tasks.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, the Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) represents the logical execution plan of a Spark application. It captures the sequence of transformations and the dependencies between them. When an action is triggered, Spark uses the DAG to determine how the job should be executed.\n\nThe execution hierarchy in Spark follows this structure:\n\n- \nApplication\n\n- \nJob\n\n- \nStage\n\n- \nTask\n\nExecution flow:\n\n- \nWhen an action (such as `collect()`, `count()`, or writing data) is triggered, Spark creates a job.\n\n- \nThe DAG scheduler analyzes transformation dependencies and splits the job into stages.\n\n- \nEach stage contains multiple tasks, which are executed in parallel across cluster executors.\n\nStages are determined primarily by shuffle boundaries:\n\n- \nNarrow transformations remain in the same stage.\n\n- \nWide transformations (such as `groupBy`, `join`, `reduceByKey`) create a new stage due to shuffle.","C. It represents Spark tasks as separate jobs, with each job consisting of multiple applications.\nThis reverses the hierarchy. In Spark, applications contain jobs, and jobs contain stages and tasks.","D. It represents only the final tasks in execution without considering stages or jobs.\nThe DAG describes the entire execution plan, including transformations, stage boundaries, and task parallelism, not just the final tasks."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"129af72c866e0975478b15a9b94a551ccfa48223f9a032b50819dd1c6e25f867","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"job-stage-task-hierarchy","decision_id":"job-stage-task-hierarchy"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-017","number":17,"stem":"A data engineer is working with a dataset that contains 2 billion records distributed across 10 Spark partitions. The engineer must compute an approximate count of distinct users based on the `user_id` column and also calculate the average of `transaction_amount`. Both calculations should be executed within a single aggregation step to minimize shuffle operations and improve performance.\n\nWhich Spark code snippet correctly performs these calculations while maintaining optimal performance?","options":["A.\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import approx_count_distinct, avg\n \ndf.agg(\n    approx_count_distinct(\"user_id\").alias(\"approx_count_distinct\"),\n    avg(\"transaction_amount\").alias(\"average_transaction\")\n).show()\n```","B.\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import countDistinct, avg\n \ndf.agg(\n    countDistinct(\"user_id\").alias(\"approx_count_distinct\"),\n    avg(\"transaction_amount\").alias(\"average_transaction\")\n).show()\n```","C.\n\n```\ndf.createOrReplaceTempView(\"transactions\")\n \nspark.sql(\"\"\"\nSELECT\nCOUNT(DISTINCT user_id) AS approx_count_distinct,\nAVG(transaction_amount) AS average_transaction\nFROM transactions\n\"\"\").show()\n```","D.\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import col\n \napprox_count = df.select(\"user_id\").distinct().count()\naverage_transaction = df.select(\"transaction_amount\").groupBy().avg().collect()[0][0]\n \nprint(approx_count, average_transaction)\n```"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"aggregation","concept":"aggregation","concept_label":"aggregation","explanation_ja":"approx_count_distinctとavgを同じaggに渡す。countDistinctは近似ではなく厳密集計。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html#aggregate-functions","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement is to:\n\n- \nCompute an approximate distinct count of `user_id`\n\n- \nCompute the average of `transaction_amount`\n\n- \nPerform both calculations within a single aggregation step\n\n- \nMinimize shuffle operations for performance\n\nOption A correctly satisfies all these requirements.\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import approx_count_distinct, avg\n \ndf.agg(\n    approx_count_distinct(\"user_id\").alias(\"approx_count_distinct\"),\n    avg(\"transaction_amount\").alias(\"average_transaction\")\n).show()\n```\n\nKey foundational concepts:\n\n- \nSingle aggregation stage\n\n`df.agg()` allows multiple aggregation functions to be executed in one aggregation step. Spark constructs a single logical plan where both aggregates are computed together, minimizing shuffle stages.\n\n- \nApproximate distinct counting\n\n`approx_count_distinct()` uses the HyperLogLog++ algorithm, which provides a fast and memory-efficient approximation of distinct values. This is particularly useful for very large datasets such as the 2 billion records mentioned in the scenario.\n\n- \nPerformance benefit\n\nCompared to exact distinct counting, approximate counting:\n\n- \nReduces memory usage\n\n- \nReduces shuffle overhead\n\n- \nImproves scalability for very large datasets\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html#aggregate-functions](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html#aggregate-functions)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nB.\n\n```\ncountDistinct(\"user_id\")\n```\n\n`countDistinct()` performs an exact distinct count, which requires a much heavier shuffle and aggregation process across partitions. For very large datasets, this is significantly more expensive than `approx_count_distinct()`. The question explicitly asks for an approximate count to improve performance.\n\nC.\n\n```\nCOUNT(DISTINCT user_id)\n```\n\nThis SQL expression performs the same exact distinct aggregation as `countDistinct()`. While it technically runs in a single aggregation step, it still requires a full shuffle for exact distinct computation, making it less performant than the approximate method.\n\nD.\n\n```\ndf.select(\"user_id\").distinct().count()\ndf.select(\"transaction_amount\").groupBy().avg()\n```\n\nThis approach is inefficient because:\n\n- \nIt performs two separate actions\n\n- \nIt triggers multiple Spark jobs\n\n- \n`distinct()` introduces a large shuffle\n\n- \n`collect()` brings results to the driver unnecessarily\n\nThis violates the requirement to perform both calculations in a single aggregation step and minimize shuffle operations.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark provides approximate aggregation functions (such as `approx_count_distinct`) specifically designed for large-scale analytics workloads. These functions trade a small amount of accuracy for significantly better performance and scalability, making them ideal for datasets containing billions of records.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement is to:\n\n- \nCompute an approximate distinct count of `user_id`\n\n- \nCompute the average of `transaction_amount`\n\n- \nPerform both calculations within a single aggregation step\n\n- \nMinimize shuffle operations for performance\n\nOption A correctly satisfies all these requirements.\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import approx_count_distinct, avg\n \ndf.agg(\n    approx_count_distinct(\"user_id\").alias(\"approx_count_distinct\"),\n    avg(\"transaction_amount\").alias(\"average_transaction\")\n).show()\n```\n\nKey foundational concepts:\n\n- \nSingle aggregation stage\n\n`df.agg()` allows multiple aggregation functions to be executed in one aggregation step. Spark constructs a single logical plan where both aggregates are computed together, minimizing shuffle stages.\n\n- \nApproximate distinct counting\n\n`approx_count_distinct()` uses the HyperLogLog++ algorithm, which provides a fast and memory-efficient approximation of distinct values. This is particularly useful for very large datasets such as the 2 billion records mentioned in the scenario.\n\n- \nPerformance benefit\n\nCompared to exact distinct counting, approximate counting:\n\n- \nReduces memory usage\n\n- \nReduces shuffle overhead\n\n- \nImproves scalability for very large datasets","B.\n\n```\ncountDistinct(\"user_id\")\n```\n\n`countDistinct()` performs an exact distinct count, which requires a much heavier shuffle and aggregation process across partitions. For very large datasets, this is significantly more expensive than `approx_count_distinct()`. The question explicitly asks for an approximate count to improve performance.","C.\n\n```\nCOUNT(DISTINCT user_id)\n```\n\nThis SQL expression performs the same exact distinct aggregation as `countDistinct()`. While it technically runs in a single aggregation step, it still requires a full shuffle for exact distinct computation, making it less performant than the approximate method.","D.\n\n```\ndf.select(\"user_id\").distinct().count()\ndf.select(\"transaction_amount\").groupBy().avg()\n```\n\nThis approach is inefficient because:\n\n- \nIt performs two separate actions\n\n- \nIt triggers multiple Spark jobs\n\n- \n`distinct()` introduces a large shuffle\n\n- \n`collect()` brings results to the driver unnecessarily"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html#aggregate-functions"],"images":[],"source_hash":"19d037d8457ae3e57f6020183d713891439c7814c6938cb3105ee8ff651f5166","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"approx-distinct-and-average-single-agg","decision_id":"approx-distinct-and-average-single-agg"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-018","number":18,"stem":"A developer is examining a DataFrame named df_epoch_time that contains a column called epoch_time, which stores Unix epoch timestamps (seconds since January 1, 1970, 00:00:00 UTC). The developer needs to extract the month from this timestamp and add it as a new column named month.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly accomplishes this requirement?","options":["A. df_epoch_time = df_epoch_time.withColumn(\"month\", from_unixtime(\"epoch_time\").substr(4,2))","B. df_epoch_time = df_epoch_time.withColumn(\"month\", month(from_unixtime(\"epoch_time\")))","C. df_epoch_time = df_epoch_time.withColumn(\"month\", month(\"epoch_time\"))","D. df_epoch_time = df_epoch_time.withColumn(\"month\", col(\"epoch_time\").substr(6,2))"],"correct":1,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"epoch-date","concept":"epoch-date","concept_label":"epoch-date","explanation_ja":"epoch秒を日時表現へ変換してmonthで月を取り出す。数値列にmonthを直接適用しない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe column `epoch_time` contains Unix epoch timestamps, which represent the number of seconds since January 1, 1970 (UTC). To extract date components such as the month, the epoch value must first be converted into a timestamp.\n\nIn Spark, the function `from_unixtime()` converts Unix epoch seconds into a timestamp representation. Once converted, the `month()` function can be used to extract the month value.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\ndf_epoch_time = df_epoch_time.withColumn(\n    \"month\",\n    month(from_unixtime(\"epoch_time\"))\n)\n```\n\nExecution steps:\n\n- \n`from_unixtime(\"epoch_time\")` converts the epoch seconds into a timestamp.\n\n- \n`month()` extracts the month component from that timestamp.\n\n- \n`withColumn()` creates the new column named month.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA.\n\n```\nfrom_unixtime(\"epoch_time\").substr(4,2)\n```\n\nThis approach converts the timestamp to a string representation and then extracts characters using substring. This is unreliable because the position of the month depends on the timestamp format and string parsing is inefficient compared to using built-in date functions.\n\nC.\n\n```\nmonth(\"epoch_time\")\n```\n\nThe `month()` function expects a date or timestamp column, not an integer representing epoch seconds. Since `epoch_time` is a numeric Unix timestamp, Spark cannot directly extract the month without converting it first.\n\nD.\n\n```\ncol(\"epoch_time\").substr(6,2)\n```\n\nThis treats the epoch timestamp as a string and attempts substring extraction, which is incorrect because epoch timestamps represent seconds, not formatted date strings.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark provides date and time extraction functions such as `year()`, `month()`, and `dayofmonth()`, which operate on timestamp or date types. When working with Unix epoch values, they must first be converted using functions like `from_unixtime()` before applying these date extraction functions.","source_option_explanations":["A.\n\n```\nfrom_unixtime(\"epoch_time\").substr(4,2)\n```\n\nThis approach converts the timestamp to a string representation and then extracts characters using substring. This is unreliable because the position of the month depends on the timestamp format and string parsing is inefficient compared to using built-in date functions.","Correct Answer: B.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe column `epoch_time` contains Unix epoch timestamps, which represent the number of seconds since January 1, 1970 (UTC). To extract date components such as the month, the epoch value must first be converted into a timestamp.\n\nIn Spark, the function `from_unixtime()` converts Unix epoch seconds into a timestamp representation. Once converted, the `month()` function can be used to extract the month value.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\ndf_epoch_time = df_epoch_time.withColumn(\n    \"month\",\n    month(from_unixtime(\"epoch_time\"))\n)\n```\n\nExecution steps:\n\n- \n`from_unixtime(\"epoch_time\")` converts the epoch seconds into a timestamp.\n\n- \n`month()` extracts the month component from that timestamp.\n\n- \n`withColumn()` creates the new column named month.","C.\n\n```\nmonth(\"epoch_time\")\n```\n\nThe `month()` function expects a date or timestamp column, not an integer representing epoch seconds. Since `epoch_time` is a numeric Unix timestamp, Spark cannot directly extract the month without converting it first.","D.\n\n```\ncol(\"epoch_time\").substr(6,2)\n```\n\nThis treats the epoch timestamp as a string and attempts substring extraction, which is incorrect because epoch timestamps represent seconds, not formatted date strings."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"59dd7a89ff31fd60a5c84f83c8a01b45e38a04cda9c693a01c6afd5f0a6b7141","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"epoch-date-part","decision_id":"epoch-date-part"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-019","number":19,"stem":"A Parquet file contains two columns named name and address, both stored as String types.\n\nWhat will be the schema of the DataFrame created using the following code?\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"path/to/file\")\n```","options":["A.\nname: String, address: String","B.\nc0: String","C.\nName: String, Address: String","D.\nc0: String, c2: String"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"parquet-schema","concept":"parquet-schema","concept_label":"parquet-schema","explanation_ja":"Parquetはスキーマをファイルに保持するため列名と型を読める。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A. name: String, address: String\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark automatically infers the schema from Parquet files because Parquet is a self-describing columnar storage format. This means the file stores metadata about the column names and data types inside the file itself.\n\nWhen Spark reads a Parquet file using:\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"path/to/file\")\n```\n\nSpark extracts the schema directly from the file metadata and constructs the DataFrame with the same column names and data types defined in the Parquet file.\n\nSince the Parquet file contains:\n\n- \n`name` (String)\n\n- \n`address` (String)\n\nthe resulting DataFrame schema will be:\n\n```\nname: String\naddress: String\n```\n\nNo manual schema definition is required because Parquet preserves schema information.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nB. `c0: String`\nThis would occur when reading schema-less formats (such as raw text files) where Spark assigns default column names. Parquet files already store column metadata, so Spark preserves the original names.\n\nC. `Name: String, Address: String`\nSpark does not change column casing automatically. Column names remain exactly as defined in the Parquet file metadata.\n\nD. `c0: String, c2: String`\nSpark only generates default column names like `c0`, `c1`, etc., when reading data formats that do not store schema information, such as CSV without headers or plain text files.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nParquet is a columnar storage format with embedded schema metadata, which allows Spark to:\n\n- \nAutomatically infer schema\n\n- \nPerform predicate pushdown\n\n- \nRead only required columns (column pruning)\n\nThese capabilities make Parquet highly efficient for large-scale analytics workloads in Spark.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A. name: String, address: String\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark automatically infers the schema from Parquet files because Parquet is a self-describing columnar storage format. This means the file stores metadata about the column names and data types inside the file itself.\n\nWhen Spark reads a Parquet file using:\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"path/to/file\")\n```\n\nSpark extracts the schema directly from the file metadata and constructs the DataFrame with the same column names and data types defined in the Parquet file.\n\nSince the Parquet file contains:\n\n- \n`name` (String)\n\n- \n`address` (String)\n\nthe resulting DataFrame schema will be:\n\n```\nname: String\naddress: String\n```\n\nNo manual schema definition is required because Parquet preserves schema information.","B. `c0: String`\nThis would occur when reading schema-less formats (such as raw text files) where Spark assigns default column names. Parquet files already store column metadata, so Spark preserves the original names.","C. `Name: String, Address: String`\nSpark does not change column casing automatically. Column names remain exactly as defined in the Parquet file metadata.","D. `c0: String, c2: String`\nSpark only generates default column names like `c0`, `c1`, etc., when reading data formats that do not store schema information, such as CSV without headers or plain text files."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"bc17863f36d0bb2667e85676c62a698669a2b783fc5225e940d10fc7683e0bdc","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"parquet-preserves-schema","decision_id":"parquet-preserves-schema"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-020","number":20,"stem":"An organization has been operating a Spark application in production and is evaluating whether to disable the Spark History Server to reduce resource consumption.\n\nWhat would be the consequence of disabling the Spark History Server in a production environment?","options":["A. It would prevent driver logs from accumulating during long-running jobs.","B. It would increase job execution speed by reducing logging overhead.","C. It would result in losing access to historical job logs and limit the ability to debug completed jobs.","D. It would improve executor performance because log sizes would be reduced."],"correct":2,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"history-logs","concept":"history-logs","concept_label":"history-logs","explanation_ja":"History Serverはevent logから完了済みアプリの実行情報を表示する。停止しても保存済みevent log自体が消えるわけではない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/monitoring.html#viewing-after-the-fact","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C. It would result in losing access to historical job logs and limit the ability to debug completed jobs.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe Spark History Server is a component used to view and analyze completed Spark applications. It reads event logs that are written by Spark applications (when event logging is enabled) and presents them through a web UI.\n\nThese logs contain important execution details such as:\n\n- \nJob and stage execution timelines\n\n- \nTask metrics (execution time, shuffle read/write, memory usage)\n\n- \nDAG visualization\n\n- \nFailed task diagnostics\n\n- \nExecutor utilization and performance metrics\n\nIn production environments, the Spark History Server is critical for debugging, performance tuning, and auditing completed jobs.\n\nIf the Spark History Server is disabled, the cluster will no longer provide a web interface to inspect past applications, making it difficult for engineers to troubleshoot failures or analyze performance issues after a job completes.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/monitoring.html#viewing-after-the-fact](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/monitoring.html#viewing-after-the-fact)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. It would prevent driver logs from accumulating during long-running jobs.\nDriver logs are managed separately through logging systems or cluster managers. The Spark History Server does not control driver log accumulation.\n\nB. It would increase job execution speed by reducing logging overhead.\nThe Spark History Server itself does not run during job execution. Applications simply write event logs. The server later reads these logs to display historical information, so disabling it does not meaningfully improve execution speed.\n\nD. It would improve executor performance because log sizes would be reduced.\nExecutor performance is unrelated to the History Server. Executors still generate logs regardless of whether the History Server is running.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark monitoring is typically divided into two parts:\n\n- \nSpark Web UI (live UI)\nShows information about running applications.\n\n- \nSpark History Server\nAllows engineers to inspect completed applications using stored event logs.\n\nFor production Spark clusters, the History Server is essential for post-execution debugging and performance analysis.","source_option_explanations":["A. It would prevent driver logs from accumulating during long-running jobs.\nDriver logs are managed separately through logging systems or cluster managers. The Spark History Server does not control driver log accumulation.","B. It would increase job execution speed by reducing logging overhead.\nThe Spark History Server itself does not run during job execution. Applications simply write event logs. The server later reads these logs to display historical information, so disabling it does not meaningfully improve execution speed.","Correct Answer: C. It would result in losing access to historical job logs and limit the ability to debug completed jobs.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe Spark History Server is a component used to view and analyze completed Spark applications. It reads event logs that are written by Spark applications (when event logging is enabled) and presents them through a web UI.\n\nThese logs contain important execution details such as:\n\n- \nJob and stage execution timelines\n\n- \nTask metrics (execution time, shuffle read/write, memory usage)\n\n- \nDAG visualization\n\n- \nFailed task diagnostics\n\n- \nExecutor utilization and performance metrics","D. It would improve executor performance because log sizes would be reduced.\nExecutor performance is unrelated to the History Server. Executors still generate logs regardless of whether the History Server is running."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/monitoring.html#viewing-after-the-fact"],"images":[],"source_hash":"eb8713710558205a468c3a63f80cd87ca09c9c4a32c345a2a5f3faa36949b009","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"history-server-completed-ui","decision_id":"history-server-completed-ui"}]},{"id":"EX4-S2","title":"原文セット 2","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-021","number":21,"stem":"A data engineer has a PySpark DataFrame named sales_data that contains millions of records. They need to compute the total sales amount grouped by region and want to use Spark SQL to make the logic easier to read and maintain.\n\nWhich steps should the data engineer take to accomplish this task?","options":["A.\nWrite the aggregation directly on the DataFrame using PySpark’s `groupBy` and `agg` methods.","B.\nExport the DataFrame as a CSV file, load it into a relational database, and execute SQL queries there.","C.\nRegister `sales_data` as a temporary view using the `createOrReplaceTempView` method and then run the aggregation using Spark SQL.","D.\nConvert `sales_data` to a Pandas DataFrame and perform the grouping using SQL-like operations in Pandas."],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"temp-view-query","concept":"temp-view-query","concept_label":"temp-view-query","explanation_ja":"DataFrameを一時viewに登録し、spark.sqlから集計できる。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C. Register sales_data as a temporary view using the createOrReplaceTempView method and then run the aggregation using Spark SQL.\n\nExplanation:\n\nWhen a data engineer wants to use Spark SQL syntax on a DataFrame, the standard approach is to register the DataFrame as a temporary view within the SparkSession. Once registered, the DataFrame can be queried using SQL statements through `spark.sql()`.\n\nThe correct steps are:\n\n- \nRegister the DataFrame as a temporary view.\n\n- \nRun a SQL query to perform the aggregation.\n\nExample implementation:\n\n```\nsales_data.createOrReplaceTempView(\"sales_data\")\n \nspark.sql(\"\"\"\nSELECT region, SUM(sales_amount) AS total_sales\nFROM sales_data\nGROUP BY region\n\"\"\").show()\n```\n\nThis approach provides several benefits:\n\n- \nMaintains readability using SQL syntax.\n\n- \nExecutes queries using Spark’s distributed engine.\n\n- \nAllows DataFrame and SQL APIs to be used interchangeably.\n\nSpark SQL operates on the same underlying execution engine as DataFrame APIs, so performance remains distributed and scalable even when using SQL queries.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. Write the aggregation directly on the DataFrame using PySpark’s groupBy and agg methods.\nThis would technically work but does not satisfy the requirement to use Spark SQL for readability and maintainability.\n\nB. Export the DataFrame as a CSV file, load it into a relational database, and execute SQL queries there.\nThis approach is inefficient because it introduces unnecessary data movement and additional systems, defeating the purpose of using Spark for large-scale distributed processing.\n\nD. Convert sales_data to a Pandas DataFrame and perform the grouping using SQL-like operations in Pandas.\nPandas operates on a single machine in memory, which is unsuitable for a DataFrame containing millions of records in a distributed Spark environment. This could lead to memory exhaustion.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark provides two equivalent APIs for structured data processing:\n\n- \nDataFrame API\n\n- \nSpark SQL\n\nBoth APIs generate the same optimized execution plans using Catalyst Optimizer and the Tungsten execution engine, allowing developers to choose whichever interface improves code readability without sacrificing performance.","source_option_explanations":["A. Write the aggregation directly on the DataFrame using PySpark’s groupBy and agg methods.\nThis would technically work but does not satisfy the requirement to use Spark SQL for readability and maintainability.","B. Export the DataFrame as a CSV file, load it into a relational database, and execute SQL queries there.\nThis approach is inefficient because it introduces unnecessary data movement and additional systems, defeating the purpose of using Spark for large-scale distributed processing.","Correct Answer: C. Register sales_data as a temporary view using the createOrReplaceTempView method and then run the aggregation using Spark SQL.\n\nExplanation:\n\nWhen a data engineer wants to use Spark SQL syntax on a DataFrame, the standard approach is to register the DataFrame as a temporary view within the SparkSession. Once registered, the DataFrame can be queried using SQL statements through `spark.sql()`.\n\nThe correct steps are:\n\n- \nRegister the DataFrame as a temporary view.\n\n- \nRun a SQL query to perform the aggregation.\n\nExample implementation:\n\n```\nsales_data.createOrReplaceTempView(\"sales_data\")\n \nspark.sql(\"\"\"\nSELECT region, SUM(sales_amount) AS total_sales\nFROM sales_data\nGROUP BY region\n\"\"\").show()\n```\n\nThis approach provides several benefits:\n\n- \nMaintains readability using SQL syntax.\n\n- \nExecutes queries using Spark’s distributed engine.\n\n- \nAllows DataFrame and SQL APIs to be used interchangeably.","D. Convert sales_data to a Pandas DataFrame and perform the grouping using SQL-like operations in Pandas.\nPandas operates on a single machine in memory, which is unsuitable for a DataFrame containing millions of records in a distributed Spark environment. This could lead to memory exhaustion."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"65e0cb9563193a7b7fd4b672ab2b8777d78daf51de20d3b1b86bf17075aa4c5d","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"dataframe-to-sql-temp-view","decision_id":"dataframe-to-sql-temp-view"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-022","number":22,"stem":"A data engineer wants to control the size of micro-batches when reading a file source with the Available-now micro-batch option in Spark Structured Streaming.\n\nWhich input-source option should the engineer use to control the batch size?","options":["A. maxExecutorsPerTrigger","B. maxCoresPerTrigger","C. maxBytesPerTrigger","D. maxFilesPerTrigger"],"correct":3,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"trigger-input-limit","concept":"trigger-input-limit","concept_label":"trigger-input-limit","explanation_ja":"AvailableNowの処理量はソースオプションで調整する。ファイル入力ではmaxFilesPerTrigger。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#input-sources","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#input-sources","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"998638ff2fa8013e5d5d4f5f6db309d241c166e67205951a2c8c16e517c474fd","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"maxFilesPerTriggerはファイル入力のソースオプション。trigger引数ではない。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#input-sources"],"original_stem":"A data engineer wants to control the size of micro-batches when using the Available-now micro-batch option in Spark Structured Streaming.\n\nWhich trigger configuration should the engineer use to control the batch size?","original_options":["A. maxExecutorsPerTrigger","B. maxCoresPerTrigger","C. maxBytesPerTrigger","D. maxFilesPerTrigger"],"original_correct":["D"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"file-stream-max-files-per-trigger","decision_id":"file-stream-max-files-per-trigger"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-023","number":23,"stem":"What occurs if a developer registers two different DataFrames with the same view name myView using the createOrReplaceTempView method?","options":["A.\nBoth DataFrames become accessible simultaneously under the same view name.","B.\nThe two DataFrames are merged into a single view named myView.","C.\nAn exception is raised indicating that a duplicate view named myView already exists.","D.\nThe existing view is replaced by the new DataFrame registered with the same view name."],"correct":3,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"temp-view-replace","concept":"temp-view-replace","concept_label":"temp-view-replace","explanation_ja":"createOrReplaceTempViewは同名viewを置き換える。DataFrame同士をunionしない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html#running-sql-queries-programmatically","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. The existing view is replaced by the new DataFrame registered with the same view name.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, the method `createOrReplaceTempView()` registers a DataFrame as a temporary view within the current SparkSession. The key behavior of this method is that it will create the view if it does not exist, or replace the existing view if a view with the same name already exists.\n\nExample behavior:\n\n```\ndf1.createOrReplaceTempView(\"myView\")\ndf2.createOrReplaceTempView(\"myView\")\n```\n\nAfter the second statement executes:\n\n- \nThe original view created from `df1` is replaced\n\n- \nThe view `myView` now references df2\n\nOnly one view with that name exists at any time within the session catalog.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html#running-sql-queries-programmatically](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html#running-sql-queries-programmatically)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. Both DataFrames become accessible simultaneously under the same view name.\nSpark does not allow multiple views with the same name within the same catalog namespace. Registering the second view replaces the first one.\n\nB. The two DataFrames are merged into a single view named myView.\n`createOrReplaceTempView()` does not merge datasets. It only registers a DataFrame as a SQL-accessible view.\n\nC. An exception is raised indicating that a duplicate view named myView already exists.\nAn exception would occur if the method `createTempView()` were used instead. However, `createOrReplaceTempView()` is specifically designed to avoid this error by replacing the existing view.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark provides two similar methods for creating temporary views:\n\n- \ncreateTempView()\n\n- \nFails if the view already exists\n\n- \ncreateOrReplaceTempView()\n\n- \nCreates the view or replaces the existing one\n\nThis behavior is useful in iterative workflows and notebooks where the same view name may be reused during data transformations.","source_option_explanations":["A. Both DataFrames become accessible simultaneously under the same view name.\nSpark does not allow multiple views with the same name within the same catalog namespace. Registering the second view replaces the first one.","B. The two DataFrames are merged into a single view named myView.\n`createOrReplaceTempView()` does not merge datasets. It only registers a DataFrame as a SQL-accessible view.","C. An exception is raised indicating that a duplicate view named myView already exists.\nAn exception would occur if the method `createTempView()` were used instead. However, `createOrReplaceTempView()` is specifically designed to avoid this error by replacing the existing view.","Correct Answer: D. The existing view is replaced by the new DataFrame registered with the same view name.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, the method `createOrReplaceTempView()` registers a DataFrame as a temporary view within the current SparkSession. The key behavior of this method is that it will create the view if it does not exist, or replace the existing view if a view with the same name already exists.\n\nExample behavior:\n\n```\ndf1.createOrReplaceTempView(\"myView\")\ndf2.createOrReplaceTempView(\"myView\")\n```\n\nAfter the second statement executes:\n\n- \nThe original view created from `df1` is replaced\n\n- \nThe view `myView` now references df2"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-getting-started.html#running-sql-queries-programmatically"],"images":[],"source_hash":"2191850154925cbe466595e27f5bfdb8db6e16e39e6709f25f584924df5da9e4","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"temp-view-replace","decision_id":"temp-view-replace"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-024","number":24,"stem":"A data engineer needs to overwrite the existing underlying data stored at the location path/to/data.\n\nWhich code snippet should be used to overwrite the data at the target path?","options":["A.\nINSERT 'path/to/data' USING parquet OPTIONS (col1 1, col2 2, col3 'test')\nSELECT * FROM source_data;","B.\nINSERT OVERWRITE DIRECTORY 'path/to/data' USING parquet OPTIONS (col1 1, col2 2, col3 'test')\nSELECT * FROM source_data;","C.\nINSERT OVERWRITE 'path/to/data' USING parquet OPTIONS (col1 1, col2 2, col3 'test')\nSELECT * FROM source_data;","D.\nOVERWRITE 'path/to/data' USING parquet OPTIONS (col1 1, col2 2, col3 'test')\nSELECT * FROM source_data;"],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"write-overwrite","concept":"write-overwrite","concept_label":"write-overwrite","explanation_ja":"ディレクトリへの上書きSQLはINSERT OVERWRITE DIRECTORY。ここでは対応形式Parquetを使う。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-dml-insert-overwrite-directory.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-dml-insert-overwrite-directory.html","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/migration/etl","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-syntax-dml-insert-overwrite-directory.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"f9d4b34c776901037e3fdb65973ea9064056b7076c934e8f74301e6a7700da79","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"DeltaへのINSERT OVERWRITE DIRECTORYは対応しないため、同構文に対応するParquetへ修正。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-dml-insert-overwrite-directory.html","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/migration/etl"],"original_stem":"A data engineer needs to overwrite the existing underlying data stored at the location path/to/data.\n\nWhich code snippet should be used to overwrite the data at the target path?","original_options":["A.\nINSERT 'path/to/data' USING delta OPTIONS (col1 1, col2 2, col3 'test')\nSELECT * FROM source_data;","B.\nINSERT OVERWRITE DIRECTORY 'path/to/data' USING delta OPTIONS (col1 1, col2 2, col3 'test')\nSELECT * FROM source_data;","C.\nINSERT OVERWRITE 'path/to/data' USING delta OPTIONS (col1 1, col2 2, col3 'test')\nSELECT * FROM source_data;","D.\nOVERWRITE 'path/to/data' USING delta OPTIONS (col1 1, col2 2, col3 'test')\nSELECT * FROM source_data;"],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"sql-insert-overwrite-directory","decision_id":"sql-insert-overwrite-directory"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-025","number":25,"stem":"A developer needs to work with large-scale structured data that cannot fit into the memory of a single machine. The developer must also run queries involving complex aggregations and transformations on this dataset.\n\nWhich two Apache Spark modules or components provide the functionality required for this scenario? (Choose two.)","options":["A.\nStructured Streaming","B.\nSpark SQL","C.\nDataFrames","D.\nGraphX","E.\nMLlib"],"correct":1,"correct_many":[1,2],"domain":"Architecture","topic":"spark-modules","concept":"spark-modules","concept_label":"spark-modules","explanation_ja":"Spark SQLとDataFrame APIは大規模structured dataの問合せ・変換を担う。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answers: B and C\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe scenario requires:\n\n- \nProcessing large-scale structured data\n\n- \nRunning complex aggregations and transformations\n\n- \nHandling datasets larger than a single machine's memory\n\nTwo Spark components designed specifically for these needs are Spark SQL and DataFrames.\n\nB. Spark SQL\n\nSpark SQL is the module that enables Spark to process structured data using SQL queries and relational operations. It supports:\n\n- \nComplex aggregations (`GROUP BY`, `SUM`, `AVG`)\n\n- \nJoins and filters\n\n- \nSQL-based analytics on large datasets\n\nSpark SQL also uses the Catalyst Optimizer, which analyzes query plans and automatically optimizes execution strategies such as predicate pushdown and join optimization.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html)\n\nC. DataFrames\n\nDataFrames provide a distributed tabular abstraction similar to relational tables. They allow developers to perform structured data transformations using a high-level API while leveraging Spark’s distributed computation engine.\n\nKey benefits include:\n\n- \nSchema-aware data processing\n\n- \nEfficient distributed execution\n\n- \nIntegration with Spark SQL\n\n- \nOptimization through the Catalyst optimizer and Tungsten execution engine\n\nExample operations include:\n\n- \n`select()`\n\n- \n`groupBy()`\n\n- \n`agg()`\n\n- \n`join()`\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#dataframes](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#dataframes)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. Structured Streaming\nStructured Streaming is used for real-time data stream processing, not primarily for batch analytics on large structured datasets.\n\nD. GraphX\nGraphX is Spark’s graph-processing library used for graph algorithms such as PageRank and connected components. It is not intended for relational aggregations or structured data transformations.\n\nE. MLlib\nMLlib provides machine learning algorithms and pipelines. While it can operate on structured data, it is designed for model training and analytics, not general-purpose data transformation and aggregation queries.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark provides multiple APIs for structured data processing:\n\n- \nDataFrames provide a distributed tabular abstraction.\n\n- \nSpark SQL enables SQL queries on structured datasets.\n\nBoth share the same underlying execution engine and optimizations, allowing developers to perform large-scale structured data processing efficiently across distributed clusters.","source_option_explanations":["A. Structured Streaming\nStructured Streaming is used for real-time data stream processing, not primarily for batch analytics on large structured datasets.","B. Spark SQL\n\nSpark SQL is the module that enables Spark to process structured data using SQL queries and relational operations. It supports:\n\n- \nComplex aggregations (`GROUP BY`, `SUM`, `AVG`)\n\n- \nJoins and filters\n\n- \nSQL-based analytics on large datasets\n\nSpark SQL also uses the Catalyst Optimizer, which analyzes query plans and automatically optimizes execution strategies such as predicate pushdown and join optimization.","C. DataFrames\n\nDataFrames provide a distributed tabular abstraction similar to relational tables. They allow developers to perform structured data transformations using a high-level API while leveraging Spark’s distributed computation engine.\n\nKey benefits include:\n\n- \nSchema-aware data processing\n\n- \nEfficient distributed execution\n\n- \nIntegration with Spark SQL\n\n- \nOptimization through the Catalyst optimizer and Tungsten execution engine","D. GraphX\nGraphX is Spark’s graph-processing library used for graph algorithms such as PageRank and connected components. It is not intended for relational aggregations or structured data transformations.","E. MLlib\nMLlib provides machine learning algorithms and pipelines. While it can operate on structured data, it is designed for model training and analytics, not general-purpose data transformation and aggregation queries."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#dataframes"],"images":[],"source_hash":"11dda169f6590636f9371a6e9009f36586e5c9611d087e33c6d82a207f82e117","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"sql-dataframe-modules","decision_id":"sql-dataframe-modules"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-026","number":26,"stem":"A data engineer is reviewing a scheduled Spark application that has recently started running slower than usual. After examining the query plan, the engineer observes that Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) is no longer converting a sort-merge join into a broadcast join, which is now causing additional shuffle operations in the Spark job.\n\nWhat is the most likely reason for this change in the application's behavior?","options":["A. The size of the smaller table has increased beyond the configured threshold at which AQE can convert the join into a broadcast join.","B. It has been too long since `ANALYZE TABLE` was executed, resulting in outdated table statistics.","C. A Spark version upgrade has disabled AQE.","D. The distribution of the join keys between the two tables has become increasingly skewed over time."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"aqe-broadcast","concept":"aqe-broadcast","concept_label":"aqe-broadcast","explanation_ja":"AQEのbroadcast変換はruntimeのサイズとしきい値に依存する。表がしきい値を超えると変換できなくなる。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#adaptive-query-execution","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A. The size of the smaller table has increased beyond the configured threshold at which AQE can convert the join into a broadcast join.\n\nExplanation:\n\nAdaptive Query Execution (AQE) is a Spark optimization feature that dynamically adjusts query plans during execution based on runtime statistics. One of the optimizations AQE performs is converting a sort-merge join into a broadcast join when it detects that one side of the join is small enough to broadcast.\n\nSpark uses a configuration parameter called `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` to determine the maximum size of a dataset that can be broadcast to executors. If the smaller table exceeds this threshold, Spark cannot broadcast it and will instead use a sort-merge join, which requires a shuffle.\n\nIf the dataset that was previously small enough to broadcast has grown beyond the broadcast threshold, AQE will stop converting the join to a broadcast join. As a result, Spark falls back to a shuffle-based join, causing the additional shuffle operations observed in the query plan.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#adaptive-query-execution](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#adaptive-query-execution)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nB. It has been too long since ANALYZE TABLE was executed, resulting in outdated table statistics.\nAQE relies primarily on runtime statistics, not just catalog statistics collected by `ANALYZE TABLE`. Even without refreshed statistics, AQE can determine dataset sizes during execution and make join strategy decisions.\n\nC. A Spark version upgrade has disabled AQE.\nAQE is enabled by default in modern Spark versions. A version upgrade would not normally disable it unless the configuration `spark.sql.adaptive.enabled` was explicitly turned off, which is not indicated in the scenario.\n\nD. The distribution of the join keys between the two tables has become increasingly skewed over time.\nData skew can affect join performance and trigger other AQE optimizations (such as skew join handling), but it does not prevent Spark from converting a join to a broadcast join if the table size is still within the broadcast threshold.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark supports several join strategies:\n\n- \nBroadcast Hash Join\n\n- \nUsed when one table is small enough to broadcast\n\n- \nAvoids shuffle\n\n- \nFastest for large-small joins\n\n- \nSort-Merge Join\n\n- \nUsed when both tables are large\n\n- \nRequires shuffle and sorting\n\n- \nMore expensive\n\nAQE dynamically chooses between these strategies based on runtime data sizes, which is why increases in dataset size can change the selected join strategy.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A. The size of the smaller table has increased beyond the configured threshold at which AQE can convert the join into a broadcast join.\n\nExplanation:\n\nAdaptive Query Execution (AQE) is a Spark optimization feature that dynamically adjusts query plans during execution based on runtime statistics. One of the optimizations AQE performs is converting a sort-merge join into a broadcast join when it detects that one side of the join is small enough to broadcast.\n\nSpark uses a configuration parameter called `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` to determine the maximum size of a dataset that can be broadcast to executors. If the smaller table exceeds this threshold, Spark cannot broadcast it and will instead use a sort-merge join, which requires a shuffle.\n\nIf the dataset that was previously small enough to broadcast has grown beyond the broadcast threshold, AQE will stop converting the join to a broadcast join. As a result, Spark falls back to a shuffle-based join, causing the additional shuffle operations observed in the query plan.","B. It has been too long since ANALYZE TABLE was executed, resulting in outdated table statistics.\nAQE relies primarily on runtime statistics, not just catalog statistics collected by `ANALYZE TABLE`. Even without refreshed statistics, AQE can determine dataset sizes during execution and make join strategy decisions.","C. A Spark version upgrade has disabled AQE.\nAQE is enabled by default in modern Spark versions. A version upgrade would not normally disable it unless the configuration `spark.sql.adaptive.enabled` was explicitly turned off, which is not indicated in the scenario.","D. The distribution of the join keys between the two tables has become increasingly skewed over time.\nData skew can affect join performance and trigger other AQE optimizations (such as skew join handling), but it does not prevent Spark from converting a join to a broadcast join if the table size is still within the broadcast threshold."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#adaptive-query-execution"],"images":[],"source_hash":"762b0087b7c5ceaf6e09007e9ed1faddaf4a9676a550853e9a19076c3cd9e1a5","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"aqe-broadcast-threshold-crossed","decision_id":"aqe-broadcast-threshold-crossed"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-027","number":27,"stem":"A data engineer is processing streaming data where records arrive at irregular intervals. The engineer needs to remove duplicate records based on the `order_id` field, with duplicate copies of an order arriving within a known bounded event-time interval. The solution must also handle late-arriving data efficiently and limit excessive memory usage.\n\nWhich strategy should the engineer use to perform this de-duplication?","options":["A. Apply `withWatermark()` with a delay greater than the maximum event-time difference between duplicate copies, then use `dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark([\"order_id\"])`.","B.\nUse `dropDuplicates()` on the timestamp column to remove duplicates based on the most recent timestamp.","C.\nDe-duplication is not supported in streaming scenarios when late-arriving data must be considered.","D.\nUse `dropDuplicates()` on `order_id` without applying watermarking to eliminate duplicates."],"correct":0,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"stream-dedup-watermark","concept":"stream-dedup-watermark","concept_label":"stream-dedup-watermark","explanation_ja":"watermark付きdedupは状態サイズを制限するが、最新行を保持する処理ではない。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#streaming-deduplication","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#streaming-deduplication","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#streaming-deduplication"],"images":[],"source_hash":"ee64e5594a0fbb4b3d576552c12793dd5083d773155f97ec2468420a5907ad8c","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"watermark付きdedupは最新レコード保持を保証しない。イベント時刻が重複キーに含まれない場合は3.5のWithinWatermark APIを使う。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#streaming-deduplication"],"original_stem":"A data engineer is processing streaming data where records arrive at irregular intervals. The engineer needs to remove duplicate records based on the `order_id` field, while ensuring that the most recent records are retained. The solution must also handle late-arriving data efficiently and limit excessive memory usage.\n\nWhich strategy should the engineer use to perform this de-duplication?","original_options":["A.\nApply `withWatermark()` and then use `dropDuplicates()` on `order_id` to remove duplicate records.","B.\nUse `dropDuplicates()` on the timestamp column to remove duplicates based on the most recent timestamp.","C.\nDe-duplication is not supported in streaming scenarios when late-arriving data must be considered.","D.\nUse `dropDuplicates()` on `order_id` without applying watermarking to eliminate duplicates."],"original_correct":["A"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"watermark-dedup","decision_id":"watermark-dedup"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-028","number":28,"stem":"A developer has a file named employee.csv that contains the columns ID, Name, and Salary. The developer needs to load this file into a DataFrame and explicitly define the schema so that the columns are interpreted as Integer, String, and Float types respectively.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly accomplishes this requirement?","options":["A.\n\n```\nschema = StructType([\n    StructField(\"ID\", IntegerType(), True),\n    StructField(\"Name\", StringType(), True)],\n    StructField(\"Salary\", FloatType(), True)\n)\n \ndf = spark.read.option(\"header\", \"true\") \\\n    .csv(\"employee.csv\")\n```","B.\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.option(\"inferSchema\", \"true\") \\\n    .csv(\"employee.csv\")\n```","C.\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.option(\"header\", \"true\") \\\n    .csv(\"employee.csv\", schema=\"ID INT, Name STRING, Salary FLOAT\")\n```","D.\n\n```\nschema = StructType([\n    StructField(\"ID\", IntegerType(), True),\n    StructField(\"Name\", StringType(), True),\n    StructField(\"Salary\", FloatType(), True)\n])\n \ndf = spark.read.option(\"header\", \"true\") \\\n    .csv(\"employee.csv\", schema=schema)\n```"],"correct":2,"correct_many":[2,3],"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"csv-schema","concept":"csv-schema","concept_label":"csv-schema","explanation_ja":"StructTypeとDDL文字列の両方で明示schemaを渡せる。型推論との違いを確認する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.csv.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.csv.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-csv.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"ada53ea5adaa05dddb41e03d168e664938b88589865bd92d6a904f77265f2f4b","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"CSV schemaはStructTypeだけでなくDDL文字列も受け付ける。CとDが有効。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.csv.html"],"original_stem":"A developer has a file named employee.csv that contains the columns ID, Name, and Salary. The developer needs to load this file into a DataFrame and explicitly define the schema so that the columns are interpreted as Integer, String, and Float types respectively.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly accomplishes this requirement?","original_options":["A.\n\n```\nschema = StructType([\n    StructField(\"ID\", IntegerType(), True),\n    StructField(\"Name\", StringType(), True)],\n    StructField(\"Salary\", FloatType(), True)\n)\n \ndf = spark.read.option(\"header\", \"true\") \\\n    .csv(\"employee.csv\")\n```","B.\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.option(\"inferSchema\", \"true\") \\\n    .csv(\"employee.csv\")\n```","C.\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.option(\"header\", \"true\") \\\n    .csv(\"employee.csv\", schema=\"ID INT, Name STRING, Salary FLOAT\")\n```","D.\n\n```\nschema = StructType([\n    StructField(\"ID\", IntegerType(), True),\n    StructField(\"Name\", StringType(), True),\n    StructField(\"Salary\", FloatType(), True)\n])\n \ndf = spark.read.option(\"header\", \"true\") \\\n    .csv(\"employee.csv\", schema=schema)\n```"],"original_correct":["D"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"csv-schema","decision_id":"csv-schema"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-029","number":29,"stem":"Consider the following code fragment:\n\n```\ndiv_by_2 = my_df.filter(\"number % 2 = 0\")\nprint(div_by_2)\n```\n\nThe output of this code is:\n\n```\nDataFrame[number: bigint]\n```\n\nWhich statement best explains this output in the context of Apache Spark’s execution model?","options":["A.\nThe `filter` transformation immediately triggers computation and returns only the rows that satisfy the condition.","B.\nAll transformations in Spark are lazy, meaning they do not compute results immediately.","C.\nAfter every transformation in Spark, the results are processed automatically using an action.","D.\nThe `filter` operation is an action in Spark and immediately executes the filtering operation on `my_df`, returning the results as `div_by_2`."],"correct":1,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"lazy-execution","concept":"lazy-execution","concept_label":"lazy-execution","explanation_ja":"print(DataFrame)は通常スキーマの表現を示す。行を取得するactionとは別。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations-and-actions","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B. All transformations in Spark are lazy, meaning they do not compute results immediately.\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark follows a lazy evaluation model. Transformations such as `filter()`, `map()`, `select()`, and `groupBy()` do not execute immediately. Instead, Spark builds a logical execution plan (DAG) describing the sequence of operations to perform.\n\nIn the code:\n\n```\ndiv_by_2 = my_df.filter(\"number % 2 = 0\")\nprint(div_by_2)\n```\n\n- \n`filter()` is a transformation.\n\n- \nSpark records the transformation but does not execute it yet.\n\n- \nThe variable `div_by_2` therefore holds a DataFrame representing the planned transformation, not the computed result.\n\nWhen `print(div_by_2)` is executed, Spark prints the DataFrame schema representation, which is why the output appears as:\n\n```\nDataFrame[number: bigint]\n```\n\nThe filtering operation will only be executed when an action is triggered, such as:\n\n- \n`show()`\n\n- \n`collect()`\n\n- \n`count()`\n\n- \n`write()`\n\nExample that triggers execution:\n\n```\ndiv_by_2.show()\n```\n\nAt that point, Spark executes the entire DAG and computes the results.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations-and-actions](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations-and-actions)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA. The filter transformation immediately triggers computation and returns only the rows that satisfy the condition.\nThis is incorrect because transformations are lazy and do not execute immediately.\n\nC. After every transformation in Spark, the results are processed automatically using an action.\nSpark does not automatically run actions after transformations. Actions must be explicitly called by the user.\n\nD. The filter operation is an action in Spark and immediately executes the filtering operation.\n`filter()` is a transformation, not an action. It only defines the computation plan.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark operations are divided into two categories:\n\nTransformations\n\n- \nLazy operations that build a DAG\n\n- \nExamples: `filter`, `map`, `select`, `groupBy`\n\nActions\n\n- \nTrigger execution of the DAG\n\n- \nExamples: `show`, `count`, `collect`, `write`\n\nThis lazy execution model allows Spark to optimize the entire computation plan before execution, improving performance.","source_option_explanations":["A. The filter transformation immediately triggers computation and returns only the rows that satisfy the condition.\nThis is incorrect because transformations are lazy and do not execute immediately.","Correct Answer: B. All transformations in Spark are lazy, meaning they do not compute results immediately.\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark follows a lazy evaluation model. Transformations such as `filter()`, `map()`, `select()`, and `groupBy()` do not execute immediately. Instead, Spark builds a logical execution plan (DAG) describing the sequence of operations to perform.\n\nIn the code:\n\n```\ndiv_by_2 = my_df.filter(\"number % 2 = 0\")\nprint(div_by_2)\n```\n\n- \n`filter()` is a transformation.\n\n- \nSpark records the transformation but does not execute it yet.\n\n- \nThe variable `div_by_2` therefore holds a DataFrame representing the planned transformation, not the computed result.\n\nWhen `print(div_by_2)` is executed, Spark prints the DataFrame schema representation, which is why the output appears as:\n\n```\nDataFrame[number: bigint]\n```\n\nThe filtering operation will only be executed when an action is triggered, such as:\n\n- \n`show()`\n\n- \n`collect()`\n\n- \n`count()`\n\n- \n`write()`","C. After every transformation in Spark, the results are processed automatically using an action.\nSpark does not automatically run actions after transformations. Actions must be explicitly called by the user.","D. The filter operation is an action in Spark and immediately executes the filtering operation.\n`filter()` is a transformation, not an action. It only defines the computation plan."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations-and-actions"],"images":[],"source_hash":"4a485baa580fcecb5fd8861d3901502d4d0f2660001a338d537712056f57baf0","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"lazy-action-trigger","decision_id":"lazy-action-trigger"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-030","number":30,"stem":"Which feature of Apache Spark’s execution model delays the execution of transformations and allows Spark to optimize the execution plan before an action is triggered?","options":["A.\nImmediate Execution","B.\nLazy Evaluation with the Catalyst Optimizer","C.\nTransformations Pre-Caching","D.\nParallel Execution of Transformations"],"correct":1,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"lazy-execution","concept":"lazy-execution","concept_label":"lazy-execution","explanation_ja":"lazy evaluationにより変換を先に組み立て、action時に最適化された計画を実行する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#catalyst-optimizer","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B. Lazy Evaluation with the Catalyst Optimizer\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark uses lazy evaluation, meaning that transformations such as `filter()`, `map()`, `select()`, and `groupBy()` are not executed immediately. Instead, Spark records these operations and builds a logical execution plan (a DAG) describing how the data should be processed.\n\nExecution only begins when an action is triggered, such as:\n\n- \n`show()`\n\n- \n`count()`\n\n- \n`collect()`\n\n- \n`write()`\n\nBefore executing the plan, Spark applies optimizations through the Catalyst Optimizer, which analyzes the logical plan and produces an optimized physical execution plan. Catalyst performs optimizations such as:\n\n- \nPredicate pushdown\n\n- \nColumn pruning\n\n- \nJoin reordering\n\n- \nConstant folding\n\nThis combination of lazy evaluation and Catalyst optimization allows Spark to optimize the entire query pipeline before execution, improving performance.\n\nExample concept:\n\n```\ndf.filter(\"age > 30\").groupBy(\"region\").count()\n```\n\nSpark records the transformations but does not execute them until an action like `show()` or `write()` is called.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#catalyst-optimizer](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#catalyst-optimizer)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. Immediate Execution\nSpark does not execute transformations immediately. Immediate execution would prevent Spark from optimizing the full execution plan.\n\nC. Transformations Pre-Caching\nCaching stores intermediate results in memory for reuse, but it does not delay execution or optimize query plans.\n\nD. Parallel Execution of Transformations\nSpark executes tasks in parallel across executors, but parallel execution is not the feature responsible for delaying transformations or optimizing the plan.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark operations are divided into two categories:\n\nTransformations\n\n- \nLazy operations that build a DAG\n\n- \nExamples: `filter`, `map`, `groupBy`\n\nActions\n\n- \nTrigger execution of the DAG\n\n- \nExamples: `count`, `collect`, `show`, `write`\n\nLazy evaluation enables Spark to optimize the full execution plan before computation begins, which is a key reason for Spark’s performance advantages.","source_option_explanations":["A. Immediate Execution\nSpark does not execute transformations immediately. Immediate execution would prevent Spark from optimizing the full execution plan.","Correct Answer: B. Lazy Evaluation with the Catalyst Optimizer\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark uses lazy evaluation, meaning that transformations such as `filter()`, `map()`, `select()`, and `groupBy()` are not executed immediately. Instead, Spark records these operations and builds a logical execution plan (a DAG) describing how the data should be processed.\n\nExecution only begins when an action is triggered, such as:\n\n- \n`show()`\n\n- \n`count()`\n\n- \n`collect()`\n\n- \n`write()`\n\nBefore executing the plan, Spark applies optimizations through the Catalyst Optimizer, which analyzes the logical plan and produces an optimized physical execution plan. Catalyst performs optimizations such as:\n\n- \nPredicate pushdown\n\n- \nColumn pruning\n\n- \nJoin reordering\n\n- \nConstant folding","C. Transformations Pre-Caching\nCaching stores intermediate results in memory for reuse, but it does not delay execution or optimize query plans.","D. Parallel Execution of Transformations\nSpark executes tasks in parallel across executors, but parallel execution is not the feature responsible for delaying transformations or optimizing the plan."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#catalyst-optimizer"],"images":[],"source_hash":"d66abb8703dcc495969f933b5ae95c6c6f4a2d2d7d6e3aa5067d3eebd7b12777","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"lazy-action-trigger","decision_id":"lazy-action-trigger"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-031","number":31,"stem":"What is a defining characteristic of client mode in Apache Spark?","options":["A.\nIn client mode, tasks are sent directly from the client to worker nodes, improving performance by reducing communication overhead.","B.\nIn client mode, the driver runs on the client machine, which may introduce higher latency due to network communication.","C.\nIn client mode, the driver runs on the cluster manager, enabling resource usage across multiple machines.","D.\nIn client mode, the driver runs on the machine that submits the application, which simplifies debugging but may limit available resources."],"correct":1,"correct_many":[1,3],"domain":"Architecture","topic":"deployment-mode","concept":"deployment-mode","concept_label":"deployment-mode","explanation_ja":"client modeではdriverがsubmit側に存在する。BとDは同じ配置を説明する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/submitting-applications.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/submitting-applications.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"b0421b9242df0dc4ecd0a843a706ce41c8f8da73af6570ca393c5e260d4d5c73","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"BもDもclient modeのdriver配置を正しく説明している。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/submitting-applications.html"],"original_stem":"What is a defining characteristic of client mode in Apache Spark?","original_options":["A.\nIn client mode, tasks are sent directly from the client to worker nodes, improving performance by reducing communication overhead.","B.\nIn client mode, the driver runs on the client machine, which may introduce higher latency due to network communication.","C.\nIn client mode, the driver runs on the cluster manager, enabling resource usage across multiple machines.","D.\nIn client mode, the driver runs on the machine that submits the application, which simplifies debugging but may limit available resources."],"original_correct":["D"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"client-cluster-driver-placement","decision_id":"client-cluster-driver-placement"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-032","number":32,"stem":"A DataFrame df contains null values in the salary column.\n\nWhich command will replace all null values in this column with 0?","options":["A.\ndf.fillna(\"salary\", 0)","B.\ndf.replaceNulls(\"salary\", 0)","C.\ndf.dropna(subset=[\"salary\"])","D.\ndf.fillna({\"salary\": 0})"],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"null-fill","concept":"null-fill","concept_label":"null-fill","explanation_ja":"fillnaは辞書で列別に置換値を渡せる。dropnaはNULL行を削除する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.fillna.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. df.fillna({\"salary\": 0})\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn PySpark, the `fillna()` method is used to replace `NULL` values in a DataFrame. It allows specifying either:\n\n- \nA single value to apply to all columns, or\n\n- \nA dictionary mapping column names to replacement values\n\nTo replace null values specifically in the `salary` column with `0`, the correct syntax is:\n\n```\ndf = df.fillna({\"salary\": 0})\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark scans the `salary` column.\n\n- \nAll `NULL` values are replaced with `0`.\n\n- \nOther columns remain unchanged.\n\nThis method is efficient because it operates using Spark’s distributed DataFrame execution model.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.fillna.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.fillna.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. `df.fillna(\"salary\", 0)`\nThis syntax is incorrect. `fillna()` expects either a single value or a dictionary mapping columns to values, not `(column, value)` arguments.\n\nB. `df.replaceNulls(\"salary\", 0)`\nThere is no method named `replaceNulls()` in the PySpark DataFrame API.\n\nC. `df.dropna(subset=[\"salary\"])`\nThis command removes rows containing null values in the `salary` column rather than replacing them with `0`.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark provides two common methods for handling missing values:\n\n- \nfillna()\n\n- \nReplaces null values with a specified value\n\n- \ndropna()\n\n- \nRemoves rows containing null values\n\nUsing `fillna()` is preferred when missing values should be imputed rather than removed, especially in analytics or machine learning pipelines.","source_option_explanations":["A. `df.fillna(\"salary\", 0)`\nThis syntax is incorrect. `fillna()` expects either a single value or a dictionary mapping columns to values, not `(column, value)` arguments.","B. `df.replaceNulls(\"salary\", 0)`\nThere is no method named `replaceNulls()` in the PySpark DataFrame API.","C. `df.dropna(subset=[\"salary\"])`\nThis command removes rows containing null values in the `salary` column rather than replacing them with `0`.","Correct Answer: D. df.fillna({\"salary\": 0})\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn PySpark, the `fillna()` method is used to replace `NULL` values in a DataFrame. It allows specifying either:\n\n- \nA single value to apply to all columns, or\n\n- \nA dictionary mapping column names to replacement values\n\nTo replace null values specifically in the `salary` column with `0`, the correct syntax is:\n\n```\ndf = df.fillna({\"salary\": 0})\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark scans the `salary` column.\n\n- \nAll `NULL` values are replaced with `0`.\n\n- \nOther columns remain unchanged."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.fillna.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"5a37b0f3b576bd7de0a4a71089bc5ddcfc7ca2a236ee78addf734f7b9ec35358","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"null-fill-api","decision_id":"fill-null-specified-column"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-033","number":33,"stem":"A data analyst at an e-commerce company needs to process daily sales data consisting of approximately 50,000 records stored in a single CSV file, with a total size of around 20 MB. The analyst must perform aggregations and generate a summary report.\n\nWhich approach would be most appropriate for this scenario?","options":["A.\nDeploy a real-time streaming pipeline using Spark Streaming to process the incoming data.","B.\nUse a local Python script with the pandas library to load and analyze the CSV file.","C.\nSet up Apache Spark on a distributed cluster to process the data in parallel.","D.\nConfigure a Hadoop ecosystem with HDFS and MapReduce to perform distributed processing."],"correct":1,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"spark-fit","concept":"spark-fit","concept_label":"spark-fit","explanation_ja":"20MB程度の単一ファイルでは、通常はpandas等の単一マシン処理のほうが構成負担が小さい。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.read_csv.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B. Use a local Python script with the pandas library to load and analyze the CSV file.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe dataset described in the scenario is:\n\n- \n50,000 records\n\n- \n~20 MB file size\n\n- \nStored in a single CSV file\n\nThis is a small dataset that easily fits into memory on a single machine. Using distributed systems such as Spark or Hadoop would introduce unnecessary complexity and overhead.\n\nA simple Python script using pandas is the most appropriate solution because:\n\n- \npandas loads datasets directly into memory\n\n- \nIt provides efficient aggregation functions (`groupby`, `sum`, `mean`, etc.)\n\n- \nIt requires minimal setup\n\n- \nIt executes faster than distributed frameworks for small datasets\n\nExample approach:\n\n```\nimport pandas as pd\n \ndf = pd.read_csv(\"sales.csv\")\nsummary = df.groupby(\"region\")[\"sales_amount\"].sum()\nprint(summary)\n```\n\nFor datasets of this size, pandas is typically faster and simpler than Spark because Spark introduces overhead related to cluster coordination and distributed execution.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.read_csv.html](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.read_csv.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. Deploy a real-time streaming pipeline using Spark Streaming to process the incoming data.\nStreaming pipelines are used for continuous real-time data ingestion, not for processing a small static file.\n\nC. Set up Apache Spark on a distributed cluster to process the data in parallel.\nSpark is optimized for large-scale distributed datasets (GBs to PBs). Using Spark for a 20 MB dataset would introduce unnecessary overhead.\n\nD. Configure a Hadoop ecosystem with HDFS and MapReduce to perform distributed processing.\nHadoop and MapReduce are designed for massive distributed batch processing. Setting up such infrastructure for a 20 MB dataset would be excessive and inefficient.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nData processing tools should be selected based on data size and workload complexity:\n\n- \nSmall datasets (MBs) → pandas / local scripts\n\n- \nMedium datasets (GBs) → single-node optimized processing\n\n- \nLarge datasets (TBs+) → distributed frameworks such as Spark\n\nUsing distributed systems for small workloads can actually reduce performance due to orchestration overhead.","source_option_explanations":["A. Deploy a real-time streaming pipeline using Spark Streaming to process the incoming data.\nStreaming pipelines are used for continuous real-time data ingestion, not for processing a small static file.","Correct Answer: B. Use a local Python script with the pandas library to load and analyze the CSV file.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe dataset described in the scenario is:\n\n- \n50,000 records\n\n- \n~20 MB file size\n\n- \nStored in a single CSV file\n\nThis is a small dataset that easily fits into memory on a single machine. Using distributed systems such as Spark or Hadoop would introduce unnecessary complexity and overhead.\n\nA simple Python script using pandas is the most appropriate solution because:\n\n- \npandas loads datasets directly into memory\n\n- \nIt provides efficient aggregation functions (`groupby`, `sum`, `mean`, etc.)\n\n- \nIt requires minimal setup\n\n- \nIt executes faster than distributed frameworks for small datasets","C. Set up Apache Spark on a distributed cluster to process the data in parallel.\nSpark is optimized for large-scale distributed datasets (GBs to PBs). Using Spark for a 20 MB dataset would introduce unnecessary overhead.","D. Configure a Hadoop ecosystem with HDFS and MapReduce to perform distributed processing.\nHadoop and MapReduce are designed for massive distributed batch processing. Setting up such infrastructure for a 20 MB dataset would be excessive and inefficient."],"references":["https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.read_csv.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"ac87ce925ad5bcd1544141f1f0815c96873c53bd962d5343f415ceafb96b6ba3","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"small-data-local-pandas-fit","decision_id":"small-data-local-pandas-fit"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-034","number":34,"stem":"A data engineer is tuning a Spark application that performs the following sequence of operations:\n\n- \nReads data from distributed storage into a DataFrame.\n\n- \nApplies a filter transformation to remove unwanted records.\n\n- \nPerforms a join with another large DataFrame.\n\n- \nApplies groupBy and agg operations to aggregate the data.\n\n- \nWrites the final results to storage.\n\nWhich two statements correctly describe how Apache Spark builds the execution hierarchy for this application? (Choose two.)","options":["A.\nThe groupBy and agg operations may trigger a shuffle, which can create additional stages in the execution plan.","B.\nThe entire application will execute as a single job triggered by the write action.","C.\nThe DataFrame API removes all shuffle operations through automatic optimization.","D.\nThe join operation can cause a shuffle, resulting in a new stage in the execution plan.","E.\nThe filter and join transformations will run in the same stage because both are narrow transformations."],"correct":0,"correct_many":[0,3],"domain":"Architecture","topic":"execution-shuffle","concept":"execution-shuffle","concept_label":"execution-shuffle","explanation_ja":"joinやgroupByのshuffleがstageの境界を作り得る。writeが1回でも常にjobが1個とは限らない。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#shuffle-operations","source_explanation":"Correct Answers: A and D\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark executes applications using a hierarchy of jobs → stages → tasks. The DAG scheduler analyzes transformations and splits execution into stages based on shuffle boundaries. A shuffle occurs when data must move across partitions (for example, grouping or joining large datasets).\n\nTwo operations in the scenario are likely to trigger shuffles and therefore create new stages.\n\nA. The groupBy and agg operations may trigger a shuffle, which can create additional stages in the execution plan.\n\nOperations such as `groupBy()` followed by aggregations require Spark to redistribute data across partitions based on the grouping key. This redistribution is a shuffle operation.\n\nBecause shuffles break stage boundaries, Spark creates a new stage after the shuffle.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#shuffle-operations](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#shuffle-operations)\n\nD. The join operation can cause a shuffle, resulting in a new stage in the execution plan.\n\nWhen joining two large DataFrames, Spark often performs a shuffle join (such as a sort-merge join). In this case, both datasets must be repartitioned based on the join key, which requires data movement across the cluster.\n\nThis shuffle introduces another stage boundary in the execution plan.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#join-strategies](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#join-strategies)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nB. The entire application will execute as a single job triggered by the write action.\nWhile the write operation is an action that triggers execution, the job will be broken into multiple stages, not executed as a single stage.\n\nC. The DataFrame API removes all shuffle operations through automatic optimization.\nSpark’s Catalyst optimizer can optimize queries, but shuffle operations cannot be completely removed when required by operations such as joins or aggregations.\n\nE. The filter and join transformations will run in the same stage because both are narrow transformations.\n`filter()` is a narrow transformation, but joins between large datasets are wide transformations that typically trigger shuffles. Therefore, they do not necessarily run in the same stage.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark transformations fall into two categories:\n\n- \nNarrow transformations\n\n- \nNo data movement between partitions\n\n- \nExamples: `filter`, `map`, `select`\n\n- \nWide transformations\n\n- \nRequire shuffle operations\n\n- \nExamples: `groupBy`, `join`, `reduceByKey`\n\nWide transformations create stage boundaries in the DAG, which is how Spark organizes distributed execution.","source_option_explanations":["A. The groupBy and agg operations may trigger a shuffle, which can create additional stages in the execution plan.\n\nOperations such as `groupBy()` followed by aggregations require Spark to redistribute data across partitions based on the grouping key. This redistribution is a shuffle operation.\n\nBecause shuffles break stage boundaries, Spark creates a new stage after the shuffle.","B. The entire application will execute as a single job triggered by the write action.\nWhile the write operation is an action that triggers execution, the job will be broken into multiple stages, not executed as a single stage.","C. The DataFrame API removes all shuffle operations through automatic optimization.\nSpark’s Catalyst optimizer can optimize queries, but shuffle operations cannot be completely removed when required by operations such as joins or aggregations.","D. The join operation can cause a shuffle, resulting in a new stage in the execution plan.\n\nWhen joining two large DataFrames, Spark often performs a shuffle join (such as a sort-merge join). In this case, both datasets must be repartitioned based on the join key, which requires data movement across the cluster.\n\nThis shuffle introduces another stage boundary in the execution plan.","E. The filter and join transformations will run in the same stage because both are narrow transformations.\n`filter()` is a narrow transformation, but joins between large datasets are wide transformations that typically trigger shuffles. Therefore, they do not necessarily run in the same stage."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#shuffle-operations","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#join-strategies"],"images":[],"source_hash":"547a66c785c36f16542f8cead9b48cf2755717ebb9dff8002163a8759aa44941","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"narrow-wide-shuffle","decision_id":"narrow-wide-shuffle"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-035","number":35,"stem":"To improve performance, a developer plans to reduce the number of partitions of a DataFrame to 100. The developer must decide whether to use coalesce() or repartition() for this task.\n\nWhat is the behavior of these two functions?","options":["A.\n`coalesce()` reduces the number of partitions by merging existing partitions with minimal shuffling, whereas `repartition()` performs a full shuffle of the data.","B.\nBoth methods evenly shuffle the data across the cluster.","C.\nBoth methods maintain the current partitioning and require the same amount of memory.","D.\n`coalesce()` performs a full shuffle of the data, while `repartition()` reduces partitions with minimal shuffling."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"partition-resize","concept":"partition-resize","concept_label":"partition-resize","explanation_ja":"coalesceは既存partitionをまとめ、repartitionはshuffleで再配置する。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.repartition.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A. coalesce() reduces the number of partitions by merging existing partitions with minimal shuffling, whereas repartition() performs a full shuffle of the data.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, both `coalesce()` and `repartition()` are used to change the number of partitions in a DataFrame or RDD, but they behave differently in terms of data movement and performance.\n\ncoalesce()\n\n`coalesce()` is primarily used to reduce the number of partitions. It works by merging existing partitions without performing a full shuffle of the data. Because it avoids expensive data redistribution across the cluster, it is generally more efficient when decreasing the number of partitions.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf = df.coalesce(100)\n```\n\nThis operation combines existing partitions into fewer partitions with minimal data movement, which improves performance when reducing partitions.\n\nrepartition()\n\n`repartition()` always performs a full shuffle of the data across the cluster. This ensures that the data is evenly redistributed across the specified number of partitions.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf = df.repartition(100)\n```\n\nThis is useful when:\n\n- \nIncreasing the number of partitions\n\n- \nRedistributing data evenly across partitions\n\n- \nAvoiding data skew\n\nBecause it requires a shuffle, `repartition()` is more expensive than `coalesce()`.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.repartition.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.repartition.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nB. Both methods evenly shuffle the data across the cluster.\nThis is incorrect because `coalesce()` avoids a full shuffle, while `repartition()` explicitly performs one.\n\nC. Both methods maintain the current partitioning and require the same amount of memory.\nBoth methods change the partition count, and their resource usage differs due to shuffle behavior.\n\nD. coalesce() performs a full shuffle of the data, while repartition() reduces partitions with minimal shuffling.\nThis reverses the actual behavior of the two functions.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nPartitioning directly affects Spark performance because each partition corresponds to a task executed by an executor core. Efficient partition management helps control:\n\n- \nParallelism\n\n- \nShuffle overhead\n\n- \nTask scheduling efficiency\n\nGeneral rule:\n\n- \nUse `coalesce()` when decreasing partitions\n\n- \nUse `repartition()` when increasing partitions or requiring balanced distribution","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A. coalesce() reduces the number of partitions by merging existing partitions with minimal shuffling, whereas repartition() performs a full shuffle of the data.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, both `coalesce()` and `repartition()` are used to change the number of partitions in a DataFrame or RDD, but they behave differently in terms of data movement and performance.\n\ncoalesce()\n\n`coalesce()` is primarily used to reduce the number of partitions. It works by merging existing partitions without performing a full shuffle of the data. Because it avoids expensive data redistribution across the cluster, it is generally more efficient when decreasing the number of partitions.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf = df.coalesce(100)\n```\n\nThis operation combines existing partitions into fewer partitions with minimal data movement, which improves performance when reducing partitions.\n\nrepartition()\n\n`repartition()` always performs a full shuffle of the data across the cluster. This ensures that the data is evenly redistributed across the specified number of partitions.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf = df.repartition(100)\n```\n\nThis is useful when:\n\n- \nIncreasing the number of partitions\n\n- \nRedistributing data evenly across partitions\n\n- \nAvoiding data skew","B. Both methods evenly shuffle the data across the cluster.\nThis is incorrect because `coalesce()` avoids a full shuffle, while `repartition()` explicitly performs one.","C. Both methods maintain the current partitioning and require the same amount of memory.\nBoth methods change the partition count, and their resource usage differs due to shuffle behavior.","D. coalesce() performs a full shuffle of the data, while repartition() reduces partitions with minimal shuffling.\nThis reverses the actual behavior of the two functions."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.repartition.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"1c63322720abd68c4b73fdc4041bd35c523aff61d3270ab7543ae6ed81fa7f75","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"coalesce-repartition","decision_id":"coalesce-no-shuffle-vs-repartition"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-036","number":36,"stem":"A developer wants to refactor some legacy Spark code to take advantage of built-in functions introduced in Spark 3.5.0.\n\nThe developer encounters the following existing DataFrame code:\n\n```\nimport pyspark.sql.functions as F\n \nmin_price = 110.50\n \nresult_df = prices_df \\\n    .filter(F.col(\"spot_price\") >= F.lit(min_price)) \\\n    .agg(F.count(\"*\"))\n```\n\nWhich code snippet should the developer use to refactor this logic using the newer built-in functionality?","options":["A.\n\n```\nresult_df = prices_df \\\n    .withColumn(\"valid_price\",\n        F.when(F.col(\"spot_price\") > F.lit(min_price), 1).otherwise(0)\n    )\n```","B.\n\n```\nresult_df = prices_df \\\n    .agg(F.count_if(F.col(\"spot_price\") >= F.lit(min_price)))\n```","C.\n\n```\nresult_df = prices_df \\\n    .agg(F.min(\"spot_price\"), F.max(\"spot_price\"))\n```","D.\n\n```\nresult_df = prices_df \\\n    .agg(F.count(\"spot_price\").alias(\"spot_price\")) \\\n    .filter(F.col(\"spot_price\") > F.lit(\"min_price\"))\n```"],"correct":1,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"conditional-count","concept":"conditional-count","concept_label":"conditional-count","explanation_ja":"count_ifで条件がtrueの行を集計できる。filter後のcountと行数の意図を照合する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.count_if.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe original code performs two operations:\n\n- \nFilter rows where `spot_price >= min_price`\n\n- \nCount the remaining rows\n\n```\nmin_price = 110.50\n \nresult_df = prices_df \\\n    .filter(F.col(\"spot_price\") >= F.lit(min_price)) \\\n    .agg(F.count(\"*\"))\n```\n\nIn Spark 3.5, a built-in function `count_if()` was introduced that directly counts rows satisfying a condition. This allows the filtering and counting logic to be combined into a single aggregation expression, simplifying the code and improving readability.\n\nRefactored version:\n\n```\nresult_df = prices_df \\\n    .agg(F.count_if(F.col(\"spot_price\") >= F.lit(min_price)))\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \n`count_if()` evaluates the condition for each row.\n\n- \nOnly rows where the condition is true are counted.\n\n- \nThis removes the need for a separate `filter()` step.\n\nThis approach reduces code complexity and leverages Spark’s optimized built-in aggregation.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.count_if.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.count_if.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA.\nThis creates a new column `valid_price` using `when()` but does not perform the aggregation or count the valid rows. It changes the logic rather than refactoring it.\n\nC.\nThis computes the minimum and maximum prices, which is unrelated to counting records above a threshold.\n\nD.\nThis counts all rows in `spot_price` first and then attempts to filter on the aggregated result, which does not represent the original filtering logic and incorrectly treats `\"min_price\"` as a string.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark continuously introduces higher-level built-in functions to simplify common patterns. Functions like `count_if()` allow conditional aggregation directly in the query plan, reducing unnecessary transformations and improving code clarity.","source_option_explanations":["A.\nThis creates a new column `valid_price` using `when()` but does not perform the aggregation or count the valid rows. It changes the logic rather than refactoring it.","Correct Answer: B.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe original code performs two operations:\n\n- \nFilter rows where `spot_price >= min_price`\n\n- \nCount the remaining rows\n\n```\nmin_price = 110.50\n \nresult_df = prices_df \\\n    .filter(F.col(\"spot_price\") >= F.lit(min_price)) \\\n    .agg(F.count(\"*\"))\n```\n\nIn Spark 3.5, a built-in function `count_if()` was introduced that directly counts rows satisfying a condition. This allows the filtering and counting logic to be combined into a single aggregation expression, simplifying the code and improving readability.","C.\nThis computes the minimum and maximum prices, which is unrelated to counting records above a threshold.","D.\nThis counts all rows in `spot_price` first and then attempts to filter on the aggregated result, which does not represent the original filtering logic and incorrectly treats `\"min_price\"` as a string."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.count_if.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"ab06ac7e82550873b2ba45c259df664625df88c2be44aff5f4ebfa48cfb1008c","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"count-if-refactor","decision_id":"count-if-refactor"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-037","number":37,"stem":"A data engineer has created a large Spark DataFrame df with 1000 partitions and needs to reduce it to 100 partitions.\n\nWhich method allows reducing the number of partitions without reshuffling the data across partitions?","options":["A.\nnew_df = df.repartition(100)","B.\nnew_df = df.limit(100)","C.\nnew_df = df.head(100)","D.\nnew_df = df.coalesce(100)"],"correct":3,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"partition-resize","concept":"partition-resize","concept_label":"partition-resize","explanation_ja":"既存partitionをshuffleせず減らすにはcoalesce。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. new_df = df.coalesce(100)\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement is to reduce the number of partitions from 1000 to 100 without reshuffling the data.\n\nIn Apache Spark, the function designed specifically for this purpose is `coalesce()`. It reduces the number of partitions by merging existing partitions, avoiding a full shuffle of the data across the cluster. Because it minimizes data movement, it is more efficient when decreasing the number of partitions.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\nnew_df = df.coalesce(100)\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark combines existing partitions into fewer partitions.\n\n- \nData remains mostly in the same nodes.\n\n- \nNo full shuffle is triggered.\n\nThis makes `coalesce()` significantly faster and less resource-intensive than operations that require a full data redistribution.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. `new_df = df.repartition(100)`\n`repartition()` always triggers a full shuffle of the data across partitions to evenly redistribute the data. While it can change the partition count, it does not meet the requirement of avoiding a shuffle.\n\nB. `new_df = df.limit(100)`\n`limit()` restricts the number of rows returned, not the number of partitions. It does not modify the partition structure of the DataFrame.\n\nC. `new_df = df.head(100)`\n`head()` retrieves the first 100 rows to the driver program, returning a local collection rather than a DataFrame with fewer partitions. It is unrelated to partition management.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark provides two primary methods for changing partition counts:\n\n- \ncoalesce()\n\n- \nReduces partitions\n\n- \nAvoids full shuffle\n\n- \nFaster for decreasing partition count\n\n- \nrepartition()\n\n- \nPerforms a full shuffle\n\n- \nEnsures even distribution of data\n\n- \nUseful when increasing partitions or addressing data skew\n\nFor reducing partitions efficiently, `coalesce()` is the preferred method.","source_option_explanations":["A. `new_df = df.repartition(100)`\n`repartition()` always triggers a full shuffle of the data across partitions to evenly redistribute the data. While it can change the partition count, it does not meet the requirement of avoiding a shuffle.","B. `new_df = df.limit(100)`\n`limit()` restricts the number of rows returned, not the number of partitions. It does not modify the partition structure of the DataFrame.","C. `new_df = df.head(100)`\n`head()` retrieves the first 100 rows to the driver program, returning a local collection rather than a DataFrame with fewer partitions. It is unrelated to partition management.","Correct Answer: D. new_df = df.coalesce(100)\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement is to reduce the number of partitions from 1000 to 100 without reshuffling the data.\n\nIn Apache Spark, the function designed specifically for this purpose is `coalesce()`. It reduces the number of partitions by merging existing partitions, avoiding a full shuffle of the data across the cluster. Because it minimizes data movement, it is more efficient when decreasing the number of partitions.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\nnew_df = df.coalesce(100)\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark combines existing partitions into fewer partitions.\n\n- \nData remains mostly in the same nodes.\n\n- \nNo full shuffle is triggered.\n\nThis makes `coalesce()` significantly faster and less resource-intensive than operations that require a full data redistribution."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"e09dec34a4b029192a9bd43cbaf9f06c60e0ed9e4c29c64a49842424d1aa1d7f","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"coalesce-repartition","decision_id":"coalesce-repartition"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-038","number":38,"stem":"What is the primary benefit of Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) in Apache Spark?","options":["A.\nIt allows Spark to optimize the query plan before execution but does not make adjustments during runtime.","B.\nIt enables Spark to dynamically adjust the query plan during runtime, addressing data skew, optimizing join strategies, and improving overall query performance.","C.\nIt improves query execution by parallelizing tasks but does not change strategies based on runtime metrics such as data skew.","D.\nIt automatically distributes tasks across cluster nodes but does not modify the query plan during execution."],"correct":1,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"aqe","concept":"aqe","concept_label":"aqe","explanation_ja":"AQEはruntime統計を使いjoin戦略やpartition構成を調整する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#adaptive-query-execution","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B. It enables Spark to dynamically adjust the query plan during runtime, addressing data skew, optimizing join strategies, and improving overall query performance.\n\nExplanation:\n\nAdaptive Query Execution (AQE) is a Spark optimization framework that allows Spark to modify the physical execution plan during runtime based on actual statistics collected while the query is executing.\n\nTraditional Spark query planning occurs before execution, using estimated statistics. AQE improves this by allowing Spark to adapt the execution plan dynamically when real runtime information becomes available.\n\nKey optimizations performed by AQE include:\n\n- \nDynamic join strategy selection\n\n- \nExample: converting a Sort-Merge Join into a Broadcast Join if a dataset is smaller than expected.\n\n- \nHandling data skew\n\n- \nSplitting skewed partitions so that large partitions do not slow down specific tasks.\n\n- \nShuffle partition coalescing\n\n- \nReducing the number of shuffle partitions when the data volume is smaller than expected.\n\nExample configuration:\n\n```\nspark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.adaptive.enabled\", \"true\")\n```\n\nWith AQE enabled, Spark can improve performance automatically without requiring manual tuning.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#adaptive-query-execution](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#adaptive-query-execution)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. It allows Spark to optimize the query plan before execution but does not make adjustments during runtime.\nThis describes static query optimization, not AQE. AQE specifically performs runtime adjustments.\n\nC. It improves query execution by parallelizing tasks but does not change strategies based on runtime metrics such as data skew.\nParallel execution is a general feature of Spark, but AQE specifically uses runtime statistics to modify execution strategies.\n\nD. It automatically distributes tasks across cluster nodes but does not modify the query plan during execution.\nTask distribution across nodes is part of Spark’s scheduling system, not AQE. AQE explicitly modifies the query plan during execution.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nBefore AQE, Spark generated a static physical execution plan before execution began. With AQE enabled, Spark can re-optimize the plan during runtime using real metrics such as partition sizes and shuffle statistics, leading to improved performance for joins, aggregations, and skewed datasets.","source_option_explanations":["A. It allows Spark to optimize the query plan before execution but does not make adjustments during runtime.\nThis describes static query optimization, not AQE. AQE specifically performs runtime adjustments.","Correct Answer: B. It enables Spark to dynamically adjust the query plan during runtime, addressing data skew, optimizing join strategies, and improving overall query performance.\n\nExplanation:\n\nAdaptive Query Execution (AQE) is a Spark optimization framework that allows Spark to modify the physical execution plan during runtime based on actual statistics collected while the query is executing.\n\nTraditional Spark query planning occurs before execution, using estimated statistics. AQE improves this by allowing Spark to adapt the execution plan dynamically when real runtime information becomes available.\n\nKey optimizations performed by AQE include:\n\n- \nDynamic join strategy selection\n\n- \nExample: converting a Sort-Merge Join into a Broadcast Join if a dataset is smaller than expected.\n\n- \nHandling data skew\n\n- \nSplitting skewed partitions so that large partitions do not slow down specific tasks.\n\n- \nShuffle partition coalescing\n\n- \nReducing the number of shuffle partitions when the data volume is smaller than expected.\n\nExample configuration:","C. It improves query execution by parallelizing tasks but does not change strategies based on runtime metrics such as data skew.\nParallel execution is a general feature of Spark, but AQE specifically uses runtime statistics to modify execution strategies.","D. It automatically distributes tasks across cluster nodes but does not modify the query plan during execution."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#adaptive-query-execution"],"images":[],"source_hash":"ba781bb6e981d76bd3dec5bee10bda01b20fd02983b302e2dcba022c5fccbb02","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"aqe-runtime-change","decision_id":"aqe-runtime-change"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-039","number":39,"stem":"A Data Engineer created a view named users_vw using the following code:\n\n```\ndf.createOrReplaceTempView(\"users_vw\")\n```\n\nWhich approach can be used to query the users_vw view after the Spark session has been terminated?","options":["A.\nQuery the users_vw view directly using Spark.","B.\nPersist the users_vw data as a table.","C.\nRecreate the users_vw view and then query it using Spark.","D.\nSave the definition of users_vw and query it later using Spark."],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"temp-view-scope","concept":"temp-view-scope","concept_label":"temp-view-scope","explanation_ja":"session終了後もデータを使うには永続table等への保存が必要。temp viewだけは残らない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#running-sql-queries-programmatically","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B. Persist the users_vw data as a table.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe view in the scenario is created using:\n\n```\ndf.createOrReplaceTempView(\"users_vw\")\n```\n\nThis creates a temporary view that exists only within the current SparkSession. Temporary views are stored in the session catalog and are automatically removed when the Spark session ends.\n\nTherefore, once the Spark session is terminated:\n\n- \nThe view users_vw no longer exists\n\n- \nIt cannot be queried again\n\nTo make the data accessible across sessions, the DataFrame must be persisted as a table in a metastore or storage system.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.write.saveAsTable(\"users_table\")\n```\n\nThen it can be queried later using:\n\n```\nSELECT * FROM users_table\n```\n\nPersisting the DataFrame as a table ensures:\n\n- \nThe data is stored in persistent storage\n\n- \nThe table can be queried across Spark sessions\n\n- \nThe schema and metadata are maintained in the catalog\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#running-sql-queries-programmatically](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#running-sql-queries-programmatically)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. Query the users_vw view directly using Spark.\nThis will not work because temporary views are removed when the Spark session ends.\n\nC. Recreate the users_vw view and then query it using Spark.\nWhile this could work, it requires re-running the original DataFrame creation logic, which may not always be possible if the underlying data or transformations are not readily available.\n\nD. Save the definition of users_vw and query it later using Spark.\nSpark does not automatically persist temporary view definitions across sessions. Without the underlying DataFrame or data source, the view cannot be queried.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark supports three types of views/tables:\n\nTemporary View\n\n- \nScope: Current Spark session only\n\n- \nCreated with `createOrReplaceTempView()`\n\nGlobal Temporary View\n\n- \nScope: All Spark sessions in the same application\n\n- \nStored in `global_temp` database\n\nPersistent Table\n\n- \nStored in the metastore\n\n- \nAvailable across sessions and applications\n\nFor long-term accessibility, data must be stored as a persistent table rather than a temporary view.","source_option_explanations":["A. Query the users_vw view directly using Spark.\nThis will not work because temporary views are removed when the Spark session ends.","Correct Answer: B. Persist the users_vw data as a table.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe view in the scenario is created using:\n\n```\ndf.createOrReplaceTempView(\"users_vw\")\n```\n\nThis creates a temporary view that exists only within the current SparkSession. Temporary views are stored in the session catalog and are automatically removed when the Spark session ends.\n\nTherefore, once the Spark session is terminated:\n\n- \nThe view users_vw no longer exists\n\n- \nIt cannot be queried again\n\nTo make the data accessible across sessions, the DataFrame must be persisted as a table in a metastore or storage system.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.write.saveAsTable(\"users_table\")\n```\n\nThen it can be queried later using:\n\n```\nSELECT * FROM users_table\n```\n\nPersisting the DataFrame as a table ensures:\n\n- \nThe data is stored in persistent storage\n\n- \nThe table can be queried across Spark sessions\n\n- \nThe schema and metadata are maintained in the catalog","C. Recreate the users_vw view and then query it using Spark.\nWhile this could work, it requires re-running the original DataFrame creation logic, which may not always be possible if the underlying data or transformations are not readily available.","D. Save the definition of users_vw and query it later using Spark.\nSpark does not automatically persist temporary view definitions across sessions. Without the underlying DataFrame or data source, the view cannot be queried."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#running-sql-queries-programmatically"],"images":[],"source_hash":"d291476f84688a27aac827e313608f97754a6fa2452198c3684849615400ea02","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"temp-view-scope","decision_id":"temp-view-scope"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-040","number":40,"stem":"A data engineer needs to persist a file-based data source to a specific storage location. However, when saving the data as a table, it is automatically stored in the default warehouse directory.\n\nWhich line of code should the data engineer use to ensure the data is written to a custom location instead of the default warehouse path?","options":["A.\nusers.write(path=\"/some/path\").saveAsTable(\"default_table\")","B.\nusers.write.saveAsTable(\"default_table\").option(\"path\", \"/some/path\")","C.\nusers.write.option(\"path\", \"/some/path\").saveAsTable(\"default_table\")","D.\nusers.write.saveAsTable(\"default_table\", path=\"/some/path\")"],"correct":2,"correct_many":[2,3],"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"write-custom-path","concept":"write-custom-path","concept_label":"write-custom-path","explanation_ja":"pathオプションはoption経由でもsaveAsTableのキーワード引数でも渡せる。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.saveAsTable.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.saveAsTable.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-load-save-functions.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"632f7f965cd5c7a9d30f0306e7fab8a94fd0e773416ac7370736fd97084452e2","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"saveAsTableの**optionsでpathを渡せるためCとDが有効。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.saveAsTable.html"],"original_stem":"A data engineer needs to persist a file-based data source to a specific storage location. However, when saving the data as a table, it is automatically stored in the default warehouse directory.\n\nWhich line of code should the data engineer use to ensure the data is written to a custom location instead of the default warehouse path?","original_options":["A.\nusers.write(path=\"/some/path\").saveAsTable(\"default_table\")","B.\nusers.write.saveAsTable(\"default_table\").option(\"path\", \"/some/path\")","C.\nusers.write.option(\"path\", \"/some/path\").saveAsTable(\"default_table\")","D.\nusers.write.saveAsTable(\"default_table\", path=\"/some/path\")"],"original_correct":["C"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"write-custom-path","decision_id":"write-custom-path"}]},{"id":"EX4-S3","title":"原文セット 3","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-041","number":41,"stem":"A data scientist is analyzing user profile data to create features for a machine learning model. During exploratory analysis, the scientist discovers that some records contain NULL values in multiple fields, making those records unreliable for modeling.\n\nThe schema of the user profile table is as follows:\n\n```\nuser_id STRING\nusername STRING\nfull_name STRING\ndate_of_birth DATE\nprimary_email STRING\ncreated_ts TIMESTAMP\nupdated_ts TIMESTAMP\nlast_login_ts TIMESTAMP\n```\n\nThe data scientist decides that any record containing a NULL value in any column should be removed before further processing.\n\nWhich Spark code snippet satisfies this requirement?","options":["A.\nfiltered_df = users_raw_df.na.drop(thresh=0)","B.\nfiltered_df = users_raw_df.na.drop(how='all')","C.\nfiltered_df = users_raw_df.na.drop(how='any')","D.\nfiltered_df = users_raw_df.na.drop(how='all', thresh=None)"],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"null-drop","concept":"null-drop","concept_label":"null-drop","explanation_ja":"na.drop(how=any)はどれかの列がNULLの行を除く。allは全列NULLのみ。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameNaFunctions.drop.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C. filtered_df = users_raw_df.na.drop(how='any')\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement is to remove any record that contains a NULL value in any column. In PySpark, the `DataFrameNaFunctions.drop()` method (accessible through `.na.drop()`) is used to remove rows containing null values.\n\nThe parameter `how='any'` specifies that if any column in the row contains NULL, that row should be removed.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\nfiltered_df = users_raw_df.na.drop(how='any')\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark scans each row of the DataFrame.\n\n- \nIf any column contains NULL, that row is removed.\n\n- \nOnly rows with complete data across all columns remain.\n\nThis approach ensures that all unreliable records containing missing values are removed before feature engineering or model training.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameNaFunctions.drop.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameNaFunctions.drop.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA. `users_raw_df.na.drop(thresh=0)`\n`thresh` specifies the minimum number of non-null values required to keep a row. With `thresh=0`, every row meets the condition, so no rows would be removed.\n\nB. `users_raw_df.na.drop(how='all')`\nThis removes rows only if all columns are NULL. Rows containing partial NULL values would still remain.\n\nD. `users_raw_df.na.drop(how='all', thresh=None)`\nThis effectively behaves the same as option B and removes rows only when all columns are NULL, which does not satisfy the requirement.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark provides two main approaches for handling missing values:\n\n- \ndropna() / na.drop()\n\n- \nRemoves rows containing NULL values\n\n- \nSupports parameters like `how`, `subset`, and `thresh`\n\n- \nfillna() / na.fill()\n\n- \nReplaces NULL values with specified defaults\n\nFor machine learning pipelines, removing rows with missing values using `how='any'` is often used when complete feature records are required.","source_option_explanations":["A. `users_raw_df.na.drop(thresh=0)`\n`thresh` specifies the minimum number of non-null values required to keep a row. With `thresh=0`, every row meets the condition, so no rows would be removed.","B. `users_raw_df.na.drop(how='all')`\nThis removes rows only if all columns are NULL. Rows containing partial NULL values would still remain.","Correct Answer: C. filtered_df = users_raw_df.na.drop(how='any')\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement is to remove any record that contains a NULL value in any column. In PySpark, the `DataFrameNaFunctions.drop()` method (accessible through `.na.drop()`) is used to remove rows containing null values.\n\nThe parameter `how='any'` specifies that if any column in the row contains NULL, that row should be removed.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\nfiltered_df = users_raw_df.na.drop(how='any')\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark scans each row of the DataFrame.\n\n- \nIf any column contains NULL, that row is removed.\n\n- \nOnly rows with complete data across all columns remain.","D. `users_raw_df.na.drop(how='all', thresh=None)`\nThis effectively behaves the same as option B and removes rows only when all columns are NULL, which does not satisfy the requirement."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameNaFunctions.drop.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"eea40beb00bea1f31f1914e18a4ecfb7d7f49cedafb23ef8fd5477bddc7889ae","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"drop-any-null-row","decision_id":"drop-any-null-row"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-042","number":42,"stem":"A data engineer is building a real-time analytics pipeline using Apache Spark Structured Streaming. The engineer wants the system to process incoming data in micro-batches that run at a fixed interval of 5 seconds. The trigger should control when each micro-batch execution starts.\n\nWhich code snippet satisfies this requirement?","options":["A.\n\n```\nquery = df.writeStream \\\n    .outputMode(\"append\") \\\n    .trigger(continuous=\"5 seconds\") \\\n    .start()\n```","B.\n\n```\nquery = df.writeStream \\\n    .outputMode(\"append\") \\\n    .trigger() \\\n    .start()\n```","C.\n\n```\nquery = df.writeStream \\\n    .outputMode(\"append\") \\\n    .trigger(processingTime=\"5 seconds\") \\\n    .start()\n```","D.\n\n```\nquery = df.writeStream \\\n    .outputMode(\"append\") \\\n    .trigger(processingTime=5000) \\\n    .start()\n```"],"correct":2,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"stream-trigger","concept":"stream-trigger","concept_label":"stream-trigger","explanation_ja":"固定間隔のmicro-batchはprocessingTimeの文字列で指定する。continuousは別実行方式。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#triggers","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Spark Structured Streaming, the trigger determines when each micro-batch is executed. To run micro-batches at a fixed interval, Spark provides the processing time trigger.\n\nThe correct syntax is:\n\n```\nquery = df.writeStream \\\n    .outputMode(\"append\") \\\n    .trigger(processingTime=\"5 seconds\") \\\n    .start()\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark checks for new data every 5 seconds.\n\n- \nA micro-batch job is triggered at that interval.\n\n- \nEach batch processes any newly available data.\n\nThis is the most common trigger used for fixed-interval micro-batch streaming pipelines.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#triggers](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#triggers)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA.\n\n```\n.trigger(continuous=\"5 seconds\")\n```\n\nThis refers to continuous processing mode, which is different from micro-batch processing and is used for low-latency continuous execution rather than fixed micro-batch scheduling.\n\nB.\n\n```\n.trigger()\n```\n\nThis uses the default trigger, which processes data as soon as possible rather than at a fixed interval.\n\nD.\n\n```\n.trigger(processingTime=5000)\n```\n\nThe `processingTime` parameter expects a time duration string such as `\"5 seconds\"` or `\"10 minutes\"`. Passing an integer like `5000` is not valid syntax.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark Structured Streaming supports several trigger types:\n\n- \nprocessingTime\n\n- \nRuns micro-batches at a fixed interval (most common)\n\n- \nonce\n\n- \nProcesses all available data once and stops\n\n- \navailableNow\n\n- \nProcesses all available data in micro-batches and stops\n\n- \ncontinuous\n\n- \nLow-latency continuous processing mode\n\nFor fixed micro-batch intervals, `processingTime` is the correct trigger configuration.","source_option_explanations":["A.\n\n```\n.trigger(continuous=\"5 seconds\")\n```\n\nThis refers to continuous processing mode, which is different from micro-batch processing and is used for low-latency continuous execution rather than fixed micro-batch scheduling.","B.\n\n```\n.trigger()\n```\n\nThis uses the default trigger, which processes data as soon as possible rather than at a fixed interval.","Correct Answer: C.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Spark Structured Streaming, the trigger determines when each micro-batch is executed. To run micro-batches at a fixed interval, Spark provides the processing time trigger.\n\nThe correct syntax is:\n\n```\nquery = df.writeStream \\\n    .outputMode(\"append\") \\\n    .trigger(processingTime=\"5 seconds\") \\\n    .start()\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark checks for new data every 5 seconds.\n\n- \nA micro-batch job is triggered at that interval.\n\n- \nEach batch processes any newly available data.","D.\n\n```\n.trigger(processingTime=5000)\n```\n\nThe `processingTime` parameter expects a time duration string such as `\"5 seconds\"` or `\"10 minutes\"`. Passing an integer like `5000` is not valid syntax."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#triggers"],"images":[],"source_hash":"09f97ef29ad97d4a33f9968e86365df4e92eea6f5812bf542ac7e821069c43c6","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"processing-time-trigger-interval","decision_id":"processing-time-trigger-interval"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-043","number":43,"stem":"A data analyst is working with a DataFrame named dates_df and needs to create a new column called date, derived from the existing timestamp column. The new column must have Spark DateType.\n\nWhich code snippet should be used to extract the date portion from the timestamp?","options":["A.\ndates_df.withColumn(\"date\", f.unix_timestamp(\"timestamp\")).show()","B.\ndates_df.withColumn(\"date\", f.to_date(\"timestamp\")).show()","C.\ndates_df.withColumn(\"date\", f.date_format(\"timestamp\", \"yyyy-MM-dd\")).show()","D.\ndates_df.withColumn(\"date\", f.from_unixtime(\"timestamp\")).show()"],"correct":1,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"date-convert","concept":"date-convert","concept_label":"date-convert","explanation_ja":"DateTypeが必要ならto_date。date_formatは文字列を作る。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.to_date.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.to_date.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.date_format.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.to_date.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"1010c4e75f5d7bbda4ca1c07b872fcadccbb6ca5b5c2e373e03d6a2fdfd1fb57","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"date_formatは文字列を返すため、設問でDateTypeの出力を明記。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.to_date.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.date_format.html"],"original_stem":"A data analyst is working with a DataFrame named dates_df and needs to create a new column called date, derived from the existing timestamp column.\n\nWhich code snippet should be used to extract the date portion from the timestamp?","original_options":["A.\ndates_df.withColumn(\"date\", f.unix_timestamp(\"timestamp\")).show()","B.\ndates_df.withColumn(\"date\", f.to_date(\"timestamp\")).show()","C.\ndates_df.withColumn(\"date\", f.date_format(\"timestamp\", \"yyyy-MM-dd\")).show()","D.\ndates_df.withColumn(\"date\", f.from_unixtime(\"timestamp\")).show()"],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"timestamp-to-date-type","decision_id":"timestamp-to-date-type"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-044","number":44,"stem":"A data scientist is conducting a large-scale data analysis project using Apache Spark on Databricks. The task involves joining a large DataFrame of customer transactions (200 million rows) with a much smaller DataFrame containing product information (3,000 rows).\n\nWhat is the best approach to optimize the join operation for improved performance?","options":["A.\nPerform a broadcast join by broadcasting the larger DataFrame.","B.\nUse a Sort-Merge Join by sorting both DataFrames before performing the join.","C.\nUse a shuffle hash join by distributing both DataFrames evenly across the cluster.","D.\nPerform a broadcast join by broadcasting the smaller DataFrame."],"correct":3,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"broadcast-choice","concept":"broadcast-choice","concept_label":"broadcast-choice","explanation_ja":"十分小さい側をbroadcastし、大きい側のshuffleを減らす。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. Perform a broadcast join by broadcasting the smaller DataFrame.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe scenario involves joining:\n\n- \nA large DataFrame (200 million rows of transactions)\n\n- \nA very small DataFrame (3,000 rows of product information)\n\nThe most efficient strategy in Spark for this pattern is a broadcast join where the small table is broadcast to all executors.\n\nIn a broadcast join:\n\n- \nSpark sends the entire small dataset to every executor node.\n\n- \nEach executor performs the join locally against its partition of the large dataset.\n\n- \nThis eliminates expensive shuffle operations.\n\nExample implementation:\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast\n \njoined_df = transactions_df.join(\n    broadcast(products_df),\n    \"product_id\"\n)\n```\n\nExecution benefits:\n\n- \nAvoids shuffle across the cluster\n\n- \nReduces network I/O\n\n- \nImproves join performance dramatically when one table is small\n\nSpark automatically chooses broadcast joins when the smaller dataset size is below the configuration threshold:\n\n`spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold`\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. Perform a broadcast join by broadcasting the larger DataFrame.\nBroadcasting the large dataset (200 million rows) would require sending massive data to every executor, which is extremely inefficient and could exceed memory limits.\n\nB. Use a Sort-Merge Join by sorting both DataFrames before performing the join.\nSort-Merge Join is used when both datasets are large. It requires shuffle and sorting, which is slower than a broadcast join when one dataset is small.\n\nC. Use a shuffle hash join by distributing both DataFrames evenly across the cluster.\nShuffle hash joins still require data shuffling across nodes, which introduces significant overhead compared to broadcasting the small table.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark supports several join strategies:\n\nBroadcast Hash Join\n\n- \nBest when one table is small\n\n- \nAvoids shuffle\n\nSort-Merge Join\n\n- \nUsed when both tables are large\n\nShuffle Hash Join\n\n- \nRequires data redistribution\n\nFor large-to-small joins, broadcasting the small table is typically the most efficient strategy.","source_option_explanations":["A. Perform a broadcast join by broadcasting the larger DataFrame.\nBroadcasting the large dataset (200 million rows) would require sending massive data to every executor, which is extremely inefficient and could exceed memory limits.","B. Use a Sort-Merge Join by sorting both DataFrames before performing the join.\nSort-Merge Join is used when both datasets are large. It requires shuffle and sorting, which is slower than a broadcast join when one dataset is small.","C. Use a shuffle hash join by distributing both DataFrames evenly across the cluster.\nShuffle hash joins still require data shuffling across nodes, which introduces significant overhead compared to broadcasting the small table.","Correct Answer: D. Perform a broadcast join by broadcasting the smaller DataFrame.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe scenario involves joining:\n\n- \nA large DataFrame (200 million rows of transactions)\n\n- \nA very small DataFrame (3,000 rows of product information)\n\nThe most efficient strategy in Spark for this pattern is a broadcast join where the small table is broadcast to all executors.\n\nIn a broadcast join:\n\n- \nSpark sends the entire small dataset to every executor node.\n\n- \nEach executor performs the join locally against its partition of the large dataset.\n\n- \nThis eliminates expensive shuffle operations."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries"],"images":[],"source_hash":"6c4b71a3b6ec9cd678426f14ad1011fb881797f228786adf9a31eab4535e9ef7","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"broadcast-small-side","decision_id":"broadcast-small-side"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-045","number":45,"stem":"What is the correct relationship between jobs, stages, and tasks during execution in Apache Spark?","options":["A.\nA job consists of multiple stages, and each stage is composed of multiple tasks.","B.\nA job contains multiple tasks, and each task includes multiple stages.","C.\nA stage consists of multiple jobs, and each job includes multiple tasks.","D.\nA stage contains multiple tasks, and each task includes multiple jobs."],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"execution-hierarchy","concept":"execution-hierarchy","concept_label":"execution-hierarchy","explanation_ja":"job→stage→taskという階層。単一stageのjobもあるため必ず複数stageという意味ではない。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A. A job consists of multiple stages, and each stage is composed of multiple tasks.\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark executes applications using a hierarchical execution structure composed of jobs, stages, and tasks.\n\nExecution hierarchy:\n\nApplication\n→ Job\n→ Stage\n→ Task\n\nHow this hierarchy works:\n\n- \nAction triggers a job\n\nWhen an action such as `count()`, `collect()`, or `write()` is executed, Spark creates a job.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.filter(\"age > 30\").count()\n```\n\nThe `count()` action triggers a job.\n\n- \nJob is divided into stages\n\nSpark analyzes the DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) of transformations and divides the job into stages. Stages are separated by shuffle boundaries caused by wide transformations such as:\n\n- \n`groupBy`\n\n- \n`join`\n\n- \n`reduceByKey`\n\n- \nStages are executed as parallel tasks\n\nEach stage is broken into tasks, where each task processes one partition of data.\n\nExample hierarchy:\n\nJob\n\n- \nStage 1\n\n- \nTask 1\n\n- \nTask 2\n\n- \nTask 3\n\n- \nStage 2\n\n- \nTask 1\n\n- \nTask 2\n\n- \nTask 3\n\nTasks are distributed across executors and executed in parallel.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nB. A job contains multiple tasks, and each task includes multiple stages.\nThis reverses the correct hierarchy. Tasks are the lowest execution unit, not containers for stages.\n\nC. A stage consists of multiple jobs, and each job includes multiple tasks.\nStages do not contain jobs. Instead, jobs contain stages.\n\nD. A stage contains multiple tasks, and each task includes multiple jobs.\nTasks never contain jobs. Jobs are the highest-level execution unit triggered by an action.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark’s execution model works as follows:\n\n- \nJob\n\n- \nTriggered by an action\n\n- \nRepresents a full computation\n\n- \nStage\n\n- \nLogical grouping of transformations\n\n- \nSeparated by shuffle operations\n\n- \nTask\n\n- \nSmallest unit of execution\n\n- \nRuns on a single partition of data\n\nUnderstanding this hierarchy is important for performance tuning and debugging Spark jobs.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A. A job consists of multiple stages, and each stage is composed of multiple tasks.\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark executes applications using a hierarchical execution structure composed of jobs, stages, and tasks.\n\nExecution hierarchy:\n\nApplication\n→ Job\n→ Stage\n→ Task\n\nHow this hierarchy works:\n\n- \nAction triggers a job\n\nWhen an action such as `count()`, `collect()`, or `write()` is executed, Spark creates a job.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.filter(\"age > 30\").count()\n```\n\nThe `count()` action triggers a job.\n\n- \nJob is divided into stages\n\nSpark analyzes the DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) of transformations and divides the job into stages. Stages are separated by shuffle boundaries caused by wide transformations such as:\n\n- \n`groupBy`\n\n- \n`join`\n\n- \n`reduceByKey`\n\n- \nStages are executed as parallel tasks","B. A job contains multiple tasks, and each task includes multiple stages.\nThis reverses the correct hierarchy. Tasks are the lowest execution unit, not containers for stages.","C. A stage consists of multiple jobs, and each job includes multiple tasks.\nStages do not contain jobs. Instead, jobs contain stages.","D. A stage contains multiple tasks, and each task includes multiple jobs.\nTasks never contain jobs. Jobs are the highest-level execution unit triggered by an action."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"9b7a44d4c0031a1ced7fe5d1862e01a1901493b91a02e0e7471291ce46848398","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"job-stage-task-hierarchy","decision_id":"job-stage-task-hierarchy"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-046","number":46,"stem":"How can a Spark developer ensure optimal resource utilization when running Spark jobs in Local Mode during testing?","options":["A.\nConfigure the application to run in cluster mode instead of local mode.","B.\nIncrease the number of local threads according to the number of available CPU cores.","C.\nEnable the `spark.dynamicAllocation.enabled` property to dynamically scale resources.","D.\nSet the `spark.executor.memory` property to a very large value."],"correct":1,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"local-parallelism","concept":"local-parallelism","concept_label":"local-parallelism","explanation_ja":"local[N]のNはローカルで使うスレッド数。利用可能CPUと処理内容を踏まえて選ぶ。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/submitting-applications.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B. Increase the number of local threads according to the number of available CPU cores.\n\nExplanation:\n\nWhen running Spark in Local Mode, the entire Spark application (driver and executors) runs on a single machine rather than on a cluster. In this mode, Spark uses local threads to simulate parallel task execution.\n\nTo ensure optimal resource utilization during testing, the developer should configure Spark to use multiple local threads based on the available CPU cores. This allows Spark to execute tasks in parallel and better simulate distributed execution.\n\nTypical configuration:\n\n```\nspark = SparkSession.builder \\\n    .master(\"local[*]\") \\\n    .getOrCreate()\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- \n`local[*]` tells Spark to use all available CPU cores on the machine.\n\n- \nAlternatively, a specific number of cores can be used (e.g., `local[4]`).\n\nThis configuration ensures efficient parallel processing during development and testing.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/submitting-applications.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/submitting-applications.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. Configure the application to run in cluster mode instead of local mode.\nCluster mode requires a cluster manager and distributed environment, which defeats the purpose of local testing.\n\nC. Enable the spark.dynamicAllocation.enabled property to dynamically scale resources.\nDynamic allocation applies to cluster deployments with executors, not to Local Mode where execution happens within a single JVM.\n\nD. Set the spark.executor.memory property to a very large value.\nIn Local Mode, executors are not managed the same way as in cluster mode. Setting extremely large memory values does not improve CPU utilization and may cause memory pressure on the local machine.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark Local Mode execution options:\n\n- \n`local` → single-thread execution\n\n- \n`local[N]` → use N parallel threads\n\n- \n`local[*]` → use all available CPU cores\n\nFor testing workloads locally while simulating distributed execution, `local[*]` is the recommended configuration.","source_option_explanations":["A. Configure the application to run in cluster mode instead of local mode.\nCluster mode requires a cluster manager and distributed environment, which defeats the purpose of local testing.","Correct Answer: B. Increase the number of local threads according to the number of available CPU cores.\n\nExplanation:\n\nWhen running Spark in Local Mode, the entire Spark application (driver and executors) runs on a single machine rather than on a cluster. In this mode, Spark uses local threads to simulate parallel task execution.\n\nTo ensure optimal resource utilization during testing, the developer should configure Spark to use multiple local threads based on the available CPU cores. This allows Spark to execute tasks in parallel and better simulate distributed execution.\n\nTypical configuration:\n\n```\nspark = SparkSession.builder \\\n    .master(\"local[*]\") \\\n    .getOrCreate()\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- \n`local[*]` tells Spark to use all available CPU cores on the machine.","C. Enable the spark.dynamicAllocation.enabled property to dynamically scale resources.\nDynamic allocation applies to cluster deployments with executors, not to Local Mode where execution happens within a single JVM.","D. Set the spark.executor.memory property to a very large value.\nIn Local Mode, executors are not managed the same way as in cluster mode. Setting extremely large memory values does not improve CPU utilization and may cause memory pressure on the local machine."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/submitting-applications.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"47b4734043cdfb7ad78db48d117cb041ce9ed4b03366ef83f2fd0d7acf4d4453","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"local-thread-parallelism","decision_id":"local-thread-parallelism"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-047","number":47,"stem":"Which Spark configuration determines the number of tasks that can run concurrently on each executor?","options":["A.\nspark.executor.cores","B.\nspark.task.maxFailures","C.\nspark.driver.cores","D.\nspark.executor.memory"],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"executor-concurrency","concept":"executor-concurrency","concept_label":"executor-concurrency","explanation_ja":"同時task数はexecutor coresとtask当たりcores等で決まる。ここではtask.cpus=1の通常設定を想定。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/configuration.html#application-properties","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A. spark.executor.cores\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, the configuration `spark.executor.cores` specifies the number of CPU cores allocated to each executor. Since each Spark task typically requires one CPU core, this configuration effectively determines how many tasks an executor can run concurrently.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nspark.executor.cores=4\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nEach executor receives 4 CPU cores.\n\n- \nThe executor can run up to 4 tasks simultaneously.\n\n- \nAdditional tasks are queued until cores become available.\n\nThis configuration directly impacts parallelism and resource utilization in a Spark cluster.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/configuration.html#application-properties](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/configuration.html#application-properties)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nB. spark.task.maxFailures\nThis setting controls the maximum number of times a task can fail before Spark aborts the job. It does not affect how many tasks run concurrently.\n\nC. spark.driver.cores\nThis specifies the number of cores assigned to the driver program, not the executors that run the distributed tasks.\n\nD. spark.executor.memory\nThis configuration controls the amount of memory allocated to each executor, which affects memory-intensive workloads but does not determine the number of concurrent tasks.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark task concurrency is determined by the relationship between:\n\n- \nExecutor cores (`spark.executor.cores`)\n\n- \nNumber of executors (`spark.executor.instances`)\n\n- \nTotal cluster cores\n\nTotal concurrent tasks in a Spark application can be approximated as:\n\n```\nTotal parallel tasks ≈ executor_instances × executor_cores\n```\n\nThis configuration is critical for tuning parallel execution efficiency and cluster resource utilization.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A. spark.executor.cores\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, the configuration `spark.executor.cores` specifies the number of CPU cores allocated to each executor. Since each Spark task typically requires one CPU core, this configuration effectively determines how many tasks an executor can run concurrently.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nspark.executor.cores=4\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nEach executor receives 4 CPU cores.\n\n- \nThe executor can run up to 4 tasks simultaneously.\n\n- \nAdditional tasks are queued until cores become available.","B. spark.task.maxFailures\nThis setting controls the maximum number of times a task can fail before Spark aborts the job. It does not affect how many tasks run concurrently.","C. spark.driver.cores\nThis specifies the number of cores assigned to the driver program, not the executors that run the distributed tasks.","D. spark.executor.memory\nThis configuration controls the amount of memory allocated to each executor, which affects memory-intensive workloads but does not determine the number of concurrent tasks."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/configuration.html#application-properties"],"images":[],"source_hash":"73d405f288d54b0a4f39be05f8020c7b6b6f866a576a3df83db2592ec5fe9314","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"executor-cores-task-concurrency","decision_id":"executor-cores-task-concurrency"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-048","number":48,"stem":"A data scientist needs to ingest a directory containing multiple plain text files such that each row in the resulting DataFrame contains the full content of a single file along with the file’s full path.\n\nThe initial code successfully reads the files but produces one row per line, rather than one row per file:\n\n```\nraw_txt_path = '/datasets/raw_txt/*'\n \ncorpus = spark.read.text(raw_txt_path) \\\n    .select(\"*\", \"_metadata.file_path\")\n```\n\nWhich code modification should be made so that each row contains the entire content of a file instead of individual lines?","options":["A.\nAdd the option `wholetext=True` to the `text()` function.","B.\nAdd the option `lineSep=\"\\n\"` to the `text()` function.","C.\nAdd the option `wholetext=False` to the `text()` function.","D.\nAdd the option `lineSep=\",\"` to the `text()` function."],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"wholetext-read","concept":"wholetext-read","concept_label":"wholetext-read","explanation_ja":"wholetext=Trueで1ファイルの内容を1行にまとめる。_metadata.file_pathはDatabricks固有の前提。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-text.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A. Add the option wholetext=True to the text() function.\n\nExplanation:\n\nBy default, when Spark reads text files using:\n\n```\nspark.read.text(...)\n```\n\nSpark creates one row per line in the file. Each row contains a single line of text in the column named `value`.\n\nHowever, the requirement in this scenario is different:\n\n- \nEach row should represent one entire file\n\n- \nThe row should contain the full content of the file\n\n- \nThe file path metadata should also be available\n\nSpark provides a configuration option called `wholetext` to support this behavior. When `wholetext=True`, Spark reads each file as a single record, placing the entire file content into one row.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\ncorpus = spark.read \\\n    .option(\"wholetext\", True) \\\n    .text(raw_txt_path) \\\n    .select(\"*\", \"_metadata.file_path\")\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nEach input file becomes one row\n\n- \nThe full file content appears in the `value` column\n\n- \n`_metadata.file_path` contains the source file path\n\nThis is commonly used in scenarios such as:\n\n- \nDocument ingestion\n\n- \nNLP pipelines\n\n- \nProcessing logs or reports stored as individual files\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-text.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-text.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nB. Add the option `lineSep=\"\\n\"` to the text() function.\nThis only specifies the line separator used to split lines. Spark will still produce one row per line, which does not satisfy the requirement.\n\nC. Add the option `wholetext=False` to the text() function.\n`wholetext=False` is the default behavior, which results in one row per line instead of one row per file.\n\nD. Add the option `lineSep=\",\"` to the text() function.\nChanging the line separator would split rows based on commas, which is unrelated to reading the entire file as a single row.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark’s text data source supports two modes:\n\n- \nDefault mode (`wholetext=False`)\n\n- \nEach line becomes one row\n\n- \nWhole file mode (`wholetext=True`)\n\n- \nEach file becomes one row\n\n- \nThe entire file content is stored in the `value` column\n\nWhole-file mode is particularly useful for document processing and text analytics pipelines.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A. Add the option wholetext=True to the text() function.\n\nExplanation:\n\nBy default, when Spark reads text files using:\n\n```\nspark.read.text(...)\n```\n\nSpark creates one row per line in the file. Each row contains a single line of text in the column named `value`.\n\nHowever, the requirement in this scenario is different:\n\n- \nEach row should represent one entire file\n\n- \nThe row should contain the full content of the file\n\n- \nThe file path metadata should also be available\n\nSpark provides a configuration option called `wholetext` to support this behavior. When `wholetext=True`, Spark reads each file as a single record, placing the entire file content into one row.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\ncorpus = spark.read \\\n    .option(\"wholetext\", True) \\\n    .text(raw_txt_path) \\\n    .select(\"*\", \"_metadata.file_path\")\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nEach input file becomes one row\n\n- \nThe full file content appears in the `value` column\n\n- \n`_metadata.file_path` contains the source file path","B. Add the option `lineSep=\"\\n\"` to the text() function.\nThis only specifies the line separator used to split lines. Spark will still produce one row per line, which does not satisfy the requirement.","C. Add the option `wholetext=False` to the text() function.\n`wholetext=False` is the default behavior, which results in one row per line instead of one row per file.","D. Add the option `lineSep=\",\"` to the text() function.\nChanging the line separator would split rows based on commas, which is unrelated to reading the entire file as a single row."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-text.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"7c70ab37b550f96b65a6da9169f96fb9e0013e9853b4a2e4851a8b9b10efb1ec","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"text-whole-file-record","decision_id":"text-whole-file-record"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-049","number":49,"stem":"What is the primary benefit of using Pandas API on Spark for performing data transformations?","options":["A.\nIt is available only in Python, which reduces the learning curve for developers.","B.\nIt executes computations immediately using eager execution, making it easier to use.","C.\nIt runs only on a single node and relies on memory-bound DataFrames, making it cost-efficient.","D.\nIt allows queries to run faster by utilizing all available cores across the cluster while still providing the rich functionality of the Pandas API."],"correct":3,"domain":"Pandas API","topic":"pandas-distributed","concept":"pandas-distributed","concept_label":"pandas-distributed","explanation_ja":"pandasに近いAPIでSparkの分散処理を使える。常にpandasより速いという保証ではない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/pandas_on_spark/index.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. It allows queries to run faster by utilizing all available cores across the cluster while still providing the rich functionality of the Pandas API.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe Pandas API on Spark (previously called Koalas) provides a familiar pandas-like interface while leveraging Spark’s distributed execution engine. This allows developers who are comfortable with pandas to write similar code while still benefiting from cluster-scale parallel processing.\n\nKey advantage:\n\n- \nDevelopers can use pandas-style syntax.\n\n- \nExecution occurs on Spark’s distributed cluster, not a single machine.\n\n- \nOperations can scale to large datasets that exceed single-machine memory limits.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nimport pyspark.pandas as ps\n \ndf = ps.read_parquet(\"/data/sales\")\nresult = df.groupby(\"region\")[\"sales\"].sum()\n```\n\nAlthough the syntax resembles pandas, the computation is executed using Spark’s distributed processing, enabling the use of all available cores across executors.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/pandas_on_spark/index.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/pandas_on_spark/index.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. It is available only in Python, which reduces the learning curve for developers.\nWhile the Pandas API on Spark is primarily used in Python, the primary benefit is scalability, not language exclusivity.\n\nB. It executes computations immediately using eager execution, making it easier to use.\nSpark operations are generally lazy, meaning transformations are recorded and executed when an action is triggered. The Pandas API on Spark still relies on Spark’s lazy execution model.\n\nC. It runs only on a single node and relies on memory-bound DataFrames, making it cost-efficient.\nThis describes standard pandas, not the Pandas API on Spark. The Pandas API on Spark runs on distributed clusters.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nThree common data processing approaches:\n\n- \npandas\n\n- \nSingle machine\n\n- \nMemory-bound\n\n- \nPySpark DataFrames\n\n- \nDistributed\n\n- \nScales to large datasets\n\n- \nPandas API on Spark\n\n- \npandas-like syntax\n\n- \nDistributed execution using Spark\n\nThe Pandas API on Spark provides the ease of pandas with the scalability of Spark, making it ideal for large-scale analytics workloads.","source_option_explanations":["A. It is available only in Python, which reduces the learning curve for developers.\nWhile the Pandas API on Spark is primarily used in Python, the primary benefit is scalability, not language exclusivity.","B. It executes computations immediately using eager execution, making it easier to use.\nSpark operations are generally lazy, meaning transformations are recorded and executed when an action is triggered. The Pandas API on Spark still relies on Spark’s lazy execution model.","C. It runs only on a single node and relies on memory-bound DataFrames, making it cost-efficient.\nThis describes standard pandas, not the Pandas API on Spark. The Pandas API on Spark runs on distributed clusters.","Correct Answer: D. It allows queries to run faster by utilizing all available cores across the cluster while still providing the rich functionality of the Pandas API.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe Pandas API on Spark (previously called Koalas) provides a familiar pandas-like interface while leveraging Spark’s distributed execution engine. This allows developers who are comfortable with pandas to write similar code while still benefiting from cluster-scale parallel processing.\n\nKey advantage:\n\n- \nDevelopers can use pandas-style syntax.\n\n- \nExecution occurs on Spark’s distributed cluster, not a single machine.\n\n- \nOperations can scale to large datasets that exceed single-machine memory limits."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/pandas_on_spark/index.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"621b87dd44ae514571e5fe6fc1f48a70d4bb75a152f8cae4b3f84a724d9a951b","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"pandas-api-distributed-compute","decision_id":"pandas-api-distributed-compute"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-050","number":50,"stem":"A data engineering team is evaluating different technology stacks for big data processing. The team needs a framework that can process large-scale datasets, provide high-level APIs for SQL-based querying, and efficiently support both batch and streaming workloads.\n\nWhich two advantages of Apache Spark best satisfy these requirements? (Choose two.)","options":["A.\nIt operates using a serverless architecture, removing the need to manage clusters.","B.\nIt integrates seamlessly with web development frameworks for building front-end applications.","C.\nIt provides built-in distributed machine learning model training capabilities out of the box.","D.\nIt includes native support for SQL-based querying through Spark SQL.","E.\nIt offers mature APIs such as DataFrames and Datasets for structured data processing and manipulation."],"correct":3,"correct_many":[3,4],"domain":"Architecture","topic":"spark-modules","concept":"spark-modules","concept_label":"spark-modules","explanation_ja":"SQLとstructured data APIが要件に対応する。MLlib自体は存在するがこの要件の主眼ではない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answers: D and E\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirements in the scenario are:\n\n- \nProcess large-scale datasets\n\n- \nProvide SQL-based querying\n\n- \nSupport batch and streaming workloads\n\n- \nProvide high-level APIs for structured data processing\n\nTwo Apache Spark capabilities that directly satisfy these requirements are Spark SQL and the DataFrame/Dataset APIs.\n\nD. It includes native support for SQL-based querying through Spark SQL.\n\nSpark SQL enables users to run SQL queries on large distributed datasets. It supports structured data processing using familiar SQL syntax and integrates with the Spark engine for distributed execution.\n\nSpark SQL also includes the Catalyst Optimizer, which improves query performance through optimizations such as:\n\n- \nPredicate pushdown\n\n- \nJoin optimization\n\n- \nColumn pruning\n\nExample:\n\n```\nSELECT region, SUM(sales)\nFROM transactions\nGROUP BY region\n```\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html)\n\nE. It offers mature APIs such as DataFrames and Datasets for structured data processing and manipulation.\n\nSpark provides high-level APIs like DataFrames and Datasets that simplify large-scale data transformations. These APIs allow developers to perform operations such as:\n\n- \nFiltering\n\n- \nAggregations\n\n- \nJoins\n\n- \nTransformations\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.groupBy(\"region\").sum(\"sales\")\n```\n\nThese APIs are optimized using Spark’s execution engine and allow developers to build scalable batch and streaming pipelines.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#dataframes](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#dataframes)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. It operates using a serverless architecture, removing the need to manage clusters.\nApache Spark requires cluster resources (or local mode) and is not inherently serverless.\n\nB. It integrates seamlessly with web development frameworks for building front-end applications.\nSpark is designed for data processing and analytics, not for building front-end applications.\n\nC. It provides built-in distributed machine learning model training capabilities out of the box.\nSpark MLlib does provide machine learning features, but this capability does not directly address the requirements of SQL querying and structured data processing stated in the scenario.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark provides multiple modules for different workloads:\n\n- \nSpark SQL → Structured data processing and SQL queries\n\n- \nDataFrames / Datasets → High-level APIs for transformations\n\n- \nStructured Streaming → Streaming workloads\n\n- \nMLlib → Machine learning\n\n- \nGraphX → Graph analytics\n\nFor structured analytics pipelines involving SQL and transformations, Spark SQL and DataFrame APIs are the primary components used.","source_option_explanations":["A. It operates using a serverless architecture, removing the need to manage clusters.\nApache Spark requires cluster resources (or local mode) and is not inherently serverless.","B. It integrates seamlessly with web development frameworks for building front-end applications.\nSpark is designed for data processing and analytics, not for building front-end applications.","C. It provides built-in distributed machine learning model training capabilities out of the box.\nSpark MLlib does provide machine learning features, but this capability does not directly address the requirements of SQL querying and structured data processing stated in the scenario.","D. It includes native support for SQL-based querying through Spark SQL.\n\nSpark SQL enables users to run SQL queries on large distributed datasets. It supports structured data processing using familiar SQL syntax and integrates with the Spark engine for distributed execution.\n\nSpark SQL also includes the Catalyst Optimizer, which improves query performance through optimizations such as:\n\n- \nPredicate pushdown\n\n- \nJoin optimization\n\n- \nColumn pruning\n\nExample:\n\n```\nSELECT region, SUM(sales)\nFROM transactions\nGROUP BY region\n```","E. It offers mature APIs such as DataFrames and Datasets for structured data processing and manipulation.\n\nSpark provides high-level APIs like DataFrames and Datasets that simplify large-scale data transformations. These APIs allow developers to perform operations such as:\n\n- \nFiltering\n\n- \nAggregations\n\n- \nJoins\n\n- \nTransformations\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.groupBy(\"region\").sum(\"sales\")\n```"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html#dataframes"],"images":[],"source_hash":"ef48f07e010c030d04444a018501279e3ac984850fc7fefd2db03d04a1e6ab56","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"sql-dataframe-modules","decision_id":"sql-dataframe-modules"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-051","number":51,"stem":"A data engineer observes that a Spark application is running slowly due to too many partitions in a DataFrame, resulting in a large number of small tasks.\n\nHow can the engineer reduce the number of partitions to improve performance without triggering a full shuffle?","options":["A.\nApply the `distinct()` transformation to merge similar partitions.","B.\nUse the `coalesce()` transformation with a smaller number of partitions.","C.\nUse the `sortBy()` transformation to reorganize the data.","D.\nUse the `repartition()` transformation with a smaller number of partitions."],"correct":1,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"partition-resize","concept":"partition-resize","concept_label":"partition-resize","explanation_ja":"小さいtaskが多すぎる場合はcoalesceでpartitionを減らす選択肢。極端な集約には注意する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B. Use the coalesce() transformation with a smaller number of partitions.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe problem described is:\n\n- \nThe DataFrame has too many partitions\n\n- \nThis creates many small tasks\n\n- \nThe goal is to reduce the number of partitions\n\n- \nAvoid a full shuffle\n\nThe correct solution in Spark is `coalesce()`.\n\n`coalesce()` reduces the number of partitions by merging existing partitions without performing a full shuffle. Because it minimizes data movement across the cluster, it is the most efficient method when decreasing partition counts.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf_reduced = df.coalesce(100)\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nExisting partitions are merged into fewer partitions.\n\n- \nData largely remains on the same nodes.\n\n- \nNo expensive shuffle stage is introduced.\n\nThis helps reduce scheduling overhead and improves performance when the DataFrame contains many small partitions.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. Apply the `distinct()` transformation to merge similar partitions.\n`distinct()` removes duplicate rows but does not change partition counts intentionally and may introduce shuffle operations.\n\nC. Use the `sortBy()` transformation to reorganize the data.\nSorting requires data shuffling and ordering across partitions, which is expensive and unrelated to reducing partition count.\n\nD. Use the `repartition()` transformation with a smaller number of partitions.\n`repartition()` always performs a full shuffle of the data, redistributing records across partitions. While it changes partition counts, it does not meet the requirement of avoiding a shuffle.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark provides two main methods for adjusting partition counts:\n\n- \ncoalesce()\n\n- \nUsed to reduce partitions\n\n- \nAvoids full shuffle\n\n- \nFaster when decreasing partitions\n\n- \nrepartition()\n\n- \nPerforms full shuffle\n\n- \nUsed when increasing partitions or redistributing data evenly\n\nFor reducing partitions efficiently, coalesce() is the recommended approach.","source_option_explanations":["A. Apply the `distinct()` transformation to merge similar partitions.\n`distinct()` removes duplicate rows but does not change partition counts intentionally and may introduce shuffle operations.","Correct Answer: B. Use the coalesce() transformation with a smaller number of partitions.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe problem described is:\n\n- \nThe DataFrame has too many partitions\n\n- \nThis creates many small tasks\n\n- \nThe goal is to reduce the number of partitions\n\n- \nAvoid a full shuffle\n\nThe correct solution in Spark is `coalesce()`.\n\n`coalesce()` reduces the number of partitions by merging existing partitions without performing a full shuffle. Because it minimizes data movement across the cluster, it is the most efficient method when decreasing partition counts.","C. Use the `sortBy()` transformation to reorganize the data.\nSorting requires data shuffling and ordering across partitions, which is expensive and unrelated to reducing partition count.","D. Use the `repartition()` transformation with a smaller number of partitions.\n`repartition()` always performs a full shuffle of the data, redistributing records across partitions. While it changes partition counts, it does not meet the requirement of avoiding a shuffle."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"30995ea31369c2083fbabd25b064953a701d4c509222f854fd3649eb92caf118","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"coalesce-repartition","decision_id":"coalesce-repartition"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-052","number":52,"stem":"An engineer has two DataFrames, df1 and df2, where df1 is small and df2 is large. To improve the performance of the join operation, the engineer uses a broadcast join:\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast\n \nresult = df2.join(broadcast(df1), on='id', how='inner')\n```\n\nWhat is the purpose of using `broadcast()` in this situation?","options":["A.\nIt filters the `id` values before executing the join operation.","B.\nIt increases the partition size for both `df1` and `df2`.","C.\nIt reduces shuffle operations by replicating the smaller DataFrame across all executor nodes.","D.\nIt ensures that the join occurs only when the `id` values exactly match."],"correct":2,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"broadcast-mechanism","concept":"broadcast-mechanism","concept_label":"broadcast-mechanism","explanation_ja":"broadcast hintは小さい側のデータをexecutorに配り、大きい側のshuffleを回避するために使う。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C. It reduces shuffle operations by replicating the smaller DataFrame across all executor nodes.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, a broadcast join is an optimization used when one dataset is much smaller than the other. Instead of shuffling both DataFrames across the cluster, Spark broadcasts the smaller DataFrame to every executor node.\n\nIn the given code:\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast\n \nresult = df2.join(broadcast(df1), on='id', how='inner')\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \n`df1` (the small DataFrame) is sent to all executor nodes.\n\n- \nEach executor performs the join locally with its partition of `df2`.\n\n- \nThis avoids expensive shuffle operations, which significantly improves performance when joining large and small datasets.\n\nWithout broadcasting, Spark might perform a shuffle-based join (such as a Sort-Merge Join), where data from both DataFrames must be redistributed across the cluster.\n\nBroadcast joins are particularly effective when:\n\n- \nOne dataset is small enough to fit in executor memory\n\n- \nThe other dataset is very large\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. It filters the id values before executing the join operation.\nBroadcasting does not filter values. It only changes the join execution strategy.\n\nB. It increases the partition size for both df1 and df2.\nBroadcasting does not modify partition sizes. It simply copies the small DataFrame to each executor.\n\nD. It ensures that the join occurs only when the id values exactly match.\nMatching `id` values is determined by the join condition (`on='id'`), not by broadcasting.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark supports several join strategies:\n\nBroadcast Hash Join\n\n- \nUsed when one dataset is small\n\n- \nAvoids shuffle\n\n- \nOften the fastest option\n\nSort-Merge Join\n\n- \nUsed when both datasets are large\n\n- \nRequires shuffle and sorting\n\nShuffle Hash Join\n\n- \nRequires data redistribution\n\nBroadcast joins are one of the most important performance optimizations in Spark when joining large datasets with small lookup tables.","source_option_explanations":["A. It filters the id values before executing the join operation.\nBroadcasting does not filter values. It only changes the join execution strategy.","B. It increases the partition size for both df1 and df2.\nBroadcasting does not modify partition sizes. It simply copies the small DataFrame to each executor.","Correct Answer: C. It reduces shuffle operations by replicating the smaller DataFrame across all executor nodes.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, a broadcast join is an optimization used when one dataset is much smaller than the other. Instead of shuffling both DataFrames across the cluster, Spark broadcasts the smaller DataFrame to every executor node.\n\nIn the given code:\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast\n \nresult = df2.join(broadcast(df1), on='id', how='inner')\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \n`df1` (the small DataFrame) is sent to all executor nodes.\n\n- \nEach executor performs the join locally with its partition of `df2`.\n\n- \nThis avoids expensive shuffle operations, which significantly improves performance when joining large and small datasets.","D. It ensures that the join occurs only when the id values exactly match.\nMatching `id` values is determined by the join condition (`on='id'`), not by broadcasting."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries"],"images":[],"source_hash":"5237d7a935a0402b42adff43998f98edafbb807eed702076afa27cc6d3c00103","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"broadcast-replication-mechanism","decision_id":"broadcast-replication-mechanism"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-053","number":53,"stem":"A data engineer has identified duplicate records in a Parquet table generated from ingesting IoT sensor data.\n\nThe table has the following schema:\n\n```\nevent_ts TIMESTAMP\nsensor_id STRING\nmetric_value LONG\ningest_ts TIMESTAMP\nsource_file_path STRING\n```\n\nEach IoT sensor event originally contains event_ts, sensor_id, and metric_value. During ingestion, additional fields ingest_ts and source_file_path are added. Analysis reveals that the ingestion process is creating duplicate records, even though the actual event fields remain the same.\n\nWhich code snippet should the engineer use to correctly remove duplicate records based on the original event fields?","options":["A.\ndedup_df = iot_bronze_df.dropDuplicates([\"ingest_ts\", \"sensor_id\", \"metric_value\", \"source_file_path\"])","B.\ndedup_df = iot_bronze_df.dropDuplicates()","C.\ndedup_df = iot_bronze_df.groupBy([\"event_ts\", \"sensor_id\", \"metric_value\"])","D.\ndedup_df = iot_bronze_df.dropDuplicates([\"event_ts\", \"sensor_id\", \"metric_value\"])"],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"dedup-keys","concept":"dedup-keys","concept_label":"dedup-keys","explanation_ja":"元イベントの3列だけを重複判定キーにする。ingest時刻等を含めると同一イベントも別行になる。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicates.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. dedup_df = iot_bronze_df.dropDuplicates([\"event_ts\", \"sensor_id\", \"metric_value\"])\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement is to remove duplicate IoT events based on the original event fields, which are:\n\n- \n`event_ts`\n\n- \n`sensor_id`\n\n- \n`metric_value`\n\nDuring ingestion, two additional metadata columns were added:\n\n- \n`ingest_ts`\n\n- \n`source_file_path`\n\nSince these metadata fields can differ between duplicate ingestions, they cannot be used to determine event uniqueness. Therefore, de-duplication must be performed using only the original event fields.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\ndedup_df = iot_bronze_df.dropDuplicates([\"event_ts\", \"sensor_id\", \"metric_value\"])\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark compares rows based only on the specified columns.\n\n- \nRows with the same `event_ts`, `sensor_id`, and `metric_value` are treated as duplicates.\n\n- \nOnly one of the duplicate rows is retained.\n\nThis ensures duplicates created by repeated ingestion are removed correctly.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicates.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicates.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA.\n\n```\ndropDuplicates([\"ingest_ts\", \"sensor_id\", \"metric_value\", \"source_file_path\"])\n```\n\nThis incorrectly includes ingestion metadata fields. Since `ingest_ts` and `source_file_path` differ between ingestions, duplicates would not be removed correctly.\n\nB.\n\n```\ndropDuplicates()\n```\n\nThis removes duplicates only if all columns match exactly. Because ingestion metadata differs, duplicate events would still remain.\n\nC.\n\n```\ngroupBy([\"event_ts\", \"sensor_id\", \"metric_value\"])\n```\n\n`groupBy()` creates grouped aggregations and does not return the original rows unless an aggregation function is applied. It is not a correct approach for simple deduplication.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark’s `dropDuplicates()` allows specifying a subset of columns that define record uniqueness. This is particularly important in ingestion pipelines where:\n\n- \nBusiness keys represent the true event identity.\n\n- \nMetadata fields vary between ingestion attempts.\n\nBest practice is to deduplicate based on the original event keys, not ingestion metadata.","source_option_explanations":["A.\n\n```\ndropDuplicates([\"ingest_ts\", \"sensor_id\", \"metric_value\", \"source_file_path\"])\n```\n\nThis incorrectly includes ingestion metadata fields. Since `ingest_ts` and `source_file_path` differ between ingestions, duplicates would not be removed correctly.","B.\n\n```\ndropDuplicates()\n```\n\nThis removes duplicates only if all columns match exactly. Because ingestion metadata differs, duplicate events would still remain.","C.\n\n```\ngroupBy([\"event_ts\", \"sensor_id\", \"metric_value\"])\n```\n\n`groupBy()` creates grouped aggregations and does not return the original rows unless an aggregation function is applied. It is not a correct approach for simple deduplication.","Correct Answer: D. dedup_df = iot_bronze_df.dropDuplicates([\"event_ts\", \"sensor_id\", \"metric_value\"])\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement is to remove duplicate IoT events based on the original event fields, which are:\n\n- \n`event_ts`\n\n- \n`sensor_id`\n\n- \n`metric_value`\n\nDuring ingestion, two additional metadata columns were added:\n\n- \n`ingest_ts`\n\n- \n`source_file_path`\n\nSince these metadata fields can differ between duplicate ingestions, they cannot be used to determine event uniqueness. Therefore, de-duplication must be performed using only the original event fields."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicates.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"d2a217e72443263705cdb810b2c26973ba2db42db04fe2bbd54dd78b5461ad59","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"dedup-subset-business-fields","decision_id":"dedup-subset-business-fields"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-054","number":54,"stem":"Which command should be used to overwrite an existing JSON file when writing a DataFrame?","options":["A. df.write.mode(\"overwrite\").json(\"path/to/file\")","B. df.write.overwrite.json(\"path/to/file\")","C. df.write.json(\"path/to/file\", overwrite=True)","D. df.write.format(\"json\").save(\"path/to/file\", mode=\"overwrite\")"],"correct":0,"correct_many":[0,3],"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"write-overwrite","concept":"write-overwrite","concept_label":"write-overwrite","explanation_ja":"modeチェーンとsaveのmode引数の両方でoverwriteを書ける。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.save.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.save.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.mode.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"1d34bd21cf6fa3f1288b201e8b36c17f5657c42b6d52540aaecafc1adbb89b00","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"saveのmode引数も有効なのでAとDが正解。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.save.html"],"original_stem":"Which command should be used to overwrite an existing JSON file when writing a DataFrame?","original_options":["A. df.write.mode(\"overwrite\").json(\"path/to/file\")","B. df.write.overwrite.json(\"path/to/file\")","C. df.write.json(\"path/to/file\", overwrite=True)","D. df.write.format(\"json\").save(\"path/to/file\", mode=\"overwrite\")"],"original_correct":["A"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"json-overwrite-api","decision_id":"json-overwrite-api"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-055","number":55,"stem":"A developer has a DataFrame with the columns color, fruit, v1, and v2. The developer writes the DataFrame using the following code:\n\n```\ndf.write.partitionBy(\"color\", \"fruit\").parquet(\"/path/to/output\")\n```\n\nWhat will be the result of executing this code?","options":["A.\nAll data will be written into a single Parquet file.","B.\nThe operation will fail if there are null values in either of the partition columns.","C.\nNew partitions will automatically be appended to an existing Parquet file.","D.\nSeparate directories will be created for each unique combination of color and fruit."],"correct":3,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"storage-partition","concept":"storage-partition","concept_label":"storage-partition","explanation_ja":"partitionByの列値の組合せごとにディレクトリができる。出力ファイル数は組合せ数と同じとは限らない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. Separate directories will be created for each unique combination of color and fruit.\n\nExplanation:\n\nWhen a DataFrame is written using `partitionBy()`, Spark organizes the output data into directory-based partitions based on the specified columns. Each unique combination of partition column values results in a separate directory.\n\nGiven the code:\n\n```\ndf.write.partitionBy(\"color\", \"fruit\").parquet(\"/path/to/output\")\n```\n\nSpark will create a directory structure like:\n\n```\n/path/to/output/\n   color=red/\n       fruit=apple/\n           part-00000.parquet\n   color=red/\n       fruit=banana/\n           part-00001.parquet\n   color=green/\n       fruit=apple/\n           part-00002.parquet\n```\n\nImportant behavior:\n\n- \nThe partition columns (`color`, `fruit`) become directory names.\n\n- \nThe remaining columns (`v1`, `v2`) are stored inside the Parquet files.\n\n- \nEach partition contains one or more Parquet files depending on the number of partitions/tasks.\n\nThis partitioning improves query performance because Spark can skip irrelevant partitions when filtering on partition columns.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice:\n\nA. All data will be written into a single Parquet file.\nSpark writes data in multiple output files based on partitions/tasks, not a single file.\n\nB. The operation will fail if there are null values in either of the partition columns.\nSpark does not fail on null partition values. Instead, it creates a special directory such as:\n\n```\ncolor=null/\n```\n\nC. New partitions will automatically be appended to an existing Parquet file.\nPartitioning creates directories, not partitions inside an existing Parquet file.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark partitioning organizes data physically on storage using directory structures. This enables partition pruning, which significantly improves query performance.\n\nExample query:\n\n```\nSELECT * FROM table WHERE color='red'\n```\n\nSpark will read only the directory:\n\n```\ncolor=red/\n```\n\ninstead of scanning the entire dataset.\n\nThis is a key optimization used in large-scale data lakes and Delta Lake tables.","source_option_explanations":["A. All data will be written into a single Parquet file.\nSpark writes data in multiple output files based on partitions/tasks, not a single file.","B. The operation will fail if there are null values in either of the partition columns.\nSpark does not fail on null partition values. Instead, it creates a special directory such as:\n\n```\ncolor=null/\n```","C. New partitions will automatically be appended to an existing Parquet file.\nPartitioning creates directories, not partitions inside an existing Parquet file.","Correct Answer: D. Separate directories will be created for each unique combination of color and fruit.\n\nExplanation:\n\nWhen a DataFrame is written using `partitionBy()`, Spark organizes the output data into directory-based partitions based on the specified columns. Each unique combination of partition column values results in a separate directory.\n\nGiven the code:\n\n```\ndf.write.partitionBy(\"color\", \"fruit\").parquet(\"/path/to/output\")\n```\n\nSpark will create a directory structure like:\n\n```\n/path/to/output/\n   color=red/\n       fruit=apple/\n           part-00000.parquet\n   color=red/\n       fruit=banana/\n           part-00001.parquet\n   color=green/\n       fruit=apple/\n           part-00002.parquet\n```\n\nImportant behavior:\n\n- \nThe partition columns (`color`, `fruit`) become directory names.\n\n- \nThe remaining columns (`v1`, `v2`) are stored inside the Parquet files.\n\n- \nEach partition contains one or more Parquet files depending on the number of partitions/tasks.\n\nThis partitioning improves query performance because Spark can skip irrelevant partitions when filtering on partition columns."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"1d3b328bbac83cf05ae86031b78f72ffc8a0ad0edc7bbe4050ee33103af5fffb","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"partition-by-directory-layout","decision_id":"partition-by-directory-layout"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-056","number":56,"stem":"A data engineer is building a streaming pipeline using Apache Spark Structured Streaming and applies watermarking to handle late-arriving events. The following code is used:\n\n```\ninputStream\n.withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"10 minutes\")\n.groupBy(window(\"event_time\", \"15 minutes\"))\n.count()\n```\n\nWhich statement accurately describes the guarantee provided by this watermark?","options":["A.\nRecords that arrive later than the watermark threshold (10 minutes) are automatically included in the aggregation if they fall within the 15-minute window.","B.\nAny data that arrives more than 10 minutes later than the watermark threshold is ignored and excluded from the aggregation.","C.\nData arriving more than 10 minutes after the latest watermark will still be processed but will be assigned to the next window.","D.\nThe watermark guarantees that late records arriving within 10 minutes of the latest `event_time` will still be processed and included in the windowed aggregation."],"correct":3,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"watermark-late-data","concept":"watermark-late-data","concept_label":"watermark-late-data","explanation_ja":"遅延幅内のイベントは落とさない保証がある。しきい値を超えたデータが必ず落ちるとは限らない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#semantic-guarantees-of-aggregation-with-watermarking","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#semantic-guarantees-of-aggregation-with-watermarking","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#handling-late-data-and-watermarking"],"images":[],"source_hash":"759c7ba86816cf6bfabf583b5915e3afc57ed03520bf568bd8b80704299b918b","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"watermarkの遅延保証は一方向。設定した遅延内のデータを落とさないDが正しい。基準から更に10分というBは誤り。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#semantic-guarantees-of-aggregation-with-watermarking"],"original_stem":"A data engineer is building a streaming pipeline using Apache Spark Structured Streaming and applies watermarking to handle late-arriving events. The following code is used:\n\n```\ninputStream\n.withWatermark(\"event_time\", \"10 minutes\")\n.groupBy(window(\"event_time\", \"15 minutes\"))\n.count()\n```\n\nWhat happens to records that arrive after the watermark threshold?","original_options":["A.\nRecords that arrive later than the watermark threshold (10 minutes) are automatically included in the aggregation if they fall within the 15-minute window.","B.\nAny data that arrives more than 10 minutes later than the watermark threshold is ignored and excluded from the aggregation.","C.\nData arriving more than 10 minutes after the latest watermark will still be processed but will be assigned to the next window.","D.\nThe watermark guarantees that late records arriving within 10 minutes of the latest `event_time` will still be processed and included in the windowed aggregation."],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"watermark-one-sided-lateness-guarantee","decision_id":"watermark-one-sided-lateness-guarantee"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-057","number":57,"stem":"A data engineer is working with Spark SQL and has a large JSON file stored at /data/input.json. The file contains records with varying schemas, and the engineer wants to create an external table in Spark SQL that can query this file directly without first loading it into a DataFrame.\n\nThe engineer must:\n\n- \nCreate an external table named users that reads data from /data/input.json.\n\n- \nAllow Spark to infer the schema automatically.\n\n- \nHandle records with different schemas by merging them.\n\nWhich code snippet should be used to accomplish this task?","options":["A.\n\n```\nCREATE TABLE users USING json\nOPTIONS (path '/data/input.json')\n```","B.\n\n```\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE users USING json\nOPTIONS (path '/data/input.json')\n```","C.\n\n```\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE users USING json\nOPTIONS (path '/data/input.json', mergeSchema 'true')\n```","D.\n\n```\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE users USING json\nOPTIONS (path '/data/input.json', schemaMerge 'true')\n```"],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"json-external-table","concept":"json-external-table","concept_label":"json-external-table","explanation_ja":"JSONのschema推論にはParquet向けのmergeSchema指定は不要。選択肢の構文と同値性は追加確認中。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-json.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C.\n\n```\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE users USING json\nOPTIONS (path '/data/input.json', mergeSchema 'true')\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirements are:\n\n- \nCreate an external table\n\n- \nRead directly from a JSON file\n\n- \nInfer schema automatically\n\n- \nHandle records with varying schemas by merging them\n\nSpark SQL supports creating tables directly over file-based data sources using the Data Source Table syntax.\n\nIn this syntax:\n\n- \n`USING json` tells Spark to use the JSON data source\n\n- \n`OPTIONS (path '...')` specifies the file location\n\n- \n`mergeSchema 'true'` enables schema merging, allowing Spark to reconcile differences between records with varying structures\n\nSpark automatically infers the schema when it reads JSON files unless a schema is explicitly provided.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-json.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-json.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not suitable:\n\nA.\n\n```\nCREATE TABLE users USING json\nOPTIONS (path '/data/input.json')\n```\n\nThis would create a table over the JSON file, but it does not enable schema merging, which is required when records contain varying schemas.\n\nB.\n\n```\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE users USING json\nOPTIONS (path '/data/input.json')\n```\n\nThis also reads the JSON file, but it does not handle schema evolution or varying schemas, which was explicitly required.\n\nD.\n\n```\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE users USING json\nOPTIONS (path '/data/input.json', schemaMerge 'true')\n```\n\n`scehmaMerge` is not a valid Spark option. The correct option name is `mergeSchema`.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark supports schema inference for semi-structured data formats such as:\n\n- \nJSON\n\n- \nParquet\n\n- \nORC\n\nWhen working with evolving schemas, Spark can merge multiple schemas automatically. This is particularly useful when ingesting logs or event data where records may contain optional or newly introduced fields.\n\nSchema merging allows Spark to produce a unified schema across all records, preventing query failures due to structural differences.","source_option_explanations":["A.\n\n```\nCREATE TABLE users USING json\nOPTIONS (path '/data/input.json')\n```\n\nThis would create a table over the JSON file, but it does not enable schema merging, which is required when records contain varying schemas.","B.\n\n```\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE users USING json\nOPTIONS (path '/data/input.json')\n```\n\nThis also reads the JSON file, but it does not handle schema evolution or varying schemas, which was explicitly required.","Correct Answer: C.\n\n```\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE users USING json\nOPTIONS (path '/data/input.json', mergeSchema 'true')\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirements are:\n\n- \nCreate an external table\n\n- \nRead directly from a JSON file\n\n- \nInfer schema automatically\n\n- \nHandle records with varying schemas by merging them\n\nSpark SQL supports creating tables directly over file-based data sources using the Data Source Table syntax.\n\nIn this syntax:\n\n- \n`USING json` tells Spark to use the JSON data source\n\n- \n`OPTIONS (path '...')` specifies the file location\n\n- \n`mergeSchema 'true'` enables schema merging, allowing Spark to reconcile differences between records with varying structures","D.\n\n```\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE users USING json\nOPTIONS (path '/data/input.json', schemaMerge 'true')\n```\n\n`scehmaMerge` is not a valid Spark option. The correct option name is `mergeSchema`."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-json.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"b0cb9e8a09bc125af473cce7ef1ca20c9b4930b41ea3f9251ae3ec4a91b59bf0","revision":null,"grading_hold":"JSONのschema推論とmergeSchemaの関係、およびEXTERNAL/path構文の選択肢の同値性を追加検証中。単一正解を断定せず、模試採点から除外します。","validation":"原文保存・採点保留","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"json-external-table","decision_id":"json-external-table"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-058","number":58,"stem":"A data scientist is analyzing a large dataset using Apache Spark and has written the following code:\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.schema(\"error_column STRING, timestamp STRING\").option(\"header\", True).csv(\"large_dataset.csv\")\n \nfiltered_df = df.filter(col(\"error_column\").contains(\"error\"))\n \nmapped_df = filtered_df.select(\n    split(col(\"timestamp\"), \" \").getItem(0).alias(\"date\"),\n    lit(1).alias(\"count\")\n)\n \nreduced_df = mapped_df.groupBy(\"date\").sum(\"count\")\n \nreduced_df.count()\nreduced_df.show()\n```\n\nAfter the DataFrame has been constructed, which listed step first executes the transformation pipeline that produces reduced_df?","options":["A.\nWhen the `filter` transformation is applied.","B.\nWhen the `count` action is executed.","C.\nWhen the `groupBy` transformation is applied.","D.\nWhen the `show` action is executed."],"correct":1,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"lazy-execution","concept":"lazy-execution","concept_label":"lazy-execution","explanation_ja":"明示schemaでDataFrameを作った後、集計パイプラインを最初に実行するのはcount action。showもaction。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.csv.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.csv.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/rdd-programming-guide.html#rdd-operations","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations-and-actions"],"images":[],"source_hash":"7562b07263be2c91858f3c926653779081bf8aeee106f04fe41dd780de32efde","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"headerもschemaもないCSV読取では列名が成立しない。schemaを明示し、対象を後続の変換パイプラインの実行に限定。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.csv.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/rdd-programming-guide.html#rdd-operations"],"original_stem":"A data scientist is analyzing a large dataset using Apache Spark and has written the following code:\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.csv(\"large_dataset.csv\")\n \nfiltered_df = df.filter(col(\"error_column\").contains(\"error\"))\n \nmapped_df = filtered_df.select(\n    split(col(\"timestamp\"), \" \").getItem(0).alias(\"date\"),\n    lit(1).alias(\"count\")\n)\n \nreduced_df = mapped_df.groupBy(\"date\").sum(\"count\")\n \nreduced_df.count()\nreduced_df.show()\n```\n\nAt which step will Spark actually start executing the computation on the dataset?","original_options":["A.\nWhen the `filter` transformation is applied.","B.\nWhen the `count` action is executed.","C.\nWhen the `groupBy` transformation is applied.","D.\nWhen the `show` action is executed."],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"lazy-action-trigger","decision_id":"first-action-executes-pipeline"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-059","number":59,"stem":"A data scientist is working with a Spark DataFrame named customerDF that stores customer details. One of the columns, email, contains customer email addresses. The data scientist needs to split this column into two new columns: username and domain.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly separates the email column into username and domain?","options":["A.\n\n```\ncustomerDF.select(\n    col(\"email\").substr(0, 5).alias(\"username\"),\n    col(\"email\").substr(-5).alias(\"domain\")\n)\n```","B.\n\n```\ncustomerDF.withColumn(\"username\", split(col(\"email\"), \"@\").getItem(0)) \\\n          .withColumn(\"domain\", split(col(\"email\"), \"@\").getItem(1))\n```","C.\n\n```\ncustomerDF.withColumn(\"username\", substring_index(col(\"email\"), \"@\", 1)) \\\n          .withColumn(\"domain\", substring_index(col(\"email\"), \"@\", -1))\n```","D.\n\n```\ncustomerDF.select(\n    regexp_replace(col(\"email\"), \"@\", \"\").alias(\"username\"),\n    regexp_replace(col(\"email\"), \"@\", \"\").alias(\"domain\")\n)\n```"],"correct":1,"correct_many":[1,2],"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"string-split","concept":"string-split","concept_label":"string-split","explanation_ja":"通常のemailならsplitとsubstring_indexの両方でusernameとdomainを取り出せる。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.substring_index.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.substring_index.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.split.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.split.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"54462117858d219b19298ae3ea54994832b6d1b6a35bfbc0306d7b0cb51ef16d","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"split/getItemとsubstring_indexのどちらでも通常のemailを分割できる。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.substring_index.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.split.html"],"original_stem":"A data scientist is working with a Spark DataFrame named customerDF that stores customer details. One of the columns, email, contains customer email addresses. The data scientist needs to split this column into two new columns: username and domain.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly separates the email column into username and domain?","original_options":["A.\n\n```\ncustomerDF.select(\n    col(\"email\").substr(0, 5).alias(\"username\"),\n    col(\"email\").substr(-5).alias(\"domain\")\n)\n```","B.\n\n```\ncustomerDF.withColumn(\"username\", split(col(\"email\"), \"@\").getItem(0)) \\\n          .withColumn(\"domain\", split(col(\"email\"), \"@\").getItem(1))\n```","C.\n\n```\ncustomerDF.withColumn(\"username\", substring_index(col(\"email\"), \"@\", 1)) \\\n          .withColumn(\"domain\", substring_index(col(\"email\"), \"@\", -1))\n```","D.\n\n```\ncustomerDF.select(\n    regexp_replace(col(\"email\"), \"@\", \"\").alias(\"username\"),\n    regexp_replace(col(\"email\"), \"@\", \"\").alias(\"domain\")\n)\n```"],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"string-split","decision_id":"string-split"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-060","number":60,"stem":"A data engineer needs to write a DataFrame `df` to a Parquet file, partition the output by the country column, and overwrite any existing data at the target location.\n\nWhich code snippet should be used in Apache Spark to achieve this?","options":["A.\n\n```\ndf.write.mode(\"overwrite\").partitionBy(\"country\").parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```","B.\n\n```\ndf.write.mode(\"append\").partitionBy(\"country\").parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```","C.\n\n```\ndf.write.mode(\"overwrite\").parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```","D.\n\n```\ndf.write.partitionBy(\"country\").parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```"],"correct":0,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"write-partition","concept":"write-partition","concept_label":"write-partition","explanation_ja":"overwrite・partitionBy・Parquetの3条件をすべて満たす必要がある。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.partitionBy.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A.\n\n```\ndf.write.mode(\"overwrite\").partitionBy(\"country\").parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement specifies three behaviors:\n\n- \nWrite the DataFrame to Parquet\n\n- \nPartition the output by `country`\n\n- \nOverwrite existing data\n\nSpark’s DataFrameWriter API supports these operations using:\n\n- \n`mode(\"overwrite\")` → replaces existing data at the target path\n\n- \n`partitionBy(\"country\")` → physically partitions the data by the specified column\n\n- \n`.parquet(path)` → writes the output in Parquet format\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\ndf.write \\\n  .mode(\"overwrite\") \\\n  .partitionBy(\"country\") \\\n  .parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\nSpark creates a directory structure such as:\n\n```\n/data/output/\n   country=US/\n       part-00000.parquet\n   country=IN/\n       part-00001.parquet\n   country=UK/\n       part-00002.parquet\n```\n\nThis partitioning enables partition pruning, allowing Spark to read only relevant directories when filtering by `country`.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.partitionBy.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.partitionBy.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not suitable:\n\nB.\n\n```\ndf.write.mode(\"append\").partitionBy(\"country\").parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```\n\n`append` adds new data to the existing dataset instead of replacing it. This does not meet the requirement to overwrite existing data.\n\nC.\n\n```\ndf.write.mode(\"overwrite\").parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```\n\nThis overwrites the data but does not partition by `country`, which is explicitly required.\n\nD.\n\n```\ndf.write.partitionBy(\"country\").parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```\n\nThis partitions the output but uses the default write mode (`errorIfExists`), meaning the operation will fail if data already exists at the target location.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark supports several write modes:\n\n- \n`overwrite` → replaces existing data\n\n- \n`append` → adds new records\n\n- \n`ignore` → skips writing if data exists\n\n- \n`errorIfExists` → fails if the path exists\n\nPartitioning is widely used in data lakes and Delta tables because it allows Spark to skip irrelevant partitions, improving query performance.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A.\n\n```\ndf.write.mode(\"overwrite\").partitionBy(\"country\").parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement specifies three behaviors:\n\n- \nWrite the DataFrame to Parquet\n\n- \nPartition the output by `country`\n\n- \nOverwrite existing data\n\nSpark’s DataFrameWriter API supports these operations using:\n\n- \n`mode(\"overwrite\")` → replaces existing data at the target path\n\n- \n`partitionBy(\"country\")` → physically partitions the data by the specified column\n\n- \n`.parquet(path)` → writes the output in Parquet format\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\ndf.write \\\n  .mode(\"overwrite\") \\\n  .partitionBy(\"country\") \\\n  .parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\nSpark creates a directory structure such as:\n\n```\n/data/output/\n   country=US/\n       part-00000.parquet\n   country=IN/\n       part-00001.parquet\n   country=UK/\n       part-00002.parquet\n```\n\nThis partitioning enables partition pruning, allowing Spark to read only relevant directories when filtering by `country`.","B.\n\n```\ndf.write.mode(\"append\").partitionBy(\"country\").parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```\n\n`append` adds new data to the existing dataset instead of replacing it. This does not meet the requirement to overwrite existing data.","C.\n\n```\ndf.write.mode(\"overwrite\").parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```\n\nThis overwrites the data but does not partition by `country`, which is explicitly required.","D.\n\n```\ndf.write.partitionBy(\"country\").parquet(\"/data/output\")\n```\n\nThis partitions the output but uses the default write mode (`errorIfExists`), meaning the operation will fail if data already exists at the target location."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.partitionBy.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"be1f7cf84855745f6df4a401c70feaa6777ba8cbaee43651980f5f16e73ad8e8","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"write-overwrite-partition-parquet","decision_id":"write-overwrite-partition-parquet"}]},{"id":"EX4-S4","title":"原文セット 4","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-061","number":61,"stem":"A data scientist is working on a project that involves processing large volumes of structured data, executing SQL-based queries, and applying machine learning algorithms. The data scientist plans to use Apache Spark for this workflow.\n\nWhich combination of Apache Spark modules best supports these requirements?","options":["A.\nSpark DataFrames, Structured Streaming, and GraphX","B.\nSpark SQL, Pandas API on Spark, and Structured Streaming","C.\nSpark Streaming, GraphX, and Pandas API on Spark","D.\nSpark DataFrames, Spark SQL, and MLlib"],"correct":3,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"spark-modules","concept":"spark-modules","concept_label":"spark-modules","explanation_ja":"DataFrameとSpark SQLでstructured dataを扱い、MLlibで機械学習を扱う。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. Spark DataFrames, Spark SQL, and MLlib\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe scenario requires support for three capabilities:\n\n- \nProcessing large volumes of structured data\n\n- \nExecuting SQL-based queries\n\n- \nApplying machine learning algorithms\n\nApache Spark provides specialized modules for each of these tasks.\n\nSpark DataFrames\n\nDataFrames provide a distributed tabular abstraction for structured data processing. They allow efficient transformations such as filtering, joins, aggregations, and column operations while leveraging Spark’s distributed execution engine.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.groupBy(\"region\").sum(\"sales\")\n```\n\nDataFrames are optimized using Spark’s Catalyst Optimizer and Tungsten execution engine, which improve query performance.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html)\n\nSpark SQL\n\nSpark SQL allows users to perform SQL-based querying on structured datasets. DataFrames can be registered as temporary views and queried using SQL syntax.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nSELECT region, SUM(sales)\nFROM transactions\nGROUP BY region\n```\n\nSpark SQL integrates with the DataFrame API and enables efficient analytics on large datasets.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html)\n\nMLlib\n\nMLlib is Spark’s distributed machine learning library. It provides scalable algorithms for tasks such as:\n\n- \nClassification\n\n- \nRegression\n\n- \nClustering\n\n- \nRecommendation systems\n\n- \nFeature engineering\n\nExample:\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.ml.classification import LogisticRegression\n```\n\nMLlib allows machine learning models to be trained on large distributed datasets.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/ml-guide.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/ml-guide.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not suitable:\n\nA. Spark DataFrames, Structured Streaming, and GraphX\nStructured Streaming is used for real-time data processing, which is not required in the scenario. GraphX is used for graph analytics, not machine learning or SQL analytics.\n\nB. Spark SQL, Pandas API on Spark, and Structured Streaming\nWhile Spark SQL is relevant, Structured Streaming is unnecessary for this requirement, and the Pandas API on Spark is primarily a compatibility layer rather than a core module for ML workflows.\n\nC. Spark Streaming, GraphX, and Pandas API on Spark\nSpark Streaming and GraphX are specialized modules unrelated to the requirements of structured analytics and machine learning.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark is built around several core modules:\n\n- \nSpark SQL / DataFrames / Datasets → structured data analytics\n\n- \nMLlib → distributed machine learning\n\n- \nStructured Streaming → streaming pipelines\n\n- \nGraphX → graph processing\n\nFor most data science pipelines, the common stack is:\n\nDataFrames → Spark SQL → MLlib.","source_option_explanations":["A. Spark DataFrames, Structured Streaming, and GraphX\nStructured Streaming is used for real-time data processing, which is not required in the scenario. GraphX is used for graph analytics, not machine learning or SQL analytics.","B. Spark SQL, Pandas API on Spark, and Structured Streaming\nWhile Spark SQL is relevant, Structured Streaming is unnecessary for this requirement, and the Pandas API on Spark is primarily a compatibility layer rather than a core module for ML workflows.","C. Spark Streaming, GraphX, and Pandas API on Spark\nSpark Streaming and GraphX are specialized modules unrelated to the requirements of structured analytics and machine learning.","Correct Answer: D. Spark DataFrames, Spark SQL, and MLlib\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe scenario requires support for three capabilities:\n\n- \nProcessing large volumes of structured data\n\n- \nExecuting SQL-based queries\n\n- \nApplying machine learning algorithms\n\nApache Spark provides specialized modules for each of these tasks.\n\nSpark DataFrames\n\nDataFrames provide a distributed tabular abstraction for structured data processing. They allow efficient transformations such as filtering, joins, aggregations, and column operations while leveraging Spark’s distributed execution engine."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/ml-guide.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"5524cf8be91486a3d84c4c588fbaacea883ffcfd0ee030f018af31e0a0babf18","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"spark-sql-and-mllib-module-selection","decision_id":"spark-sql-and-mllib-module-selection"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-062","number":62,"stem":"A data engineer needs to create a Streaming DataFrame that reads messages from a Kafka topic named `feed`.\n\nThe following code is used:\n\n```\n1. spark\n2. .readStream\n3. .format(\"kafka\")\n4. .option(\"kafka.bootstrap.servers\", \"host1:port1,host2:port2\")\n5. ._____________\n6. .load()\n```\n\nWhich code fragment should be added on line 5 to subscribe to the Kafka topic?","options":["A.\n.option(\"subscribe\", \"feed\")","B.\n.option(\"subscribe.topic\", \"feed\")","C.\n.option(\"kafka.topic\", \"feed\")","D.\n.option(\"topic\", \"feed\")"],"correct":0,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"kafka-subscribe","concept":"kafka-subscribe","concept_label":"kafka-subscribe","explanation_ja":"Kafkaの購読topicはsubscribeオプション。kafka.topicというキーではない。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-kafka-integration.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A. `.option(\"subscribe\", \"feed\")`\n\nExplanation:\n\nWhen using Spark Structured Streaming with Kafka, the Kafka data source requires specifying which topic(s) the streaming query should read from. This is done using the `subscribe` option.\n\nCorrect code:\n\n```\nspark \\\n.readStream \\\n.format(\"kafka\") \\\n.option(\"kafka.bootstrap.servers\", \"host1:port1,host2:port2\") \\\n.option(\"subscribe\", \"feed\") \\\n.load()\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \n`format(\"kafka\")` tells Spark to use the Kafka connector.\n\n- \n`kafka.bootstrap.servers` specifies the Kafka brokers.\n\n- \n`subscribe` tells Spark which topic to read messages from.\n\n- \n`readStream` creates a Streaming DataFrame.\n\nSpark will continuously read messages from the Kafka topic `feed` and process them as streaming data.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-kafka-integration.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-kafka-integration.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not suitable:\n\nB. `.option(\"subscribe.topic\", \"feed\")`\nThis is not a valid Kafka option in Spark. Spark expects the key to be exactly `\"subscribe\"`.\n\nC. `.option(\"kafka.topic\", \"feed\")`\nSpark’s Kafka connector does not recognize `kafka.topic` as a configuration option.\n\nD. `.option(\"topic\", \"feed\")`\n`topic` alone is not supported by Spark’s Kafka source configuration.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nSpark Kafka integration supports three primary ways to specify topics:\n\n- \nSubscribe to specific topics\n\n```\n.option(\"subscribe\", \"topic1\")\n```\n\n- \nSubscribe to multiple topics\n\n```\n.option(\"subscribe\", \"topic1,topic2\")\n```\n\n- \nSubscribe using a pattern\n\n```\n.option(\"subscribePattern\", \"topic.*\")\n```\n\nThese options allow Spark Structured Streaming to continuously consume messages from Kafka topics.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A. `.option(\"subscribe\", \"feed\")`\n\nExplanation:\n\nWhen using Spark Structured Streaming with Kafka, the Kafka data source requires specifying which topic(s) the streaming query should read from. This is done using the `subscribe` option.\n\nCorrect code:\n\n```\nspark \\\n.readStream \\\n.format(\"kafka\") \\\n.option(\"kafka.bootstrap.servers\", \"host1:port1,host2:port2\") \\\n.option(\"subscribe\", \"feed\") \\\n.load()\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \n`format(\"kafka\")` tells Spark to use the Kafka connector.\n\n- \n`kafka.bootstrap.servers` specifies the Kafka brokers.\n\n- \n`subscribe` tells Spark which topic to read messages from.\n\n- \n`readStream` creates a Streaming DataFrame.","B. `.option(\"subscribe.topic\", \"feed\")`\nThis is not a valid Kafka option in Spark. Spark expects the key to be exactly `\"subscribe\"`.","C. `.option(\"kafka.topic\", \"feed\")`\nSpark’s Kafka connector does not recognize `kafka.topic` as a configuration option.","D. `.option(\"topic\", \"feed\")`\n`topic` alone is not supported by Spark’s Kafka source configuration."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-kafka-integration.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"cc3435bb278df50ba7c88ade2e8e0ffa832c17929071c2871628ac75dd651a7d","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"kafka-subscribe-reader-option","decision_id":"kafka-subscribe-reader-option"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-063","number":63,"stem":"A data engineer is working with a large JSON dataset containing order information stored in a distributed file system. The dataset needs to be loaded into a Spark DataFrame for analysis. The engineer wants to ensure that the schema is explicitly defined and that the data is read efficiently.\n\nWhich approach should be used to efficiently load the JSON data into a Spark DataFrame with a predefined schema?","options":["A.\nUse `spark.read.json()` to load the data, then call `DataFrame.printSchema()` to view the inferred schema, and finally use `DataFrame.cast()` to adjust the column data types.","B.\nUse `spark.read.json()` with the `inferSchema` option set to `true`.","C.\nUse `spark.read.format(\"json\").load()` and then apply `DataFrame.withColumn()` to cast each column to the required data type.","D.\nDefine a `StructType` schema and use `spark.read.schema(predefinedSchema).json()` to load the data."],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"json-schema","concept":"json-schema","concept_label":"json-schema","explanation_ja":"読み込む前にschemaを定義してreaderへ渡す。後からcastする方法とは区別する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-json.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. Define a StructType schema and use `spark.read.schema(predefinedSchema).json()` to load the data.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement specifies two important conditions:\n\n- \nThe schema must be explicitly defined\n\n- \nThe data should be read efficiently\n\nIn Spark, the most efficient way to load semi-structured data such as JSON with a known schema is to define the schema explicitly using `StructType` and provide it during the read operation.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType, IntegerType\n \nschema = StructType([\n    StructField(\"order_id\", StringType(), True),\n    StructField(\"customer_id\", StringType(), True),\n    StructField(\"amount\", IntegerType(), True)\n])\n \ndf = spark.read.schema(schema).json(\"/path/orders.json\")\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark does not scan the entire dataset to infer schema\n\n- \nSchema inference overhead is avoided\n\n- \nData is parsed directly using the provided schema\n\n- \nThis significantly improves performance for large datasets\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-json.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-json.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not suitable:\n\nA. Use `spark.read.json()` and later adjust schema with `cast()`.\nThis approach first infers the schema, then performs additional transformations to cast columns. It introduces extra computation and scanning, making it inefficient.\n\nB. Use `spark.read.json()` with `inferSchema=true`.\nSchema inference requires Spark to inspect a portion of the dataset, which can be expensive for large datasets. It also does not meet the requirement of explicitly defining the schema.\n\nC. Load the data and then cast columns using `withColumn()`.\nCasting columns after reading still requires schema inference first, resulting in unnecessary processing steps.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept:\n\nWhen reading large datasets in Spark:\n\n- \nSchema inference\n\n- \nRequires scanning the dataset\n\n- \nSlower for large files\n\n- \nCan produce inconsistent results\n\n- \nExplicit schema definition\n\n- \nFaster\n\n- \nMore reliable\n\n- \nRecommended for production pipelines\n\nBest practice for large-scale ingestion is:\n\n```\nspark.read.schema(schema).json(path)\n```\n\nThis avoids unnecessary data scans and ensures consistent schema enforcement.","source_option_explanations":["A. Use `spark.read.json()` and later adjust schema with `cast()`.\nThis approach first infers the schema, then performs additional transformations to cast columns. It introduces extra computation and scanning, making it inefficient.","B. Use `spark.read.json()` with `inferSchema=true`.\nSchema inference requires Spark to inspect a portion of the dataset, which can be expensive for large datasets. It also does not meet the requirement of explicitly defining the schema.","C. Load the data and then cast columns using `withColumn()`.\nCasting columns after reading still requires schema inference first, resulting in unnecessary processing steps.","Correct Answer: D. Define a StructType schema and use `spark.read.schema(predefinedSchema).json()` to load the data.\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement specifies two important conditions:\n\n- \nThe schema must be explicitly defined\n\n- \nThe data should be read efficiently\n\nIn Spark, the most efficient way to load semi-structured data such as JSON with a known schema is to define the schema explicitly using `StructType` and provide it during the read operation.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType, IntegerType\n \nschema = StructType([\n    StructField(\"order_id\", StringType(), True),\n    StructField(\"customer_id\", StringType(), True),\n    StructField(\"amount\", IntegerType(), True)\n])\n \ndf = spark.read.schema(schema).json(\"/path/orders.json\")\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark does not scan the entire dataset to infer schema\n\n- \nSchema inference overhead is avoided\n\n- \nData is parsed directly using the provided schema\n\n- \nThis significantly improves performance for large datasets"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-json.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"c65212d605948d19b8c31f4f3dcd6aacbd7c6622ace9a85442d48f65f8c1f5dc","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"json-explicit-schema-before-read","decision_id":"json-explicit-schema-before-read"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-064","number":64,"stem":"A data scientist at a financial services company is working with a Spark DataFrame that contains millions of transaction records. The DataFrame includes the columns transaction_id, account_number, transaction_amount, and timestamp. Due to an issue in the source system, some transactions were recorded multiple times with identical values across all fields. The data scientist needs to remove these duplicate rows to ensure accurate financial reporting.\n\nWhich PySpark approach should be used to deduplicate records across all columns?","options":["A.\n\n```\ndf = df.dropDuplicates()\n```","B.\n\n```\ndf = df.groupBy(\"transaction_id\").agg(\n    F.first(\"account_number\"),\n    F.first(\"transaction_amount\"),\n    F.first(\"timestamp\")\n)\n```","C.\n\n```\ndf = df.filter(F.col(\"transaction_id\").isNotNull())\n```","D.\n\n```\ndf = df.dropDuplicates([\"transaction_amount\"])\n```"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"dedup-all","concept":"dedup-all","concept_label":"dedup-all","explanation_ja":"全列が一致する重複行は引数なしdropDuplicatesで取り除ける。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicates.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A.\n\n```\ndf = df.dropDuplicates()\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement is to remove duplicate rows where all columns have identical values. In PySpark, the simplest and most appropriate method is `dropDuplicates()` without specifying columns.\n\nWhen `dropDuplicates()` is used without arguments, Spark compares all columns in the DataFrame to determine whether rows are duplicates.\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark scans all rows.\n\n- \nRows with identical values across every column are considered duplicates.\n\n- \nOnly one instance of each duplicate row is retained.\n\nExample:\n\nInput:\n\ntransaction_id | account_number | transaction_amount | timestamp\n1 | A100 | 500 | 2024-01-01\n1 | A100 | 500 | 2024-01-01\n\nAfter:\n\n```\ndf = df.dropDuplicates()\n```\n\nOutput:\n\ntransaction_id | account_number | transaction_amount | timestamp\n1 | A100 | 500 | 2024-01-01\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicates.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicates.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not suitable:\n\nB.\n\n```\ndf.groupBy(\"transaction_id\").agg(...)\n```\n\nThis groups rows by transaction_id only, which can incorrectly merge records that have the same transaction_id but different amounts or timestamps. It also performs unnecessary aggregations.\n\nC.\n\n```\ndf.filter(F.col(\"transaction_id\").isNotNull())\n```\n\nThis only removes rows with NULL transaction IDs and does not address duplicate records.\n\nD.\n\n```\ndf.dropDuplicates([\"transaction_amount\"])\n```\n\nThis deduplicates only based on the transaction_amount column, meaning transactions with different IDs but the same amount could be incorrectly removed.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark provides two ways to deduplicate data:\n\n`dropDuplicates()`\n\n- \nRemoves duplicates across all columns\n\n`dropDuplicates(subset_columns)`\n\n- \nRemoves duplicates based on specific columns\n\nExamples:\n\n```\ndf.dropDuplicates()\ndf.dropDuplicates([\"user_id\"])\n```\n\nFor exact row-level duplicates, using `dropDuplicates()` with no column list is the correct approach.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A.\n\n```\ndf = df.dropDuplicates()\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\nThe requirement is to remove duplicate rows where all columns have identical values. In PySpark, the simplest and most appropriate method is `dropDuplicates()` without specifying columns.\n\nWhen `dropDuplicates()` is used without arguments, Spark compares all columns in the DataFrame to determine whether rows are duplicates.\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark scans all rows.\n\n- \nRows with identical values across every column are considered duplicates.\n\n- \nOnly one instance of each duplicate row is retained.","B.\n\n```\ndf.groupBy(\"transaction_id\").agg(...)\n```\n\nThis groups rows by transaction_id only, which can incorrectly merge records that have the same transaction_id but different amounts or timestamps. It also performs unnecessary aggregations.","C.\n\n```\ndf.filter(F.col(\"transaction_id\").isNotNull())\n```\n\nThis only removes rows with NULL transaction IDs and does not address duplicate records.","D.\n\n```\ndf.dropDuplicates([\"transaction_amount\"])\n```\n\nThis deduplicates only based on the transaction_amount column, meaning transactions with different IDs but the same amount could be incorrectly removed."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicates.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"652f4a76f4036787d8718c2f30f8ddbe8d0142e777879b8bb19fc4ffcce5d7d3","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"all-column-dedup","decision_id":"dedup-entire-row"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-065","number":65,"stem":"The following code fragment results in an error:\n\n```\n@F.udf(T.IntegerType())\ndef simple_udf(t: str) -> str:\n    return answer * 3.14159\n```\n\nWhich code snippet should be used instead to correctly define the UDF?","options":["A.\n\n```\n@F.udf(T.IntegerType())\ndef simple_udf(t: int) -> int:\n    return t * 3.14159\n```","B.\n\n```\n@F.udf(T.DoubleType())\ndef simple_udf(t: float) -> float:\n    return t * 3.14159\n```","C.\n\n```\n@F.udf(T.DoubleType())\ndef simple_udf(t: int) -> int:\n    return t * 3.14159\n```","D.\n\n```\n@F.udf(T.IntegerType())\ndef simple_udf(t: float) -> float:\n    return t * 3.14159\n```"],"correct":1,"correct_many":[1,2],"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"udf-types","concept":"udf-types","concept_label":"udf-types","explanation_ja":"通常UDFのPython型注釈は値を自動castしない。DoubleTypeに対応するPython floatを返すBとCが成立する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.udf.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.udf.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.udf.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"57acf29950b5ddaa68f467df93ca477a1a70174a75ac2e3bb98fdb3b57f5f44d","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"通常のPython UDFの型注釈は実行時の型検査にならない。BもCもDoubleTypeに適合する値を返せる。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.udf.html"],"original_stem":"The following code fragment results in an error:\n\n```\n@F.udf(T.IntegerType())\ndef simple_udf(t: str) -> str:\n    return answer * 3.14159\n```\n\nWhich code snippet should be used instead to correctly define the UDF?","original_options":["A.\n\n```\n@F.udf(T.IntegerType())\ndef simple_udf(t: int) -> int:\n    return t * 3.14159\n```","B.\n\n```\n@F.udf(T.DoubleType())\ndef simple_udf(t: float) -> float:\n    return t * 3.14159\n```","C.\n\n```\n@F.udf(T.DoubleType())\ndef simple_udf(t: int) -> int:\n    return t * 3.14159\n```","D.\n\n```\n@F.udf(T.IntegerType())\ndef simple_udf(t: float) -> float:\n    return t * 3.14159\n```"],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"udf-types","decision_id":"udf-types"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-066","number":66,"stem":"A developer initializes a SparkSession using the following code:\n\n```\nspark = SparkSession.builder \\\n    .appName(\"Analytics Application\") \\\n    .getOrCreate()\n```\n\nWhich statement correctly describes the behavior of the SparkSession created in this code?","options":["A.\nThe `getOrCreate()` method explicitly destroys any existing SparkSession and then creates a new one.","B.\nA SparkSession is unique for each `appName`, and calling `getOrCreate()` with the same name will return the previously created SparkSession.","C.\nIf a SparkSession already exists, this code returns the existing session instead of creating a new one.","D.\nA new SparkSession is created every time the `getOrCreate()` method is called."],"correct":2,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"session-lifecycle","concept":"session-lifecycle","concept_label":"session-lifecycle","explanation_ja":"getOrCreateは利用可能な既存SparkSessionを返す。appName別にセッションを分離する機能ではない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C. If a SparkSession already exists, this code returns the existing session instead of creating a new one.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, the `SparkSession` is the entry point for working with Spark functionality such as:\n\n- \nSpark SQL\n\n- \nDataFrames\n\n- \nStructured Streaming\n\n- \nCatalog operations\n\nThe method `getOrCreate()` follows a simple rule:\n\n- \nIf a SparkSession already exists → return the existing session\n\n- \nIf no SparkSession exists → create a new one\n\nExample:\n\n```\nspark = SparkSession.builder \\\n    .appName(\"Analytics Application\") \\\n    .getOrCreate()\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark checks whether a SparkSession already exists in the current JVM.\n\n- \nIf it exists, Spark returns the existing session.\n\n- \nIf it does not exist, Spark creates a new SparkSession with the specified configuration.\n\nThis ensures that an application maintains a single shared SparkSession, which avoids unnecessary resource creation.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not suitable:\n\nA. The getOrCreate() method explicitly destroys any existing SparkSession and then creates a new one.\nThis is incorrect. `getOrCreate()` does not destroy existing sessions.\n\nB. A SparkSession is unique for each appName.\n`appName` is only a label for the application and does not determine session uniqueness.\n\nD. A new SparkSession is created every time the getOrCreate() method is called.\nThis would lead to unnecessary session creation and resource usage. Instead, Spark reuses the existing session.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark has two key entry objects:\n\nSparkSession\n\n- \nUnified entry point for Spark (Spark 2.0+)\n\n- \nReplaces `SQLContext`, `HiveContext`, etc.\n\nSparkContext\n\n- \nLower-level object that manages cluster execution\n\nA typical Spark application should use one SparkSession, which internally manages the underlying SparkContext.","source_option_explanations":["A. The getOrCreate() method explicitly destroys any existing SparkSession and then creates a new one.\nThis is incorrect. `getOrCreate()` does not destroy existing sessions.","B. A SparkSession is unique for each appName.\n`appName` is only a label for the application and does not determine session uniqueness.","Correct Answer: C. If a SparkSession already exists, this code returns the existing session instead of creating a new one.\n\nExplanation:\n\nIn Apache Spark, the `SparkSession` is the entry point for working with Spark functionality such as:\n\n- \nSpark SQL\n\n- \nDataFrames\n\n- \nStructured Streaming\n\n- \nCatalog operations\n\nThe method `getOrCreate()` follows a simple rule:\n\n- \nIf a SparkSession already exists → return the existing session\n\n- \nIf no SparkSession exists → create a new one\n\nExample:\n\n```\nspark = SparkSession.builder \\\n    .appName(\"Analytics Application\") \\\n    .getOrCreate()\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark checks whether a SparkSession already exists in the current JVM.\n\n- \nIf it exists, Spark returns the existing session.\n\n- \nIf it does not exist, Spark creates a new SparkSession with the specified configuration.\n\nThis ensures that an application maintains a single shared SparkSession, which avoids unnecessary resource creation.","D. A new SparkSession is created every time the getOrCreate() method is called.\nThis would lead to unnecessary session creation and resource usage. Instead, Spark reuses the existing session."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"8ea5bae9ee37583a5ef8d0d4a720f9ab2cc17b8650eb50d541ae7afe39bd4584","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"session-reuse","decision_id":"get-or-create-existing-session"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-067","number":67,"stem":"A Spark engineer must choose an appropriate deployment mode for running Spark jobs.\n\nWhat is a key benefit of using cluster mode in Apache Spark?","options":["A.\nIn cluster mode, resources are allocated by a resource manager within the cluster, which improves performance and scalability for large workloads.","B.\nIn cluster mode, the driver executes all tasks locally without distributing them to worker nodes.","C.\nIn cluster mode, the driver runs on the client machine, which may limit the application's ability to process large datasets efficiently.","D.\nIn cluster mode, the driver program runs on one of the worker nodes, enabling the application to fully utilize the cluster’s distributed resources."],"correct":3,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"deployment-mode","concept":"deployment-mode","concept_label":"deployment-mode","explanation_ja":"cluster modeではdriverをクラスタ側で起動する。executorがすべての処理をdriverへ送る方式ではない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D. In cluster mode, the driver program runs on one of the worker nodes, enabling the application to fully utilize the cluster’s distributed resources.\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark supports two primary deployment modes:\n\n- \nClient mode\n\n- \nCluster mode\n\nThe key difference between them is where the Spark driver runs.\n\nIn cluster mode, the driver program runs inside the cluster (typically on one of the worker nodes or cluster-managed nodes). This allows the application to fully utilize cluster resources without depending on the client machine that submitted the job.\n\nExecution flow in cluster mode:\n\n- \nThe application is submitted to the cluster manager (YARN, Kubernetes, or Standalone).\n\n- \nThe cluster manager launches the driver inside the cluster.\n\n- \nExecutors are allocated on worker nodes.\n\n- \nTasks are distributed across executors for parallel processing.\n\nBecause the driver runs inside the cluster:\n\n- \nNetwork latency between driver and executors is reduced.\n\n- \nThe client machine does not need to stay connected.\n\n- \nThe application can scale efficiently for large workloads.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not suitable:\n\nA. In cluster mode, resources are allocated by a resource manager within the cluster, which improves performance and scalability for large workloads.\nAlthough resource managers do allocate resources, this does not specifically describe the key benefit of cluster mode. Resource allocation happens regardless of driver location.\n\nB. In cluster mode, the driver executes all tasks locally without distributing them to worker nodes.\nThis is incorrect. Spark always distributes tasks to executors running on worker nodes.\n\nC. In cluster mode, the driver runs on the client machine.\nThis describes client mode, not cluster mode.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark deployment modes:\n\nClient Mode\n\n- \nDriver runs on the machine submitting the job\n\n- \nExecutors run on cluster nodes\n\n- \nUseful for interactive jobs and debugging\n\nCluster Mode\n\n- \nDriver runs inside the cluster\n\n- \nExecutors run on worker nodes\n\n- \nBetter for production jobs and large workloads\n\nCluster mode is preferred in production because it removes dependency on the client machine and improves reliability and scalability.","source_option_explanations":["A. In cluster mode, resources are allocated by a resource manager within the cluster, which improves performance and scalability for large workloads.\nAlthough resource managers do allocate resources, this does not specifically describe the key benefit of cluster mode. Resource allocation happens regardless of driver location.","B. In cluster mode, the driver executes all tasks locally without distributing them to worker nodes.\nThis is incorrect. Spark always distributes tasks to executors running on worker nodes.","C. In cluster mode, the driver runs on the client machine.\nThis describes client mode, not cluster mode.","Correct Answer: D. In cluster mode, the driver program runs on one of the worker nodes, enabling the application to fully utilize the cluster’s distributed resources.\n\nExplanation:\n\nApache Spark supports two primary deployment modes:\n\n- \nClient mode\n\n- \nCluster mode\n\nThe key difference between them is where the Spark driver runs.\n\nIn cluster mode, the driver program runs inside the cluster (typically on one of the worker nodes or cluster-managed nodes). This allows the application to fully utilize cluster resources without depending on the client machine that submitted the job.\n\nExecution flow in cluster mode:\n\n- \nThe application is submitted to the cluster manager (YARN, Kubernetes, or Standalone).\n\n- \nThe cluster manager launches the driver inside the cluster.\n\n- \nExecutors are allocated on worker nodes.\n\n- \nTasks are distributed across executors for parallel processing."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"b6c6a5e5a92171dd17771cf522a05391c572bf8cbab7afc38337672c2e9e49d7","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"client-cluster-driver-placement","decision_id":"client-cluster-driver-placement"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-068","number":68,"stem":"An engineer observes a significant increase in execution time for a Spark job. To diagnose the issue, the engineer decides to review the logs generated by the Executors.\n\nHow should the engineer retrieve the Executor logs to investigate performance problems in the Spark application?","options":["A.\nLocate the executor logs on the Spark master node, typically in the /tmp directory.","B.\nRun the job using the `spark-submit` command with the `--verbose` flag to print executor logs to the console.","C.\nOpen the Spark UI, navigate to the relevant stage, and view the executor logs from the Stages tab.","D.\nRetrieve the logs by executing a Spark job using the spark-sql CLI tool."],"correct":2,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"executor-logs","concept":"executor-logs","concept_label":"executor-logs","explanation_ja":"ExecutorログはSpark UIのリンクから確認する。公式ガイドの表現はStagesタブだが、実環境ではExecutorsタブのstderr等の導線も確認する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/web-ui.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C\n\nWhy the correct option is correct\n\nExecutor logs are best accessed through the Spark Web UI, which provides visibility into the execution of stages, tasks, and executors. When troubleshooting performance issues, engineers commonly use the Stages tab or Executors tab to inspect task-level execution and access the associated stdout and stderr logs produced by executors.\n\nThese logs help identify issues such as slow tasks, GC pressure, skewed partitions, or failed tasks that impact execution time. The Spark UI aggregates logs from distributed executors and makes them accessible directly from the interface without needing to manually access nodes.\n\nFrom the UI, an engineer can:\n\n- \nIdentify the stage experiencing delays\n\n- \nInspect task execution details\n\n- \nClick executor links to open stdout/stderr logs generated by executors\n\nThis is the standard operational method for diagnosing Spark job performance.\n\nRelevant documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/web-ui.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/web-ui.html)\n\nKey foundational concept:\nSpark applications run distributed tasks across executors, and each executor generates logs during execution. The Spark Web UI acts as the centralized monitoring interface that aggregates execution metrics and executor logs, enabling engineers to debug performance problems in distributed jobs.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA.\nExecutor logs are not stored on the Spark master node by default, and `/tmp` is not a reliable or standard location for retrieving executor logs. Executors run on worker nodes, and logs are typically managed by the cluster manager (YARN, Kubernetes, Standalone worker nodes, etc.). Manually searching `/tmp` on the master node will not provide the executor logs needed for troubleshooting.\n\nB.\nThe `--verbose` flag in `spark-submit` increases submission-level logging, such as dependency resolution and configuration details. It does not stream executor logs to the console during runtime. Executor logs remain on the worker nodes or accessible through the cluster manager or Spark UI.\n\nD.\nThe `spark-sql` CLI is used to execute SQL queries against Spark, not to retrieve executor logs. Running a job with the CLI does not expose executor logs for previously executed Spark applications and does not help diagnose performance issues.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark jobs execute as stages composed of tasks distributed to executors. Each executor produces logs during task execution. The Spark Web UI is the primary debugging and monitoring tool, allowing engineers to analyze stages, tasks, executor performance, and logs in a centralized way without manually accessing worker machines.","source_option_explanations":["A.\nExecutor logs are not stored on the Spark master node by default, and `/tmp` is not a reliable or standard location for retrieving executor logs. Executors run on worker nodes, and logs are typically managed by the cluster manager (YARN, Kubernetes, Standalone worker nodes, etc.). Manually searching `/tmp` on the master node will not provide the executor logs needed for troubleshooting.","B.\nThe `--verbose` flag in `spark-submit` increases submission-level logging, such as dependency resolution and configuration details. It does not stream executor logs to the console during runtime. Executor logs remain on the worker nodes or accessible through the cluster manager or Spark UI.","Why the correct option is correct\n\nExecutor logs are best accessed through the Spark Web UI, which provides visibility into the execution of stages, tasks, and executors. When troubleshooting performance issues, engineers commonly use the Stages tab or Executors tab to inspect task-level execution and access the associated stdout and stderr logs produced by executors.\n\nThese logs help identify issues such as slow tasks, GC pressure, skewed partitions, or failed tasks that impact execution time. The Spark UI aggregates logs from distributed executors and makes them accessible directly from the interface without needing to manually access nodes.\n\nFrom the UI, an engineer can:\n\n- \nIdentify the stage experiencing delays\n\n- \nInspect task execution details\n\n- \nClick executor links to open stdout/stderr logs generated by executors","D.\nThe `spark-sql` CLI is used to execute SQL queries against Spark, not to retrieve executor logs. Running a job with the CLI does not expose executor logs for previously executed Spark applications and does not help diagnose performance issues."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/web-ui.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"69fb97173a31f733c0371f8860004be0f73ca21852a79c54128da3935e516edd","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"retrieve-executor-logs-ui","decision_id":"retrieve-executor-logs-ui"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-069","number":69,"stem":"A data engineer is working on a Spark application with two DataFrames:\n\n- \nDataFrame A contains detailed transaction records for the past year and is 128 GB in size.\n\n- \nDataFrame B contains a user lookup table and is 1 GB in size.\n\nTo optimize performance, the engineer plans to use a broadcast join. Assume the driver and every executor have enough memory for DataFrame B and the broadcast size limit is satisfied.\n\nWhich strategy should the engineer use when deciding which DataFrame to broadcast?","options":["A.\nBroadcast DataFrame B because it is smaller and this avoids shuffling the broadcasted DataFrame itself.","B.\nBroadcast DataFrame B because it is smaller and this avoids shuffling DataFrame A during the join.","C.\nBroadcast DataFrame A because it is larger and this avoids shuffling DataFrame B.","D.\nBroadcast DataFrame A because it is smaller and this avoids shuffling the broadcasted DataFrame itself."],"correct":1,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"broadcast-choice","concept":"broadcast-choice","concept_label":"broadcast-choice","explanation_ja":"broadcast対象は収容可能な小さい側。大きい側のshuffleを避ける効果とbroadcast側の転送を区別する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-performance-tuning.html#join-strategy-hints-for-sql-queries","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-performance-tuning.html#join-strategy-hints-for-sql-queries","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hash-join"],"images":[],"source_hash":"76a027813dab1c1dff069bcfa813422a541a1195bdcaebcd7544271940b76664","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"1GBのbroadcastを無条件に推奨しない。driverと各executorに収容可能な前提を追記。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-performance-tuning.html#join-strategy-hints-for-sql-queries"],"original_stem":"A data engineer is working on a Spark application with two DataFrames:\n\n- \nDataFrame A contains detailed transaction records for the past year and is 128 GB in size.\n\n- \nDataFrame B contains a user lookup table and is 1 GB in size.\n\nTo optimize performance, the engineer plans to use a broadcast join.\n\nWhich strategy should the engineer use when deciding which DataFrame to broadcast?","original_options":["A.\nBroadcast DataFrame B because it is smaller and this avoids shuffling the broadcasted DataFrame itself.","B.\nBroadcast DataFrame B because it is smaller and this avoids shuffling DataFrame A during the join.","C.\nBroadcast DataFrame A because it is larger and this avoids shuffling DataFrame B.","D.\nBroadcast DataFrame A because it is smaller and this avoids shuffling the broadcasted DataFrame itself."],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"broadcast-small-side","decision_id":"broadcast-small-side"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-070","number":70,"stem":"A data scientist is working with a large dataset in Apache Spark using PySpark. The DataFrame df contains the columns user_id, product_id, and purchase_amount. The scientist wants to apply a sequence of operations and is analyzing which operations will first trigger a shuffle and then perform transformations that do not require a shuffle.\n\nWhich sequence of operations fits this pattern?","options":["A.\ndf.groupBy(\"user_id\").agg(sum(\"purchase_amount\").alias(\"total_purchase\")).filter(col(\"total_purchase\") > 100)","B.\ndf.withColumn(\"discount\", df.purchase_amount * 0.1).select(\"discount\")","C.\ndf.withColumn(\"purchase_date\", current_date()).where(\"total_purchase > 50\")","D.\ndf.groupBy(\"user_id\").agg(sum(\"purchase_amount\").alias(\"total_purchase\")).repartition(10)"],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"execution-shuffle","concept":"execution-shuffle","concept_label":"execution-shuffle","explanation_ja":"groupBy/aggでshuffleした後にfilterする順序。filterだけなら通常shuffleしない。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.groupBy.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.groupBy.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"6c6be96e24153d400843dfa63f5812294a6c29b452b6e2893c6f374a20cd1faf","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"shuffleの後に非shuffleという設問の順序に、元の正解Aが反しているためAの順序を修正。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.groupBy.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html"],"original_stem":"A data scientist is working with a large dataset in Apache Spark using PySpark. The DataFrame df contains the columns user_id, product_id, and purchase_amount. The scientist wants to apply a sequence of operations and is analyzing which operations will first trigger a shuffle and then perform transformations that do not require a shuffle.\n\nWhich sequence of operations fits this pattern?","original_options":["A.\ndf.filter(df.purchase_amount > 100).groupBy(\"user_id\").sum(\"purchase_amount\")","B.\ndf.withColumn(\"discount\", df.purchase_amount * 0.1).select(\"discount\")","C.\ndf.withColumn(\"purchase_date\", current_date()).where(\"total_purchase > 50\")","D.\ndf.groupBy(\"user_id\").agg(sum(\"purchase_amount\").alias(\"total_purchase\")).repartition(10)"],"original_correct":["A"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"narrow-wide-shuffle","decision_id":"wide-then-narrow-transformations"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-071","number":71,"stem":"A data engineer is processing a streaming DataFrame that receives sensor readings every second with the columns sensor_id, temperature, and timestamp. The engineer needs to compute the average temperature for each sensor for each fixed, non-overlapping 5-minute event-time window while the data stream is continuously processed.\n\nWhich code implementation correctly satisfies this requirement?","options":["A.\n\n```\ndf.withColumn(\"avg_temp\",\n    avg(\"temperature\").over(\n        Window.partitionBy(\"sensor_id\")\n        .orderBy(\"timestamp\")\n        .rangeBetween(-300, 0)\n    )\n)\n```","B.\n\n```\ndf.groupBy(\"sensor_id\", \"timestamp\") \\\n  .agg(avg(\"temperature\").alias(\"avg_temp\"))\n```","C.\n\n```\ndf.groupBy(\"sensor_id\").avg(\"temperature\")\n```","D.\n\n```\ndf.withWatermark(\"timestamp\", \"5 minutes\") \\\n  .groupBy(\"sensor_id\", window(\"timestamp\", \"5 minutes\")) \\\n  .agg(avg(\"temperature\").alias(\"avg_temp\"))\n```"],"correct":3,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"stream-window","concept":"stream-window","concept_label":"stream-window","explanation_ja":"window(timestamp,5 minutes)は固定幅のtumbling window。watermarkの遅延許容と窓の幅は別の設定。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.window.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.window.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#window-operations-on-event-time"],"images":[],"source_hash":"72570888a910a34cfd3d3671f97933939cfee5c06b14dfca5b50b9deba120ee8","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"window(timestamp,5 minutes)は5分のtumbling windowであり各時点から遡るローリング窓ではない。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.window.html"],"original_stem":"A data engineer is processing a streaming DataFrame that receives sensor readings every second with the columns sensor_id, temperature, and timestamp. The engineer needs to compute the average temperature for each sensor over the previous 5 minutes while the data stream is continuously processed.\n\nWhich code implementation correctly satisfies this requirement?","original_options":["A.\n\n```\ndf.withColumn(\"avg_temp\",\n    avg(\"temperature\").over(\n        Window.partitionBy(\"sensor_id\")\n        .orderBy(\"timestamp\")\n        .rangeBetween(-300, 0)\n    )\n)\n```","B.\n\n```\ndf.groupBy(\"sensor_id\", \"timestamp\") \\\n  .agg(avg(\"temperature\").alias(\"avg_temp\"))\n```","C.\n\n```\ndf.groupBy(\"sensor_id\").avg(\"temperature\")\n```","D.\n\n```\ndf.withWatermark(\"timestamp\", \"5 minutes\") \\\n  .groupBy(\"sensor_id\", window(\"timestamp\", \"5 minutes\")) \\\n  .agg(avg(\"temperature\").alias(\"avg_temp\"))\n```"],"original_correct":["D"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"tumbling-event-time-window","decision_id":"tumbling-event-time-window"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-072","number":72,"stem":"Which of the following is an accurate feature of Spark Connect?","options":["A. It supports the streaming APIs `DataStreamReader`, `DataStreamWriter`, and `StreamingQuery`.","B. It supports the PySpark APIs `DataFrame`, `Functions`, `Column`, and `SparkContext`.","C. It only supports applications written in PySpark.","D. It provides built-in authentication out of the box."],"correct":0,"domain":"Spark Connect","topic":"connect-stream-api","concept":"connect-stream-api","concept_label":"connect-stream-api","explanation_ja":"Spark Connect 3.5はStructured Streaming APIに対応するがSparkContext/RDDには対応しない。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html","source_explanation":"Answer: A\n\n\"It supports the streaming APIs `DataStreamReader`, `DataStreamWriter`, and `StreamingQuery`.\"\n\nWhy A is correct\n\nAccording to the official Spark Connect documentation, the majority of the Streaming API is supported, including DataStreamReader, DataStreamWriter, StreamingQuery and StreamingQueryListener. This confirms that Structured Streaming works through Spark Connect, letting client applications build and run streaming queries remotely against a Spark cluster over the gRPC-based Connect protocol. [Apache Spark](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html)\n\nWhy the other options are wrong\n\nB — \"It supports the PySpark APIs `DataFrame`, `Functions`, `Column`, and `SparkContext`.\"\nThis is almost right but includes one critical error: since Spark 3.4, Spark Connect supports most PySpark APIs, including DataFrame, Functions, and Column. However, some APIs such as SparkContext and RDD are not supported. `SparkContext` and `RDD` are explicitly excluded from Spark Connect — this is because Spark Connect's client-server architecture means that the client does not have access to properties of the cluster that manipulate the environment for all connected clients; most importantly, the client does not have access to the static Spark configuration or the SparkContext. This option is a classic \"3 out of 4 correct\" trap — swapping in the one unsupported API among several supported ones. [Apache SparkApache Spark](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html)\n\nC — \"It only supports applications written in PySpark.\"\nFalse. Spark Connect is multi-language: starting with Spark 3.4, Spark Connect is available and supports PySpark and Scala applications. The docs further confirm since Spark 3.5, Spark Connect supports most Scala APIs, including Dataset, functions, Column, Catalog and KeyValueGroupedDataset. So Spark Connect is not PySpark-exclusive. [Databricks](https://www.databricks.com/blog/2023/04/18/spark-connect-available-apache-spark.html)[Apache Spark](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html)\n\nD — \"It provides built-in authentication out of the box.\"\nThis is not an accurate characterization from the official documentation — Spark Connect's core design focus is on remote connectivity via a thin client/gRPC protocol, decoupling the client from the driver JVM, not on providing built-in authentication as a headline feature. No official source describes \"built-in authentication out of the box\" as a defining Spark Connect capability; this is a fabricated-sounding distractor designed to sound plausible without documentation support.\n\nExam trap summary\n\n- \nNear-complete-but-wrong lists (B): watch closely when an option lists several genuinely correct items alongside one incorrect one (`SparkContext`) — always verify every item in a compound answer, not just most of them.\n\n- \nOvergeneralizing language support (C): Spark Connect is explicitly cross-language (PySpark and Scala) — don't assume PySpark-only just because most exam material and tutorials emphasize Python.\n\n- \nPlausible-sounding but undocumented claims (D): options can sound reasonable (\"of course a client-server system would need auth\") but aren't part of the documented feature set — always anchor to what's actually stated in official docs, not intuition about what \"should\" be true.\n\nFoundational point:\n\nSpark Connect's core architectural principle is decoupling the client from the driver JVM: this means that the client cannot directly access and interact with the driver JVM to manipulate the execution environment, and by design, it uses Spark's logical plans as the abstraction to be able to declaratively describe the operations to be executed on the server. This is why SparkContext/RDD access is fundamentally incompatible with the architecture — those APIs require direct low-level JVM access that the Connect protocol intentionally abstracts away. [Apache SparkApache Spark](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html)\n\nReal-time connect\n\nSpark Connect is especially valuable for building lightweight client applications (e.g., notebooks, IDEs, or microservices) that need to submit Spark jobs to a remote cluster without embedding a full Spark driver locally — useful for multi-tenant environments where clients shouldn't have direct access to shared cluster configuration or resources.\n\nReference\n\n- \n[Spark Connect Overview — Apache Spark official docs](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html)\n\n- \n[Spark Connect Available in Apache Spark 3.4 — Databricks Blog](https://www.databricks.com/blog/2023/04/18/spark-connect-available-apache-spark.html)","source_option_explanations":["Answer: A\n\n\"It supports the streaming APIs `DataStreamReader`, `DataStreamWriter`, and `StreamingQuery`.\"\n\nWhy A is correct\n\nAccording to the official Spark Connect documentation, the majority of the Streaming API is supported, including DataStreamReader, DataStreamWriter, StreamingQuery and StreamingQueryListener. This confirms that Structured Streaming works through Spark Connect, letting client applications build and run streaming queries remotely against a Spark cluster over the gRPC-based Connect protocol. [Apache Spark](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html)","B — \"It supports the PySpark APIs `DataFrame`, `Functions`, `Column`, and `SparkContext`.\"\nThis is almost right but includes one critical error: since Spark 3.4, Spark Connect supports most PySpark APIs, including DataFrame, Functions, and Column. However, some APIs such as SparkContext and RDD are not supported. `SparkContext` and `RDD` are explicitly excluded from Spark Connect — this is because Spark Connect's client-server architecture means that the client does not have access to properties of the cluster that manipulate the environment for all connected clients; most importantly, the client does not have access to the static Spark configuration or the SparkContext. This option is a classic \"3 out of 4 correct\" trap — swapping in the one unsupported API among several supported ones. [Apache SparkApache Spark](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html)","C — \"It only supports applications written in PySpark.\"\nFalse. Spark Connect is multi-language: starting with Spark 3.4, Spark Connect is available and supports PySpark and Scala applications. The docs further confirm since Spark 3.5, Spark Connect supports most Scala APIs, including Dataset, functions, Column, Catalog and KeyValueGroupedDataset. So Spark Connect is not PySpark-exclusive. [Databricks](https://www.databricks.com/blog/2023/04/18/spark-connect-available-apache-spark.html)[Apache Spark](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html)","D — \"It provides built-in authentication out of the box.\"\nThis is not an accurate characterization from the official documentation — Spark Connect's core design focus is on remote connectivity via a thin client/gRPC protocol, decoupling the client from the driver JVM, not on providing built-in authentication as a headline feature. No official source describes \"built-in authentication out of the box\" as a defining Spark Connect capability; this is a fabricated-sounding distractor designed to sound plausible without documentation support."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html","https://www.databricks.com/blog/2023/04/18/spark-connect-available-apache-spark.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"3fdd679ceab4b6fa562c3c270793c7c3de47e55054d9005bbbc27a0758c9ea5e","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"connect-supported-streaming-api","decision_id":"connect-supported-streaming-api"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-073","number":73,"stem":"A data engineer uses a broadcast join to distribute the rows of a small-side DataFrame containing millions of rows to all executors for lookup purposes.\n\nWhat is the likely outcome of this approach?","options":["A.\nThe job may fail if the memory available on each executor is insufficient to store the broadcasted DataFrame.","B.\nThe job may fail if the executors do not have enough CPU cores to process the broadcasted dataset.","C.\nThe job will hang indefinitely because Spark cannot distribute or serialize such a large broadcast variable.","D.\nThe job may fail because the driver does not have enough CPU cores to serialize the large DataFrame."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"broadcast-memory","concept":"broadcast-memory","concept_label":"broadcast-memory","explanation_ja":"broadcast joinでも各executorのメモリに収まる必要がある。DataFrameをbroadcast変数として渡す操作とは区別する。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/rdd-programming-guide.html#broadcast-variables","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/rdd-programming-guide.html#broadcast-variables","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.broadcast.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#broadcast-variables"],"images":[],"source_hash":"a8f68038861f6301a659f6ba3d128fbf298a39b7e63fd3d3b3fe50cbe6a5416b","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"Spark DataFrameオブジェクトをSparkContext.broadcastすることとbroadcast joinを混同しない。データがexecutorへ配布されるbroadcast joinとして明記。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/rdd-programming-guide.html#broadcast-variables","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.broadcast.html"],"original_stem":"A data engineer uses a broadcast variable to distribute a DataFrame containing millions of rows to all executors for lookup purposes.\n\nWhat is the likely outcome of this approach?","original_options":["A.\nThe job may fail if the memory available on each executor is insufficient to store the broadcasted DataFrame.","B.\nThe job may fail if the executors do not have enough CPU cores to process the broadcasted dataset.","C.\nThe job will hang indefinitely because Spark cannot distribute or serialize such a large broadcast variable.","D.\nThe job may fail because the driver does not have enough CPU cores to serialize the large DataFrame."],"original_correct":["A"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"broadcast-side-executor-memory-risk","decision_id":"broadcast-side-executor-memory-risk"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-074","number":74,"stem":"Which feature of Spark Connect should be considered when designing an application that intends to use Spark Connect to enable remote interaction with a Spark cluster?","options":["A.\nIt provides a mechanism to run Spark applications remotely using any programming language.","B.\nIt enables interaction with any remote cluster through a REST API.","C.\nIt allows Spark jobs to be executed remotely through a client–server architecture.","D.\nIt is mainly designed for ingesting data from external sources into Spark."],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark Connect","topic":"connect-architecture","concept":"connect-architecture","concept_label":"connect-architecture","explanation_ja":"Spark Connectはclient–server構成でremoteにSpark処理を要求する。REST APIではなくgRPCを使う。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C\n\nWhy the correct option is correct\n\nSpark Connect introduces a client–server architecture that allows applications to interact with a remote Spark cluster without embedding the Spark driver inside the client process. Instead of running the driver locally, the client builds queries using Spark APIs and sends them as logical plans to a remote Spark server, which then executes them on the cluster.\n\nThis design enables remote execution of Spark workloads, where the client acts as a lightweight interface while the actual computation happens on the Spark cluster.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nTraditional Spark applications run with the driver program embedded in the client application, which means the client must have the full Spark runtime and dependencies installed.\n\nSpark Connect changes this model by:\n\n- \nseparating the client environment from the Spark driver\n\n- \nsending unresolved logical query plans over gRPC\n\n- \nallowing the Spark server to resolve and execute those plans on the cluster\n\nThis architecture enables better remote development, IDE integration, and lightweight clients interacting with large Spark clusters.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA.\nSpark Connect is not a mechanism to run Spark applications in any programming language. While it is designed to support multiple languages, the supported APIs are still tied to Spark client libraries. It does not allow arbitrary programming languages to execute Spark applications directly.\n\nB.\nSpark Connect does not use a REST API. Instead, it communicates using gRPC and protocol buffers to send logical plans from the client to the Spark server. Therefore, interaction with Spark clusters is not performed through REST endpoints.\n\nD.\nSpark Connect is not designed specifically for data ingestion. Data ingestion in Spark is handled through APIs such as `DataFrameReader`, streaming sources, and connectors. Spark Connect focuses on remote interaction with Spark through a client-server model, not on ingesting data from external sources.","source_option_explanations":["A.\nSpark Connect is not a mechanism to run Spark applications in any programming language. While it is designed to support multiple languages, the supported APIs are still tied to Spark client libraries. It does not allow arbitrary programming languages to execute Spark applications directly.","B.\nSpark Connect does not use a REST API. Instead, it communicates using gRPC and protocol buffers to send logical plans from the client to the Spark server. Therefore, interaction with Spark clusters is not performed through REST endpoints.","Correct Answer: C\n\nWhy the correct option is correct\n\nSpark Connect introduces a client–server architecture that allows applications to interact with a remote Spark cluster without embedding the Spark driver inside the client process. Instead of running the driver locally, the client builds queries using Spark APIs and sends them as logical plans to a remote Spark server, which then executes them on the cluster.\n\nThis design enables remote execution of Spark workloads, where the client acts as a lightweight interface while the actual computation happens on the Spark cluster.","D.\nSpark Connect is not designed specifically for data ingestion. Data ingestion in Spark is handled through APIs such as `DataFrameReader`, streaming sources, and connectors. Spark Connect focuses on remote interaction with Spark through a client-server model, not on ingesting data from external sources."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"415d6554667b598e4c3e0e2f9a4de775c32aad44577cddddb4c59a3b1cb04d47","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"connect-client-server-architecture","decision_id":"connect-client-server-architecture"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-075","number":75,"stem":"A CSV file contains the following records:\n\nbambi, hello\nalladin, 20\n\nConsider the following code snippet:\n\nfrom pyspark.sql.types import *\nschema = StructType([StructField(\"name\", StringType()), StructField(\"age\", IntegerType())])\nspark.read.schema(schema).csv(path).collect()\n\nWhat will be returned when this code is executed?","options":["A.\n[Row(name='bambi'), Row(name='alladin', age=20)]","B.\n[Row(name='alladin', age=20)]","C.\n[Row(name='bambi', age=None), Row(name='alladin', age=20)]","D.\nThe code fails with an error because the CSV data does not match the defined schema."],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"csv-malformed","concept":"csv-malformed","concept_label":"csv-malformed","explanation_ja":"既定のPERMISSIVE読取では型に合わないageをNULLにし、他の列は保持する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-csv.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: C\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nWhen Spark reads CSV data using an explicit schema, it attempts to parse each column according to the defined data type. If a value cannot be converted to the specified type, Spark does not fail the job by default. Instead, the value is set to `null`.\n\nIn the question:\n\nSchema defined:\n\n- \n`name` → `StringType`\n\n- \n`age` → `IntegerType`\n\nCSV records:\n\n```\nbambi, hello\nalladin, 20\n```\n\nRow-by-row behavior:\n\nRow 1\n\n```\nbambi, hello\n```\n\n- \n`\"bambi\"` → valid `StringType`\n\n- \n`\"hello\"` → cannot be converted to `IntegerType`\n\nSpark converts the invalid integer value to `null`.\n\nResult:\n\n```\nRow(name='bambi', age=None)\n```\n\nRow 2\n\n```\nalladin, 20\n```\n\n- \n`\"alladin\"` → valid `StringType`\n\n- \n`\"20\"` → valid integer\n\nResult:\n\n```\nRow(name='alladin', age=20)\n```\n\nFinal collected output:\n\n```\n[Row(name='bambi', age=None), Row(name='alladin', age=20)]\n```\n\nThis behavior is part of Spark’s permissive parsing mode, which tolerates type conversion failures by producing null values instead of failing the read operation.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-csv.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-csv.html)\n\nThe documentation explains that Spark CSV parsing attempts type conversion and produces null when conversion fails in permissive mode.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\n```\n[Row(name='bambi'), Row(name='alladin', age=20)]\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because Spark does not drop schema fields when a value fails parsing. The schema explicitly defines two columns (`name`, `age`), so both columns will always appear in the resulting rows.\n\nOption B\n\n```\n[Row(name='alladin', age=20)]\n```\n\nThis assumes Spark drops rows with invalid values, which is incorrect. In permissive mode (default), Spark keeps the row and sets the invalid field to null instead of removing the row.\n\nOption D\n\nThe code does not fail with an error because Spark CSV reading is tolerant by default. Errors only occur if the CSV reader is configured with stricter modes such as `FAILFAST`. Since no such mode is specified, Spark proceeds and assigns `null` to the invalid integer value.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark CSV parsing operates with three modes:\n\n- \nPERMISSIVE (default) – invalid values become `null`\n\n- \nDROPMALFORMED – rows with malformed records are dropped\n\n- \nFAILFAST – parsing errors cause the job to fail\n\nBecause the question does not change the mode, Spark uses PERMISSIVE, which leads to the `null` value in the first row’s `age` column.","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\n```\n[Row(name='bambi'), Row(name='alladin', age=20)]\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because Spark does not drop schema fields when a value fails parsing. The schema explicitly defines two columns (`name`, `age`), so both columns will always appear in the resulting rows.","Option B\n\n```\n[Row(name='alladin', age=20)]\n```\n\nThis assumes Spark drops rows with invalid values, which is incorrect. In permissive mode (default), Spark keeps the row and sets the invalid field to null instead of removing the row.","Correct answer: C\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nWhen Spark reads CSV data using an explicit schema, it attempts to parse each column according to the defined data type. If a value cannot be converted to the specified type, Spark does not fail the job by default. Instead, the value is set to `null`.\n\nIn the question:\n\nSchema defined:\n\n- \n`name` → `StringType`\n\n- \n`age` → `IntegerType`\n\nCSV records:\n\n```\nbambi, hello\nalladin, 20\n```\n\nRow-by-row behavior:\n\nRow 1\n\n```\nbambi, hello\n```\n\n- \n`\"bambi\"` → valid `StringType`\n\n- \n`\"hello\"` → cannot be converted to `IntegerType`","Option D\n\nThe code does not fail with an error because Spark CSV reading is tolerant by default. Errors only occur if the CSV reader is configured with stricter modes such as `FAILFAST`. Since no such mode is specified, Spark proceeds and assigns `null` to the invalid integer value."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-csv.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"eda830c47d319d3f799fcb2bd9563ef672797ee43249b683d3750326456b9ad1","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"csv-permissive-type-null","decision_id":"csv-permissive-type-null"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-076","number":76,"stem":"A data engineer is addressing complaints that a feature engineering job has been slowing down over time. After discussions with the data science team, it was agreed to replace the `percentile()` Spark function with `approx_percentile()` to trade some accuracy for improved performance.\n\nThe updated code is:\n\n```\nfeatures_df = raw_df \\\n.select(\n    F.approx_percentile(\n        \"price\",\n        percentage=[0.25, 0.5, 0.75],\n        accuracy=1000\n    ).alias(\"price_perc\")\n)\n```\n\nAlthough the change significantly improved runtime, the data scientists observed that the returned percentile values now deviate too much from the actual values.\n\nWhat modification should the data engineer make to resolve this issue?","options":["A.\nDecrease the first value in the `percentage` parameter to increase the accuracy of the percentile ranges.","B.\nDecrease the value of the `accuracy` parameter to reduce memory usage while improving accuracy.","C.\nIncrease the last value in the `percentage` parameter to increase the accuracy of the percentile ranges.","D.\nIncrease the value of the `accuracy` parameter to use more memory but improve the accuracy of the computed percentiles."],"correct":3,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"approx-accuracy","concept":"approx-accuracy","concept_label":"approx-accuracy","explanation_ja":"accuracyを上げるとメモリ使用量と引き換えに近似percentileの精度が向上する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/sql/index.html#approx_percentile","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D\n\nWhy the correct option is correct\n\nThe `approx_percentile()` function in Spark computes approximate percentiles using a probabilistic algorithm instead of calculating exact percentiles. This improves performance significantly for large datasets because Spark avoids expensive full data sorting.\n\nThe accuracy parameter controls the trade-off between memory usage and approximation error.\n\n- \nA higher accuracy value allows Spark to maintain a larger internal summary structure.\n\n- \nThis improves the precision of the estimated percentiles.\n\n- \nHowever, it also increases memory consumption.\n\nIn the scenario, the engineer set:\n\n`accuracy = 1000`\n\nIf the percentile values deviate too much from the actual values, the correct adjustment is to increase the accuracy parameter, which reduces approximation error and produces values closer to the real percentiles.\n\nRelevant documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/sql/index.html#approx_percentile](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/sql/index.html#approx_percentile)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nComputing exact percentiles requires sorting or heavy aggregation across the dataset, which can be expensive for large distributed datasets.\n\nSpark provides `approx_percentile()` to improve performance by using approximation algorithms with bounded error. The accuracy parameter controls the error bound:\n\n- \nHigher accuracy → more memory, better precision\n\n- \nLower accuracy → less memory, larger approximation error\n\nThus, tuning accuracy allows engineers to balance performance vs statistical precision.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA.\nThe `percentage` parameter specifies which percentiles to compute (e.g., 25th, 50th, 75th percentile). Changing the first value does not affect the accuracy of the percentile estimation, only which percentile is calculated.\n\nB.\nDecreasing the accuracy value would reduce memory usage but worsen approximation error. This would make the percentile estimates less accurate, which is the opposite of the desired outcome.\n\nC.\nIncreasing the last value in the `percentage` parameter would only change which percentile is computed (for example, moving from the 75th percentile to a higher percentile). It does not affect the accuracy of the approximation algorithm.","source_option_explanations":["A.\nThe `percentage` parameter specifies which percentiles to compute (e.g., 25th, 50th, 75th percentile). Changing the first value does not affect the accuracy of the percentile estimation, only which percentile is calculated.","B.\nDecreasing the accuracy value would reduce memory usage but worsen approximation error. This would make the percentile estimates less accurate, which is the opposite of the desired outcome.","C.\nIncreasing the last value in the `percentage` parameter would only change which percentile is computed (for example, moving from the 75th percentile to a higher percentile). It does not affect the accuracy of the approximation algorithm.","Correct Answer: D\n\nWhy the correct option is correct\n\nThe `approx_percentile()` function in Spark computes approximate percentiles using a probabilistic algorithm instead of calculating exact percentiles. This improves performance significantly for large datasets because Spark avoids expensive full data sorting.\n\nThe accuracy parameter controls the trade-off between memory usage and approximation error.\n\n- \nA higher accuracy value allows Spark to maintain a larger internal summary structure.\n\n- \nThis improves the precision of the estimated percentiles.\n\n- \nHowever, it also increases memory consumption.\n\nIn the scenario, the engineer set:\n\n`accuracy = 1000`\n\nIf the percentile values deviate too much from the actual values, the correct adjustment is to increase the accuracy parameter, which reduces approximation error and produces values closer to the real percentiles."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/sql/index.html#approx_percentile"],"images":[],"source_hash":"2238e6b04d84b1b9e292a518a8e55856c045f1639cd5834728ca3b01227de285","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"approx-percentile-accuracy-memory","decision_id":"approx-percentile-accuracy-memory"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-077","number":77,"stem":"A data engineer is building a Structured Streaming pipeline that reads data from a Kafka topic. The engineer wants the pipeline to operate with very low latency, while still maintaining an Exactly-Once processing guarantee.\n\nAssume checkpointing and a sink that supports exactly-once recovery are configured. Which trigger mode should be used in the `writeStream` configuration?","options":["A.\n.trigger(processingTime='1 second')","B.\n.trigger(continuous=True)","C.\n.trigger(continuous='1 second')","D.\n.trigger(availableNow=True)"],"correct":0,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"stream-trigger","concept":"stream-trigger","concept_label":"stream-trigger","explanation_ja":"低遅延micro-batchはprocessingTime。continuous processingはat-least-onceなので同じ保証ではない。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#fault-tolerance-semantics","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#fault-tolerance-semantics","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#triggers"],"images":[],"source_hash":"36421992e6c2f570131b5b813b6e82521b9322b34dcff341031f46c02aafe09c","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"exactly-onceの成立はtriggerだけでは決まらず、再生可能ソース・対応sink・checkpointを前提とする。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#fault-tolerance-semantics"],"original_stem":"A data engineer is building a Structured Streaming pipeline that reads data from a Kafka topic. The engineer wants the pipeline to operate with very low latency, while still maintaining an Exactly-Once processing guarantee.\n\nWhich trigger mode should be used in the `writeStream` configuration?","original_options":["A.\n.trigger(processingTime='1 second')","B.\n.trigger(continuous=True)","C.\n.trigger(continuous='1 second')","D.\n.trigger(availableNow=True)"],"original_correct":["A"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"microbatch-vs-continuous-delivery","decision_id":"microbatch-vs-continuous-delivery"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-078","number":78,"stem":"A data engineer needs to write a Spark job that creates a new managed table containing finance data. If the table already exists, the job must fail immediately without modifying any existing data.\n\nWhich save mode and method should the data engineer use?","options":["A.\nUse `saveAsTable` with mode `ErrorIfExists`","B.\nUse `saveAsTable` with mode `Overwrite`","C.\nUse `save` with mode `Ignore`","D.\nUse `save` with mode `ErrorIfExists`"],"correct":0,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"write-errorifexists","concept":"write-errorifexists","concept_label":"write-errorifexists","explanation_ja":"存在時に失敗するsaveAsTableのErrorIfExistsモードを使う。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-load-save-functions.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\nWhy the correct option is correct\n\nWhen creating a managed table in Spark, the typical method used is `saveAsTable()`. This method creates a table in the metastore and stores the underlying data in Spark-managed storage.\n\nIf the requirement is:\n\n- \ncreate the table\n\n- \nfail immediately if the table already exists\n\n- \ndo not modify existing data\n\nthe correct save mode is `ErrorIfExists`.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.write.mode(\"errorifexists\").saveAsTable(\"finance_table\")\n```\n\nBehavior of `ErrorIfExists`:\n\n- \nIf the table does not exist, Spark creates the managed table.\n\n- \nIf the table already exists, Spark throws an error and stops the job immediately.\n\n- \nNo existing data is modified.\n\nThis exactly satisfies the requirement of protecting existing data and failing fast.\n\nRelevant documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-load-save-functions.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-load-save-functions.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark supports four main SaveModes when writing data:\n\n- \n`ErrorIfExists` (default) → fail if the table already exists\n\n- \n`Overwrite` → replace existing data\n\n- \n`Append` → add new records to existing data\n\n- \n`Ignore` → skip the write if the table exists\n\nWhen working with managed tables, `saveAsTable()` should be used instead of `save()` because it:\n\n- \nregisters the table in the Spark metastore\n\n- \nmanages the table lifecycle\n\n- \nallows querying via SQL\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nB.\n`mode(\"Overwrite\")` would replace the existing table data if the table already exists. This directly violates the requirement that existing data must not be modified.\n\nC.\n`save()` writes files to a filesystem path, not to a managed table in the Spark metastore. Additionally, `mode(\"Ignore\")` would silently skip the write if the table exists rather than failing the job.\n\nD.\n`save()` does not create a managed table. It writes data to a storage path instead of registering a table in the metastore. Therefore, it does not satisfy the requirement to create a managed table.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\nWhy the correct option is correct\n\nWhen creating a managed table in Spark, the typical method used is `saveAsTable()`. This method creates a table in the metastore and stores the underlying data in Spark-managed storage.\n\nIf the requirement is:\n\n- \ncreate the table\n\n- \nfail immediately if the table already exists\n\n- \ndo not modify existing data\n\nthe correct save mode is `ErrorIfExists`.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.write.mode(\"errorifexists\").saveAsTable(\"finance_table\")\n```\n\nBehavior of `ErrorIfExists`:\n\n- \nIf the table does not exist, Spark creates the managed table.\n\n- \nIf the table already exists, Spark throws an error and stops the job immediately.\n\n- \nNo existing data is modified.","B.\n`mode(\"Overwrite\")` would replace the existing table data if the table already exists. This directly violates the requirement that existing data must not be modified.","C.\n`save()` writes files to a filesystem path, not to a managed table in the Spark metastore. Additionally, `mode(\"Ignore\")` would silently skip the write if the table exists rather than failing the job.","D.\n`save()` does not create a managed table. It writes data to a storage path instead of registering a table in the metastore. Therefore, it does not satisfy the requirement to create a managed table."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-load-save-functions.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"0f846a87e8715063526f71fb09044727424c108ddc239cf8852170ca4f770bb7","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"managed-table-error-if-exists","decision_id":"managed-table-error-if-exists"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-079","number":79,"stem":"A data engineer upgraded their Spark applications from Spark 3.0 to Spark 3.5 and noticed that several scheduled jobs now run faster. Upon further investigation, the engineer discovers that Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) is now enabled.\n\nWhich operation does AQE automatically perform to improve Spark application performance?","options":["A.\nDynamically switching join strategies during query execution.","B.\nCollecting persistent table statistics and storing them in the metastore for later use.","C.\nImproving the execution performance of single-stage Spark jobs.","D.\nOptimizing the physical layout of Delta files stored on disk."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"aqe","concept":"aqe","concept_label":"aqe","explanation_ja":"AQEは実行中にjoin戦略等を変える。Deltaファイルの物理最適化とは別。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\nWhy the correct option is correct\n\nAdaptive Query Execution (AQE) is a Spark SQL optimization framework that modifies the physical execution plan during runtime based on actual data statistics collected during execution. This allows Spark to make better decisions than those made during the initial query planning phase.\n\nOne of the most important optimizations AQE performs is dynamically switching join strategies during query execution. For example, Spark may initially plan a sort-merge join, but after seeing the actual size of the data during runtime, it may switch to a broadcast hash join if one side of the join turns out to be small enough.\n\nThis dynamic adjustment improves performance because Spark can adapt to real data characteristics rather than relying solely on estimated statistics.\n\nRelevant documentation:\nhttps://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#adaptive-query-execution\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nBefore AQE, Spark determined the entire physical execution plan at compile time using static statistics. If those statistics were inaccurate or outdated, Spark could choose inefficient strategies.\n\nAQE improves this by enabling runtime optimizations such as:\n\n- \nDynamic join strategy selection\n\n- \nCoalescing shuffle partitions\n\n- \nHandling skewed joins\n\nThese runtime adjustments significantly improve performance, especially for large distributed joins and shuffle-heavy workloads.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nB.\nAQE does not collect and store table statistics in the metastore. Table statistics are generated using commands like `ANALYZE TABLE`. AQE instead uses runtime statistics gathered during query execution, not persistent metadata stored for later use.\n\nC.\nAQE primarily improves performance in multi-stage queries that involve shuffles, such as joins and aggregations. Single-stage jobs typically do not benefit from AQE because they lack the shuffle boundaries where AQE optimizations occur.\n\nD.\nAQE does not optimize the physical layout of Delta files on disk. File layout optimization (such as compaction or `OPTIMIZE` in Delta Lake) is a storage-level optimization, not a query execution optimization performed by AQE.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\nWhy the correct option is correct\n\nAdaptive Query Execution (AQE) is a Spark SQL optimization framework that modifies the physical execution plan during runtime based on actual data statistics collected during execution. This allows Spark to make better decisions than those made during the initial query planning phase.\n\nOne of the most important optimizations AQE performs is dynamically switching join strategies during query execution. For example, Spark may initially plan a sort-merge join, but after seeing the actual size of the data during runtime, it may switch to a broadcast hash join if one side of the join turns out to be small enough.\n\nThis dynamic adjustment improves performance because Spark can adapt to real data characteristics rather than relying solely on estimated statistics.","B.\nAQE does not collect and store table statistics in the metastore. Table statistics are generated using commands like `ANALYZE TABLE`. AQE instead uses runtime statistics gathered during query execution, not persistent metadata stored for later use.","C.\nAQE primarily improves performance in multi-stage queries that involve shuffles, such as joins and aggregations. Single-stage jobs typically do not benefit from AQE because they lack the shuffle boundaries where AQE optimizations occur.","D.\nAQE does not optimize the physical layout of Delta files on disk. File layout optimization (such as compaction or `OPTIMIZE` in Delta Lake) is a storage-level optimization, not a query execution optimization performed by AQE."],"references":[],"images":[],"source_hash":"69020af6dfa230045dfd3a130b8b01e2317ae52058dd6de9d51bcbbfae6bc691","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"aqe-runtime-change","decision_id":"aqe-runtime-join-strategy"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-080","number":80,"stem":"A data engineer needs to write a Streaming DataFrame to Parquet files.\n\nThe code template is as follows:\n\n```\ndf\n.writeStream\n// -- insert code here --\n.option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"path/to/checkpoint/dir\")\n.start()\n```\n\nWhich code fragment should be inserted to correctly write the streaming output as Parquet files?","options":["A.\n.format(\"parquet\")\n.option(\"location\", \"path/to/destination/dir\")","B.\n.format(\"format\", \"parquet\")\n.option(\"destination\", \"path/to/destination/dir\")","C.\n.format(\"format\", \"parquet\")\n.option(\"location\", \"path/to/destination/dir\")","D.\n.format(\"parquet\")\n.option(\"path\", \"path/to/destination/dir\")"],"correct":3,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"stream-writer","concept":"stream-writer","concept_label":"stream-writer","explanation_ja":"streamingファイル出力はformatとpathを指定し、checkpointLocationはoptionで渡す。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.ss/api/pyspark.sql.streaming.DataStreamWriter.option.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.ss/api/pyspark.sql.streaming.DataStreamWriter.option.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"d4a38c12b465fca054a32d4428717cf7e853b482f934ecc5704cfcfbe19f3135","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"PySpark DataStreamWriterにcheckpointLocationメソッドはない。optionで指定する。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.ss/api/pyspark.sql.streaming.DataStreamWriter.option.html"],"original_stem":"A data engineer needs to write a Streaming DataFrame to Parquet files.\n\nThe code template is as follows:\n\n```\ndf\n.writeStream\n// -- insert code here --\n.checkpointLocation(\"path/to/checkpoint/dir\")\n.start()\n```\n\nWhich code fragment should be inserted to correctly write the streaming output as Parquet files?","original_options":["A.\n.format(\"parquet\")\n.option(\"location\", \"path/to/destination/dir\")","B.\n.format(\"format\", \"parquet\")\n.option(\"destination\", \"path/to/destination/dir\")","C.\n.format(\"format\", \"parquet\")\n.option(\"location\", \"path/to/destination/dir\")","D.\n.format(\"parquet\")\n.option(\"path\", \"path/to/destination/dir\")"],"original_correct":["D"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"stream-file-sink-path","decision_id":"stream-file-sink-path"}]},{"id":"EX4-S5","title":"原文セット 5","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-081","number":81,"stem":"A data engineer writes the following code to perform a join between two DataFrames, df1 and df2:\n\n```\ndf1 = spark.read.option(\"header\", True).csv(\"sales_data.csv\")\ndf2 = spark.read.option(\"header\", True).csv(\"product_data.csv\")\nresult = df1.join(df2, df1.product_id == df2.product_id)\n```\n\nThe DataFrame df1 contains e-commerce sales data and is approximately 10 GB in size, while df2 contains product reference data and is approximately 8 MB.\n\nAssume Spark estimates df2 at 8 MB, the join is an eligible equi-join, and spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold is 10 MB. Which join strategy will Apache Spark most likely apply in this situation?","options":["A.\nA shuffle join will be used because Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) is not enabled and Spark relies on a static execution plan.","B.\nA broadcast join will be used because df2 is smaller than the default broadcast threshold.","C.\nA shuffle join will be chosen because the size difference between df1 and df2 is too large for an efficient broadcast join.","D.\nA shuffle join will occur because no broadcast hint was explicitly specified."],"correct":1,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"broadcast-auto","concept":"broadcast-auto","concept_label":"broadcast-auto","explanation_ja":"推定サイズがbroadcastしきい値以下で対応するequi-joinなら小さい側をbroadcastできる。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.csv.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.csv.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-performance-tuning.html#other-configuration-options","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hash-join"],"images":[],"source_hash":"3350c47f875e8008c51165c01978532385ee22a88f2f6fa589df18a8bd754404","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"CSVヘッダーとプラン上のサイズ統計・broadcastしきい値の前提を明確化。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.csv.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-performance-tuning.html#other-configuration-options"],"original_stem":"A data engineer writes the following code to perform a join between two DataFrames, df1 and df2:\n\n```\ndf1 = spark.read.csv(\"sales_data.csv\")\ndf2 = spark.read.csv(\"product_data.csv\")\nresult = df1.join(df2, df1.product_id == df2.product_id)\n```\n\nThe DataFrame df1 contains e-commerce sales data and is approximately 10 GB in size, while df2 contains product reference data and is approximately 8 MB.\n\nWhich join strategy will Apache Spark most likely apply in this situation?","original_options":["A.\nA shuffle join will be used because Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) is not enabled and Spark relies on a static execution plan.","B.\nA broadcast join will be used because df2 is smaller than the default broadcast threshold.","C.\nA shuffle join will be chosen because the size difference between df1 and df2 is too large for an efficient broadcast join.","D.\nA shuffle join will occur because no broadcast hint was explicitly specified."],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"static-auto-broadcast-size-threshold","decision_id":"static-auto-broadcast-size-threshold"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-082","number":82,"stem":"A data analyst is working with `sensor_df`. This DataFrame contains two columns: `record_datetime`, which is a timestamp column, and `record`, which is an array column.\n\nWhich code fragment returns a DataFrame that expands the `record` array so that each array element becomes a separate row, and the fields inside each array element are selected as separate columns?","options":["A.\n\n```\nexploded_df = sensor_df.withColumn(\"record_exploded\", explode(\"record\"))\n \nexploded_df = exploded_df.select(\n    \"record_datetime\",\n    \"sensor_id\",\n    \"status\",\n    \"health\"\n)\n```","B.\n\n```\nexploded_df = sensor_df.withColumn(\"record_exploded\", col(\"record\"))\n \nexploded_df = exploded_df.select(\n    \"record_datetime\",\n    \"record_exploded.sensor_id\",\n    \"record_exploded.status\",\n    \"record_exploded.health\"\n)\n```","C.\n\n```\nexploded_df = sensor_df.withColumn(\"record_exploded\", explode(\"record\"))\n \nexploded_df = exploded_df.select(\n    \"record_datetime\",\n    \"record_exploded.sensor_id\",\n    \"record_exploded.status\",\n    \"record_exploded.health\"\n)\n```","D.\n\n```\nexploded_df = sensor_df.withColumn(\"record_exploded\", explode(\"record\"))\n \nexploded_df = exploded_df.select(\n    \"record_datetime\",\n    \"record_exploded\"\n)\n```"],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"explode-struct","concept":"explode-struct","concept_label":"explode-struct","explanation_ja":"explodeしたstruct列のフィールドをrecord_exploded.xxxとして選択する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.explode.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: C\n\nThe correct code fragment is:\n\n```\nexploded_df = sensor_df.withColumn(\"record_exploded\", explode(\"record\"))\n \nexploded_df = exploded_df.select(\n    \"record_datetime\",\n    \"record_exploded.sensor_id\",\n    \"record_exploded.status\",\n    \"record_exploded.health\"\n)\n```\n\nOption C is correct because `explode(\"record\")` converts each element of the `record` array into a separate row. Since each array element appears to be a structured value containing fields such as `sensor_id`, `status`, and `health`, those fields can then be selected using dot notation, such as `record_exploded.sensor_id`. The Spark documentation explains that `explode()` returns a new row for each element in an array or map, and the DataFrame `select()` method is used to project columns from a DataFrame. Documentation: [PySpark explode function](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.explode.html), [PySpark DataFrame.select](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.select.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A is incorrect because it uses `explode(\"record\")`, but then tries to select `\"sensor_id\"`, `\"status\"`, and `\"health\"` directly. These fields are not top-level columns in the DataFrame. After exploding, they are nested inside the new `record_exploded` column, so they must be referenced as `\"record_exploded.sensor_id\"`, `\"record_exploded.status\"`, and `\"record_exploded.health\"`.\n\nOption B is incorrect because it uses `col(\"record\")` instead of `explode(\"record\")`. This does not expand the array into separate rows. It only copies or references the original array column as `record_exploded`, so each array element will not become a separate row.\n\nOption D is incorrect because it explodes the array but selects the whole `record_exploded` struct instead of selecting the individual fields as separate columns. The question specifically asks for the fields inside each array element to be selected as separate columns.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nIn Spark, use:\n\n```\nexplode(array_column)\n```\n\nwhen each array element must become a separate row.\n\nUse dot notation:\n\n```\n\"struct_column.field_name\"\n```\n\nwhen selecting fields from a struct column.\n\nSo the standard pattern is:\n\n```\ndf.withColumn(\"exploded_col\", explode(\"array_col\")) \\\n  .select(\"other_col\", \"exploded_col.field1\", \"exploded_col.field2\")\n```\n\nThat is why C is the correct answer.","source_option_explanations":["Option A is incorrect because it uses `explode(\"record\")`, but then tries to select `\"sensor_id\"`, `\"status\"`, and `\"health\"` directly. These fields are not top-level columns in the DataFrame. After exploding, they are nested inside the new `record_exploded` column, so they must be referenced as `\"record_exploded.sensor_id\"`, `\"record_exploded.status\"`, and `\"record_exploded.health\"`.","Option B is incorrect because it uses `col(\"record\")` instead of `explode(\"record\")`. This does not expand the array into separate rows. It only copies or references the original array column as `record_exploded`, so each array element will not become a separate row.","Correct answer: C\n\nThe correct code fragment is:\n\n```\nexploded_df = sensor_df.withColumn(\"record_exploded\", explode(\"record\"))\n \nexploded_df = exploded_df.select(\n    \"record_datetime\",\n    \"record_exploded.sensor_id\",\n    \"record_exploded.status\",\n    \"record_exploded.health\"\n)\n```\n\nOption C is correct because `explode(\"record\")` converts each element of the `record` array into a separate row. Since each array element appears to be a structured value containing fields such as `sensor_id`, `status`, and `health`, those fields can then be selected using dot notation, such as `record_exploded.sensor_id`. The Spark documentation explains that `explode()` returns a new row for each element in an array or map, and the DataFrame `select()` method is used to project columns from a DataFrame. Documentation: [PySpark explode function](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.explode.html), [PySpark DataFrame.select](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.select.html)","Option D is incorrect because it explodes the array but selects the whole `record_exploded` struct instead of selecting the individual fields as separate columns. The question specifically asks for the fields inside each array element to be selected as separate columns."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.explode.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.select.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"d725d7b97de037caafef3cd8b16a99c348faf2e23e8d84564ff909b7a406418f","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"explode-array-of-struct-fields","decision_id":"explode-array-of-struct-fields"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-083","number":83,"stem":"A developer wants to experiment with Spark Connect using an existing Spark application. The developer prefers to connect to an already running local Spark Connect server without making any modifications to the existing application code.\n\nWhich two methods allow the developer to connect to that local Spark Connect server in this scenario? (Choose two.)","options":["A.\nStart the `pyspark` shell with the option `--remote \"https://localhost\"`.","B.\nStart the `pyspark` shell with the option `--remote \"sc://localhost\"`.","C.\nSet the environment variable `SPARK_REMOTE=\"sc://localhost\"` before launching the `pyspark` shell.","D.\nModify the application code by adding `.remote(\"sc://localhost\")` in the `SparkSession.builder` configuration.","E.\nSet the Spark configuration property `spark.connect.grpc.binding.port` to `15002` within the application code."],"correct":1,"correct_many":[1,2],"domain":"Spark Connect","topic":"connect-start-connect","concept":"connect-start-connect","concept_label":"connect-start-connect","explanation_ja":"--remoteとSPARK_REMOTEは起動済みConnectサーバーへの接続指定。起動は別手順。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/spark-connect-overview.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"631f39a0979f9f6c2e7d8a6645038728dbbd07268e944392e41baad275adbf57","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"sc://localhostは起動済みサーバーへの接続指定。サーバー起動と取り違えた設問を修正。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html"],"original_stem":"A developer wants to experiment with Spark Connect using an existing Spark application. The developer prefers to start a local Spark Connect server without making any modifications to the existing application code.\n\nWhich two methods allow the developer to start a local Spark Connect server in this scenario? (Choose two.)","original_options":["A.\nStart the `pyspark` shell with the option `--remote \"https://localhost\"`.","B.\nStart the `pyspark` shell with the option `--remote \"sc://localhost\"`.","C.\nSet the environment variable `SPARK_REMOTE=\"sc://localhost\"` before launching the `pyspark` shell.","D.\nModify the application code by adding `.remote(\"sc://localhost\")` in the `SparkSession.builder` configuration.","E.\nSet the Spark configuration property `spark.connect.grpc.binding.port` to `15002` within the application code."],"original_correct":["B","C"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"connect-start-connect","decision_id":"connect-start-connect"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-084","number":84,"stem":"A Spark application running in Client Mode is experiencing performance issues because the driver process is constrained by limited resources.\n\nThe client machine cannot be upgraded, while cluster nodes have sufficient driver resources. What is the best way to resolve this problem?","options":["A.\nAdd more executor instances to the cluster.","B.\nIncrease the driver memory on the client machine.","C.\nSwitch the deployment mode to cluster mode.","D.\nSwitch the deployment mode to local mode."],"correct":2,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"driver-placement","concept":"driver-placement","concept_label":"driver-placement","explanation_ja":"client側を増強できない条件ではdriverをクラスタ側へ移すcluster modeが選択肢。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/submitting-applications.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/submitting-applications.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/submitting-applications.html#launching-applications-with-spark-submit"],"images":[],"source_hash":"086456a631a2167e367fea0723d0efd211207fe8c5f87c60d7897ca764922fc8","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"driverメモリを増やす選択肢Bも状況によって有効なため、client側を増強できずclusterに余力がある前提を追記。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/submitting-applications.html"],"original_stem":"A Spark application running in Client Mode is experiencing performance issues because the driver process is constrained by limited resources.\n\nWhat is the best way to resolve this problem?","original_options":["A.\nAdd more executor instances to the cluster.","B.\nIncrease the driver memory on the client machine.","C.\nSwitch the deployment mode to cluster mode.","D.\nSwitch the deployment mode to local mode."],"original_correct":["C"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"client-cluster-driver-placement","decision_id":"move-resource-constrained-client-driver"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-085","number":85,"stem":"A Data Analyst is working with a DataFrame named employees_df and wants to retrieve employees who have five or more years of tenure so they can be recognized.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly filters the DataFrame and displays the employees who meet this condition?","options":["A.\nemployees_df.filter(employees_df.tenure >= 5).show()","B.\nemployees_df.where(employees_df.tenure >= 5)","C.\nfilter(employees_df.tenure >= 5)","D.\nemployees_df.filter(employees_df.tenure >= 5).collect()"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"filter-condition","concept":"filter-condition","concept_label":"filter-condition","explanation_ja":"条件に合う行をfilterしshowで表示する。whereだけでは結果を表示しない。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn PySpark, the `DataFrame.filter()` method is used to return rows that satisfy a given condition. After filtering, the `show()` method is commonly used to display the resulting rows in a readable tabular format in the console.\n\nThe correct code:\n\n```\nemployees_df.filter(employees_df.tenure >= 5).show()\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- \n`employees_df.filter(...)` applies the condition to the DataFrame.\n\n- \n`employees_df.tenure >= 5` selects employees with five or more years of tenure.\n\n- \n`.show()` prints the filtered results.\n\nThis satisfies both requirements:\n\n- \nFilter employees with tenure ≥ 5\n\n- \nDisplay the resulting records\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html)\n\nThe documentation states that `filter()` returns a new DataFrame containing rows that satisfy the given condition, and `show()` displays those rows.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption B\n\n```\nemployees_df.where(employees_df.tenure >= 5)\n```\n\n`where()` is actually an alias of `filter()`, so the filtering logic is correct. However, this code does not display the results. It simply returns a new DataFrame. Since the question explicitly requires displaying the employees, the code is incomplete without `.show()`.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.where.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.where.html)\n\nOption C\n\n```\nfilter(employees_df.tenure >= 5)\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because:\n\n- \n`filter()` is not called on the DataFrame.\n\n- \nPySpark filtering must be applied through `DataFrame.filter()` or `DataFrame.where()`.\n\n- \nThis code would raise an error because it lacks the DataFrame context.\n\nOption D\n\n```\nemployees_df.filter(employees_df.tenure >= 5).collect()\n```\n\nWhile this code does correctly filter the records, `collect()` returns all rows to the driver as a Python list, which is not intended for simply displaying results.\n\nKey issues:\n\n- \n`collect()` is not meant for display.\n\n- \nIt can cause memory issues if the dataset is large.\n\n- \nIn Spark workflows, `show()` is the standard method for displaying results safely.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.collect.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.collect.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nIn Spark DataFrames:\n\n- \nTransformations (e.g., `filter`, `select`, `where`) define logical operations and produce new DataFrames.\n\n- \nActions (e.g., `show`, `collect`, `count`) trigger execution of the computation.\n\nHere:\n\n- \n`filter()` defines the filtering transformation.\n\n- \n`show()` is the action that executes the query plan and prints the results.\n\nUnderstanding the distinction between transformations and actions is fundamental for Spark performance and correct code behavior.","source_option_explanations":["Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn PySpark, the `DataFrame.filter()` method is used to return rows that satisfy a given condition. After filtering, the `show()` method is commonly used to display the resulting rows in a readable tabular format in the console.\n\nThe correct code:\n\n```\nemployees_df.filter(employees_df.tenure >= 5).show()\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- \n`employees_df.filter(...)` applies the condition to the DataFrame.\n\n- \n`employees_df.tenure >= 5` selects employees with five or more years of tenure.\n\n- \n`.show()` prints the filtered results.","Option B\n\n```\nemployees_df.where(employees_df.tenure >= 5)\n```\n\n`where()` is actually an alias of `filter()`, so the filtering logic is correct. However, this code does not display the results. It simply returns a new DataFrame. Since the question explicitly requires displaying the employees, the code is incomplete without `.show()`.","Option C\n\n```\nfilter(employees_df.tenure >= 5)\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because:\n\n- \n`filter()` is not called on the DataFrame.\n\n- \nPySpark filtering must be applied through `DataFrame.filter()` or `DataFrame.where()`.\n\n- \nThis code would raise an error because it lacks the DataFrame context.","Option D\n\n```\nemployees_df.filter(employees_df.tenure >= 5).collect()\n```\n\nWhile this code does correctly filter the records, `collect()` returns all rows to the driver as a Python list, which is not intended for simply displaying results."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.where.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.collect.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"049e0bba450c8c53748198b344defe8e940af86daf26fba7ca9eacb6f32d0102","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"filter-and-display-show","decision_id":"filter-and-display-show"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-086","number":86,"stem":"A data engineer notices that the upstream streaming source frequently sends duplicate records to the event table. After reviewing the production data, the engineer determines that the maximum time difference between duplicate records in the `event_timestamp` column is 30 minutes.\n\nAssume event_id identifies an event and the engineer adds the following code:\n\nevents.withWatermark(\"event_timestamp\", \"31 minutes\").dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark([\"event_id\"])\n\nWhat will be the outcome of this change?","options":["A.\nThe method cannot perform deduplication for this situation.","B. It removes duplicate event_id values within the configured watermark horizon; the 31-minute delay exceeds the 30-minute maximum timestamp difference.","C.\nIt removes every duplicate record, regardless of when it arrives.","D.\nWatermarks must be specified in seconds, so the code produces an error."],"correct":1,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"stream-dedup-watermark","concept":"stream-dedup-watermark","concept_label":"stream-dedup-watermark","explanation_ja":"withWatermarkで遅延を設定し、WithinWatermarkには重複キーのlistを渡す。遅延幅は重複時刻差より大きく設定する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withWatermark.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"02fec81b0bf209f35f12016e5e132fc127e93cecbe2ba2efc34d90a9968caaf9","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"WithinWatermarkの引数はsubsetでwatermarkは別途設定する。境界30分ではなくそれを超える31分を指定。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark.html"],"original_stem":"A data engineer notices that the upstream streaming source frequently sends duplicate records to the event table. After reviewing the production data, the engineer determines that the maximum time difference between duplicate records in the `event_timestamp` column is 30 minutes.\n\nTo address the issue, the engineer adds the following code:\n\ndropDuplicatesWithinWatermark(\"event_timestamp\", \"30 minutes\")\n\nWhat will be the outcome of this change?","original_options":["A.\nThe method cannot perform deduplication for this situation.","B.\nIt removes duplicate records that arrive within the 30-minute watermark window.","C.\nIt removes every duplicate record, regardless of when it arrives.","D.\nWatermarks must be specified in seconds, so the code produces an error."],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"watermark-dedup","decision_id":"within-watermark-business-key-dedup"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-087","number":87,"stem":"Which UDF implementation calculates the length of strings in a Spark DataFrame?","options":["A. `productsDF.withcolumn(\"nameLength\", spark.udf(\"len\", StringType()))`","B. `productsDF.select(length(col(\"productName\"))-alias(\"nameLength\"))`","C. `spark.udf.register(\"computeLength\", lambda s: len(s))`","D. `productsDF.withcolumn(\"nameLength\", udf(lambda s: len(s), StringType()))`"],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"sql-udf-registration","concept":"sql-udf-registration","concept_label":"sql-udf-registration","explanation_ja":"spark.udf.registerでPython関数を登録できる。NULL対応と戻り型の明示は実装時に検討する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.UDFRegistration.register.html","source_explanation":"Answer: C\n\n```\nspark.udf.register(\"computeLength\", lambda s: len(s))\n```\n\nWhy C is correct\n\n- \n`spark.udf.register(name, f, returnType=...)` is the official PySpark API for registering a Python function as a UDF. The official docs' own example mirrors this exact pattern: strlen = spark.udf.register(\"stringLengthString\", lambda x: len(x)) — a lambda computing `len(x)` registered directly, with no explicit return type required. [Apache](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.UDFRegistration.register.html)\n\n- \nOption C is syntactically valid, executable code that correctly implements string-length logic via a lambda (`lambda s: len(s)`), matching the documented pattern precisely.\n\n- \nNote the return type isn't mandatory: returnType can be optionally specified when f is a Python function... returnType defaults to string type and can be optionally specified. So even without specifying `IntegerType()`, this line runs without error (the length values will just come back as their string representation, e.g., `'4'` instead of `4`, unless a numeric returnType is added) — but it still correctly calculates the length, which is what the question asks. [Apache](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.UDFRegistration.register.html)\n\nWhy the other options are wrong\n\nA — `spark.udf(\"len\", StringType())`\n`spark.udf` is a `UDFRegistration` object, not a callable function — it only exposes methods like `.register()`. Calling it directly as `spark.udf(...)` raises `TypeError: 'UDFRegistration' object is not callable`. Additionally, `withcolumn` (lowercase \"c\") is not a valid DataFrame method — the real method is `withColumn` (capital \"C\"), so this line would fail on an `AttributeError` even before reaching the `spark.udf(...)` problem.\n\nB — `productsDF.select(length(col(\"productName\"))-alias(\"nameLength\"))`\nThis isn't a UDF at all — it uses the built-in `length()` function. But more fundamentally, the syntax is broken: `.alias()` is a method that must be chained onto a Column (`length(col(...)).alias(\"nameLength\")`), not a standalone function you can subtract (`-alias(...)`) from another expression. `alias` isn't even an importable top-level function in `pyspark.sql.functions`, so this raises a `NameError`, compounded by an invalid unary-minus/subtraction operation on a Column object.\n\nD — `productsDF.withcolumn(\"nameLength\", udf(lambda s: len(s), StringType()))`\nTwo problems: first, `withcolumn` (lowercase) is invalid — raises `AttributeError` since the real method is `withColumn`. Second, even if that were fixed, `udf(lambda s: len(s), StringType())` only creates a `UserDefinedFunction` object — it does not apply it to any column. To actually use it, you must call the returned UDF on a column: `udf(lambda s: len(s), StringType())(col(\"productName\"))`. As written, you'd be assigning the UDF object itself as the column's value, not the computed lengths — this would raise a type error since `withColumn`'s second argument must be a `Column`, not a bare `UserDefinedFunction`.\n\nExam trap summary\n\n- \nRegistration object vs. callable function (A): `spark.udf` is a namespace/registration object exposing methods — never callable on its own.\n\n- \nCase-sensitive method names (A, D): `withColumn` — not `withcolumn`. Python attribute lookups are case-sensitive, and this typo is a common, deliberately-placed exam trap.\n\n- \nCreating a UDF vs. applying a UDF (D): `udf(func, returnType)` only constructs the UDF; you must still invoke it on a column (`my_udf(col(\"x\"))`) to actually transform data. Constructing without applying is a frequent conceptual gap tested here.\n\n- \nMethod vs. function invocation on Columns (B): `.alias()` is a Column method, not a standalone function — and definitely not something you subtract from another expression.\n\nFoundational point:\n\n`spark.udf.register()` serves a dual purpose: it makes the Python function callable both from Spark SQL (`spark.sql(\"SELECT computeLength(name) FROM ...\")`) and, since it returns a `UserDefinedFunction` object, from the DataFrame API if you capture the return value (e.g., `compute_length = spark.udf.register(...)`, then `df.select(compute_length(\"productName\"))`). This dual-registration behavior is a key UDF concept tested on this exam, distinct from `pyspark.sql.functions.udf()`, which only creates a DataFrame-API-usable UDF (not SQL-callable unless separately registered).\n\nReal-time connect\n\nUDF registration via `spark.udf.register()` is especially useful in mixed workflows — for example, when analysts querying a table through Spark SQL (in a notebook or BI tool) need access to the same custom logic (like string length, custom parsing, or business-rule scoring) that data engineers apply via the DataFrame API elsewhere in the pipeline.\n\nReference\n\n- \n[UDFRegistration.register — PySpark official docs](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.UDFRegistration.register.html)\n\n- \n[User-defined scalar functions (Python) — Databricks docs](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/udf/python)","source_option_explanations":["A — `spark.udf(\"len\", StringType())`\n`spark.udf` is a `UDFRegistration` object, not a callable function — it only exposes methods like `.register()`. Calling it directly as `spark.udf(...)` raises `TypeError: 'UDFRegistration' object is not callable`. Additionally, `withcolumn` (lowercase \"c\") is not a valid DataFrame method — the real method is `withColumn` (capital \"C\"), so this line would fail on an `AttributeError` even before reaching the `spark.udf(...)` problem.","B — `productsDF.select(length(col(\"productName\"))-alias(\"nameLength\"))`\nThis isn't a UDF at all — it uses the built-in `length()` function. But more fundamentally, the syntax is broken: `.alias()` is a method that must be chained onto a Column (`length(col(...)).alias(\"nameLength\")`), not a standalone function you can subtract (`-alias(...)`) from another expression. `alias` isn't even an importable top-level function in `pyspark.sql.functions`, so this raises a `NameError`, compounded by an invalid unary-minus/subtraction operation on a Column object.","Answer: C\n\n```\nspark.udf.register(\"computeLength\", lambda s: len(s))\n```\n\nWhy C is correct\n\n- \n`spark.udf.register(name, f, returnType=...)` is the official PySpark API for registering a Python function as a UDF. The official docs' own example mirrors this exact pattern: strlen = spark.udf.register(\"stringLengthString\", lambda x: len(x)) — a lambda computing `len(x)` registered directly, with no explicit return type required. [Apache](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.UDFRegistration.register.html)\n\n- \nOption C is syntactically valid, executable code that correctly implements string-length logic via a lambda (`lambda s: len(s)`), matching the documented pattern precisely.\n\n- \nNote the return type isn't mandatory: returnType can be optionally specified when f is a Python function... returnType defaults to string type and can be optionally specified. So even without specifying `IntegerType()`, this line runs without error (the length values will just come back as their string representation, e.g., `'4'` instead of `4`, unless a numeric returnType is added) — but it still correctly calculates the length, which is what the question asks. [Apache](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.UDFRegistration.register.html)","D — `productsDF.withcolumn(\"nameLength\", udf(lambda s: len(s), StringType()))`\nTwo problems: first, `withcolumn` (lowercase) is invalid — raises `AttributeError` since the real method is `withColumn`. Second, even if that were fixed, `udf(lambda s: len(s), StringType())` only creates a `UserDefinedFunction` object — it does not apply it to any column. To actually use it, you must call the returned UDF on a column: `udf(lambda s: len(s), StringType())(col(\"productName\"))`. As written, you'd be assigning the UDF object itself as the column's value, not the computed lengths — this would raise a type error since `withColumn`'s second argument must be a `Column`, not a bare `UserDefinedFunction`."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.UDFRegistration.register.html","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/udf/python"],"images":[],"source_hash":"77a9958c8edf300d4bd7478cc55c33296a8f647cda789b4a66546efba5376c58","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"sql-udf-register","decision_id":"sql-udf-register"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-088","number":88,"stem":"A Spark application developer is evaluating the performance of a job that performs multiple transformations on a large distributed dataset. The developer wants to determine which operation causes data shuffling, thereby introducing a new stage boundary in the Spark execution plan.\n\nWhich operation triggers a shuffle and results in a new stage?","options":["A.\nDataFrame.groupBy().agg()","B.\nDataFrame.filter()","C.\nDataFrame.withColumn()","D.\nDataFrame.select()"],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"execution-shuffle","concept":"execution-shuffle","concept_label":"execution-shuffle","explanation_ja":"通常のgroupBy集計はkeyごとのデータを集めるshuffleを伴う。既存partitioningで不要になる場合はある。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#shuffle-operations","source_explanation":"Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\n`DataFrame.groupBy().agg()` triggers a shuffle operation because Spark must repartition data across executors so that all records belonging to the same grouping key are brought together. This redistribution of data across the cluster creates a shuffle boundary, which results in the creation of a new stage in the Spark execution plan.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.groupBy(\"department\").agg({\"salary\": \"avg\"})\n```\n\nIf records with the same `department` value exist on multiple partitions, Spark must move data across the network to regroup them. This network data exchange is called a shuffle.\n\nIn Spark’s execution model:\n\n- \nTransformations that do not require data movement are called narrow transformations.\n\n- \nTransformations that require data to move between partitions are wide transformations.\n\n`groupBy()` is a wide transformation, and wide transformations introduce stage boundaries because Spark must complete one stage before starting the next.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#shuffle-operations](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#shuffle-operations)\n\nThe documentation explains that operations such as groupByKey, reduceByKey, and aggregations require shuffling data across partitions, which creates new stages in Spark jobs.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption B — `DataFrame.filter()`\n\n`filter()` is a narrow transformation. Each partition processes its own rows independently without moving data across nodes. Because no data redistribution occurs, no shuffle and no new stage boundary is created.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html)\n\nOption C — `DataFrame.withColumn()`\n\n`withColumn()` simply adds or modifies a column based on existing data within the same partition. It does not require data exchange between executors. Therefore, it remains a narrow transformation and does not trigger a shuffle.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html)\n\nOption D — `DataFrame.select()`\n\n`select()` only projects or selects columns from the existing dataset. It operates within each partition independently and therefore does not require data movement.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.select.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.select.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark divides jobs into stages based on shuffle boundaries.\n\nKey rule:\n\n- \nNarrow transformations (e.g., `filter`, `select`, `withColumn`)\n\n- \nOperate within a single partition\n\n- \nDo not create new stages\n\n- \nWide transformations (e.g., `groupBy`, `join`, `distinct`, `repartition`)\n\n- \nRequire data redistribution across partitions\n\n- \nTrigger shuffle and create new stages\n\nUnderstanding wide vs narrow transformations is fundamental for optimizing Spark performance because shuffle operations are one of the most expensive operations in distributed data processing.","source_option_explanations":["Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\n`DataFrame.groupBy().agg()` triggers a shuffle operation because Spark must repartition data across executors so that all records belonging to the same grouping key are brought together. This redistribution of data across the cluster creates a shuffle boundary, which results in the creation of a new stage in the Spark execution plan.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.groupBy(\"department\").agg({\"salary\": \"avg\"})\n```\n\nIf records with the same `department` value exist on multiple partitions, Spark must move data across the network to regroup them. This network data exchange is called a shuffle.\n\nIn Spark’s execution model:\n\n- \nTransformations that do not require data movement are called narrow transformations.\n\n- \nTransformations that require data to move between partitions are wide transformations.\n\n`groupBy()` is a wide transformation, and wide transformations introduce stage boundaries because Spark must complete one stage before starting the next.","Option B — `DataFrame.filter()`\n\n`filter()` is a narrow transformation. Each partition processes its own rows independently without moving data across nodes. Because no data redistribution occurs, no shuffle and no new stage boundary is created.","Option C — `DataFrame.withColumn()`\n\n`withColumn()` simply adds or modifies a column based on existing data within the same partition. It does not require data exchange between executors. Therefore, it remains a narrow transformation and does not trigger a shuffle.","Option D — `DataFrame.select()`\n\n`select()` only projects or selects columns from the existing dataset. It operates within each partition independently and therefore does not require data movement."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#shuffle-operations","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.select.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"19891d2e8bcbd8f9bd8d53232d8443c749eac30d4fbad5fd5889b617c2f76af3","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"narrow-wide-shuffle","decision_id":"narrow-wide-shuffle"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-089","number":89,"stem":"A Spark developer is building an application that monitors task execution performance across the cluster. One requirement is to determine the maximum processing time for tasks executed on each worker node and then aggregate this information on the driver for further analysis.\n\nWhich approach should the developer use to accomplish this?","options":["A.\nUse an RDD action such as `reduce()` to calculate the maximum processing time.","B.\nUse an accumulator to capture and report the maximum time back to the driver.","C.\nBroadcast a variable so that workers can share the maximum processing time.","D.\nConfigure the Spark UI so it automatically gathers the maximum processing times."],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"rdd-reduce-metrics","concept":"rdd-reduce-metrics","concept_label":"rdd-reduce-metrics","explanation_ja":"通常の数値accumulatorは加算用。値を集約して最大を求めるならreduce等を使える。監視目的ならSparkのtask metricsも有用。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#actions","source_explanation":"Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nTo determine the maximum processing time across tasks executed on worker nodes and aggregate the result on the driver, the developer should use an RDD action such as `reduce()`.\n\nIn Spark, actions trigger execution and return results to the driver program. The `reduce()` action aggregates values across partitions using an associative function. This makes it ideal for computing global statistics such as maximum, minimum, or sum.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nmax_time = task_times_rdd.reduce(lambda a, b: max(a, b))\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- \nEach partition contributes its task processing times.\n\n- \nThe `reduce()` operation combines results across partitions.\n\n- \nThe final maximum value is returned to the driver for analysis.\n\nThis approach is efficient because the aggregation happens in parallel across executors, and only the final result is sent to the driver.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#actions](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#actions)\n\nThe documentation explains that actions like `reduce()` aggregate elements of an RDD using a function and return the result to the driver.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption B — Use an accumulator\n\nAccumulators are designed primarily for counters and sums, and they are intended for write-only updates from executors and read access on the driver.\n\nWhile accumulators can track metrics, they are not suitable for computing distributed maximum values, especially because task retries can lead to duplicate updates and incorrect results.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#accumulators](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#accumulators)\n\nOption C — Broadcast a variable\n\nBroadcast variables are used to send read-only data from the driver to all worker nodes. They are not meant for collecting results back from executors to the driver.\n\nTherefore, broadcasting cannot be used to compute or aggregate maximum task processing times.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#broadcast-variables](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#broadcast-variables)\n\nOption D — Configure the Spark UI\n\nThe Spark UI is a monitoring and debugging tool that displays runtime metrics such as task duration, stages, and executor statistics. However, it does not provide a programmatic mechanism for aggregating task processing times for application logic.\n\nThus, it cannot be used to compute the required metric inside the Spark application.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/web-ui.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/web-ui.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark aggregates distributed data using actions that operate across partitions.\n\nCommon aggregation actions include:\n\n- \n`reduce()` – combines elements using an associative function\n\n- \n`max()` / `min()` – compute extrema\n\n- \n`count()` – counts elements\n\nThe key principle is that executors perform partial computations locally, and Spark merges them to produce a final result returned to the driver. This distributed reduction model enables efficient computation on large datasets across the cluster.","source_option_explanations":["Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nTo determine the maximum processing time across tasks executed on worker nodes and aggregate the result on the driver, the developer should use an RDD action such as `reduce()`.\n\nIn Spark, actions trigger execution and return results to the driver program. The `reduce()` action aggregates values across partitions using an associative function. This makes it ideal for computing global statistics such as maximum, minimum, or sum.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nmax_time = task_times_rdd.reduce(lambda a, b: max(a, b))\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- \nEach partition contributes its task processing times.\n\n- \nThe `reduce()` operation combines results across partitions.\n\n- \nThe final maximum value is returned to the driver for analysis.\n\nThis approach is efficient because the aggregation happens in parallel across executors, and only the final result is sent to the driver.","Option B — Use an accumulator\n\nAccumulators are designed primarily for counters and sums, and they are intended for write-only updates from executors and read access on the driver.\n\nWhile accumulators can track metrics, they are not suitable for computing distributed maximum values, especially because task retries can lead to duplicate updates and incorrect results.","Option C — Broadcast a variable\n\nBroadcast variables are used to send read-only data from the driver to all worker nodes. They are not meant for collecting results back from executors to the driver.\n\nTherefore, broadcasting cannot be used to compute or aggregate maximum task processing times.","Option D — Configure the Spark UI\n\nThe Spark UI is a monitoring and debugging tool that displays runtime metrics such as task duration, stages, and executor statistics. However, it does not provide a programmatic mechanism for aggregating task processing times for application logic."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#actions","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#accumulators","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#broadcast-variables","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/web-ui.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"4787b7bf9133d89eca5f9438604f1713310efba62a170e5150aafcfe38e08981","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"rdd-reduce-metrics","decision_id":"rdd-reduce-metrics"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-090","number":90,"stem":"A Spark application must read several Parquet files from a directory, where the files contain different but compatible schemas. The data engineer wants to create a DataFrame that includes every column present across all the files.\n\nWhich code snippet should the data engineer use in Apache Spark to read the Parquet files while ensuring all columns are merged into the resulting DataFrame?","options":["A.\ndf = spark.read.option(\"mergeSchema\", \"true\").parquet(\"/data/parquet_files\")","B.\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"/data/parquet_files\").mergeAllColumns()","C.\ndf = spark.read.schema(\"merge\").parquet(\"/data/parquet_files\")","D.\ndf = spark.read.format(\"parquet\").load(\"/data/parquet_files\")"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"parquet-schema-merge","concept":"parquet-schema-merge","concept_label":"parquet-schema-merge","explanation_ja":"Parquetでファイル間のschemaを統合するにはmergeSchema=True。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nWhen multiple Parquet files contain different but compatible schemas, Spark can merge the schemas so that the resulting DataFrame contains all columns from all files. This is done by enabling the `mergeSchema` option during the read operation.\n\nThe correct implementation is:\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.option(\"mergeSchema\", \"true\").parquet(\"/data/parquet_files\")\n```\n\nWhen `mergeSchema` is enabled:\n\n- \nSpark scans the schema metadata of all Parquet files.\n\n- \nIt computes the union of all columns across files.\n\n- \nMissing columns in individual files are filled with null values.\n\nExample scenario:\n\nFile 1 schema\n\n```\nname, age\n```\n\nFile 2 schema\n\n```\nname, salary\n```\n\nMerged schema produced by Spark:\n\n```\nname, age, salary\n```\n\nRows from files that do not contain a column will have null values for that column.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html)\n\nThe documentation explains that setting `mergeSchema` to true enables Spark to merge schemas from multiple Parquet files into a unified schema.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption B\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"/data/parquet_files\").mergeAllColumns()\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because `mergeAllColumns()` is not a valid Spark DataFrame API method. Spark performs schema merging only through the `mergeSchema` configuration option.\n\nOption C\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.schema(\"merge\").parquet(\"/data/parquet_files\")\n```\n\nThis is invalid because `schema()` expects a StructType schema definition, not a string instruction like `\"merge\"`. This code would fail.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.schema.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.schema.html)\n\nOption D\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.format(\"parquet\").load(\"/data/parquet_files\")\n```\n\nThis simply reads the files using the default schema inference behavior. Spark typically uses the schema from the first file it encounters, which means columns present in other files may be ignored unless schema merging is explicitly enabled.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nParquet stores schema metadata within each file. When datasets evolve over time, different files may contain additional or missing columns.\n\nSpark handles this through schema evolution.\n\nKey points:\n\n- \nBy default, Spark does not merge schemas automatically for performance reasons.\n\n- \nEnabling `mergeSchema` forces Spark to inspect all file schemas and compute a unified schema.\n\n- \nMissing values in individual files appear as null in the final DataFrame.\n\nBecause schema merging requires scanning metadata across files, it can increase read latency, so it should be used only when necessary.","source_option_explanations":["Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nWhen multiple Parquet files contain different but compatible schemas, Spark can merge the schemas so that the resulting DataFrame contains all columns from all files. This is done by enabling the `mergeSchema` option during the read operation.\n\nThe correct implementation is:\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.option(\"mergeSchema\", \"true\").parquet(\"/data/parquet_files\")\n```\n\nWhen `mergeSchema` is enabled:\n\n- \nSpark scans the schema metadata of all Parquet files.\n\n- \nIt computes the union of all columns across files.\n\n- \nMissing columns in individual files are filled with null values.\n\nExample scenario:\n\nFile 1 schema\n\n```\nname, age\n```\n\nFile 2 schema\n\n```\nname, salary\n```\n\nMerged schema produced by Spark:\n\n```\nname, age, salary\n```\n\nRows from files that do not contain a column will have null values for that column.","Option B\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"/data/parquet_files\").mergeAllColumns()\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because `mergeAllColumns()` is not a valid Spark DataFrame API method. Spark performs schema merging only through the `mergeSchema` configuration option.","Option C\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.schema(\"merge\").parquet(\"/data/parquet_files\")\n```\n\nThis is invalid because `schema()` expects a StructType schema definition, not a string instruction like `\"merge\"`. This code would fail.","Option D\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.format(\"parquet\").load(\"/data/parquet_files\")\n```\n\nThis simply reads the files using the default schema inference behavior. Spark typically uses the schema from the first file it encounters, which means columns present in other files may be ignored unless schema merging is explicitly enabled."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.schema.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"2c5e77072bc8b95f65395361bc955109502d24d759c301ba5e88f51ccd1d476e","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"parquet-merge-compatible-schemas","decision_id":"parquet-merge-compatible-schemas"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-091","number":91,"stem":"Assume no Arrow configuration has already been set. Which Spark configuration can be enabled to optimize conversions between Pandas DataFrames and PySpark DataFrames using Apache Arrow?","options":["A.\nspark.conf.set(\"spark.pandas.arrow.enabled\", \"true\")","B.\nspark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.execution.arrow.pyspark.enabled\", \"true\")","C.\nspark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.execution.arrow.enabled\", \"true\")","D.\nspark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.arrow.pandas.enabled\", \"true\")"],"correct":1,"correct_many":[1,2],"domain":"Pandas API","topic":"arrow-config","concept":"arrow-config","concept_label":"arrow-config","explanation_ja":"新設定が未指定なら旧arrow.enabledもfallbackとして効く。推奨はpyspark.enabledを直接指定する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-pyspark-pandas-with-arrow.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"def8f2dc7eb76a33bca3ae4ad486f8ae9929df82a59d105fb2503a707ac0c163","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"Spark 3.5では旧arrow.enabledから新設定へのfallbackがある。明示的な新設定が未設定ならBとCが有効。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html"],"original_stem":"Which Spark configuration must be enabled to optimize conversions between Pandas DataFrames and PySpark DataFrames using Apache Arrow?","original_options":["A.\nspark.conf.set(\"spark.pandas.arrow.enabled\", \"true\")","B.\nspark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.execution.arrow.pyspark.enabled\", \"true\")","C.\nspark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.execution.arrow.enabled\", \"true\")","D.\nspark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.arrow.pandas.enabled\", \"true\")"],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"arrow-config","decision_id":"arrow-config"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-092","number":92,"stem":"A data engineer is running a Spark job to process 1 TB of data stored in a distributed storage system such as S3 or ADLS. The Spark cluster consists of 10 nodes, each providing 16 CPU cores. When reviewing the Spark UI, the engineer observes the following:\n\n• On the Executors page, the number of Active Tasks is much lower than the total available cores.\n• On the Stages page, many tasks are finishing within milliseconds.\n• On the Jobs page, the total number of tasks is significantly smaller than the number of available CPUs.\n\nWhich approach should the engineer use to adjust partitioning for optimal resource utilization?","options":["A.\nConfigure the number of partitions to match the total number of CPUs in the cluster to maximize parallel processing.","B.\nSet a fixed partition count, such as 200, to maintain consistency across different jobs.","C.\nSet the number of partitions equal to the number of cluster nodes to evenly distribute data across the nodes.","D.\nDetermine the number of partitions by dividing the dataset size (1 TB) by an appropriate partition size, such as 128 MB."],"correct":3,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"partition-sizing","concept":"partition-sizing","concept_label":"partition-sizing","explanation_ja":"データ量と目標partitionサイズから目安を出し、CPU数・task時間・skewを見て調整する。128MBは固定の正解値ではない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Spark, optimal partitioning is typically determined by the size of the dataset and the desired partition size, not simply by CPU count or number of nodes.\n\nA common best practice is to create partitions that are approximately 128 MB to 256 MB in size. This size balances:\n\n- \nEfficient disk and network throughput\n\n- \nParallel processing\n\n- \nReasonable task scheduling overhead\n\nFor the dataset in this scenario:\n\nDataset size:\n\n```\n1 TB ≈ 1024 GB\n```\n\nUsing a 128 MB partition size:\n\n```\n1 TB / 128 MB ≈ 8192 partitions\n```\n\nThis produces thousands of tasks, allowing Spark to:\n\n- \nFully utilize available executors\n\n- \nMaintain high parallelism\n\n- \nAvoid tasks that are too small or too large\n\nSince the Spark UI shows very few tasks relative to the available cores, the dataset is currently under-partitioned, causing low cluster utilization.\n\nDetermining partitions based on data size divided by an optimal partition size is the recommended approach.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html)\n\nThe Spark performance tuning guide explains that partition sizing should generally target 100–200 MB per partition for efficient distributed processing.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A — Match partitions to total CPUs\n\nThe cluster has:\n\n```\n10 nodes × 16 cores = 160 cores\n```\n\nSetting partitions to 160 would create only 160 tasks, which is extremely small for processing 1 TB of data. This would lead to very large partitions and long-running tasks, limiting scalability.\n\nOption B — Fixed partition count (e.g., 200)\n\nUsing a fixed partition number across all jobs ignores dataset size variability. For large datasets like 1 TB, 200 partitions would still produce partitions that are far too large, resulting in inefficient processing.\n\nOption C — Number of partitions equal to nodes\n\nSetting partitions equal to 10 nodes would create only 10 tasks, which would drastically underutilize the cluster. Each partition would contain about 100 GB of data, causing severe performance bottlenecks.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark parallelism depends on partition count, not cluster size alone.\n\nA widely accepted rule for partition sizing:\n\n```\nPartition count ≈ Dataset size / Ideal partition size\n```\n\nTypical recommended partition size:\n\n```\n128 MB – 256 MB per partition\n```\n\nBenefits of proper partition sizing:\n\n- \nMaximizes parallelism\n\n- \nImproves cluster utilization\n\n- \nPrevents straggler tasks\n\n- \nReduces memory pressure per executor\n\nThis data-driven partitioning strategy is a fundamental Spark performance tuning practice when processing large-scale distributed datasets.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Match partitions to total CPUs\n\nThe cluster has:\n\n```\n10 nodes × 16 cores = 160 cores\n```\n\nSetting partitions to 160 would create only 160 tasks, which is extremely small for processing 1 TB of data. This would lead to very large partitions and long-running tasks, limiting scalability.","Option B — Fixed partition count (e.g., 200)\n\nUsing a fixed partition number across all jobs ignores dataset size variability. For large datasets like 1 TB, 200 partitions would still produce partitions that are far too large, resulting in inefficient processing.","Option C — Number of partitions equal to nodes\n\nSetting partitions equal to 10 nodes would create only 10 tasks, which would drastically underutilize the cluster. Each partition would contain about 100 GB of data, causing severe performance bottlenecks.","Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Spark, optimal partitioning is typically determined by the size of the dataset and the desired partition size, not simply by CPU count or number of nodes.\n\nA common best practice is to create partitions that are approximately 128 MB to 256 MB in size. This size balances:\n\n- \nEfficient disk and network throughput\n\n- \nParallel processing\n\n- \nReasonable task scheduling overhead\n\nFor the dataset in this scenario:\n\nDataset size:\n\n```\n1 TB ≈ 1024 GB\n```\n\nUsing a 128 MB partition size:\n\n```\n1 TB / 128 MB ≈ 8192 partitions\n```\n\nThis produces thousands of tasks, allowing Spark to:\n\n- \nFully utilize available executors\n\n- \nMaintain high parallelism\n\n- \nAvoid tasks that are too small or too large\n\nSince the Spark UI shows very few tasks relative to the available cores, the dataset is currently under-partitioned, causing low cluster utilization.\n\nDetermining partitions based on data size divided by an optimal partition size is the recommended approach."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"0f97625ef412dbe986b0de841240632ceac95bd0a441e525a298b1041d30db28","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"partition-sizing","decision_id":"partition-sizing"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-093","number":93,"stem":"A developer observes that all post-shuffle partitions in a dataset are smaller than the threshold configured in `spark.sql.adaptive.maxShuffledHashJoinLocalMapThreshold`.\n\nAssume AQE is enabled, the join is eligible for shuffled hash join, and this threshold is not smaller than spark.sql.adaptive.advisoryPartitionSizeInBytes. Given this situation, which join strategy will Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) select?","options":["A.\nA Cartesian join","B.\nA shuffled hash join","C.\nA broadcast nested loop join","D.\nA sort-merge join"],"correct":1,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"aqe-shuffled-hash","concept":"aqe-shuffled-hash","concept_label":"aqe-shuffled-hash","explanation_ja":"AQEのshuffled hash join切替には各partitionのサイズとadvisoryサイズとの条件がある。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-performance-tuning.html#converting-sort-merge-join-to-shuffled-hash-join","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-performance-tuning.html#converting-sort-merge-join-to-shuffled-hash-join","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#adaptive-query-execution","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-join.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"c5dc11738dc99dac33d531bde5c45ca43b38eefe457dc58208560997984c0c88","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"hash join優先にはadvisoryPartitionSizeInBytesとの大小条件もある。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-performance-tuning.html#converting-sort-merge-join-to-shuffled-hash-join"],"original_stem":"A developer observes that all post-shuffle partitions in a dataset are smaller than the threshold configured in `spark.sql.adaptive.maxShuffledHashJoinLocalMapThreshold`.\n\nGiven this situation, which join strategy will Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) select?","original_options":["A.\nA Cartesian join","B.\nA shuffled hash join","C.\nA broadcast nested loop join","D.\nA sort-merge join"],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"aqe-shuffled-hash-local-map-threshold","decision_id":"aqe-shuffled-hash-local-map-threshold"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-094","number":94,"stem":"What risk should be considered when converting a large Pandas API on Spark DataFrame back into a Pandas DataFrame?","options":["A.\nThe conversion automatically distributes the dataset across worker nodes.","B.\nThe operation fails if the resulting Pandas DataFrame contains more than 1000 rows.","C.\nSome records may be lost during the conversion process.","D.\nAll data is collected into the driver’s memory, which may lead to a memory overflow if the dataset is large."],"correct":3,"domain":"Pandas API","topic":"driver-collect","concept":"driver-collect","concept_label":"driver-collect","explanation_ja":"to_pandasは全データをdriverへ集めるため大きいデータではdriver OOMの危険がある。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/pandas_on_spark/best_practices.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nWhen converting a Pandas API on Spark DataFrame (or a PySpark DataFrame) to a Pandas DataFrame, Spark must collect all the distributed data from the cluster to the driver node.\n\nThis happens because Pandas operates only on a single machine, unlike Spark which processes data in a distributed manner across executors.\n\nTypical conversion example:\n\n```\npdf = spark_df.toPandas()\n```\n\nor with Pandas API on Spark:\n\n```\npdf = psdf.to_pandas()\n```\n\nDuring this conversion:\n\n- \nAll partitions from worker nodes are gathered to the driver\n\n- \nThe entire dataset must fit into the driver’s memory\n\nIf the dataset is large, this can cause:\n\n- \nOut-of-memory errors\n\n- \nDriver crashes\n\n- \nSignificant performance issues\n\nTherefore, converting large distributed datasets to Pandas is risky unless the dataset is small enough to fit comfortably in driver memory.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/pandas_on_spark/best_practices.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/pandas_on_spark/best_practices.html)\n\nThe documentation explains that converting to Pandas collects all data to the driver and should only be done for small datasets.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\nThis is incorrect. The conversion does the opposite of distributing data. Instead of distributing the dataset across worker nodes, it collects all partitions into the driver process.\n\nOption B\n\nThere is no fixed row limit such as 1000 rows when converting to Pandas. The limitation is based on available driver memory, not row count.\n\nOption C\n\nSpark does not lose records during conversion. The operation preserves all records; the risk is related to memory usage, not data loss.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark operates on a distributed computing model, where:\n\n- \nData is stored in partitions across worker nodes\n\n- \nComputations run in parallel across executors\n\nPandas, however, uses an in-memory single-machine model.\n\nTherefore, operations such as:\n\n- \n`toPandas()`\n\n- \n`collect()`\n\n- \n`to_pandas()` (Pandas API on Spark)\n\nrequire Spark to transfer all distributed data to the driver, which can become a major bottleneck for large datasets.\n\nA best practice in Spark is to avoid collecting large datasets to the driver and instead perform transformations using distributed Spark operations whenever possible.","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\nThis is incorrect. The conversion does the opposite of distributing data. Instead of distributing the dataset across worker nodes, it collects all partitions into the driver process.","Option B\n\nThere is no fixed row limit such as 1000 rows when converting to Pandas. The limitation is based on available driver memory, not row count.","Option C\n\nSpark does not lose records during conversion. The operation preserves all records; the risk is related to memory usage, not data loss.","Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nWhen converting a Pandas API on Spark DataFrame (or a PySpark DataFrame) to a Pandas DataFrame, Spark must collect all the distributed data from the cluster to the driver node.\n\nThis happens because Pandas operates only on a single machine, unlike Spark which processes data in a distributed manner across executors.\n\nTypical conversion example:\n\n```\npdf = spark_df.toPandas()\n```\n\nor with Pandas API on Spark:\n\n```\npdf = psdf.to_pandas()\n```\n\nDuring this conversion:\n\n- \nAll partitions from worker nodes are gathered to the driver\n\n- \nThe entire dataset must fit into the driver’s memory\n\nIf the dataset is large, this can cause:\n\n- \nOut-of-memory errors\n\n- \nDriver crashes\n\n- \nSignificant performance issues\n\nTherefore, converting large distributed datasets to Pandas is risky unless the dataset is small enough to fit comfortably in driver memory."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/pandas_on_spark/best_practices.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"b9b2f466f0afb6a1738dd7c924d63e200823082a2971e0a7d74c8d64adaa98e2","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"to-pandas-driver-memory-risk","decision_id":"to-pandas-driver-memory-risk"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-095","number":95,"stem":"A developer is building a Spark application that processes a large dataset using SQL queries. Even though the cluster has ample resources, the developer observes that executors remain idle for most of the execution time. Logs show that each stage has only a small number of tasks, which leads to poor utilization of the cluster.\n\nWhich action should the developer take to improve cluster utilization?","options":["A.\nIncrease the value of `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`.","B.\nDecrease the value of `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`.","C.\nIncrease the size of the dataset so that more partitions are created.","D.\nEnable dynamic resource allocation to automatically scale cluster resources."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"shuffle-partition-setting","concept":"shuffle-partition-setting","concept_label":"shuffle-partition-setting","explanation_ja":"shuffle後のtaskが少なすぎるならshuffle partitionsの調整を検討する。AQEが集約する設定にも注意する。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#other-configuration-options","source_explanation":"Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe observation that executors remain idle while each stage has only a small number of tasks indicates that the dataset has too few partitions. In Spark, parallelism is determined by the number of partitions, because each partition corresponds to one task.\n\nWhen the number of partitions is small:\n\n- \nOnly a few tasks are created.\n\n- \nOnly a few executors can run tasks simultaneously.\n\n- \nRemaining executors stay idle, causing poor cluster utilization.\n\nIn Spark SQL workloads, the number of partitions created during shuffle operations (such as joins, aggregations, and groupBy operations) is controlled by:\n\n```\nspark.sql.shuffle.partitions\n```\n\nThe default value is 200.\n\nIf the job currently produces fewer partitions than the available executor cores, increasing this configuration will:\n\n- \nCreate more shuffle partitions\n\n- \nGenerate more tasks per stage\n\n- \nImprove parallelism and cluster utilization\n\nExample configuration:\n\n```\nspark.conf.set(\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\", 500)\n```\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#other-configuration-options](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#other-configuration-options)\n\nThe documentation explains that `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` controls the number of partitions created for shuffle operations, which directly impacts task parallelism.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption B — Decrease `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`\n\nDecreasing the number of shuffle partitions would reduce the number of tasks even further, making the underutilization problem worse.\n\nOption C — Increase dataset size\n\nIncreasing the dataset size is not a practical or logical solution. The problem is related to partitioning and task parallelism, not dataset size.\n\nOption D — Enable dynamic resource allocation\n\nDynamic resource allocation adjusts the number of executors, but the issue here is insufficient tasks per stage, not insufficient executors. Even if more executors are available, they will remain idle if the job produces too few tasks.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark parallelism depends on partitions, not cluster size.\n\nKey rule:\n\n```\n1 partition = 1 task\n```\n\nIf a cluster has:\n\n- \n50 executor cores\n\n- \nbut only 10 partitions\n\nThen only 10 tasks can run concurrently, leaving 40 cores idle.\n\nFor SQL operations involving shuffles (joins, aggregations), the configuration controlling parallelism is:\n\n```\nspark.sql.shuffle.partitions\n```\n\nProper tuning of this parameter is critical for achieving optimal cluster utilization and job performance.","source_option_explanations":["Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe observation that executors remain idle while each stage has only a small number of tasks indicates that the dataset has too few partitions. In Spark, parallelism is determined by the number of partitions, because each partition corresponds to one task.\n\nWhen the number of partitions is small:\n\n- \nOnly a few tasks are created.\n\n- \nOnly a few executors can run tasks simultaneously.\n\n- \nRemaining executors stay idle, causing poor cluster utilization.\n\nIn Spark SQL workloads, the number of partitions created during shuffle operations (such as joins, aggregations, and groupBy operations) is controlled by:\n\n```\nspark.sql.shuffle.partitions\n```\n\nThe default value is 200.\n\nIf the job currently produces fewer partitions than the available executor cores, increasing this configuration will:\n\n- \nCreate more shuffle partitions\n\n- \nGenerate more tasks per stage\n\n- \nImprove parallelism and cluster utilization","Option B — Decrease `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`\n\nDecreasing the number of shuffle partitions would reduce the number of tasks even further, making the underutilization problem worse.","Option C — Increase dataset size\n\nIncreasing the dataset size is not a practical or logical solution. The problem is related to partitioning and task parallelism, not dataset size.","Option D — Enable dynamic resource allocation\n\nDynamic resource allocation adjusts the number of executors, but the issue here is insufficient tasks per stage, not insufficient executors. Even if more executors are available, they will remain idle if the job produces too few tasks."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#other-configuration-options"],"images":[],"source_hash":"eb5d767c97295cadf604451a5b5c9fd49b43325efbaf43b60c6b97e73b63bec4","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"raise-shuffle-partitions-underparallel","decision_id":"raise-shuffle-partitions-underparallel"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-096","number":96,"stem":"A data engineer runs a batch processing job on a Spark cluster configured with the following resources:\n\n• 10 worker nodes\n• 16 CPU cores per worker node\n• 64 GB RAM per node\n\nThe engineer configures the application to run 4 executors on each node, with each executor using 4 CPU cores.\n\nWhat will be the total number of CPU cores utilized by the application?","options":["A.\n160","B.\n64","C.\n80","D.\n40"],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"cpu-total","concept":"cpu-total","concept_label":"cpu-total","explanation_ja":"10ノード×4executor×4cores=160cores。割り当てたcore数と常時稼働task数は同義ではない。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nTo determine the total CPU cores utilized by the Spark application, calculate the total number of executors and multiply by the number of cores per executor.\n\nCluster configuration:\n\n• Worker nodes = 10\n• Executors per node = 4\n\nTotal executors:\n\n```\n10 nodes × 4 executors per node = 40 executors\n```\n\nEach executor uses:\n\n```\n4 CPU cores\n```\n\nTotal CPU cores used by the application:\n\n```\n40 executors × 4 cores per executor = 160 cores\n```\n\nThus, the Spark application will utilize 160 CPU cores across the cluster.\n\nThis aligns with the total physical CPU capacity of the cluster:\n\n```\n10 nodes × 16 cores per node = 160 cores\n```\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html)\n\nSpark’s cluster overview explains that executors run tasks using executor cores, and total compute parallelism is determined by executors × cores per executor.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption B — 64\n\nThis number does not correspond to any calculation derived from the cluster or executor configuration. It might incorrectly assume 16 cores × 4 nodes, which does not match the scenario.\n\nOption C — 80\n\nThis would result from an incorrect assumption such as:\n\n```\n10 nodes × 8 cores\n```\n\nor\n\n```\n20 executors × 4 cores\n```\n\nBut the configuration clearly specifies 4 executors per node, leading to 40 executors total, not 20.\n\nOption D — 40\n\nThis corresponds to the number of executors, not the number of CPU cores. Each executor has 4 cores, so the actual CPU utilization must be multiplied by the number of cores per executor.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nIn Spark cluster resource allocation:\n\n```\nTotal CPU cores used by application =\nNumber of executors × Executor cores\n```\n\nWhere:\n\n- \nExecutors are distributed across worker nodes\n\n- \nExecutor cores determine how many tasks an executor can run concurrently\n\nMaximum parallel tasks at a time:\n\n```\nExecutors × Executor cores\n```\n\nIn this scenario:\n\n```\n40 executors × 4 cores = 160 concurrent tasks\n```\n\nUnderstanding this relationship is essential for capacity planning and performance tuning in Spark clusters.","source_option_explanations":["Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nTo determine the total CPU cores utilized by the Spark application, calculate the total number of executors and multiply by the number of cores per executor.\n\nCluster configuration:\n\n• Worker nodes = 10\n• Executors per node = 4\n\nTotal executors:\n\n```\n10 nodes × 4 executors per node = 40 executors\n```\n\nEach executor uses:\n\n```\n4 CPU cores\n```\n\nTotal CPU cores used by the application:\n\n```\n40 executors × 4 cores per executor = 160 cores\n```\n\nThus, the Spark application will utilize 160 CPU cores across the cluster.\n\nThis aligns with the total physical CPU capacity of the cluster:\n\n```\n10 nodes × 16 cores per node = 160 cores\n```","Option B — 64\n\nThis number does not correspond to any calculation derived from the cluster or executor configuration. It might incorrectly assume 16 cores × 4 nodes, which does not match the scenario.","Option C — 80\n\nThis would result from an incorrect assumption such as:\n\n```\n10 nodes × 8 cores\n```\n\nor\n\n```\n20 executors × 4 cores\n```\n\nBut the configuration clearly specifies 4 executors per node, leading to 40 executors total, not 20.","Option D — 40\n\nThis corresponds to the number of executors, not the number of CPU cores. Each executor has 4 cores, so the actual CPU utilization must be multiplied by the number of cores per executor."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"e08fe70c8bffa5d52d830b99ef22597e9148b114c201006cf034dd2c0b6b4938","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"total-assigned-executor-cores","decision_id":"total-assigned-executor-cores"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-097","number":97,"stem":"A data engineer is developing an Apache Spark Structured Streaming application to process a continuous stream of JSON events in real time. The engineer wants the application to be fault-tolerant, ensuring that if a failure occurs, the stream can resume processing from the last successfully processed record. To support this behavior, the engineer plans to configure checkpointing.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly enables checkpointing for the streaming query?","options":["A.\nquery = streaming_df.writeStream\n.format(\"console\")\n.option(\"checkpoint\", \"/path/to/checkpoint\")\n.outputMode(\"append\")\n.start()","B.\nquery = streaming_df.writeStream\n.format(\"console\")\n.outputMode(\"append\")\n.option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/path/to/checkpoint\")\n.start()","C.\nquery = streaming_df.writeStream\n.format(\"console\")\n.outputMode(\"complete\")\n.start()","D.\nquery = streaming_df.writeStream\n.format(\"console\")\n.outputMode(\"append\")\n.start()"],"correct":1,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"stream-checkpoint","concept":"stream-checkpoint","concept_label":"stream-checkpoint","explanation_ja":"writeStreamのcheckpointLocationでoffsetや状態の回復情報を保存する。console sink自体は出力のexactly-onceを保証しない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#checkpointing","source_explanation":"Correct answer: B\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Spark Structured Streaming, checkpointing is required to achieve fault tolerance. Checkpoints store metadata such as:\n\n- \nStreaming progress (offsets)\n\n- \nState information for stateful operations\n\n- \nExecution metadata\n\nIf the streaming application fails or restarts, Spark uses the checkpoint data to resume processing from the last successfully processed record.\n\nThe correct configuration uses the `checkpointLocation` option.\n\n```\nquery = streaming_df.writeStream \\\n.format(\"console\") \\\n.outputMode(\"append\") \\\n.option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/path/to/checkpoint\") \\\n.start()\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- \n`writeStream` defines the streaming output\n\n- \n`checkpointLocation` specifies where Spark stores checkpoint metadata\n\n- \nThis ensures exactly-once processing guarantees and recovery capability\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#checkpointing](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#checkpointing)\n\nThe documentation explains that Structured Streaming uses checkpoint directories to store progress information and recover from failures.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\n```\n.option(\"checkpoint\", \"/path/to/checkpoint\")\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because `checkpoint` is not a valid configuration option in Spark Structured Streaming. The correct option name is `checkpointLocation`.\n\nOption C\n\n```\n.outputMode(\"complete\")\n```\n\nAlthough this is a valid output mode for some streaming queries, it does not enable checkpointing. Without checkpointing, the stream cannot resume correctly after a failure.\n\nOption D\n\nThis code simply starts the streaming query without configuring any checkpoint directory. Without checkpointing, the query cannot recover state or offsets after a failure, which violates the requirement for fault tolerance.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nStructured Streaming achieves fault tolerance through checkpointing.\n\nCheckpoint directories store:\n\n- \nSource offsets (where the stream last read data)\n\n- \nState store information for aggregations\n\n- \nMetadata for query recovery\n\nTypical checkpoint usage pattern:\n\n```\n.writeStream \\\n.option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/checkpoint/path\")\n```\n\nIf the application restarts, Spark will:\n\n- \nRead the checkpoint metadata\n\n- \nRestore the query state\n\n- \nContinue processing from the last committed offset\n\nThis mechanism enables reliable stream processing in production environments.","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\n```\n.option(\"checkpoint\", \"/path/to/checkpoint\")\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because `checkpoint` is not a valid configuration option in Spark Structured Streaming. The correct option name is `checkpointLocation`.","Correct answer: B\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Spark Structured Streaming, checkpointing is required to achieve fault tolerance. Checkpoints store metadata such as:\n\n- \nStreaming progress (offsets)\n\n- \nState information for stateful operations\n\n- \nExecution metadata\n\nIf the streaming application fails or restarts, Spark uses the checkpoint data to resume processing from the last successfully processed record.\n\nThe correct configuration uses the `checkpointLocation` option.\n\n```\nquery = streaming_df.writeStream \\\n.format(\"console\") \\\n.outputMode(\"append\") \\\n.option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/path/to/checkpoint\") \\\n.start()\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- \n`writeStream` defines the streaming output\n\n- \n`checkpointLocation` specifies where Spark stores checkpoint metadata\n\n- \nThis ensures exactly-once processing guarantees and recovery capability","Option C\n\n```\n.outputMode(\"complete\")\n```\n\nAlthough this is a valid output mode for some streaming queries, it does not enable checkpointing. Without checkpointing, the stream cannot resume correctly after a failure.","Option D\n\nThis code simply starts the streaming query without configuring any checkpoint directory. Without checkpointing, the query cannot recover state or offsets after a failure, which violates the requirement for fault tolerance."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#checkpointing"],"images":[],"source_hash":"43be22f6e93d5a15029fbab2b4fd1701e9bd720a631050d813f0025c90795ec5","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"stream-checkpoint-recovery","decision_id":"stream-checkpoint-location"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-098","number":98,"stem":"What happens when a negative value is passed to the `days` parameter in the Spark SQL function `date_sub(start, days)`?","options":["A.\nThe original start date is returned without any modification.","B.\nAn error is raised indicating that the parameter value is invalid.","C.\nThe specified number of days is added to the start date.","D.\nThe specified number of days is subtracted from the start date."],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"date-arithmetic","concept":"date-arithmetic","concept_label":"date-arithmetic","explanation_ja":"date_subに負数を渡すと減算の符号が反転して日付が進む。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/sql/index.html#date_sub","source_explanation":"Correct answer: C\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe Spark SQL function `date_sub(start, days)` subtracts a specified number of days from a date. However, if the value provided for `days` is negative, the subtraction of a negative number mathematically becomes addition.\n\nTherefore:\n\n```\ndate_sub(start, -n)  =  start + n days\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```\nSELECT date_sub('2024-01-10', -5);\n```\n\nResult:\n\n```\n2024-01-15\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- \nNormally `date_sub(date, 5)` → subtracts 5 days\n\n- \nBut `date_sub(date, -5)` → subtracting -5 means adding 5 days\n\nSpark follows standard arithmetic behavior when handling the `days` parameter.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/sql/index.html#date_sub](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/sql/index.html#date_sub)\n\nThe documentation states that `date_sub(start, days)` returns the date obtained by subtracting the specified number of days from the start date.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\nReturning the original start date would imply Spark ignores the parameter value. Spark does not ignore the argument; it evaluates it mathematically.\n\nOption B\n\nSpark does not restrict the `days` parameter to positive numbers. Negative values are valid and are interpreted mathematically, so no error occurs.\n\nOption D\n\nThis describes the behavior when `days` is positive. The question specifically asks what happens when a negative value is provided.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark SQL date arithmetic functions follow standard numeric semantics.\n\nKey rules:\n\n- \n`date_add(date, n)` → adds n days\n\n- \n`date_sub(date, n)` → subtracts n days\n\n- \nIf n is negative, the operation reverses mathematically.\n\nExamples:\n\n```\ndate_sub('2024-01-10', 5)   → 2024-01-05\ndate_sub('2024-01-10', -5)  → 2024-01-15\ndate_add('2024-01-10', -5)  → 2024-01-05\n```\n\nUnderstanding this behavior is important when building date-based filters, time windows, and scheduling logic in Spark SQL queries.","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\nReturning the original start date would imply Spark ignores the parameter value. Spark does not ignore the argument; it evaluates it mathematically.","Option B\n\nSpark does not restrict the `days` parameter to positive numbers. Negative values are valid and are interpreted mathematically, so no error occurs.","Correct answer: C\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe Spark SQL function `date_sub(start, days)` subtracts a specified number of days from a date. However, if the value provided for `days` is negative, the subtraction of a negative number mathematically becomes addition.\n\nTherefore:\n\n```\ndate_sub(start, -n)  =  start + n days\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```\nSELECT date_sub('2024-01-10', -5);\n```\n\nResult:\n\n```\n2024-01-15\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- \nNormally `date_sub(date, 5)` → subtracts 5 days\n\n- \nBut `date_sub(date, -5)` → subtracting -5 means adding 5 days\n\nSpark follows standard arithmetic behavior when handling the `days` parameter.","Option D\n\nThis describes the behavior when `days` is positive. The question specifically asks what happens when a negative value is provided."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/sql/index.html#date_sub"],"images":[],"source_hash":"4d8ae17453ee16c2f4043dad0d5e1f18e17486117d56d1fc40a6acce63b797bf","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"date-sub-negative-days","decision_id":"date-sub-negative-days"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-099","number":99,"stem":"A data engineer must generate a Parquet table that is overwritten daily with the latest dataset. A downstream system has a strict requirement that the write must use one output partition and sort its rows by `market_time`. A later table query still needs ORDER BY to guarantee result order.\n\nWhich Spark code snippet ensures that the Parquet table is written with records sorted by `market_time` while overwriting the table each day?","options":["A.\nfinal_df\n.sort(\"market_time\")\n.write\n.format(\"parquet\")\n.mode(\"overwrite\")\n.saveAsTable(\"output.market_events\")","B.\nfinal_df\n.orderBy(\"market_time\")\n.write\n.format(\"parquet\")\n.mode(\"overwrite\")\n.saveAsTable(\"output.market_events\")","C.\nfinal_df\n.coalesce(1)\n.sort(\"market_time\")\n.write\n.format(\"parquet\")\n.mode(\"overwrite\")\n.saveAsTable(\"output.market_events\")","D.\nfinal_df\n.sortWithinPartitions(\"market_time\")\n.write\n.format(\"parquet\")\n.mode(\"overwrite\")\n.saveAsTable(\"output.market_events\")"],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"write-global-sort","concept":"write-global-sort","concept_label":"write-global-sort","explanation_ja":"単一出力partitionにまとめた後でsortする。tableの読取結果の順番は別途ORDER BYが必要。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.sort.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.sort.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-orderby.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.sortWithinPartitions.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"94faf487f63c51d0ac6cb916717ed5e75db3e5276fb067e627e8141d7d46103e","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"保存したtableの後続SELECTの行順は保証されない。単一出力パーティション内の並びに要件を限定し、coalesce後にsortする。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.sort.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-orderby.html"],"original_stem":"A data engineer must generate a Parquet table that is overwritten daily with the latest dataset. A downstream system has a strict requirement that all records in the table must be globally sorted by the `market_time` column.\n\nWhich Spark code snippet ensures that the Parquet table is written with records sorted by `market_time` while overwriting the table each day?","original_options":["A.\nfinal_df\n.sort(\"market_time\")\n.write\n.format(\"parquet\")\n.mode(\"overwrite\")\n.saveAsTable(\"output.market_events\")","B.\nfinal_df\n.orderBy(\"market_time\")\n.write\n.format(\"parquet\")\n.mode(\"overwrite\")\n.saveAsTable(\"output.market_events\")","C.\nfinal_df\n.sort(\"market_time\")\n.coalesce(1)\n.write\n.format(\"parquet\")\n.mode(\"overwrite\")\n.saveAsTable(\"output.market_events\")","D.\nfinal_df\n.sortWithinPartitions(\"market_time\")\n.write\n.format(\"parquet\")\n.mode(\"overwrite\")\n.saveAsTable(\"output.market_events\")"],"original_correct":["C"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"single-output-partition-sorted-write","decision_id":"single-output-partition-sorted-write"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-100","number":100,"stem":"An engineer needs to join two DataFrames, df1 and df2, using the columns employee_id and emp_id, respectively.\n\n```\n• df1 schema: employee_id INT, name STRING\n• df2 schema: emp_id INT, department STRING\n```\n\nThe engineer writes the following code to perform the join:\n\n`result = df1.join(df2, df1.employee_id == df2.emp_id, how=\"inner\")`\n\nWhat will be the behavior of this code?","options":["A.\nThe code fails because the column names employee_id and emp_id do not automatically match.","B.\nThe code fails because the join must explicitly specify `on='employee_id'`.","C.\nThe code fails because PySpark cannot join DataFrames that have different schemas.","D.\nThe code executes successfully because the join condition explicitly compares employee_id from df1 with emp_id from df2."],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"join-condition","concept":"join-condition","concept_label":"join-condition","explanation_ja":"異なる列名でも等値条件を明示したjoinは成立する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nSpark DataFrame joins allow developers to specify explicit join conditions using column expressions. When the join condition is explicitly defined, Spark does not require the column names to be identical.\n\nIn the provided code:\n\n```\nresult = df1.join(df2, df1.employee_id == df2.emp_id, how=\"inner\")\n```\n\nThe join condition explicitly compares:\n\n```\ndf1.employee_id == df2.emp_id\n```\n\nSpark evaluates this expression and performs an inner join where the values of the two columns match. Because the comparison is defined explicitly, Spark does not rely on matching column names.\n\nTherefore, the join executes successfully even though the columns have different names.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html)\n\nThe documentation explains that the `join()` function accepts a Column expression as the join condition, allowing joins between columns with different names.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\nSpark does not require column names to match when an explicit join condition is provided. Automatic matching is only relevant when using shorthand syntax such as:\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, \"column_name\")\n```\n\nOption B\n\nThe `on=\"employee_id\"` syntax is used only when both DataFrames contain a column with the same name. In this scenario the columns are named differently (`employee_id` vs `emp_id`), so the engineer correctly used a boolean join condition instead.\n\nOption C\n\nSpark can join DataFrames with different schemas. The only requirement is that the join condition references compatible column types. In this case, both columns are integers, so the join works correctly.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark supports two common join styles:\n\n1. Join using a column name (same column names)\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, \"employee_id\")\n```\n\nSpark automatically matches columns with the same name.\n\n2. Join using a column expression (different column names)\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, df1.employee_id == df2.emp_id)\n```\n\nThis approach allows joins when:\n\n- \nColumn names differ\n\n- \nComplex join conditions are required\n\n- \nMultiple conditions are involved\n\nUnderstanding these join patterns is essential for building flexible Spark DataFrame pipelines.","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\nSpark does not require column names to match when an explicit join condition is provided. Automatic matching is only relevant when using shorthand syntax such as:\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, \"column_name\")\n```","Option B\n\nThe `on=\"employee_id\"` syntax is used only when both DataFrames contain a column with the same name. In this scenario the columns are named differently (`employee_id` vs `emp_id`), so the engineer correctly used a boolean join condition instead.","Option C\n\nSpark can join DataFrames with different schemas. The only requirement is that the join condition references compatible column types. In this case, both columns are integers, so the join works correctly.","Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nSpark DataFrame joins allow developers to specify explicit join conditions using column expressions. When the join condition is explicitly defined, Spark does not require the column names to be identical.\n\nIn the provided code:\n\n```\nresult = df1.join(df2, df1.employee_id == df2.emp_id, how=\"inner\")\n```\n\nThe join condition explicitly compares:\n\n```\ndf1.employee_id == df2.emp_id\n```\n\nSpark evaluates this expression and performs an inner join where the values of the two columns match. Because the comparison is defined explicitly, Spark does not rely on matching column names.\n\nTherefore, the join executes successfully even though the columns have different names."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"c29e717809154ede08090e3c923c64ed66693dfbce12cef3500cbc9ea639d26f","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"join-different-column-names-condition","decision_id":"join-different-column-names-condition"}]},{"id":"EX4-S6","title":"原文セット 6","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-101","number":101,"stem":"A DataFrame df contains the columns name, age, and salary. A developer needs to sort the DataFrame by age in ascending order and salary in descending order.\n\nWhich code snippet satisfies this requirement?","options":["A.\ndf.orderBy(col(\"age\").asc(), col(\"salary\").asc()).show()","B.\ndf.sort(\"age\", \"salary\", ascending=[True, True]).show()","C.\ndf.sort(\"age\", \"salary\", ascending=[False, True]).show()","D.\ndf.orderBy(\"age\", \"salary\", ascending=[True, False]).show()"],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"sort-mixed","concept":"sort-mixed","concept_label":"sort-mixed","explanation_ja":"ascendingのリストを列順に対応させる。age昇順・salary降順はTrue,False。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe requirement is:\n\n- \nSort by `age` in ascending order\n\n- \nSort by `salary` in descending order\n\nSpark allows sorting by multiple columns using `orderBy()` (or `sort()`) and specifying the ordering for each column using the `ascending` parameter.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\ndf.orderBy(\"age\", \"salary\", ascending=[True, False]).show()\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- \n`\"age\"` → sorted ascending (`True`)\n\n- \n`\"salary\"` → sorted descending (`False`)\n\nSpark applies the sorting sequentially:\n\n- \nRecords are first sorted by age (ascending).\n\n- \nFor rows with the same age, Spark sorts by salary (descending).\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html)\n\nThe documentation confirms that `orderBy()` supports sorting by multiple columns with a list specifying ascending or descending order.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\n```\ndf.orderBy(col(\"age\").asc(), col(\"salary\").asc())\n```\n\nBoth columns are sorted ascending, which violates the requirement that salary must be descending.\n\nOption B\n\n```\ndf.sort(\"age\", \"salary\", ascending=[True, True])\n```\n\nThis also sorts both columns ascending, which again does not satisfy the requirement.\n\nOption C\n\n```\ndf.sort(\"age\", \"salary\", ascending=[False, True])\n```\n\nThis sorts:\n\n- \n`age` → descending\n\n- \n`salary` → ascending\n\nThis is the reverse of the required ordering.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark supports multi-column sorting with flexible ordering.\n\nTwo common syntaxes:\n\nUsing ascending list\n\n```\ndf.orderBy(\"col1\", \"col2\", ascending=[True, False])\n```\n\nUsing column expressions\n\n```\ndf.orderBy(col(\"col1\").asc(), col(\"col2\").desc())\n```\n\nBoth approaches allow precise control over sorting behavior across multiple columns in distributed Spark DataFrames.","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\n```\ndf.orderBy(col(\"age\").asc(), col(\"salary\").asc())\n```\n\nBoth columns are sorted ascending, which violates the requirement that salary must be descending.","Option B\n\n```\ndf.sort(\"age\", \"salary\", ascending=[True, True])\n```\n\nThis also sorts both columns ascending, which again does not satisfy the requirement.","Option C\n\n```\ndf.sort(\"age\", \"salary\", ascending=[False, True])\n```\n\nThis sorts:\n\n- \n`age` → descending\n\n- \n`salary` → ascending","Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe requirement is:\n\n- \nSort by `age` in ascending order\n\n- \nSort by `salary` in descending order\n\nSpark allows sorting by multiple columns using `orderBy()` (or `sort()`) and specifying the ordering for each column using the `ascending` parameter.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\ndf.orderBy(\"age\", \"salary\", ascending=[True, False]).show()\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- \n`\"age\"` → sorted ascending (`True`)\n\n- \n`\"salary\"` → sorted descending (`False`)\n\nSpark applies the sorting sequentially:\n\n- \nRecords are first sorted by age (ascending).\n\n- \nFor rows with the same age, Spark sorts by salary (descending)."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"caf07041ebdf8879f4ab15f78aa80ac5a104db5fb42f70dddc27d79b3f59b060","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"sort-direction","decision_id":"mixed-column-sort-directions"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-102","number":102,"stem":"Consider the following Spark code:\n\n`spark.sparkContext.setLogLevel(\"<log_level>\")`\n\nWhich set of values represents valid configuration options for the Spark driver log level?","options":["A.\nALL, DEBUG, FAIL, INFO","B.\nERROR, WARN, TRACE, OFF","C.\nWARN, NONE, ERROR, FATAL","D.\nFATAL, NONE, INFO, DEBUG"],"correct":1,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"log-level","concept":"log-level","concept_label":"log-level","explanation_ja":"ERROR/WARN/TRACE/OFFはいずれも有効なログレベル。NONEやFAILは指定値ではない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/api/pyspark.SparkContext.setLogLevel.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: B\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe method:\n\n```\nspark.sparkContext.setLogLevel(\"<log_level>\")\n```\n\ncontrols the logging level for the Spark driver and executors. Spark uses log4j logging levels, and the valid levels supported by this API are:\n\n```\nALL\nTRACE\nDEBUG\nINFO\nWARN\nERROR\nFATAL\nOFF\n```\n\nOption B contains only valid Spark logging levels:\n\n```\nERROR\nWARN\nTRACE\nOFF\n```\n\nEach level controls how verbose the logs are:\n\n- \nTRACE – most detailed logging\n\n- \nDEBUG – debugging information\n\n- \nINFO – general runtime information\n\n- \nWARN – potential issues\n\n- \nERROR – serious failures\n\n- \nFATAL – critical failures\n\n- \nOFF – disables logging\n\nExample usage:\n\n```\nspark.sparkContext.setLogLevel(\"ERROR\")\n```\n\nThis suppresses most informational logs and shows only errors.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/api/pyspark.SparkContext.setLogLevel.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/api/pyspark.SparkContext.setLogLevel.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\n```\nALL, DEBUG, FAIL, INFO\n```\n\n`FAIL` is not a valid Spark log level. The correct level is FATAL, not FAIL.\n\nOption C\n\n```\nWARN, NONE, ERROR, FATAL\n```\n\n`NONE` is not a valid log level in Spark. The correct option for disabling logs is OFF.\n\nOption D\n\n```\nFATAL, NONE, INFO, DEBUG\n```\n\nAgain, `NONE` is not valid. Spark uses OFF instead.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark logging is controlled through Log4j, which defines hierarchical logging levels.\n\nCommon production practice:\n\n```\nspark.sparkContext.setLogLevel(\"WARN\")\n```\n\nThis reduces noisy logs from INFO and DEBUG messages while still showing warnings and errors.\n\nProper log level configuration is important for:\n\n- \nDebugging Spark jobs\n\n- \nReducing excessive log output\n\n- \nImproving observability in production pipelines.","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\n```\nALL, DEBUG, FAIL, INFO\n```\n\n`FAIL` is not a valid Spark log level. The correct level is FATAL, not FAIL.","Correct answer: B\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe method:\n\n```\nspark.sparkContext.setLogLevel(\"<log_level>\")\n```\n\ncontrols the logging level for the Spark driver and executors. Spark uses log4j logging levels, and the valid levels supported by this API are:\n\n```\nALL\nTRACE\nDEBUG\nINFO\nWARN\nERROR\nFATAL\nOFF\n```\n\nOption B contains only valid Spark logging levels:\n\n```\nERROR\nWARN\nTRACE\nOFF\n```\n\nEach level controls how verbose the logs are:\n\n- \nTRACE – most detailed logging\n\n- \nDEBUG – debugging information\n\n- \nINFO – general runtime information\n\n- \nWARN – potential issues\n\n- \nERROR – serious failures\n\n- \nFATAL – critical failures\n\n- \nOFF – disables logging\n\nExample usage:\n\n```\nspark.sparkContext.setLogLevel(\"ERROR\")\n```\n\nThis suppresses most informational logs and shows only errors.","Option C\n\n```\nWARN, NONE, ERROR, FATAL\n```\n\n`NONE` is not a valid log level in Spark. The correct option for disabling logs is OFF.","Option D\n\n```\nFATAL, NONE, INFO, DEBUG\n```\n\nAgain, `NONE` is not valid. Spark uses OFF instead."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/api/pyspark.SparkContext.setLogLevel.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"e1a2431cbca11292473c2d205d58510f3f9bbac4e14941028456aa5c6db17e55","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"valid-driver-log-level-values","decision_id":"valid-driver-log-level-values"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-103","number":103,"stem":"A data scientist is working with a large dataset and has written a PySpark script that performs several transformations and actions on a DataFrame. The script ends with a collect() action to retrieve the results to the driver.\n\nHow does Apache Spark’s execution hierarchy process these operations when the script is executed?","options":["A.\nThe script is first divided into multiple applications, and each application is then divided into jobs, stages, and finally tasks.","B.\nThe entire script is executed as a single job, which is divided into multiple stages, and each stage is further divided into tasks according to data partitions.","C.\nThe collect() action triggers a job, which is split into stages at shuffle boundaries, and each stage is further divided into tasks that operate on individual data partitions.","D.\nSpark creates one task for every transformation and action, and these tasks are grouped into stages and jobs based on their dependencies."],"correct":2,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"execution-hierarchy","concept":"execution-hierarchy","concept_label":"execution-hierarchy","explanation_ja":"collect actionが計算を要求し、shuffle境界でstage、各partitionにtaskを割り当てる。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#overview","source_explanation":"Correct answer: C\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nSpark follows a well-defined execution hierarchy:\n\n```\nApplication → Job → Stage → Task\n```\n\nIn this scenario, the script ends with the action:\n\n```\ndf.collect()\n```\n\nWhen an action such as `collect()` is executed:\n\n- \nSpark triggers a job.\n\n- \nThe job is divided into stages based on shuffle boundaries (wide transformations such as `groupBy`, `join`, `reduceByKey`).\n\n- \nEach stage is further divided into tasks, where:\n\n- \nOne task processes one partition of data.\n\nExecution flow:\n\n```\ncollect() action\n      ↓\nSpark creates a Job\n      ↓\nJob is divided into Stages (based on shuffle boundaries)\n      ↓\nEach Stage contains multiple Tasks\n      ↓\nEach Task processes one partition\n```\n\nExample:\n\nIf a DataFrame has 200 partitions, then a stage may contain 200 tasks, allowing parallel execution across the cluster.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#overview](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#overview)\n\nSpark documentation explains that actions trigger jobs, which are broken into stages and tasks for distributed execution.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\nSpark does not divide a script into multiple applications. An application corresponds to a single Spark driver program. The script itself represents one application.\n\nOption B\n\nA Spark script is not always executed as a single job. Each action triggers a separate job, so if the script contains multiple actions, multiple jobs will be created.\n\nOption D\n\nSpark does not create one task per transformation. Tasks correspond to data partitions, not transformations or actions.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark's execution model is hierarchical:\n\n```\nApplication\n   ↓\nJob (triggered by actions)\n   ↓\nStage (split at shuffle boundaries)\n   ↓\nTask (one per partition)\n```\n\nKey rules:\n\n- \nActions trigger jobs\n\n- \nShuffle operations create stage boundaries\n\n- \nTasks process partitions\n\nExample parallelism:\n\n```\n200 partitions → 200 tasks per stage\n```\n\nUnderstanding this hierarchy is essential for:\n\n- \nDebugging Spark jobs\n\n- \nOptimizing performance\n\n- \nInterpreting Spark UI metrics","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\nSpark does not divide a script into multiple applications. An application corresponds to a single Spark driver program. The script itself represents one application.","Option B\n\nA Spark script is not always executed as a single job. Each action triggers a separate job, so if the script contains multiple actions, multiple jobs will be created.","Correct answer: C\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nSpark follows a well-defined execution hierarchy:\n\n```\nApplication → Job → Stage → Task\n```\n\nIn this scenario, the script ends with the action:\n\n```\ndf.collect()\n```\n\nWhen an action such as `collect()` is executed:\n\n- \nSpark triggers a job.\n\n- \nThe job is divided into stages based on shuffle boundaries (wide transformations such as `groupBy`, `join`, `reduceByKey`).\n\n- \nEach stage is further divided into tasks, where:\n\n- \nOne task processes one partition of data.\n\nExecution flow:\n\n```\ncollect() action\n      ↓\nSpark creates a Job\n      ↓\nJob is divided into Stages (based on shuffle boundaries)\n      ↓\nEach Stage contains multiple Tasks\n      ↓\nEach Task processes one partition\n```","Option D\n\nSpark does not create one task per transformation. Tasks correspond to data partitions, not transformations or actions."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#overview"],"images":[],"source_hash":"783864cae3942cd7961104b093f0a853b6f7e0dacfd7ced164409bbe16eefc43","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"job-stage-task-hierarchy","decision_id":"action-job-stage-partition-task"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-104","number":104,"stem":"A developer is analyzing user behavior data originating from a web application and already loaded into a Spark DataFrame df. The developer wants to execute a groupBy operation in parallel across all Apache Spark 3.5 workers.\n\nWhich approach passes each complete user group, with its key, to a custom Python function operating on a pandas DataFrame, returning a DataFrame with the supplied schema?","options":["A.\nUse the applyInPandas API:\n\n```\nimport pandas as pd\n \ndef mean_func(key, pdf):\n    return pd.DataFrame([(key + (pdf['value'].mean(),))])\n \ndf.groupby('user_id').applyInPandas(mean_func, schema=\"user_id long, value double\").show()\n```","B.\nUse the mapInPandas API:\n\n```\ndef mean_func(pdf_iter):\n    for pdf in pdf_iter:\n        yield pdf.groupby('user_id').agg({'value': 'mean'}).reset_index()\n \ndf.mapInPandas(mean_func, schema=\"user_id long, value double\").show()\n```","C.\nUse a standard Spark UDF (User-Defined Function):\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import mean\ndf.groupBy('user_id').agg(mean('value')).show()\n```","D.\nDefine a Pandas UDF:\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import pandas_udf\n \n@pandas_udf(\"double\")\ndef mean_func(value: pd.Series) -> float:\n    return value.mean()\n \ndf.groupby(\"user_id\").agg(mean_func(df['value'])).show()\n```"],"correct":0,"domain":"Pandas API","topic":"grouped-apply-pandas","concept":"grouped-apply-pandas","concept_label":"grouped-apply-pandas","explanation_ja":"Sparkのgroupごとにpandas DataFrameを関数へ渡すのはapplyInPandas。単なるpartition単位のmapとは異なる。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.GroupedData.applyInPandas.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.GroupedData.applyInPandas.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.mapInPandas.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.GroupedData.applyInPandas.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-pyspark-pandas-with-arrow.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"a3d215aa27c4ea95157037d099f6266cd1a773fb981a71f9e96dbe27d612e13c","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"入力はSpark DataFrame。group全体をpandasで処理する要件を明記しbuilt-in集計との曖昧さを除く。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.GroupedData.applyInPandas.html"],"original_stem":"A developer is analyzing user behavior data stored in a pandas DataFrame that originates from a web application. The developer wants to execute a groupBy operation in parallel across all Apache Spark 3.5 workers.\n\nWhich approach should be used to achieve this distributed group-by processing?","original_options":["A.\nUse the applyInPandas API:\n\n```\nimport pandas as pd\n \ndef mean_func(key, pdf):\n    return pd.DataFrame([(key + (pdf['value'].mean(),))])\n \ndf.groupby('user_id').applyInPandas(mean_func, schema=\"user_id long, value double\").show()\n```","B.\nUse the mapInPandas API:\n\n```\ndef mean_func(pdf_iter):\n    for pdf in pdf_iter:\n        yield pdf.groupby('user_id').agg({'value': 'mean'}).reset_index()\n \ndf.mapInPandas(mean_func, schema=\"user_id long, value double\").show()\n```","C.\nUse a standard Spark UDF (User-Defined Function):\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import mean\ndf.groupBy('user_id').agg(mean('value')).show()\n```","D.\nDefine a Pandas UDF:\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import pandas_udf\n \n@pandas_udf(\"double\")\ndef mean_func(value: pd.Series) -> float:\n    return value.mean()\n \ndf.groupby(\"user_id\").agg(mean_func(df['value'])).show()\n```"],"original_correct":["A"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"grouped-apply-in-pandas-full-group","decision_id":"grouped-apply-in-pandas-full-group"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-105","number":105,"stem":"Given a DataFrame df that currently has 10 partitions, consider the following code:\n\n`result = df.coalesce(20)`\n\nAfter executing this statement, how many partitions will the resulting DataFrame contain?","options":["A. 10","B. The number of partitions will match the number of cluster executors.","C. 1","D. 20"],"correct":0,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"coalesce-increase","concept":"coalesce-increase","concept_label":"coalesce-increase","explanation_ja":"coalesceはpartition数を増やさないので既存10のまま。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe `coalesce()` transformation in Spark is used to reduce the number of partitions without performing a full shuffle. It is designed to decrease partitions efficiently, not increase them.\n\nWhen `coalesce(n)` is called with a value greater than the current number of partitions, Spark does not increase the number of partitions. Instead, the DataFrame keeps the existing partition count.\n\nGiven:\n\n```\nCurrent partitions = 10\nRequested partitions = 20\n```\n\nCode:\n\n```\nresult = df.coalesce(20)\n```\n\nBecause `coalesce()` cannot increase partitions, the resulting DataFrame will still contain:\n\n```\n10 partitions\n```\n\nTo increase partitions, Spark requires a shuffle operation, which is done using `repartition()`.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.repartition(20)\n```\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html)\n\nThe documentation explains that `coalesce()` reduces the number of partitions and avoids a full shuffle when possible.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption B\n\nThe number of partitions in a DataFrame is not automatically tied to the number of executors. Spark scheduling uses partitions to create tasks, but partition count is determined by transformations such as `repartition()`, `coalesce()`, or shuffle operations.\n\nOption C\n\n`coalesce(1)` would produce one partition, but the code explicitly requests `coalesce(20)`, so this option is unrelated to the scenario.\n\nOption D\n\nThis would only occur if `repartition(20)` were used. `repartition()` performs a shuffle and can increase or decrease partitions, whereas `coalesce()` cannot increase them.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark provides two primary APIs for partition control:\n\nCoalesce (narrow transformation)\n\n```\ndf.coalesce(n)\n```\n\n- \nEfficient for reducing partitions\n\n- \nAvoids shuffle\n\n- \nCannot increase partitions\n\nRepartition (wide transformation)\n\n```\ndf.repartition(n)\n```\n\n- \nCan increase or decrease partitions\n\n- \nRequires shuffle\n\n- \nUsed when higher parallelism is needed\n\nUnderstanding the difference between coalesce vs repartition is essential for tuning Spark job performance and resource utilization.","source_option_explanations":["Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe `coalesce()` transformation in Spark is used to reduce the number of partitions without performing a full shuffle. It is designed to decrease partitions efficiently, not increase them.\n\nWhen `coalesce(n)` is called with a value greater than the current number of partitions, Spark does not increase the number of partitions. Instead, the DataFrame keeps the existing partition count.\n\nGiven:\n\n```\nCurrent partitions = 10\nRequested partitions = 20\n```\n\nCode:\n\n```\nresult = df.coalesce(20)\n```\n\nBecause `coalesce()` cannot increase partitions, the resulting DataFrame will still contain:\n\n```\n10 partitions\n```\n\nTo increase partitions, Spark requires a shuffle operation, which is done using `repartition()`.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.repartition(20)\n```","Option B\n\nThe number of partitions in a DataFrame is not automatically tied to the number of executors. Spark scheduling uses partitions to create tasks, but partition count is determined by transformations such as `repartition()`, `coalesce()`, or shuffle operations.","Option C\n\n`coalesce(1)` would produce one partition, but the code explicitly requests `coalesce(20)`, so this option is unrelated to the scenario.","Option D\n\nThis would only occur if `repartition(20)` were used. `repartition()` performs a shuffle and can increase or decrease partitions, whereas `coalesce()` cannot increase them."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"709f4224586c3600047817e72c31096a4e57408ff51debaa46711aa785e2d355","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"coalesce-repartition","decision_id":"coalesce-repartition"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-106","number":106,"stem":"A data analyst develops a Spark application to analyze financial data and performs the following transformations on a DataFrame: filter, select, groupBy, and coalesce.\n\nWhich of these operations causes a shuffle during execution?","options":["A.\ngroupBy","B.\nfilter","C.\nselect","D.\ncoalesce"],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"execution-shuffle","concept":"execution-shuffle","concept_label":"execution-shuffle","explanation_ja":"groupByの集計は通常shuffleする。select/filter/coalesceは同じ意味の再分配ではない。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#shuffle-operations","source_explanation":"Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Spark, a shuffle occurs when data must be redistributed across partitions so that records with the same key are processed together. The `groupBy` operation requires Spark to reorganize data by the grouping key, which typically means moving records between partitions.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.groupBy(\"account_id\").agg({\"amount\": \"sum\"})\n```\n\nIf records with the same `account_id` exist across multiple partitions, Spark must shuffle the data so that all rows with the same key end up in the same partition before performing the aggregation.\n\nBecause this involves network data movement between executors, Spark introduces a shuffle stage boundary.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#shuffle-operations](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#shuffle-operations)\n\nThe Spark documentation explains that operations such as groupByKey, reduceByKey, and aggregations require a shuffle because data must be redistributed based on keys.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption B — `filter`\n\n`filter` is a narrow transformation. Each partition filters its own records independently without moving data between nodes, so no shuffle occurs.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html)\n\nOption C — `select`\n\n`select` only projects specific columns from each partition. It does not require data redistribution, so it also remains a narrow transformation with no shuffle.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.select.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.select.html)\n\nOption D — `coalesce`\n\n`coalesce` reduces the number of partitions using a narrow dependency, typically avoiding a shuffle. Spark simply merges existing partitions where possible.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark transformations are categorized into two types:\n\nNarrow transformations\n\n- \nOperate within a single partition\n\n- \nNo data movement between nodes\n\n- \nExamples: `filter`, `select`, `map`, `coalesce`\n\nWide transformations\n\n- \nRequire data redistribution across partitions\n\n- \nTrigger a shuffle\n\n- \nExamples: `groupBy`, `join`, `distinct`, `repartition`\n\nShuffle operations are expensive because they involve disk I/O, network transfer, and serialization, making them one of the most important performance considerations in Spark workloads.","source_option_explanations":["Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Spark, a shuffle occurs when data must be redistributed across partitions so that records with the same key are processed together. The `groupBy` operation requires Spark to reorganize data by the grouping key, which typically means moving records between partitions.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.groupBy(\"account_id\").agg({\"amount\": \"sum\"})\n```\n\nIf records with the same `account_id` exist across multiple partitions, Spark must shuffle the data so that all rows with the same key end up in the same partition before performing the aggregation.\n\nBecause this involves network data movement between executors, Spark introduces a shuffle stage boundary.","Option B — `filter`\n\n`filter` is a narrow transformation. Each partition filters its own records independently without moving data between nodes, so no shuffle occurs.","Option C — `select`\n\n`select` only projects specific columns from each partition. It does not require data redistribution, so it also remains a narrow transformation with no shuffle.","Option D — `coalesce`\n\n`coalesce` reduces the number of partitions using a narrow dependency, typically avoiding a shuffle. Spark simply merges existing partitions where possible."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#shuffle-operations","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.select.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"07b29fc4c365f7c15e4fbc3ad8950573d1f500a2b63dfd7a381981c0d2b20915","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"narrow-wide-shuffle","decision_id":"narrow-wide-shuffle"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-107","number":107,"stem":"A data scientist at an e-commerce company is working with subscriber information stored in a DataFrame named df_user. Before continuing with further processing, the scientist wants to create another DataFrame called df_user_non_pii that contains only non-PII columns.\n\nThe PII columns in df_user are first_name, last_name, email, and birthdate.\n\nWhich code snippet should be used to create df_user_non_pii with those columns removed?","options":["A.\ndf_user_non_pii = df_user.drop(\"first_name\", \"last_name\", \"email\", \"birthdate\")","B.\ndf_user_non_pii = df_user.drop(\"first_name\", last_name, email, birthdate)","C.\ndf_user_non_pii = df_user.dropfields(\"first_name\", \"last_name\", \"email\", \"birthdate\")","D.\ndf_user_non_pii = df_user.dropfields(\"first_name, last_name, email, birthdate\")"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"column-drop","concept":"column-drop","concept_label":"column-drop","explanation_ja":"不要な列名を文字列でdropに渡す。元DataFrameは変更されず新しいDataFrameが返る。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.drop.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nSpark DataFrames provide the `drop()` method to remove one or more columns from a DataFrame. The column names must be passed as string arguments.\n\nCorrect code:\n\n```\ndf_user_non_pii = df_user.drop(\"first_name\", \"last_name\", \"email\", \"birthdate\")\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark creates a new DataFrame with the specified columns removed.\n\n- \nThe original DataFrame (`df_user`) remains unchanged because Spark DataFrames are immutable.\n\nExample schema transformation:\n\nBefore:\n\n```\nfirst_name | last_name | email | birthdate | user_id | country\n```\n\nAfter removing PII columns:\n\n```\nuser_id | country\n```\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.drop.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.drop.html)\n\nThe documentation confirms that `drop()` removes one or more columns from a DataFrame.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption B\n\n```\ndf_user.drop(\"first_name\", last_name, email, birthdate)\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because `last_name`, `email`, and `birthdate` are not provided as string literals. Python will interpret them as variables, which will cause an error unless those variables exist.\n\nOption C\n\n```\ndf_user.dropfields(...)\n```\n\nThis is invalid because `dropfields()` is not a Spark DataFrame API method.\n\nOption D\n\n```\ndf_user.dropfields(\"first_name, last_name, email, birthdate\")\n```\n\nThis is also incorrect because:\n\n- \n`dropfields()` does not exist.\n\n- \nPassing a comma-separated string would not correctly identify individual columns even if the method existed.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark DataFrames are immutable, meaning transformations do not modify the original dataset. Instead, they return a new DataFrame.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf2 = df.drop(\"column_name\")\n```\n\nCommon column removal use cases include:\n\n- \nRemoving PII data\n\n- \nDropping unnecessary columns\n\n- \nReducing memory usage\n\nUnderstanding DataFrame immutability is fundamental to working effectively with Spark transformations.","source_option_explanations":["Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nSpark DataFrames provide the `drop()` method to remove one or more columns from a DataFrame. The column names must be passed as string arguments.\n\nCorrect code:\n\n```\ndf_user_non_pii = df_user.drop(\"first_name\", \"last_name\", \"email\", \"birthdate\")\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark creates a new DataFrame with the specified columns removed.\n\n- \nThe original DataFrame (`df_user`) remains unchanged because Spark DataFrames are immutable.\n\nExample schema transformation:\n\nBefore:\n\n```\nfirst_name | last_name | email | birthdate | user_id | country\n```\n\nAfter removing PII columns:\n\n```\nuser_id | country\n```","Option B\n\n```\ndf_user.drop(\"first_name\", last_name, email, birthdate)\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because `last_name`, `email`, and `birthdate` are not provided as string literals. Python will interpret them as variables, which will cause an error unless those variables exist.","Option C\n\n```\ndf_user.dropfields(...)\n```\n\nThis is invalid because `dropfields()` is not a Spark DataFrame API method.","Option D\n\n```\ndf_user.dropfields(\"first_name, last_name, email, birthdate\")\n```\n\nThis is also incorrect because:\n\n- \n`dropfields()` does not exist.\n\n- \nPassing a comma-separated string would not correctly identify individual columns even if the method existed."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.drop.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"14885da981658901912e7bceff6a454810f9968f0cfb5c97b3a81d450f492c6b","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"column-removal","decision_id":"column-removal"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-108","number":108,"stem":"What is the difference between df.cache() and df.persist() when working with a Spark DataFrame?","options":["A.\nBoth `cache()` and `persist()` can be used to configure the default storage level (MEMORY_AND_DISK_DESER).","B.\nBoth functions perform the same operation. However, `persist()` provides better performance because its default storage level is DISK_ONLY.","C.\n`persist()` persists the DataFrame using the default storage level (MEMORY_AND_DISK_DESER), while `cache()` allows specifying different storage levels for persisting the DataFrame.","D.\n`cache()` persists the DataFrame using the default storage level (MEMORY_AND_DISK_DESER), whereas `persist()` allows specifying different storage levels when persisting the DataFrame."],"correct":3,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"cache-persist","concept":"cache-persist","concept_label":"cache-persist","explanation_ja":"DataFrame.cacheの既定はMEMORY_AND_DISK_DESER。persistは別のstorage levelを指定できる。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.persist.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nBoth `cache()` and `persist()` are used to store a DataFrame in memory (or other storage levels) so that subsequent operations can reuse the computed results instead of recomputing them.\n\nThe key difference is:\n\n- \n`cache()` stores the DataFrame using the default storage level\n\n- \n`persist()` allows the developer to explicitly specify the storage level\n\nIn Spark, the default storage level for DataFrames is:\n\n```\nMEMORY_AND_DISK_DESER\n```\n\nExample of using `cache()`:\n\n```\ndf.cache()\n```\n\nThis is equivalent to:\n\n```\ndf.persist()\n```\n\nbecause `persist()` without parameters uses the same default storage level.\n\nHowever, `persist()` allows custom storage strategies:\n\n```\nfrom pyspark import StorageLevel\n \ndf.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY)\ndf.persist(StorageLevel.DISK_ONLY)\ndf.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK)\n```\n\nThis flexibility allows developers to optimize memory usage and performance depending on workload characteristics.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.persist.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.persist.html)\n\nThe documentation states that `persist()` allows specifying the storage level, while `cache()` uses the default storage level.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\nThis is incorrect because only `persist()` allows configuring storage levels. `cache()` always uses the default storage level.\n\nOption B\n\nThis is incorrect because:\n\n- \n`persist()` does not default to `DISK_ONLY`\n\n- \nBoth `cache()` and `persist()` default to `MEMORY_AND_DISK_DESER` for DataFrames.\n\nOption C\n\nThis reverses the behavior. It incorrectly claims that `persist()` uses the default storage level while `cache()` allows custom levels. In reality, the opposite is true.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark caching helps avoid recomputing expensive transformations in iterative or multi-step workflows.\n\nTypical workflow:\n\n```\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"data\")\ndf.cache()\ndf.count()   # triggers caching\n```\n\nStorage levels define where the cached data is stored:\n\nCommon levels include:\n\n- \nMEMORY_ONLY\n\n- \nMEMORY_AND_DISK\n\n- \nMEMORY_AND_DISK_DESER\n\n- \nDISK_ONLY\n\nUsing caching strategically can significantly improve performance when the same DataFrame is reused across multiple actions or transformations.","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\nThis is incorrect because only `persist()` allows configuring storage levels. `cache()` always uses the default storage level.","Option B\n\nThis is incorrect because:\n\n- \n`persist()` does not default to `DISK_ONLY`\n\n- \nBoth `cache()` and `persist()` default to `MEMORY_AND_DISK_DESER` for DataFrames.","Option C\n\nThis reverses the behavior. It incorrectly claims that `persist()` uses the default storage level while `cache()` allows custom levels. In reality, the opposite is true.","Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nBoth `cache()` and `persist()` are used to store a DataFrame in memory (or other storage levels) so that subsequent operations can reuse the computed results instead of recomputing them.\n\nThe key difference is:\n\n- \n`cache()` stores the DataFrame using the default storage level\n\n- \n`persist()` allows the developer to explicitly specify the storage level\n\nIn Spark, the default storage level for DataFrames is:\n\n```\nMEMORY_AND_DISK_DESER\n```\n\nExample of using `cache()`:\n\n```\ndf.cache()\n```\n\nThis is equivalent to:\n\n```\ndf.persist()\n```\n\nbecause `persist()` without parameters uses the same default storage level.\n\nHowever, `persist()` allows custom storage strategies:\n\n```\nfrom pyspark import StorageLevel\n \ndf.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_ONLY)\ndf.persist(StorageLevel.DISK_ONLY)\ndf.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK)\n```\n\nThis flexibility allows developers to optimize memory usage and performance depending on workload characteristics."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.persist.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"238df6eebc6db75bd85df4b9403dac4b5547a60b37b8b219e8e9331a249bde80","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"cache-vs-persist-storage-config","decision_id":"cache-vs-persist-storage-config"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-109","number":109,"stem":"A Spark engineer is diagnosing a Spark application that repeatedly fails with out-of-memory errors during execution. While inspecting the Spark driver logs, the engineer observes multiple messages stating “GC overhead limit exceeded.”\n\nWhich action should the engineer take to address this problem?","options":["A.\nImprove the data processing logic by repartitioning the DataFrame.","B.\nChange the Spark configuration to disable garbage collection.","C.\nIncrease the amount of memory allocated to the Spark Driver.","D.\nCache large DataFrames so they remain stored in memory."],"correct":2,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"driver-gc-oom","concept":"driver-gc-oom","concept_label":"driver-gc-oom","explanation_ja":"driverでのGC/OOMならdriverメモリとdriverへ集めたデータ量を調べる。executorメモリを増やすだけでは直らない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html#garbage-collection-tuning","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C\n\nIncrease the amount of memory allocated to the Spark Driver.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe error “GC overhead limit exceeded” indicates that the JVM is spending too much time performing garbage collection while recovering very little memory. This typically happens when the heap memory is insufficient for the workload, causing constant memory pressure and repeated garbage collection cycles.\n\nIn Spark applications, this error commonly occurs when the driver JVM does not have enough memory to hold the objects it is processing (for example during `collect()`, large aggregations, or driver-side computations).\n\nThe recommended solution is to increase the Spark driver memory, which can be configured using:\n\n```\nspark.driver.memory\n```\n\nExample configuration:\n\n```\n--driver-memory 8g\n```\n\nor\n\n```\nspark.conf.set(\"spark.driver.memory\", \"8g\")\n```\n\nIncreasing driver memory allows the JVM to allocate a larger heap, reducing the frequency and severity of garbage collection cycles.\n\nSpark tuning documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html#garbage-collection-tuning](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html#garbage-collection-tuning)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nThe JVM error GC overhead limit exceeded occurs when:\n\n• More than 98% of CPU time is spent in garbage collection\n• Less than 2% of heap memory is recovered\n\nThis indicates that the JVM heap is too small for the workload, leading to excessive GC cycles.\n\nIn Spark environments, this typically happens due to:\n\n• large datasets being collected to the driver\n• large driver-side aggregations\n• insufficient driver memory allocation\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA. Improve the data processing logic by repartitioning the DataFrame.\n\nRepartitioning affects executor-side data distribution, not the driver heap memory. It will not directly resolve driver-side GC overhead issues.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.repartition.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.repartition.html)\n\nB. Change the Spark configuration to disable garbage collection.\n\nGarbage collection cannot be disabled in the JVM. GC is essential for memory management in Java-based systems like Spark.\n\nD. Cache large DataFrames so they remain stored in memory.\n\nCaching actually increases memory consumption and could worsen out-of-memory problems if memory is already constrained.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#caching-data](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#caching-data)\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nWhen encountering GC overhead limit exceeded in Spark:\n\n• The JVM heap is typically too small for the workload\n• Increasing memory allocation is often the first remediation step\n\nCommon tuning parameters include:\n\n```\nspark.driver.memory\nspark.executor.memory\nspark.memory.fraction\n```\n\nProper memory configuration is a key part of Spark performance tuning and stability.","source_option_explanations":["A. Improve the data processing logic by repartitioning the DataFrame.\n\nRepartitioning affects executor-side data distribution, not the driver heap memory. It will not directly resolve driver-side GC overhead issues.","B. Change the Spark configuration to disable garbage collection.\n\nGarbage collection cannot be disabled in the JVM. GC is essential for memory management in Java-based systems like Spark.","Correct Answer: C\n\nIncrease the amount of memory allocated to the Spark Driver.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe error “GC overhead limit exceeded” indicates that the JVM is spending too much time performing garbage collection while recovering very little memory. This typically happens when the heap memory is insufficient for the workload, causing constant memory pressure and repeated garbage collection cycles.\n\nIn Spark applications, this error commonly occurs when the driver JVM does not have enough memory to hold the objects it is processing (for example during `collect()`, large aggregations, or driver-side computations).\n\nThe recommended solution is to increase the Spark driver memory, which can be configured using:\n\n```\nspark.driver.memory\n```\n\nExample configuration:\n\n```\n--driver-memory 8g\n```\n\nor\n\n```\nspark.conf.set(\"spark.driver.memory\", \"8g\")\n```\n\nIncreasing driver memory allows the JVM to allocate a larger heap, reducing the frequency and severity of garbage collection cycles.","D. Cache large DataFrames so they remain stored in memory.\n\nCaching actually increases memory consumption and could worsen out-of-memory problems if memory is already constrained."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html#garbage-collection-tuning","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.repartition.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#caching-data"],"images":[],"source_hash":"be05efaba5bccfa9def1d970e54787afdacd5b7ceb34e1903f21817556b6643d","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"driver-gc-out-of-memory","decision_id":"driver-gc-out-of-memory"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-110","number":110,"stem":"An engineer has a large ORC file stored at /file/test_data.orc and wants to read only specific columns (for example, col1 and col2) to minimize memory usage during processing.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly selects the required columns while reading the ORC file?","options":["A.\nspark.read.orc(\"/file/test_data.orc\").filter(\"col1 = 'value'\").select(\"col2\")","B.\nspark.read.format(\"orc\").select(\"col1\", \"col2\").load(\"/file/test_data.orc\")","C.\nspark.read.orc(\"/file/test_data.orc\").select(\"col1\", \"col2\")","D.\nspark.read.format(\"orc\").load(\"/file/test_data.orc\").select(\"col1\", \"col2\")"],"correct":2,"correct_many":[2,3],"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"orc-projection","concept":"orc-projection","concept_label":"orc-projection","explanation_ja":"read.orcとformat(orc).loadはどちらもDataFrameを返しselectできる。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.orc.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.orc.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.load.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-orc.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.load.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"05c8d39cf48d888c0537ae703e9f35697d0e1a47406a4241b3e1d2bdc65d873c","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"read.orcとread.format(orc).loadは同じselectに接続できる。CとDが有効。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.orc.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.load.html"],"original_stem":"An engineer has a large ORC file stored at /file/test_data.orc and wants to read only specific columns (for example, col1 and col2) to minimize memory usage during processing.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly selects the required columns while reading the ORC file?","original_options":["A.\nspark.read.orc(\"/file/test_data.orc\").filter(\"col1 = 'value'\").select(\"col2\")","B.\nspark.read.format(\"orc\").select(\"col1\", \"col2\").load(\"/file/test_data.orc\")","C.\nspark.read.orc(\"/file/test_data.orc\").select(\"col1\", \"col2\")","D.\nspark.read.format(\"orc\").load(\"/file/test_data.orc\").select(\"col1\", \"col2\")"],"original_correct":["C"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"orc-projection","decision_id":"orc-projection"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-111","number":111,"stem":"A data engineer is reviewing a Spark application that performs several transformations on a DataFrame, but observes that the job does not begin execution immediately after the transformations are defined.\n\nWhich two characteristics of Apache Spark’s execution model explain this behavior? (Choose two.)","options":["A.\nThe Spark engine requires manual intervention to begin executing transformations.","B.\nOnly actions trigger the execution of the transformation pipeline.","C.\nTransformations are executed immediately to construct the lineage graph.","D.\nThe Spark engine optimizes the execution plan during transformations, which delays execution.","E.\nTransformations are evaluated lazily."],"correct":1,"correct_many":[1,4],"domain":"Architecture","topic":"lazy-execution","concept":"lazy-execution","concept_label":"lazy-execution","explanation_ja":"transformationsはlazyでactionが実行を要求する。明示的な手動コンパイルは不要。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#actions","source_explanation":"Correct answers: B and E\n\nWhy these options are correct\n\nSpark follows a lazy evaluation execution model, meaning transformations are not executed immediately. Instead, Spark records them as a logical execution plan and only runs them when an action is invoked.\n\nOption B — Only actions trigger execution\n\nIn Spark, operations are divided into:\n\nTransformations\n\n- \n`filter`\n\n- \n`select`\n\n- \n`groupBy`\n\n- \n`map`\n\n- \n`join`\n\nThese operations do not execute immediately. Instead, they build a lineage graph (DAG) describing how to compute the final result.\n\nExecution only begins when an action is called.\n\nExamples of actions:\n\n```\ndf.count()\ndf.collect()\ndf.show()\ndf.write.parquet(...)\n```\n\nWhen an action is triggered, Spark:\n\n- \nBuilds the execution DAG\n\n- \nOptimizes the plan\n\n- \nSchedules tasks across the cluster\n\n- \nExecutes the job\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#actions](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#actions)\n\nThe Spark programming guide explains that actions trigger execution of transformations.\n\nOption E — Transformations are evaluated lazily\n\nSpark transformations use lazy evaluation, meaning the transformations are recorded but not executed immediately.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf2 = df.filter(\"age > 30\").select(\"name\")\n```\n\nAt this point:\n\n- \nNo computation occurs\n\n- \nSpark only records the logical plan\n\nExecution begins only when an action is called:\n\n```\ndf2.show()\n```\n\nLazy evaluation enables Spark to:\n\n- \nCombine transformations\n\n- \nEliminate unnecessary operations\n\n- \nOptimize execution plans\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\nSpark does not require manual intervention to start execution. Execution automatically begins when an action is called.\n\nOption C\n\nTransformations are not executed immediately to build the lineage graph. The lineage graph is constructed without executing the transformations.\n\nOption D\n\nSpark performs optimization using Catalyst optimizer and DAG scheduling, but this does not delay execution by itself. The primary reason execution does not start is lazy evaluation, not optimization during transformations.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark operations fall into two categories:\n\nTransformations\n\n- \nLazy\n\n- \nBuild a lineage DAG\n\n- \nExamples: `map`, `filter`, `groupBy`, `join`\n\nActions\n\n- \nTrigger execution\n\n- \nReturn results or write data\n\n- \nExamples: `count`, `collect`, `show`, `write`\n\nExecution flow:\n\n```\nTransformations → DAG creation → Action → Job execution\n```\n\nLazy evaluation allows Spark to optimize execution plans and minimize unnecessary computation, which is a key reason Spark performs efficiently at scale.","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\nSpark does not require manual intervention to start execution. Execution automatically begins when an action is called.","Option B — Only actions trigger execution\n\nIn Spark, operations are divided into:\n\nTransformations\n\n- \n`filter`\n\n- \n`select`\n\n- \n`groupBy`\n\n- \n`map`\n\n- \n`join`\n\nThese operations do not execute immediately. Instead, they build a lineage graph (DAG) describing how to compute the final result.\n\nExecution only begins when an action is called.\n\nExamples of actions:\n\n```\ndf.count()\ndf.collect()\ndf.show()\ndf.write.parquet(...)\n```\n\nWhen an action is triggered, Spark:\n\n- \nBuilds the execution DAG\n\n- \nOptimizes the plan\n\n- \nSchedules tasks across the cluster\n\n- \nExecutes the job","Option C\n\nTransformations are not executed immediately to build the lineage graph. The lineage graph is constructed without executing the transformations.","Option D\n\nSpark performs optimization using Catalyst optimizer and DAG scheduling, but this does not delay execution by itself. The primary reason execution does not start is lazy evaluation, not optimization during transformations.","Option E — Transformations are evaluated lazily\n\nSpark transformations use lazy evaluation, meaning the transformations are recorded but not executed immediately.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf2 = df.filter(\"age > 30\").select(\"name\")\n```\n\nAt this point:\n\n- \nNo computation occurs\n\n- \nSpark only records the logical plan\n\nExecution begins only when an action is called:\n\n```\ndf2.show()\n```\n\nLazy evaluation enables Spark to:\n\n- \nCombine transformations\n\n- \nEliminate unnecessary operations\n\n- \nOptimize execution plans"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#actions","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations"],"images":[],"source_hash":"ca5d0842126f71c75c1acf0fee5c8e4f7110b0d6c8b19bc1e435d8ba893c99d5","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"lazy-action-trigger","decision_id":"lazy-action-trigger"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-112","number":112,"stem":"A data engineer is developing a Structured Streaming pipeline and wants the application to recover from failures or planned shutdowns by continuing processing from the point where it previously stopped.\n\nHow can this behavior be implemented in Apache Spark?","options":["A.\nConfigure the option checkpointLocation during `readStream`.","B.\nConfigure the option recoveryLocation during SparkSession initialization.","C.\nConfigure the option recoveryLocation during `writeStream`.","D.\nConfigure the option checkpointLocation during `writeStream`."],"correct":3,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"stream-checkpoint","concept":"stream-checkpoint","concept_label":"stream-checkpoint","explanation_ja":"checkpointLocationをwriteStreamに指定して同じqueryの回復に利用する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#checkpointing","source_explanation":"Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Apache Spark Structured Streaming, fault tolerance and the ability to resume processing from the last successfully processed offset are achieved using checkpointing.\n\nCheckpointing stores important metadata such as:\n\n- \nSource offsets\n\n- \nStreaming progress information\n\n- \nState data for stateful operations\n\n- \nExecution metadata\n\nWhen a streaming query restarts, Spark reads the checkpoint directory and continues processing from the last committed offset, ensuring reliable recovery after failures or planned shutdowns.\n\nCheckpointing is configured in the `writeStream` configuration.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nquery = streaming_df.writeStream \\\n    .format(\"console\") \\\n    .outputMode(\"append\") \\\n    .option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/path/to/checkpoint\") \\\n    .start()\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark stores streaming progress in the checkpoint directory.\n\n- \nIf the application stops or crashes, the query restarts.\n\n- \nSpark reads checkpoint metadata and resumes processing from the previous position.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#checkpointing](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#checkpointing)\n\nThe documentation explains that Structured Streaming uses checkpoint directories to recover state and offsets after failures.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A — Configure checkpointLocation during readStream\n\n`readStream` defines the input source configuration, but checkpointing is associated with query output and state management, which is configured in `writeStream`.\n\nOption B — recoveryLocation during SparkSession initialization\n\n`recoveryLocation` is not a valid Spark configuration option.\n\nOption C — recoveryLocation during writeStream\n\nAgain, `recoveryLocation` is not a valid option in Spark Structured Streaming.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nStructured Streaming achieves fault tolerance using checkpointing and write-ahead logs.\n\nKey rule:\n\n```\ncheckpointLocation → required for reliable streaming recovery\n```\n\nCheckpoint directories store:\n\n- \nSource offsets\n\n- \nQuery progress metadata\n\n- \nState store data\n\nWithout checkpointing:\n\n- \nThe stream cannot recover from failures\n\n- \nProgress tracking is lost\n\n- \nExactly-once processing guarantees cannot be maintained\n\nTherefore, checkpointing is a mandatory best practice for production streaming pipelines.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Configure checkpointLocation during readStream\n\n`readStream` defines the input source configuration, but checkpointing is associated with query output and state management, which is configured in `writeStream`.","Option B — recoveryLocation during SparkSession initialization\n\n`recoveryLocation` is not a valid Spark configuration option.","Option C — recoveryLocation during writeStream\n\nAgain, `recoveryLocation` is not a valid option in Spark Structured Streaming.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept","Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Apache Spark Structured Streaming, fault tolerance and the ability to resume processing from the last successfully processed offset are achieved using checkpointing.\n\nCheckpointing stores important metadata such as:\n\n- \nSource offsets\n\n- \nStreaming progress information\n\n- \nState data for stateful operations\n\n- \nExecution metadata\n\nWhen a streaming query restarts, Spark reads the checkpoint directory and continues processing from the last committed offset, ensuring reliable recovery after failures or planned shutdowns.\n\nCheckpointing is configured in the `writeStream` configuration.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nquery = streaming_df.writeStream \\\n    .format(\"console\") \\\n    .outputMode(\"append\") \\\n    .option(\"checkpointLocation\", \"/path/to/checkpoint\") \\\n    .start()\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark stores streaming progress in the checkpoint directory.\n\n- \nIf the application stops or crashes, the query restarts.\n\n- \nSpark reads checkpoint metadata and resumes processing from the previous position."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#checkpointing"],"images":[],"source_hash":"574466683f15813abb0006a0bc91f302cf2dda75b87c975368fae330576a2077","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"stream-checkpoint-recovery","decision_id":"stream-checkpoint-recovery"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-113","number":113,"stem":"Which of the following code snippets returns a new DataFrame where the managerName column contains the uppercase version of the existing managerName column from the DataFrame storesDF? Assume storesDF is the only defined variable.","options":["A.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"managerName\", upper(managerName))","B.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"managerName\", toupper(col(\"managerName\")))","C.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"managerName\", upper(col(\"managerName\")))","D.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"managerName\", col(\"managerName\").upper())","E.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"managerName\", upper(\"managerName\"))"],"correct":2,"correct_many":[2,4],"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"string-upper","concept":"string-upper","concept_label":"string-upper","explanation_ja":"upperは列名文字列もColumnも受け付ける。CとEが正解。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.upper.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.upper.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.upper.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"406dccf03d1dc98fc3a7e9a1c2170f0a59f0ea0fbd1faaef0afcce9515be21ff","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"upperはColumnと列名文字列をどちらも受け付けるためCとEが有効。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.upper.html"],"original_stem":"Which of the following code snippets returns a new DataFrame where the managerName column contains the uppercase version of the existing managerName column from the DataFrame storesDF? Assume storesDF is the only defined variable.","original_options":["A.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"managerName\", upper(managerName))","B.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"managerName\", toupper(col(\"managerName\")))","C.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"managerName\", upper(col(\"managerName\")))","D.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"managerName\", col(\"managerName\").upper())","E.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"managerName\", upper(\"managerName\"))"],"original_correct":["C"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"string-upper","decision_id":"string-upper"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-114","number":114,"stem":"The following code block is intended to create a Python UDF named `assessPerformanceUDF` using the Python function `assessPerformance()` and then apply it to the column `customerSatisfaction` in the DataFrame `storesDF`.\n\nFill in the numbered blanks using the default UDF return type (StringType), without explicitly supplying a return type.\n\nCode block:\n\n```\nassessPerformanceUDF = _1_(_2_)\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"result\", _3_(_4_))\n```","options":["A.\n\nudf\n\nassessPerformance, IntegerType()\n\nassessPerformanceUDF\n\ncol(\"customerSatisfaction\")","B.\n\nudf\n\nassessPerformance\n\nassessPerformanceUDF\n\ncol(\"customerSatisfaction\")","C.\n\nspark.register.udf\n\nassessPerformanceUDF\n\nassessPerformance\n\ncol(\"customerSatisfaction\")","D.\n\nspark.register.udf\n\nassessPerformance, IntegerType()\n\nassessPerformanceUDF\n\ncol(\"customerSatisfaction\")","E.\n\nspark.register.udf\n\nassessPerformance\n\nassessPerformanceUDF\n\n\"customerSatisfaction\""],"correct":1,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"python-udf","concept":"python-udf","concept_label":"python-udf","explanation_ja":"udf(func)は戻り型省略時にStringType。IntegerTypeを明示する選択肢とは要件が異なる。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.udf.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.udf.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.udf.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"a6caec8e54a156f0b51f1f26f17a947b8da5360dc7dad433eeca972ea6c4fea0","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"戻り型を省略するUDFとIntegerTypeを明示するUDFは両方構文として有効。省略して既定StringTypeを使う意図を明記。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.udf.html"],"original_stem":"The following code block is intended to create a Python UDF named `assessPerformanceUDF` using the Python function `assessPerformance()` and then apply it to the column `customerSatisfaction` in the DataFrame `storesDF`.\n\nFill in the numbered blanks correctly.\n\nCode block:\n\n```\nassessPerformanceUDF = _1_(_2_)\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"result\", _3_(_4_))\n```","original_options":["A.\n\nudf\n\nassessPerformance, IntegerType()\n\nassessPerformanceUDF\n\ncol(\"customerSatisfaction\")","B.\n\nudf\n\nassessPerformance\n\nassessPerformanceUDF\n\ncol(\"customerSatisfaction\")","C.\n\nspark.register.udf\n\nassessPerformanceUDF\n\nassessPerformance\n\ncol(\"customerSatisfaction\")","D.\n\nspark.register.udf\n\nassessPerformance, IntegerType()\n\nassessPerformanceUDF\n\ncol(\"customerSatisfaction\")","E.\n\nspark.register.udf\n\nassessPerformance\n\nassessPerformanceUDF\n\n\"customerSatisfaction\""],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"python-udf-default-string-type","decision_id":"python-udf-default-string-type"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-115","number":115,"stem":"A Spark DataFrame df is cached using the MEMORY_AND_DISK storage level, but the dataset is too large to fit entirely in memory.\n\nWhat is the expected behavior when Spark runs out of memory while caching the DataFrame?","options":["A.\nSpark stores duplicate copies of the DataFrame in both memory and disk. If it does not fit in memory, the entire DataFrame is stored and accessed only from disk.","B.\nSpark evenly splits the DataFrame between memory and disk to maintain balanced storage usage.","C.\nSpark stores as much of the DataFrame as possible in memory and spills the remaining partitions to disk, continuing execution with some performance overhead.","D.\nSpark keeps frequently accessed rows in memory and stores less frequently accessed rows on disk to balance performance."],"correct":2,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"storage-level","concept":"storage-level","concept_label":"storage-level","explanation_ja":"MEMORY_AND_DISKはメモリに置けないpartitionをdiskへ置く。行単位の頻度で自動分類しない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#rdd-persistence","source_explanation":"Correct answer: C\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nWhen a DataFrame is persisted using the `MEMORY_AND_DISK` storage level, Spark attempts to store partitions in memory first. If the dataset is larger than the available executor memory, Spark will store the remaining partitions on disk instead of recomputing them.\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark computes partitions of the DataFrame.\n\n- \nIt tries to cache each partition in memory.\n\n- \nIf memory becomes full:\n\n- \nThe remaining partitions are written to disk.\n\n- \nWhen those partitions are needed later, Spark reads them from disk, which introduces some performance overhead compared to memory access.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK)\n```\n\nThis storage level ensures that Spark avoids recomputing expensive transformations, even when memory is insufficient.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#rdd-persistence](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#rdd-persistence)\n\nThe Spark documentation explains that with `MEMORY_AND_DISK`, partitions that do not fit in memory are stored on disk.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\nSpark does not store duplicate copies of the DataFrame in both memory and disk. Instead, partitions are stored either in memory or on disk, depending on available memory.\n\nOption B\n\nSpark does not evenly split the dataset between memory and disk. Partitions are cached sequentially until memory is exhausted, after which the remaining partitions are written to disk.\n\nOption D\n\nSpark does not track frequently accessed rows when caching with `MEMORY_AND_DISK`. Spark caches entire partitions, not individual rows, and it does not apply frequency-based storage decisions.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark caching operates at the partition level, not at the row level.\n\nCommon storage levels:\n\n- \nMEMORY_ONLY\nStores partitions only in memory; partitions that do not fit are recomputed when needed.\n\n- \nMEMORY_AND_DISK\nStores partitions in memory and spills excess partitions to disk.\n\n- \nDISK_ONLY\nStores all partitions on disk.\n\nTypical usage:\n\n```\ndf.cache()  # equivalent to MEMORY_AND_DISK for DataFrames\n```\n\nUsing `MEMORY_AND_DISK` is a safe option when datasets may exceed executor memory, because it prevents expensive recomputation while maintaining reasonable performance.","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\nSpark does not store duplicate copies of the DataFrame in both memory and disk. Instead, partitions are stored either in memory or on disk, depending on available memory.","Option B\n\nSpark does not evenly split the dataset between memory and disk. Partitions are cached sequentially until memory is exhausted, after which the remaining partitions are written to disk.","Correct answer: C\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nWhen a DataFrame is persisted using the `MEMORY_AND_DISK` storage level, Spark attempts to store partitions in memory first. If the dataset is larger than the available executor memory, Spark will store the remaining partitions on disk instead of recomputing them.\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \nSpark computes partitions of the DataFrame.\n\n- \nIt tries to cache each partition in memory.\n\n- \nIf memory becomes full:\n\n- \nThe remaining partitions are written to disk.\n\n- \nWhen those partitions are needed later, Spark reads them from disk, which introduces some performance overhead compared to memory access.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.persist(StorageLevel.MEMORY_AND_DISK)\n```\n\nThis storage level ensures that Spark avoids recomputing expensive transformations, even when memory is insufficient.","Option D\n\nSpark does not track frequently accessed rows when caching with `MEMORY_AND_DISK`. Spark caches entire partitions, not individual rows, and it does not apply frequency-based storage decisions."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#rdd-persistence"],"images":[],"source_hash":"d9ec5831523bb863f6ac1fda115c5e323d382d1dadd33033c30b8dbb4e22f41b","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"memory-and-disk-cache-overflow","decision_id":"memory-and-disk-cache-overflow"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-116","number":116,"stem":"Which of the following code snippets writes the DataFrame storesDF to the file path filePath in JSON format, overwriting any existing data at that location?","options":["A.\nstoresDF.write(filePath, mode=\"overwrite\", source=\"json\")","B.\nstoresDF.write.mode(\"overwrite\").json(filePath)","C.\nstoresDF.write.mode(\"overwrite\").path(filePath)","D.\nstoresDF.write.option(\"json\", \"overwrite\").path(filePath)"],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"write-overwrite","concept":"write-overwrite","concept_label":"write-overwrite","explanation_ja":"write.mode(overwrite).json(path)で既存出力を上書きする。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.json.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: B\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nTo write a DataFrame in JSON format and overwrite any existing data, Spark provides the `DataFrameWriter` API. The correct approach is to set the write mode to overwrite and then specify the output format.\n\nCorrect code:\n\n```\nstoresDF.write.mode(\"overwrite\").json(filePath)\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \n`write` creates a DataFrameWriter object.\n\n- \n`mode(\"overwrite\")` ensures that any existing data at the path is replaced.\n\n- \n`.json(filePath)` writes the DataFrame in JSON format to the specified path.\n\nSpark documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.json.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.json.html)\n\nThe documentation explains that `json(path)` writes the DataFrame to the specified location using the JSON data source.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\n```\nstoresDF.write(filePath, mode=\"overwrite\", source=\"json\")\n```\n\nThis is invalid because `write()` does not accept parameters directly. Instead, Spark uses a builder-style API with chained methods like `.mode()` and `.format()`.\n\nOption C\n\n```\nstoresDF.write.mode(\"overwrite\").path(filePath)\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because `path()` is not a DataFrameWriter method. Spark requires specifying the output format using `.json()`, `.parquet()`, `.csv()`, etc.\n\nOption D\n\n```\nstoresDF.write.option(\"json\", \"overwrite\").path(filePath)\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because:\n\n- \n`.option()` is used to configure data source options, not the format.\n\n- \n`.path()` is not a valid write method.\n\n- \nThe correct pattern requires specifying the format or using `.json()`.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark writes data using the DataFrameWriter API, which follows a builder pattern:\n\n```\ndf.write\n  .format(\"json\")\n  .mode(\"overwrite\")\n  .save(path)\n```\n\nOr using a format shortcut:\n\n```\ndf.write.mode(\"overwrite\").json(path)\n```\n\nCommon write modes include:\n\n- \noverwrite – replaces existing data\n\n- \nappend – adds new records\n\n- \nignore – skips write if data exists\n\n- \nerror / errorIfExists – throws an error if data exists\n\nUnderstanding these write modes is important when building reliable Spark data pipelines.","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\n```\nstoresDF.write(filePath, mode=\"overwrite\", source=\"json\")\n```\n\nThis is invalid because `write()` does not accept parameters directly. Instead, Spark uses a builder-style API with chained methods like `.mode()` and `.format()`.","Correct answer: B\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nTo write a DataFrame in JSON format and overwrite any existing data, Spark provides the `DataFrameWriter` API. The correct approach is to set the write mode to overwrite and then specify the output format.\n\nCorrect code:\n\n```\nstoresDF.write.mode(\"overwrite\").json(filePath)\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \n`write` creates a DataFrameWriter object.\n\n- \n`mode(\"overwrite\")` ensures that any existing data at the path is replaced.\n\n- \n`.json(filePath)` writes the DataFrame in JSON format to the specified path.\n\nSpark documentation:","Option C\n\n```\nstoresDF.write.mode(\"overwrite\").path(filePath)\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because `path()` is not a DataFrameWriter method. Spark requires specifying the output format using `.json()`, `.parquet()`, `.csv()`, etc.","Option D\n\n```\nstoresDF.write.option(\"json\", \"overwrite\").path(filePath)\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because:\n\n- \n`.option()` is used to configure data source options, not the format.\n\n- \n`.path()` is not a valid write method.\n\n- \nThe correct pattern requires specifying the format or using `.json()`."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.json.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"e2c476c4b376bb4f1f6ceac067e6ac55decd7cbfb052b2ad84851e5f0a1c7860","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"json-overwrite-api","decision_id":"json-writer-overwrite-mode"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-117","number":117,"stem":"Which of the following code snippets returns a new DataFrame that represents a left join between storesDF and employeesDF using the storeId column?","options":["A.\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"left\", col(\"storeId\"))","B.\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\")","C.\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\", \"left\")","D.\nstoresDF.merge(employeesDF, \"left\", col(\"storeId\"))","E.\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"left\", storesDF.storeId == employeesDF.storeId)"],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"join-left","concept":"join-left","concept_label":"join-left","explanation_ja":"join(other,on,how)の第3引数でleftを指定する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: C\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Spark, the DataFrame `join()` method has the following signature:\n\n```\nDataFrame.join(other, on=None, how=None)\n```\n\nWhere:\n\n- \n`other` → the DataFrame to join with\n\n- \n`on` → the join column(s) or join condition\n\n- \n`how` → the join type (`inner`, `left`, `right`, `outer`, etc.)\n\nCorrect code:\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\", \"left\")\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- \n`\"storeId\"` → specifies the join column\n\n- \n`\"left\"` → specifies a left join\n\n- \nThe result contains all rows from `storesDF` and matching rows from `employeesDF`\n\nIf no matching row exists in `employeesDF`, the corresponding columns are NULL.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html)\n\nThe documentation confirms that `join()` accepts parameters in the order `(other, on, how)`.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"left\", col(\"storeId\"))\n```\n\nThe parameters are in the wrong order. Spark expects the join column second and join type third.\n\nOption B\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\")\n```\n\nThis performs a join on `storeId`, but the default join type is inner, not left.\n\nOption D\n\n```\nstoresDF.merge(...)\n```\n\nSpark DataFrames do not have a `merge()` method. That method exists in Pandas, not PySpark.\n\nOption E\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"left\", storesDF.storeId == employeesDF.storeId)\n```\n\nAgain, the parameters are in the wrong order. The join condition must appear before the join type.\n\nCorrect condition-based syntax would be:\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, storesDF.storeId == employeesDF.storeId, \"left\")\n```\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark supports two main join styles.\n\nJoin using a shared column name\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, \"column_name\", \"left\")\n```\n\nSpark automatically matches the column in both DataFrames.\n\nJoin using a join condition\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, df1.col == df2.col, \"left\")\n```\n\nUsed when the column names differ between DataFrames.\n\nCommon join types include:\n\n- \n`inner`\n\n- \n`left`\n\n- \n`right`\n\n- \n`outer`\n\n- \n`left_semi`\n\n- \n`left_anti`\n\nUnderstanding these join patterns is essential for building correct and efficient Spark DataFrame pipelines.","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"left\", col(\"storeId\"))\n```\n\nThe parameters are in the wrong order. Spark expects the join column second and join type third.","Option B\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\")\n```\n\nThis performs a join on `storeId`, but the default join type is inner, not left.","Correct answer: C\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Spark, the DataFrame `join()` method has the following signature:\n\n```\nDataFrame.join(other, on=None, how=None)\n```\n\nWhere:\n\n- \n`other` → the DataFrame to join with\n\n- \n`on` → the join column(s) or join condition\n\n- \n`how` → the join type (`inner`, `left`, `right`, `outer`, etc.)\n\nCorrect code:\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\", \"left\")\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- \n`\"storeId\"` → specifies the join column\n\n- \n`\"left\"` → specifies a left join\n\n- \nThe result contains all rows from `storesDF` and matching rows from `employeesDF`\n\nIf no matching row exists in `employeesDF`, the corresponding columns are NULL.","Option D\n\n```\nstoresDF.merge(...)\n```\n\nSpark DataFrames do not have a `merge()` method. That method exists in Pandas, not PySpark.","Option E\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"left\", storesDF.storeId == employeesDF.storeId)\n```"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"a4b9b46fc53317275a0f38047045e443c3d388125fe29b18d1e6687e7431fca0","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"left-join-preserve","decision_id":"left-join-preserve"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-118","number":118,"stem":"Which of the following code snippets writes the DataFrame storesDF to the path filePath as text files?","options":["A.\nstoresDF.write(filePath)","B.\nstoresDF.write.path(filePath)","C.\nstoresDF.write().text(filePath)","D.\nstoresDF.write.text(filePath)","E.\nstoresDF.write.option(\"text\").path(filePath)"],"correct":3,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"write-text","concept":"write-text","concept_label":"write-text","explanation_ja":"text出力には1列のStringTypeというデータ条件が必要。write.textは存在するメソッド。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.text.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nSpark provides the DataFrameWriter API for writing DataFrames to external storage in various formats. To write data as text files, Spark provides the `.text()` method.\n\nCorrect code:\n\n```\nstoresDF.write.text(filePath)\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \n`write` creates a DataFrameWriter.\n\n- \n`.text(filePath)` writes the DataFrame to the specified path in text format.\n\n- \nEach row of the DataFrame becomes a line in the output text files.\n\nSpark documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.text.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.text.html)\n\nThe documentation confirms that `DataFrameWriter.text(path)` writes the DataFrame to text files at the specified path.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption A\n\n```\nstoresDF.write(filePath)\n```\n\nInvalid syntax. `write` returns a DataFrameWriter object, and it must be followed by methods such as `.format()`, `.json()`, `.parquet()`, `.csv()`, or `.text()`.\n\nOption B\n\n```\nstoresDF.write.path(filePath)\n```\n\n`path()` is not a valid method of `DataFrameWriter`.\n\nOption C\n\n```\nstoresDF.write().text(filePath)\n```\n\n`write` is a property, not a function. Calling `write()` results in a syntax error.\n\nOption E\n\n```\nstoresDF.write.option(\"text\").path(filePath)\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because:\n\n- \n`.option()` expects key–value pairs\n\n- \n`.path()` is not a valid write method\n\n- \nThe format must be specified via `.text()` or `.format(\"text\")`\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark uses the DataFrameWriter builder pattern for writing datasets.\n\nTwo common ways to specify format:\n\nShortcut method\n\n```\ndf.write.text(path)\n```\n\nExplicit format\n\n```\ndf.write.format(\"text\").save(path)\n```\n\nImportant note:\nWhen writing text files in Spark, the DataFrame must contain only one string column, because each row becomes a single line in the output file.","source_option_explanations":["Option A\n\n```\nstoresDF.write(filePath)\n```\n\nInvalid syntax. `write` returns a DataFrameWriter object, and it must be followed by methods such as `.format()`, `.json()`, `.parquet()`, `.csv()`, or `.text()`.","Option B\n\n```\nstoresDF.write.path(filePath)\n```\n\n`path()` is not a valid method of `DataFrameWriter`.","Option C\n\n```\nstoresDF.write().text(filePath)\n```\n\n`write` is a property, not a function. Calling `write()` results in a syntax error.","Correct answer: D\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nSpark provides the DataFrameWriter API for writing DataFrames to external storage in various formats. To write data as text files, Spark provides the `.text()` method.\n\nCorrect code:\n\n```\nstoresDF.write.text(filePath)\n```\n\nExecution behavior:\n\n- \n`write` creates a DataFrameWriter.\n\n- \n`.text(filePath)` writes the DataFrame to the specified path in text format.\n\n- \nEach row of the DataFrame becomes a line in the output text files.","Option E\n\n```\nstoresDF.write.option(\"text\").path(filePath)\n```\n\nThis is incorrect because:\n\n- \n`.option()` expects key–value pairs\n\n- \n`.path()` is not a valid write method\n\n- \nThe format must be specified via `.text()` or `.format(\"text\")`"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.text.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"7b6fdd31d1f9cf5369bba78eb525d2a561dad970529b7686d4dee2aab279187f","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"write-text","decision_id":"write-text"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-119","number":119,"stem":"The code block below contains an error. It is intended to read a CSV file from filePath into a DataFrame using the predefined schema schema.\n\nCode block:\n\n```\nspark.read.schema(\"schema\").csv(filePath)\n```\n\nWhat is the error in this code?","options":["A. The literal \"schema\" is not the predefined schema variable or a valid DDL schema here. Pass the variable as `schema`; valid DDL-formatted strings are also supported.","B.\n`DataFrameReader` does not support the `csv()` method, so it should be replaced with `load()`.","C.\n`spark.read` must be followed by parentheses to return a `DataFrameReader` object.","D.\nThere is no `read` property on `spark`; `spark` should be replaced with `DataFrame`.","E.\nThe `schema()` method of `read` expects a column, not a string, so the argument should be `col(\"schema\")`."],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"reader-schema-ddl","concept":"reader-schema-ddl","concept_label":"reader-schema-ddl","explanation_ja":"schema変数と文字列schemaは別物。schema API自体はDDL文字列を許す。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.schema.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.schema.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.schema.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.csv.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"9b6c494f50f06a798d61c3bb572ca84539f1cf7828248445a3a99a98805b52ce","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"schemaはDDL文字列にも対応する。誤りはschema変数名を文字列にしている点。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameReader.schema.html"],"original_stem":"The code block below contains an error. It is intended to read a CSV file from filePath into a DataFrame using the predefined schema schema.\n\nCode block:\n\n```\nspark.read.schema(\"schema\").csv(filePath)\n```\n\nWhat is the error in this code?","original_options":["A.\nThe `schema()` method of `read` expects a schema object, not a string, so the argument should be `schema`.","B.\n`DataFrameReader` does not support the `csv()` method, so it should be replaced with `load()`.","C.\n`spark.read` must be followed by parentheses to return a `DataFrameReader` object.","D.\nThere is no `read` property on `spark`; `spark` should be replaced with `DataFrame`.","E.\nThe `schema()` method of `read` expects a column, not a string, so the argument should be `col(\"schema\")`."],"original_correct":["A"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"schema-variable-vs-ddl-literal","decision_id":"schema-variable-vs-ddl-literal"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-120","number":120,"stem":"Which of the following Spark execution/deployment modes runs the driver and tasks locally on a single machine, without a separate distributed cluster?","options":["A.\nStandard mode","B.\nCluster mode","C.\nLocal mode","D.\nAll of these responses require executors to run on a single worker node.","E.\nClient mode"],"correct":2,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"deployment-mode","concept":"deployment-mode","concept_label":"deployment-mode","explanation_ja":"local modeではdriverとtaskを単一マシンで動かす。別々のworkerノードを必要としない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/submitting-applications.html#master-urls","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/submitting-applications.html#master-urls","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/submitting-applications.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"f2af964cb25beb39755c4f879c7aebbd00d71d751cdde538cdefa8c64ed2c4e8","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"local modeはdriverとtaskを単一マシンの同一プロセスで実行するモード。worker上に独立executorsが必要という誤解を修正。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/submitting-applications.html#master-urls"],"original_stem":"Which of the following Spark execution/deployment modes requires that all executors run on a single worker node?","original_options":["A.\nStandard mode","B.\nCluster mode","C.\nLocal mode","D.\nAll of these responses require executors to run on a single worker node.","E.\nClient mode"],"original_correct":["C"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"local-mode-single-machine","decision_id":"local-mode-single-machine"}]},{"id":"EX4-S7","title":"原文セット 7","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-121","number":121,"stem":"Which of the following code snippets returns a DataFrame sorted in ascending order, ensuring that missing (null) values appear first, based on the column sqft?","options":["A.\nstoresDF.orderBy(col(\"sqft\").asc_nulls_last())","B.\nstoresDF.orderBy(asc_nulls_first(col(\"sqft\")))","C.\nstoresDF.orderBy(col(\"sqft\").asc().nulls_first())","D.\nstoresDF.orderBy(col(\"sqft\").asc_nulls_first())","E.\nstoresDF.orderBy(col(\"sqft\").nulls_first())"],"correct":1,"correct_many":[1,3],"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"sort-null","concept":"sort-null","concept_label":"sort-null","explanation_ja":"関数版とColumnメソッド版のasc_nulls_firstはいずれも使える。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.asc_nulls_first.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.asc_nulls_first.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.Column.asc_nulls_first.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.Column.asc_nulls_first.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.Column.asc_nulls_last.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/column.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"f6fba258d5e0e86e98717a4b4201f53e162410c04bf01b8a9da3ee8f2380aa42","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"asc_nulls_first関数とColumnメソッドのどちらも有効。BとDが正解。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.asc_nulls_first.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.Column.asc_nulls_first.html"],"original_stem":"Which of the following code snippets returns a DataFrame sorted in ascending order, ensuring that missing (null) values appear first, based on the column sqft?","original_options":["A.\nstoresDF.orderBy(col(\"sqft\").asc_nulls_last())","B.\nstoresDF.orderBy(asc_nulls_first(col(\"sqft\")))","C.\nstoresDF.orderBy(col(\"sqft\").asc().nulls_first())","D.\nstoresDF.orderBy(col(\"sqft\").asc_nulls_first())","E.\nstoresDF.orderBy(col(\"sqft\").nulls_first())"],"original_correct":["D"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"sort-null","decision_id":"sort-null"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-122","number":122,"stem":"Which option lists both equivalent operations that can be used to replace missing values in a specified column with a given value in a Spark DataFrame?","options":["A.\nDataFrame.na.fill()","B.\nDataFrame.nafill()","C.\nDataFrame.fillna()","D.\nDataFrame.na.fill() and DataFrame.nafill()","E.\nDataFrame.na.fill() and DataFrame.fillna()"],"correct":4,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"null-fill","concept":"null-fill","concept_label":"null-fill","explanation_ja":"DataFrame.fillnaとDataFrame.na.fillはエイリアス。nafillというメソッドはない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.fillna.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.fillna.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.fillna.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameNaFunctions.fill.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameNaFunctions.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"48d69800e3646347481dd4cd3faedebb197365e43d682a9092f5dd1d2d40c731","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"AとCも部分的には正しいため、対応する2つのエイリアスを両方列挙する選択肢を尋ねる。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.fillna.html"],"original_stem":"Which of the following operations can be used to replace missing values in a specified column with a given value in a Spark DataFrame?","original_options":["A.\nDataFrame.na.fill()","B.\nDataFrame.nafill()","C.\nDataFrame.fillna()","D.\nDataFrame.na.fill() and DataFrame.nafill()","E.\nDataFrame.na.fill() and DataFrame.fillna()"],"original_correct":["E"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"null-fill-api","decision_id":"null-fill-api"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-123","number":123,"stem":"Which of the following code snippets returns a DataFrame in which every row is unique?","options":["A.\nstoresDF.getDistinct()","B.\nstoresDF.duplicates.drop()","C.\nstoresDF.removeDuplicates()","D.\nstoresDF.distinct()","E.\nstoresDF.duplicates()"],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"dedup-all","concept":"dedup-all","concept_label":"dedup-all","explanation_ja":"distinctで全列が同じ行をまとめる。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.distinct.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D\n\n`storesDF.distinct()`\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe `distinct()` method in Spark DataFrames returns a new DataFrame that contains only unique rows, removing duplicate rows across all columns.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nstoresDF.distinct()\n```\n\nThis operation scans the dataset and removes rows that have identical values across all columns, ensuring that every row in the resulting DataFrame is unique.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.distinct.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.distinct.html)\n\nFoundational concept\n\nSpark DataFrames may contain duplicate rows due to:\n\n- \nData ingestion from multiple sources\n\n- \nJoins or unions\n\n- \nData replication during transformations\n\nTo ensure uniqueness, Spark provides two main operations:\n\n- \n`distinct()` → removes duplicate rows across all columns\n\n- \n`dropDuplicates()` → removes duplicates based on specific columns\n\nExample of `dropDuplicates()`:\n\n```\ndf.dropDuplicates([\"storeId\"])\n```\n\nHowever, the question asks for every row to be unique, which means all columns must be considered, making `distinct()` the correct operation.\n\nWhy the other options are not suitable\n\nA. `storesDF.getDistinct()`\n\nThis method does not exist in the Spark DataFrame API. The correct method is `distinct()`.\n\nB. `storesDF.duplicates.drop()`\n\nThere is no `duplicates` attribute or object in Spark DataFrames. Duplicate removal is handled by `distinct()` or `dropDuplicates()`.\n\nC. `storesDF.removeDuplicates()`\n\nThis method does not exist in the Spark DataFrame API. Spark uses `dropDuplicates()` instead.\n\nDocumentation reference for removing duplicates:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicates.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicates.html)\n\nE. `storesDF.duplicates()`\n\nThis method is also not part of the Spark DataFrame API.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nTo ensure every row in a Spark DataFrame is unique, use:\n\n```\ndf.distinct()\n```\n\nIf uniqueness is required based on specific columns only, use:\n\n```\ndf.dropDuplicates([\"column1\", \"column2\"])\n```\n\nUnderstanding the difference between global row uniqueness (`distinct`) and column-based deduplication (`dropDuplicates`) is an important concept frequently tested in the Databricks Certified Associate Developer for Apache Spark exam.","source_option_explanations":["A. `storesDF.getDistinct()`\n\nThis method does not exist in the Spark DataFrame API. The correct method is `distinct()`.","B. `storesDF.duplicates.drop()`\n\nThere is no `duplicates` attribute or object in Spark DataFrames. Duplicate removal is handled by `distinct()` or `dropDuplicates()`.","C. `storesDF.removeDuplicates()`\n\nThis method does not exist in the Spark DataFrame API. Spark uses `dropDuplicates()` instead.","Correct Answer: D\n\n`storesDF.distinct()`\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe `distinct()` method in Spark DataFrames returns a new DataFrame that contains only unique rows, removing duplicate rows across all columns.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nstoresDF.distinct()\n```\n\nThis operation scans the dataset and removes rows that have identical values across all columns, ensuring that every row in the resulting DataFrame is unique.","E. `storesDF.duplicates()`\n\nThis method is also not part of the Spark DataFrame API."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.distinct.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.dropDuplicates.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"580ee2cda93eaf5262832b60a46000197d7056ee4a985bd84aca121d242ac72b","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"all-column-dedup","decision_id":"all-column-dedup"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-124","number":124,"stem":"Which of the following code snippets efficiently performs a broadcast join between the DataFrame storesDF and the much larger DataFrame employeesDF using the join key storeId?","options":["A.\nstoresDF.join(broadcast(employeesDF), \"storeId\")","B.\nbroadcast(storesDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\"))","C.\nbroadcast(employeesDF.join(storesDF, \"storeId\"))","D.\nemployeesDF.join(broadcast(storesDF), \"storeId\")","E.\nstoresDF.broadcastJoin(employeesDF, \"storeId\")"],"correct":3,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"broadcast-choice","concept":"broadcast-choice","concept_label":"broadcast-choice","explanation_ja":"十分小さいstoresDFをbroadcastして大きいemployeesDFへjoinする。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D\n\n`employeesDF.join(broadcast(storesDF), \"storeId\")`\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nA broadcast join is used when one DataFrame is much smaller than the other. Instead of shuffling both datasets across the cluster, Spark broadcasts the smaller DataFrame to all executor nodes, allowing each executor to join locally with partitions of the larger DataFrame. This significantly reduces shuffle cost and improves performance.\n\nIn this scenario:\n\n- \n`storesDF` = small DataFrame\n\n- \n`employeesDF` = much larger DataFrame\n\nTherefore, Spark should broadcast `storesDF`, not the larger dataset.\n\nThe correct implementation is:\n\n```\nemployeesDF.join(broadcast(storesDF), \"storeId\")\n```\n\nThis explicitly instructs Spark to broadcast `storesDF` to all workers and perform the join against partitions of `employeesDF`.\n\nDocumentation reference:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries)\n\nFoundational concept\n\nSpark normally performs joins using shuffle joins, where both DataFrames are repartitioned across the cluster based on the join key. This can be expensive.\n\nA broadcast hash join works differently:\n\n- \nThe small dataset is copied to every executor.\n\n- \nEach executor performs a local hash join with its partition of the large dataset.\n\n- \nNo shuffle is required for the large dataset, making the join far more efficient.\n\nSpark can automatically broadcast small tables if they fall under `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold`, but using `broadcast()` forces the optimization explicitly.\n\nWhy the other options are not suitable\n\nA. `storesDF.join(broadcast(employeesDF), \"storeId\")`\n\nThis attempts to broadcast `employeesDF`, which is the much larger DataFrame. Broadcasting large datasets is inefficient and may exceed memory limits. Broadcast joins should only broadcast the smaller dataset.\n\nB. `broadcast(storesDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\"))`\n\nThis broadcasts the result of the join, not one of the join inputs. The broadcast hint must be applied before the join to influence the join strategy.\n\nC. `broadcast(employeesDF.join(storesDF, \"storeId\"))`\n\nLike option B, this broadcasts the final joined DataFrame, which does not change the join execution plan and therefore does not create a broadcast join.\n\nE. `storesDF.broadcastJoin(employeesDF, \"storeId\")`\n\nThere is no `broadcastJoin()` method in the Spark DataFrame API. Broadcast joins are implemented using the `broadcast()` function from `pyspark.sql.functions`.\n\nDocumentation reference:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.broadcast.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.broadcast.html)\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nFor a broadcast join in Spark:\n\n- \nBroadcast the smaller DataFrame\n\n- \nJoin it with the larger one\n\nTypical pattern:\n\n```\nlargeDF.join(broadcast(smallDF), \"key\")\n```\n\nThis pattern is frequently tested in the Databricks Certified Associate Developer for Apache Spark exam because it reflects an important Spark performance optimization technique.","source_option_explanations":["A. `storesDF.join(broadcast(employeesDF), \"storeId\")`\n\nThis attempts to broadcast `employeesDF`, which is the much larger DataFrame. Broadcasting large datasets is inefficient and may exceed memory limits. Broadcast joins should only broadcast the smaller dataset.","B. `broadcast(storesDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\"))`\n\nThis broadcasts the result of the join, not one of the join inputs. The broadcast hint must be applied before the join to influence the join strategy.","C. `broadcast(employeesDF.join(storesDF, \"storeId\"))`\n\nLike option B, this broadcasts the final joined DataFrame, which does not change the join execution plan and therefore does not create a broadcast join.","Correct Answer: D\n\n`employeesDF.join(broadcast(storesDF), \"storeId\")`\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nA broadcast join is used when one DataFrame is much smaller than the other. Instead of shuffling both datasets across the cluster, Spark broadcasts the smaller DataFrame to all executor nodes, allowing each executor to join locally with partitions of the larger DataFrame. This significantly reduces shuffle cost and improves performance.\n\nIn this scenario:\n\n- \n`storesDF` = small DataFrame\n\n- \n`employeesDF` = much larger DataFrame\n\nTherefore, Spark should broadcast `storesDF`, not the larger dataset.\n\nThe correct implementation is:\n\n```\nemployeesDF.join(broadcast(storesDF), \"storeId\")\n```\n\nThis explicitly instructs Spark to broadcast `storesDF` to all workers and perform the join against partitions of `employeesDF`.","E. `storesDF.broadcastJoin(employeesDF, \"storeId\")`\n\nThere is no `broadcastJoin()` method in the Spark DataFrame API. Broadcast joins are implemented using the `broadcast()` function from `pyspark.sql.functions`."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#broadcast-hint-for-sql-queries","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.broadcast.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"37241bcc6b34b06101973dbbb4265f6dcac91d75c5c54aab41362ff727df1a1e","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"broadcast-small-side","decision_id":"hint-broadcast-correct-small-side"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-125","number":125,"stem":"Which of the following operations can be used to return all rows from a Spark DataFrame?","options":["A.\nDataFrame.count()","B.\nDataFrame.head()","C.\nDataFrame.collect()","D.\nDataFrame.show()","E.\nDataFrame.take()"],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"collect-rows","concept":"collect-rows","concept_label":"collect-rows","explanation_ja":"collectは全行をdriverへ返す。大きいデータではメモリ容量に注意する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.collect.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C\n\n`DataFrame.collect()`\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\n`collect()` returns all rows from a Spark DataFrame to the driver program as a list of Row objects.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nrows = df.collect()\n```\n\nThis triggers a Spark action that executes the entire query plan and retrieves every row in the DataFrame from all partitions across the cluster.\n\nBecause it returns the entire dataset, it should only be used when the dataset is small enough to fit in the driver’s memory.\n\nDocumentation reference:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.collect.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.collect.html)\n\nFoundational concept\n\nSpark operations fall into two categories:\n\nTransformations\n\n- \nReturn new DataFrames\n\n- \nLazy evaluation (not executed immediately)\n\nActions\n\n- \nTrigger execution\n\n- \nReturn results to the driver or external storage\n\n`collect()` is an action that gathers the entire distributed dataset from executors to the driver.\n\nThis is a common exam concept because retrieving large datasets with `collect()` can cause driver memory overflow.\n\nWhy the other options are not suitable\n\nA. `DataFrame.count()`\n\n`count()` returns only the number of rows, not the rows themselves. It performs a distributed aggregation and returns a single integer.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.count.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.count.html)\n\nB. `DataFrame.head()`\n\n`head()` returns only the first row (or first n rows if specified), not all rows.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.head(5)\n```\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.head.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.head.html)\n\nD. `DataFrame.show()`\n\n`show()` prints rows to the console for inspection but does not return the rows programmatically. It is mainly used for debugging and visualization.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.show.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.show.html)\n\nE. `DataFrame.take()`\n\n`take(n)` returns only the first `n` rows, not the entire dataset.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.take(10)\n```\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.take.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.take.html)\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nTo retrieve all rows from a Spark DataFrame to the driver, use:\n\n```\ndf.collect()\n```\n\nHowever, in production environments this is often avoided for large datasets because it moves all distributed data into the driver’s memory, which can lead to performance or memory issues.","source_option_explanations":["A. `DataFrame.count()`\n\n`count()` returns only the number of rows, not the rows themselves. It performs a distributed aggregation and returns a single integer.","B. `DataFrame.head()`\n\n`head()` returns only the first row (or first n rows if specified), not all rows.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.head(5)\n```","Correct Answer: C\n\n`DataFrame.collect()`\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\n`collect()` returns all rows from a Spark DataFrame to the driver program as a list of Row objects.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nrows = df.collect()\n```\n\nThis triggers a Spark action that executes the entire query plan and retrieves every row in the DataFrame from all partitions across the cluster.\n\nBecause it returns the entire dataset, it should only be used when the dataset is small enough to fit in the driver’s memory.",null,"E. `DataFrame.take()`\n\n`take(n)` returns only the first `n` rows, not the entire dataset.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.take(10)\n```"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.collect.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.count.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.head.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.show.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.take.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"e1702031ee02fc192d23864954ff1b177872d9b63cb45bd47e5e73d546350c67","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"collect-all-rows-action","decision_id":"collect-all-rows-action"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-126","number":126,"stem":"Which of the following code snippets fails to return a new DataFrame representing the result of an inner join between storesDF and employeesDF using the columns storeId and employeeId?","options":["A.\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, [col(\"storeId\"), col(\"employeeId\")])","B.\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, [storesDF.storeId == employeesDF.storeId, storesDF.employeeId == employeesDF.employeeId])","C.\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, [\"storeId\", \"employeeId\"])","D.\nstoresDF.alias(\"s\").join(employeesDF.alias(\"e\"), [col(\"s.storeId\") == col(\"e.storeId\"), col(\"s.employeeId\") == col(\"e.employeeId\")])","E.\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, [\"storeId\", \"employeeId\"], \"inner\")"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"join-multikey","concept":"join-multikey","concept_label":"join-multikey","explanation_ja":"条件にColumnを使う場合はboolean等値式が必要。曖昧な同名列参照も避ける。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\n`storesDF.join(employeesDF, [col(\"storeId\"), col(\"employeeId\")])`\n\nThis snippet fails because the `join()` API expects either:\n\n- \nColumn equality expressions (boolean conditions), or\n\n- \nA list of column names as strings when the columns exist in both DataFrames.\n\nIn option A, the list contains Column objects (`col(\"storeId\")`) instead of equality expressions, which Spark cannot interpret as join conditions. Spark requires expressions such as:\n\n```\nstoresDF.storeId == employeesDF.storeId\n```\n\nor simply the column names:\n\n```\n[\"storeId\", \"employeeId\"]\n```\n\nSince `col(\"storeId\")` and `col(\"employeeId\")` alone are not boolean expressions, the join condition is invalid and the code fails.\n\nDocumentation reference:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html)\n\nFoundational concept\n\nSpark DataFrame joins accept the `on` parameter in two main forms:\n\n1. List of column names (for natural equality joins)\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, [\"col1\", \"col2\"])\n```\n\nSpark automatically performs:\n\n```\ndf1.col1 = df2.col1 AND df1.col2 = df2.col2\n```\n\n2. Boolean join expressions\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, (df1.col1 == df2.col1) & (df1.col2 == df2.col2))\n```\n\nor\n\n```\n[df1.col1 == df2.col1, df1.col2 == df2.col2]\n```\n\nWhat cannot be used are plain column references without equality comparisons.\n\nWhy the other options are valid\n\nB.\n\n`storesDF.join(employeesDF, [storesDF.storeId == employeesDF.storeId, storesDF.employeeId == employeesDF.employeeId])`\n\nThis works because the list contains boolean equality expressions, which Spark treats as join conditions.\n\nC.\n\n`storesDF.join(employeesDF, [\"storeId\", \"employeeId\"])`\n\nThis is the most concise syntax when the join keys share the same name in both DataFrames. Spark automatically performs equality joins on those columns.\n\nD.\n\n`storesDF.alias(\"s\").join(employeesDF.alias(\"e\"), [col(\"s.storeId\") == col(\"e.storeId\"), col(\"s.employeeId\") == col(\"e.employeeId\")])`\n\nThis works correctly because:\n\n- \nDataFrames are aliased\n\n- \nJoin conditions are explicit equality expressions\n\nAliases are commonly used to avoid column ambiguity.\n\nE.\n\n`storesDF.join(employeesDF, [\"storeId\", \"employeeId\"], \"inner\")`\n\nThis is valid and explicitly specifies the inner join type. Since the default join type is already `inner`, this produces the expected result.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nSpark joins require the join condition to be either:\n\n- \nColumn name strings (for equality joins on same-named columns), or\n\n- \nBoolean equality expressions\n\nPlain column references like:\n\n```\n[col(\"storeId\"), col(\"employeeId\")]\n```\n\nare not valid join conditions and will cause the join to fail.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\n`storesDF.join(employeesDF, [col(\"storeId\"), col(\"employeeId\")])`\n\nThis snippet fails because the `join()` API expects either:\n\n- \nColumn equality expressions (boolean conditions), or\n\n- \nA list of column names as strings when the columns exist in both DataFrames.\n\nIn option A, the list contains Column objects (`col(\"storeId\")`) instead of equality expressions, which Spark cannot interpret as join conditions. Spark requires expressions such as:\n\n```\nstoresDF.storeId == employeesDF.storeId\n```\n\nor simply the column names:\n\n```\n[\"storeId\", \"employeeId\"]\n```\n\nSince `col(\"storeId\")` and `col(\"employeeId\")` alone are not boolean expressions, the join condition is invalid and the code fails.","B.\n\n`storesDF.join(employeesDF, [storesDF.storeId == employeesDF.storeId, storesDF.employeeId == employeesDF.employeeId])`\n\nThis works because the list contains boolean equality expressions, which Spark treats as join conditions.","C.\n\n`storesDF.join(employeesDF, [\"storeId\", \"employeeId\"])`\n\nThis is the most concise syntax when the join keys share the same name in both DataFrames. Spark automatically performs equality joins on those columns.","D.\n\n`storesDF.alias(\"s\").join(employeesDF.alias(\"e\"), [col(\"s.storeId\") == col(\"e.storeId\"), col(\"s.employeeId\") == col(\"e.employeeId\")])`\n\nThis works correctly because:\n\n- \nDataFrames are aliased\n\n- \nJoin conditions are explicit equality expressions\n\nAliases are commonly used to avoid column ambiguity.","E.\n\n`storesDF.join(employeesDF, [\"storeId\", \"employeeId\"], \"inner\")`\n\nThis is valid and explicitly specifies the inner join type. Since the default join type is already `inner`, this produces the expected result."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"9c044ec344cee2766ffa77959861e1504c279ca125d4661bd0af8df04bdf52a1","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"multi-key-equi-join","decision_id":"multi-key-equi-join"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-127","number":127,"stem":"Assume a multi-node Spark cluster-mode deployment with a dedicated driver node and separate worker nodes hosting executors. Under these stated assumptions, which of the following statements about cluster nodes is incorrect?","options":["A.\nThe Spark driver runs on its own node and does not host executors.","B.\nEach executor runs as a processing engine within a worker node.","C.\nThere can be more executors than nodes, or more nodes than executors, depending on configuration.","D.\nThere is always more than one node in the cluster.","E.\nThere is a single node that contains both the Spark driver and the executors."],"correct":4,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"deployment-mode","concept":"deployment-mode","concept_label":"deployment-mode","explanation_ja":"driver専用ノードと別workerという設問の前提に対し、全処理を単一ノードとするEが不整合。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/cluster-overview.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/cluster-overview.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html#components","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/configuration.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"078dc5670eb019b7c82003a4938b591ed0e80f374bcab2014bb712d3dad14b83","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"cluster modeだけではdriver専用ノードや複数ノードを保証しない。設問の構成条件を明示。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/cluster-overview.html"],"original_stem":"If Apache Spark is running in cluster mode, which of the following statements about cluster nodes is incorrect?","original_options":["A.\nThe Spark driver runs on its own node and does not host executors.","B.\nEach executor runs as a processing engine within a worker node.","C.\nThere can be more executors than nodes, or more nodes than executors, depending on configuration.","D.\nThere is always more than one node in the cluster.","E.\nThere is a single node that contains both the Spark driver and the executors."],"original_correct":["E"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"deployment-mode","decision_id":"deployment-mode"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-128","number":128,"stem":"The code block below should return a new DataFrame from `storesDF` where the column numberOfManagers is assigned the constant integer value 1.\n\nFill in the numbered blanks to correctly complete the code.\n\nCode block:\n\n```\nstoresDF._1_(_2_, _3_(_4_))\n```","options":["A.\n\nwithColumn\n\n\"numberOfManagers\"\n\nlit\n\n1","B.\n\nwithColumn\n\n\"numberOfManagers\"\n\nIntegerType\n\n1","C.\n\nnewColumn\n\nnumberOfManagers\n\nIntegerType\n\n1","D.\n\nwithColumn\n\n\"numberOfManagers\"\n\ncol\n\n1","E.\n\n1. withColumn\n\n2. \"numberOfManagers\"\n\n3. lit\n\n4. \"1\""],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"column-literal","concept":"column-literal","concept_label":"column-literal","explanation_ja":"定数1を列にするのはlit(1)。litの文字列1は整数1とは型が異なる。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\n`withColumn`\n`\"numberOfManagers\"`\n`lit`\n`1`\n\nCorrect completed code:\n\n```\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"numberOfManagers\", lit(1))\n```\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Spark, the `withColumn()` method is used to create a new column or replace an existing column in a DataFrame. The method requires:\n\n- \nThe column name (as a string)\n\n- \nA Column expression\n\nSince we want to assign a constant value, Spark requires the constant to be converted into a Column object using the `lit()` function.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- \n`withColumn` → adds the column\n\n- \n`\"numberOfManagers\"` → name of the new column\n\n- \n`lit(1)` → creates a column containing the constant integer value `1`\n\nDocumentation reference:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html)\n\nThe `lit()` function converts a literal value into a Spark Column expression.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.lit.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.lit.html)\n\nFoundational concept\n\nSpark DataFrames are immutable. Any transformation like `withColumn()` does not modify the original DataFrame. Instead, Spark returns a new DataFrame with the transformation applied.\n\nAdding constant values requires `lit()` because Spark operations must operate on Column objects, not raw Python values.\n\nWhy the other options are not suitable\n\nB.\n\n`IntegerType` is a data type, not a column expression. `withColumn()` requires a Column, so this would fail.\n\nDocumentation reference for Spark SQL data types:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-datatypes.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-datatypes.html)\n\nC.\n\n`newColumn` is not a valid DataFrame API method in Spark. The correct method to add or replace a column is `withColumn()`.\n\nD.\n\n`col(1)` is invalid because the `col()` function expects a column name string, not a literal value. It is used to reference existing columns.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.col.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.col.html)\n\nE.\n\n`lit(\"1\")` would assign a string value `\"1\"`, not the required integer value `1`. This changes the column type to string and does not satisfy the requirement.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nWhen adding a constant value column in Spark:\n\n- \nUse `withColumn()` to add the column\n\n- \nUse `lit()` to convert literals into Spark Column expressions\n\nCommon pattern:\n\n```\ndf.withColumn(\"newColumn\", lit(value))\n```","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\n`withColumn`\n`\"numberOfManagers\"`\n`lit`\n`1`\n\nCorrect completed code:\n\n```\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"numberOfManagers\", lit(1))\n```\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Spark, the `withColumn()` method is used to create a new column or replace an existing column in a DataFrame. The method requires:\n\n- \nThe column name (as a string)\n\n- \nA Column expression\n\nSince we want to assign a constant value, Spark requires the constant to be converted into a Column object using the `lit()` function.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- \n`withColumn` → adds the column\n\n- \n`\"numberOfManagers\"` → name of the new column\n\n- \n`lit(1)` → creates a column containing the constant integer value `1`","B.\n\n`IntegerType` is a data type, not a column expression. `withColumn()` requires a Column, so this would fail.","C.\n\n`newColumn` is not a valid DataFrame API method in Spark. The correct method to add or replace a column is `withColumn()`.","D.\n\n`col(1)` is invalid because the `col()` function expects a column name string, not a literal value. It is used to reference existing columns.","E.\n\n`lit(\"1\")` would assign a string value `\"1\"`, not the required integer value `1`. This changes the column type to string and does not satisfy the requirement."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.lit.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-datatypes.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.col.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"e159e15ff8304da8d97f6bdcbc8dd35ee62b2d4d28d997d96b4d8231fae5029d","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"with-column-integer-literal","decision_id":"with-column-integer-literal"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-129","number":129,"stem":"Which option lists both valid aliases that can be used to sort the rows of a Spark DataFrame?","options":["A.\nsort() and orderBy()","B.\norderBy()","C.\nsort()","D.\nsort() and orderby()","E.\norderby()"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"sort-api","concept":"sort-api","concept_label":"sort-api","explanation_ja":"sortとorderByはいずれも有効。orderbyは大文字小文字が違う別名ではない。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.sort.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.sort.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.sort.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"06816e4a30e1be0d0331743e7c59a2aa20721303ce2e9a1e3f90bf648ad690a7","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"sortとorderByは両方有効。両エイリアスを列挙する選択肢という問いに明確化。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.sort.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html"],"original_stem":"Which of the following operations can be used to sort the rows of a Spark DataFrame?","original_options":["A.\nsort() and orderBy()","B.\norderBy()","C.\nsort()","D.\nsort() and orderby()","E.\norderby()"],"original_correct":["A"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"sort-orderby-valid-aliases","decision_id":"sort-orderby-valid-aliases"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-130","number":130,"stem":"Which of the following is an advantage of lazy evaluation in Apache Spark?","options":["A.\nSpark jobs can be better optimized because all transformations are known before execution begins.","B.\nSpark jobs become less complex because they do not execute every operation in the program.","C.\nSpark jobs run faster because Spark avoids distributing small tasks across the cluster due to cluster overhead.","D.\nSpark jobs are less likely to fail because execution does not start until the job finishes compiling.","E.\nSpark jobs run faster due to increased communication between the Spark driver and worker nodes."],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"lazy-execution","concept":"lazy-execution","concept_label":"lazy-execution","explanation_ja":"lazy evaluationによりaction前に変換全体を把握して最適化できる。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations-and-actions","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\nSpark jobs can be better optimized because all transformations are known before execution begins.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nApache Spark uses lazy evaluation, meaning transformations such as `select`, `filter`, `join`, or `withColumn` are not executed immediately. Instead, Spark records them in a logical plan and builds a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) representing the full computation.\n\nExecution begins only when an action (such as `collect()`, `count()`, or `write`) is triggered. Because Spark knows the entire chain of transformations before execution, it can apply optimizations such as:\n\n• Eliminating unnecessary computations\n• Reordering operations for efficiency\n• Combining multiple transformations into a single stage\n• Reducing data shuffling across the cluster\n\nThese optimizations are performed by Spark’s Catalyst Optimizer and the DAG Scheduler, resulting in more efficient execution.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations-and-actions](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations-and-actions)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark operations fall into two categories:\n\nTransformations (lazy)\nExamples: `filter`, `map`, `select`, `join`, `withColumn`\nThese define computation but do not execute immediately.\n\nActions (trigger execution)\nExamples: `collect`, `count`, `take`, `save`\nThese trigger execution of the entire DAG.\n\nBecause Spark delays execution until an action is called, it can analyze the full pipeline and optimize it before running the job, which is the main advantage of lazy evaluation.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nB. Spark jobs become less complex because they do not execute every operation in the program.\n\nThis is incorrect. Lazy evaluation does not remove operations. All required transformations still execute when an action is triggered.\n\nC. Spark jobs run faster because Spark avoids distributing small tasks across the cluster due to cluster overhead.\n\nLazy evaluation does not specifically avoid distributing small tasks. Performance gains come from query optimization and pipeline execution, not task distribution avoidance.\n\nD. Spark jobs are less likely to fail because execution does not start until the job finishes compiling.\n\nSpark jobs are not “compiled” in this sense. Lazy evaluation delays execution but does not inherently reduce failure probability.\n\nE. Spark jobs run faster due to increased communication between the Spark driver and worker nodes.\n\nThis is incorrect because increased communication typically slows distributed systems. Spark optimizes performance by reducing communication and shuffles, not increasing them.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nLazy evaluation allows Spark to:\n\n• Build a complete DAG of transformations\n• Apply global query optimization before execution\n• Execute jobs more efficiently with fewer stages and less data movement\n\nThis optimization capability is a core architectural advantage of Spark and a frequently tested concept in the Databricks Certified Associate Developer for Apache Spark exam.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\nSpark jobs can be better optimized because all transformations are known before execution begins.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nApache Spark uses lazy evaluation, meaning transformations such as `select`, `filter`, `join`, or `withColumn` are not executed immediately. Instead, Spark records them in a logical plan and builds a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) representing the full computation.\n\nExecution begins only when an action (such as `collect()`, `count()`, or `write`) is triggered. Because Spark knows the entire chain of transformations before execution, it can apply optimizations such as:\n\n• Eliminating unnecessary computations\n• Reordering operations for efficiency\n• Combining multiple transformations into a single stage\n• Reducing data shuffling across the cluster\n\nThese optimizations are performed by Spark’s Catalyst Optimizer and the DAG Scheduler, resulting in more efficient execution.","B. Spark jobs become less complex because they do not execute every operation in the program.\n\nThis is incorrect. Lazy evaluation does not remove operations. All required transformations still execute when an action is triggered.","C. Spark jobs run faster because Spark avoids distributing small tasks across the cluster due to cluster overhead.\n\nLazy evaluation does not specifically avoid distributing small tasks. Performance gains come from query optimization and pipeline execution, not task distribution avoidance.","D. Spark jobs are less likely to fail because execution does not start until the job finishes compiling.\n\nSpark jobs are not “compiled” in this sense. Lazy evaluation delays execution but does not inherently reduce failure probability.",null],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#transformations-and-actions"],"images":[],"source_hash":"eb08c68cc857e555b6a5064c25a3c3623b496c40178f1054ca9d5de4b49e3abc","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"lazy-action-trigger","decision_id":"lazy-action-trigger"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-131","number":131,"stem":"Which of the following code snippets fails to return a DataFrame sorted alphabetically based on the column division?","options":["A.\nstoresDF.sort(asc(\"division\"))","B.\nstoresDF.orderBy([\"division\"], ascending=[1])","C.\nstoresDF.orderBy(col(\"division\").desc())","D.\nstoresDF.orderBy(\"division\")","E.\nstoresDF.sort(\"division\")"],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"sort-direction","concept":"sort-direction","concept_label":"sort-direction","explanation_ja":"アルファベット昇順に対してdescは逆方向。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.desc.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\n`storesDF.orderBy(col(\"division\").desc())` does not return a DataFrame sorted alphabetically in ascending order. Instead, it sorts the column in descending order, which means the values would appear in reverse alphabetical order.\n\nExample behavior:\n\n```\nstoresDF.orderBy(col(\"division\").desc())\n```\n\nThis sorts values like:\n\nZ → Y → X → … → A\n\nHowever, the requirement is alphabetical order, which corresponds to ascending order.\n\nSpark documentation confirms that `desc()` explicitly specifies descending sorting for a column expression.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.desc.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.desc.html)\n\nFoundational concept\n\nSpark DataFrames can be sorted using either `sort()` or `orderBy()`, which are interchangeable methods.\n\nAlphabetical sorting of strings follows the same rules as ascending sorting, meaning:\n\n- \nAscending order (A → Z) → alphabetical order\n\n- \nDescending order (Z → A) → reverse alphabetical order\n\nTypical ascending sort examples:\n\n```\ndf.orderBy(\"division\")\ndf.sort(\"division\")\ndf.orderBy(col(\"division\").asc())\n```\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html)\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA. `storesDF.sort(asc(\"division\"))`\n\nThis correctly sorts the column in ascending order, which corresponds to alphabetical sorting.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.asc.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.asc.html)\n\nB. `storesDF.orderBy([\"division\"], ascending=[1])`\n\nThis is valid syntax. The parameter `ascending=[1]` means True, so the DataFrame is sorted in ascending alphabetical order.\n\nD. `storesDF.orderBy(\"division\")`\n\nThis sorts the column in ascending order by default, which produces alphabetical ordering.\n\nE. `storesDF.sort(\"division\")`\n\n`sort()` is an alias for `orderBy()` and also sorts ascending by default, producing alphabetical order.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nIn Spark:\n\n- \nAscending sorting (`asc()` or default) → alphabetical order for strings\n\n- \nDescending sorting (`desc()`) → reverse alphabetical order\n\nTherefore, the only snippet that fails to produce alphabetical sorting is the one using `desc()`, which is option C.","source_option_explanations":["A. `storesDF.sort(asc(\"division\"))`\n\nThis correctly sorts the column in ascending order, which corresponds to alphabetical sorting.","B. `storesDF.orderBy([\"division\"], ascending=[1])`\n\nThis is valid syntax. The parameter `ascending=[1]` means True, so the DataFrame is sorted in ascending alphabetical order.","Correct Answer: C\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\n`storesDF.orderBy(col(\"division\").desc())` does not return a DataFrame sorted alphabetically in ascending order. Instead, it sorts the column in descending order, which means the values would appear in reverse alphabetical order.\n\nExample behavior:\n\n```\nstoresDF.orderBy(col(\"division\").desc())\n```\n\nThis sorts values like:\n\nZ → Y → X → … → A\n\nHowever, the requirement is alphabetical order, which corresponds to ascending order.\n\nSpark documentation confirms that `desc()` explicitly specifies descending sorting for a column expression.","D. `storesDF.orderBy(\"division\")`\n\nThis sorts the column in ascending order by default, which produces alphabetical ordering.","E. `storesDF.sort(\"division\")`\n\n`sort()` is an alias for `orderBy()` and also sorts ascending by default, producing alphabetical order."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.desc.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.asc.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"2876bf67a2913542a2a14aa8123b3fc890e4b53cfad8d3d55fc3ba0e69113717","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"sort-direction","decision_id":"sort-direction"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-132","number":132,"stem":"The following code block should return a new DataFrame with 4 partitions from the existing 8-partition DataFrame `storesDF`, without causing a shuffle.\n\nFill in the blanks to correctly complete the code.\n\nCode block:\n\n```\n_1_._2_(_3_)\n```","options":["A.\n\n1. storesDF\n\n2. coalesce\n\n3. `Nothing","B.\n\n1. storesDF\n\n2. coalesce\n\n3. 4","C.\n\n1. storesDF\n\n2. coalesce\n\n3.  4, \"storeId\"","D.\n\n1. storesDF\n\n2. coalesce\n\n3. \"storeId\""],"correct":1,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"partition-resize","concept":"partition-resize","concept_label":"partition-resize","explanation_ja":"shuffleせず8から4へ減らすならcoalesce(4)。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B\n\n1. storesDF\n2. coalesce\n3. 4\n\nCorrect completed code:\n\n```\nstoresDF.coalesce(4)\n```\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe requirement is to reduce the number of partitions from 8 to 4 without causing a shuffle. In Apache Spark, the `coalesce()` method is specifically designed for this purpose.\n\n`coalesce(n)` reduces the number of partitions without triggering a full data shuffle, making it more efficient when decreasing partitions.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nstoresDF.coalesce(4)\n```\n\nThis reduces the DataFrame to 4 partitions, while avoiding expensive data redistribution across the cluster.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark provides two primary methods to change the number of partitions:\n\ncoalesce()\n\n• Used mainly to reduce partitions\n• Avoids shuffle when decreasing partitions\n• More efficient for simple partition reduction\n\nrepartition()\n\n• Can increase or decrease partitions\n• Always triggers a full shuffle\n• Used when even distribution across partitions is required\n\nExample comparison:\n\n```\ndf.coalesce(4)      # reduce partitions without shuffle\ndf.repartition(4)   # reshuffle data across cluster\n```\n\nBecause the question explicitly states without causing a shuffle, `coalesce()` is the correct method.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA. `storesDF.coalesce(Nothing)`\n\nInvalid syntax. The method requires an integer representing the number of partitions.\n\nC. `storesDF.coalesce(4, \"storeId\")`\n\nIncorrect because `coalesce()` accepts only one argument, which is the target number of partitions. It does not support specifying columns.\n\nD. `storesDF.coalesce(\"storeId\")`\n\nIncorrect because the argument must be an integer, not a column name.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nTo reduce partitions without shuffle, use:\n\n```\ndf.coalesce(newPartitionCount)\n```\n\nTo increase partitions or force redistribution, use:\n\n```\ndf.repartition(newPartitionCount)\n```\n\nUnderstanding the difference between coalesce (no shuffle) and repartition (shuffle) is a commonly tested concept in the Databricks Certified Associate Developer for Apache Spark exam.","source_option_explanations":["A. `storesDF.coalesce(Nothing)`\n\nInvalid syntax. The method requires an integer representing the number of partitions.","Correct Answer: B\n\n1. storesDF\n2. coalesce\n3. 4\n\nCorrect completed code:\n\n```\nstoresDF.coalesce(4)\n```\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe requirement is to reduce the number of partitions from 8 to 4 without causing a shuffle. In Apache Spark, the `coalesce()` method is specifically designed for this purpose.\n\n`coalesce(n)` reduces the number of partitions without triggering a full data shuffle, making it more efficient when decreasing partitions.\n\nExample:\n\n```\nstoresDF.coalesce(4)\n```\n\nThis reduces the DataFrame to 4 partitions, while avoiding expensive data redistribution across the cluster.","C. `storesDF.coalesce(4, \"storeId\")`\n\nIncorrect because `coalesce()` accepts only one argument, which is the target number of partitions. It does not support specifying columns.","D. `storesDF.coalesce(\"storeId\")`\n\nIncorrect because the argument must be an integer, not a column name."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.coalesce.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"ca4679a3c4fa8d2fcaeedac6608517255f55dbedc59620d4dbc0796c9029f1e6","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"coalesce-repartition","decision_id":"coalesce-repartition"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-133","number":133,"stem":"Which of the following statements best describes a task in Apache Spark?","options":["A.\nA task is the smallest unit of work that can be executed on a single executor.","B.\nA task is a collection of slots located within the same executor.","C.\nA task is the smallest unit of work that can be performed without requiring a shuffle.","D.\nA task represents the highest level of execution in the Spark execution hierarchy.","E.\nA task is a combination of a block of data and a set of transformations executed on a single executor."],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"task-role","concept":"task-role","concept_label":"task-role","explanation_ja":"taskはスケジュールされる最小単位で、stageのpartitionに対する処理を担当する。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\nA task is the smallest unit of work that can be executed on a single executor.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Apache Spark, a task represents the smallest unit of work that Spark schedules for execution. Each task processes one partition of data and runs on a single executor core.\n\nSpark’s execution hierarchy generally follows this structure:\n\nApplication\n→ Job\n→ Stage\n→ Task\n\nWhen a stage is created, Spark divides the work into multiple tasks, with one task per partition. These tasks are then distributed across executor cores in the cluster.\n\nFor example:\n\nIf a DataFrame has 100 partitions, a stage will generate 100 tasks, each processing one partition in parallel across executors.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark distributes work based on data partitions.\n\nKey ideas:\n\n• Partition → a chunk of distributed data\n• Task → computation performed on one partition\n• Executor → process that runs multiple tasks\n• Core/slot → allows one task to run at a time\n\nThus:\n\nNumber of tasks in a stage ≈ number of partitions.\n\nThis design enables parallel processing across the cluster.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nB. A task is a collection of slots located within the same executor.\n\nIncorrect. Slots (CPU cores) allow an executor to run multiple tasks concurrently, but a task is not a collection of slots.\n\nC. A task is the smallest unit of work that can be performed without requiring a shuffle.\n\nIncorrect. Tasks can exist in shuffle stages as well. Shuffles affect stage boundaries, not the definition of a task.\n\nD. A task represents the highest level of execution in the Spark execution hierarchy.\n\nIncorrect. The highest level is the application, followed by jobs, stages, and then tasks.\n\nExecution hierarchy:\n\nApplication → Job → Stage → Task\n\nE. A task is a combination of a block of data and a set of transformations executed on a single executor.\n\nThis description is partially misleading. While tasks operate on partitions of data and execute transformations, the formal definition of a task is simply the smallest schedulable unit of work executed on an executor core.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nIn Spark:\n\n• Partition → determines number of tasks\n• Task → smallest executable unit\n• Stage → group of parallel tasks\n• Executor → runs tasks on worker nodes\n\nA simple rule often used in Spark architecture questions:\n\nNumber of tasks in a stage = number of partitions.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\nA task is the smallest unit of work that can be executed on a single executor.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Apache Spark, a task represents the smallest unit of work that Spark schedules for execution. Each task processes one partition of data and runs on a single executor core.\n\nSpark’s execution hierarchy generally follows this structure:\n\nApplication\n→ Job\n→ Stage\n→ Task\n\nWhen a stage is created, Spark divides the work into multiple tasks, with one task per partition. These tasks are then distributed across executor cores in the cluster.\n\nFor example:\n\nIf a DataFrame has 100 partitions, a stage will generate 100 tasks, each processing one partition in parallel across executors.","B. A task is a collection of slots located within the same executor.\n\nIncorrect. Slots (CPU cores) allow an executor to run multiple tasks concurrently, but a task is not a collection of slots.","C. A task is the smallest unit of work that can be performed without requiring a shuffle.\n\nIncorrect. Tasks can exist in shuffle stages as well. Shuffles affect stage boundaries, not the definition of a task.","D. A task represents the highest level of execution in the Spark execution hierarchy.\n\nIncorrect. The highest level is the application, followed by jobs, stages, and then tasks.","E. A task is a combination of a block of data and a set of transformations executed on a single executor.\n\nThis description is partially misleading. While tasks operate on partitions of data and execute transformations, the formal definition of a task is simply the smallest schedulable unit of work executed on an executor core."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"8848a800c53a05c26a4bd9a928b2afbfc9d2e9f53456548e4f45e00fe39f408e","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"job-stage-task-hierarchy","decision_id":"job-stage-task-hierarchy"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-134","number":134,"stem":"Which of the following strategies for reducing garbage collection (GC) time in Apache Spark is ineffective?","options":["A.\nUse the G1GC garbage collector to improve performance when garbage collection becomes a bottleneck.","B.\nUse Structured APIs (DataFrame/Dataset) as much as possible to reduce memory pressure.","C.\nUse fewer transformations and more actions.","D.\nCheck whether garbage collection is occurring too frequently.","E.\nAllocate more memory for major garbage collections."],"correct":2,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"gc-strategy","concept":"gc-strategy","concept_label":"gc-strategy","explanation_ja":"actionを増やすだけではGCの解決にならない。メモリ圧迫・オブジェクト・GCログを診断する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html#garbage-collection-tuning","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C\n\nUse fewer transformations and more actions.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThis strategy is ineffective for reducing garbage collection (GC) time in Apache Spark. In fact, increasing the number of actions can actually increase execution overhead, because each action triggers execution of the DAG and may cause additional jobs, stages, and intermediate data materialization.\n\nSpark’s lazy evaluation model means transformations are not executed immediately. Multiple transformations can often be pipelined together and optimized by the Catalyst optimizer and the physical planner before execution. Therefore, reducing transformations does not meaningfully reduce GC pressure, and replacing them with more actions is counterproductive.\n\nActions such as `collect()`, `count()`, or `write()` may cause:\n\n• additional job execution\n• materialization of intermediate data\n• increased memory pressure on executors and the driver\n\nThese effects can increase garbage collection overhead rather than reduce it.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html#garbage-collection-tuning](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html#garbage-collection-tuning)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nGarbage collection issues in Spark typically arise when:\n\n• large objects are frequently created\n• executor memory is under pressure\n• shuffle data or cached data occupies memory\n• serialization/deserialization creates many short-lived objects\n\nSpark GC tuning strategies focus on reducing object creation and improving memory management, not changing the number of transformations versus actions.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA. Use the G1GC garbage collector to improve performance when garbage collection becomes a bottleneck.\n\nThis is a valid GC optimization strategy. The G1GC collector is recommended for large heap sizes because it improves pause time behavior.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html#garbage-collection-tuning](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html#garbage-collection-tuning)\n\nB. Use Structured APIs (DataFrame/Dataset) as much as possible to reduce memory pressure.\n\nThis is effective because DataFrame/Dataset APIs use the Catalyst optimizer and Tungsten execution engine, which operate on optimized binary formats and generate fewer Java objects, reducing GC pressure.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html)\n\nD. Check whether garbage collection is occurring too frequently.\n\nThis is a recommended diagnostic step when tuning Spark applications. Monitoring GC behavior helps determine whether memory or object allocation patterns need tuning.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html)\n\nE. Allocate more memory for major garbage collections.\n\nIncreasing executor memory can reduce GC pressure because it reduces the frequency of major collections, allowing objects to survive longer in memory.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/configuration.html#memory-management](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/configuration.html#memory-management)\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nEffective Spark GC optimization techniques include:\n\n• using DataFrame/Dataset APIs instead of RDD-heavy workloads\n• tuning the JVM garbage collector (e.g., G1GC)\n• monitoring GC behavior\n• adjusting executor memory settings\n\nChanging the ratio of transformations vs actions does not meaningfully reduce GC overhead, making option C the ineffective strategy.","source_option_explanations":["A. Use the G1GC garbage collector to improve performance when garbage collection becomes a bottleneck.\n\nThis is a valid GC optimization strategy. The G1GC collector is recommended for large heap sizes because it improves pause time behavior.","B. Use Structured APIs (DataFrame/Dataset) as much as possible to reduce memory pressure.\n\nThis is effective because DataFrame/Dataset APIs use the Catalyst optimizer and Tungsten execution engine, which operate on optimized binary formats and generate fewer Java objects, reducing GC pressure.","Correct Answer: C\n\nUse fewer transformations and more actions.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThis strategy is ineffective for reducing garbage collection (GC) time in Apache Spark. In fact, increasing the number of actions can actually increase execution overhead, because each action triggers execution of the DAG and may cause additional jobs, stages, and intermediate data materialization.\n\nSpark’s lazy evaluation model means transformations are not executed immediately. Multiple transformations can often be pipelined together and optimized by the Catalyst optimizer and the physical planner before execution. Therefore, reducing transformations does not meaningfully reduce GC pressure, and replacing them with more actions is counterproductive.\n\nActions such as `collect()`, `count()`, or `write()` may cause:\n\n• additional job execution\n• materialization of intermediate data\n• increased memory pressure on executors and the driver\n\nThese effects can increase garbage collection overhead rather than reduce it.","D. Check whether garbage collection is occurring too frequently.\n\nThis is a recommended diagnostic step when tuning Spark applications. Monitoring GC behavior helps determine whether memory or object allocation patterns need tuning.","E. Allocate more memory for major garbage collections.\n\nIncreasing executor memory can reduce GC pressure because it reduces the frequency of major collections, allowing objects to survive longer in memory."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html#garbage-collection-tuning","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/configuration.html#memory-management"],"images":[],"source_hash":"8426ddcbd27aa7825dedbae8eea9aa15c4a9aa7dccbe528021294c2602b4e5f7","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"gc-strategy","decision_id":"gc-strategy"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-135","number":135,"stem":"Which of the following enables parallel execution when working with Spark DataFrames?","options":["A.\nDelta Tables","B.\nRDDs","C.\nMultiprocessing","D.\nMultithreading","E.\nVectors"],"correct":1,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"parallelism-rdd","concept":"parallelism-rdd","concept_label":"parallelism-rdd","explanation_ja":"Spark SQLの分散実行は基盤のpartitionとtaskにより並列化される。ここでのRDDはその実行基盤の説明。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#resilient-distributed-datasets-rdds","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B\n\nRDDs\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nParallel execution in Apache Spark fundamentally relies on RDDs (Resilient Distributed Datasets). Even when developers use higher-level APIs such as DataFrames or Datasets, Spark internally converts these operations into RDD-based execution plans. The data in an RDD is partitioned across the cluster, and Spark runs tasks in parallel on each partition across executor cores.\n\nThus, parallelism in Spark is enabled through distributed partitions of RDDs, which allow multiple tasks to process data simultaneously.\n\nOfficial documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#resilient-distributed-datasets-rdds](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#resilient-distributed-datasets-rdds)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark achieves parallelism through data partitioning.\n\nExecution model:\n\nApplication\n→ Job\n→ Stage\n→ Tasks (parallel execution)\n\nEach task processes one partition of an RDD. Since DataFrames are built on top of Spark SQL and executed using the underlying RDD execution engine, they inherit this parallel processing capability.\n\nExample concept:\n\nIf a DataFrame has 200 partitions, Spark can run 200 tasks in parallel, depending on the number of executor cores available.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA. Delta Tables\n\nDelta Tables provide ACID transactions, schema enforcement, and data versioning for Spark workloads. They improve reliability and data management but do not enable parallel execution.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://docs.delta.io/latest/index.html](https://docs.delta.io/latest/index.html)\n\nC. Multiprocessing\n\nMultiprocessing refers to Python local parallelism. Spark uses its own distributed execution model, not Python multiprocessing, to achieve cluster-scale parallel processing.\n\nD. Multithreading\n\nMultithreading occurs within a single process. Spark parallelism is achieved through distributed tasks across executors, not by threads in the driver program.\n\nE. Vectors\n\nVectors are used in machine learning libraries such as Spark MLlib for representing numerical features. They have nothing to do with enabling distributed parallel computation.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nSpark parallel execution is driven by:\n\n• RDD partitions\n• tasks executed on executor cores\n• distributed computation across worker nodes\n\nEven though developers commonly use DataFrames and Spark SQL, the underlying distributed execution model is still powered by RDDs.","source_option_explanations":["A. Delta Tables\n\nDelta Tables provide ACID transactions, schema enforcement, and data versioning for Spark workloads. They improve reliability and data management but do not enable parallel execution.","Correct Answer: B\n\nRDDs\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nParallel execution in Apache Spark fundamentally relies on RDDs (Resilient Distributed Datasets). Even when developers use higher-level APIs such as DataFrames or Datasets, Spark internally converts these operations into RDD-based execution plans. The data in an RDD is partitioned across the cluster, and Spark runs tasks in parallel on each partition across executor cores.\n\nThus, parallelism in Spark is enabled through distributed partitions of RDDs, which allow multiple tasks to process data simultaneously.","C. Multiprocessing\n\nMultiprocessing refers to Python local parallelism. Spark uses its own distributed execution model, not Python multiprocessing, to achieve cluster-scale parallel processing.","D. Multithreading\n\nMultithreading occurs within a single process. Spark parallelism is achieved through distributed tasks across executors, not by threads in the driver program.","E. Vectors\n\nVectors are used in machine learning libraries such as Spark MLlib for representing numerical features. They have nothing to do with enabling distributed parallel computation."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/rdd-programming-guide.html#resilient-distributed-datasets-rdds","https://docs.delta.io/latest/index.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"78c13200cbe1eab1c23ef558273075b0132ce63704ecd3223b137e23a3e198d9","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"parallelism-rdd","decision_id":"parallelism-rdd"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-136","number":136,"stem":"Which of the following code snippets returns a new DataFrame containing the mean of column `sqft` from the DataFrame `storesDF`, with the result stored in a column named sqftMean?","options":["A.\nstoresDF.withColumn(mean(col(\"sqft\")).alias(\"sqftMean\"))","B.\nstoresDF.agg(col(\"sqft\").mean().alias(\"sqftMean\"))","C.\nstoresDF.agg(mean(\"sqft\").alias(\"sqftMean\"))","D.\nstoresDF.agg(mean(col(\"sqft\")).alias(\"sqftMean\"))","E.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"sqftMean\", mean(col(\"sqft\")))"],"correct":2,"correct_many":[2,3],"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"aggregation","concept":"aggregation","concept_label":"aggregation","explanation_ja":"meanは列名文字列もColumnも受け付ける。両方をaggとaliasに接続できる。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.mean.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.mean.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.mean.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"b47e876383baa3b1ceaf52704f0973c933a9d7fce7488f5751135d0d1f064787","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"meanは列名文字列とColumnを受け付けるためCとDが有効。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.mean.html"],"original_stem":"Which of the following code snippets returns a new DataFrame containing the mean of column `sqft` from the DataFrame `storesDF`, with the result stored in a column named sqftMean?","original_options":["A.\nstoresDF.withColumn(mean(col(\"sqft\")).alias(\"sqftMean\"))","B.\nstoresDF.agg(col(\"sqft\").mean().alias(\"sqftMean\"))","C.\nstoresDF.agg(mean(\"sqft\").alias(\"sqftMean\"))","D.\nstoresDF.agg(mean(col(\"sqft\")).alias(\"sqftMean\"))","E.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"sqftMean\", mean(col(\"sqft\")))"],"original_correct":["D"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"aggregation","decision_id":"aggregation"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-137","number":137,"stem":"The code block below contains an error. It is intended to return a new DataFrame that represents an outer join between storesDF and employeesDF using the column storeId.\n\nCode block:\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\")\n```\n\nIdentify the error.","options":["A.\nThe default value of the how parameter is `\"inner\"`, so an additional argument specifying `\"outer\"` must be provided.","B.\nThe join key storeId must be wrapped using the col() function.","C.\nThe join key must be written as an explicit condition such as\n`storesDF.storeId == employeesDF.storeId`.","D.\nThe join key storeId must be placed inside a list like `[\"storeId\"]`.","E.\nThere is no DataFrame.join() method; DataFrame.merge() should be used instead."],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"join-default","concept":"join-default","concept_label":"join-default","explanation_ja":"joinの既定howはinner。outerの要件なら明示する。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\nThe default value of the `how` parameter is `\"inner\"`, so an additional argument specifying `\"outer\"` must be provided.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe code snippet:\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\")\n```\n\nperforms a join using `storeId` as the key, but Spark’s `DataFrame.join()` method uses `inner` join as the default join type. Since the requirement is to perform an outer join, the join type must be explicitly specified.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\", \"outer\")\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- \n`\"storeId\"` specifies the join key.\n\n- \n`\"outer\"` specifies the join type.\n\nSpark documentation confirms that the default join type is `inner` when the `how` parameter is omitted.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark supports multiple join types:\n\n- \n`inner` (default)\n\n- \n`left`\n\n- \n`right`\n\n- \n`outer`\n\n- \n`left_outer`\n\n- \n`right_outer`\n\n- \n`full`\n\n- \n`left_semi`\n\n- \n`left_anti`\n\nWhen no join type is specified, Spark performs an inner join, which returns only rows where the key exists in both DataFrames.\n\nAn outer join returns rows from both DataFrames, inserting `NULL` where matches are missing.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, \"key\", \"outer\")\n```\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nB. The join key storeId must be wrapped using the col() function.\n\nIncorrect. Spark allows the join key to be passed directly as a string when both DataFrames contain a column with the same name.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, \"storeId\")\n```\n\nThis is valid syntax.\n\nC. The join key must be written as an explicit condition such as\n`storesDF.storeId == employeesDF.storeId`.\n\nThis is another valid way to write a join condition, but it is not required when both columns have the same name. Using `\"storeId\"` directly is perfectly valid.\n\nD. The join key storeId must be placed inside a list like `[\"storeId\"]`.\n\nUsing a list is optional and typically used when joining on multiple columns. For a single key, `\"storeId\"` is valid syntax.\n\nE. There is no DataFrame.join() method; DataFrame.merge() should be used instead.\n\nIncorrect. `join()` is the correct Spark DataFrame method. `merge()` is used in pandas, not in Spark.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nSpark joins follow this pattern:\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, joinColumn, joinType)\n```\n\nIf the `joinType` argument is omitted:\n\n```\njoinType = \"inner\"\n```\n\nTherefore, to perform an outer join, you must explicitly specify:\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, \"key\", \"outer\")\n```","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\nThe default value of the `how` parameter is `\"inner\"`, so an additional argument specifying `\"outer\"` must be provided.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe code snippet:\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\")\n```\n\nperforms a join using `storeId` as the key, but Spark’s `DataFrame.join()` method uses `inner` join as the default join type. Since the requirement is to perform an outer join, the join type must be explicitly specified.\n\nCorrect implementation:\n\n```\nstoresDF.join(employeesDF, \"storeId\", \"outer\")\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- \n`\"storeId\"` specifies the join key.\n\n- \n`\"outer\"` specifies the join type.\n\nSpark documentation confirms that the default join type is `inner` when the `how` parameter is omitted.","B. The join key storeId must be wrapped using the col() function.\n\nIncorrect. Spark allows the join key to be passed directly as a string when both DataFrames contain a column with the same name.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, \"storeId\")\n```","C. The join key must be written as an explicit condition such as\n`storesDF.storeId == employeesDF.storeId`.\n\nThis is another valid way to write a join condition, but it is not required when both columns have the same name. Using `\"storeId\"` directly is perfectly valid.","D. The join key storeId must be placed inside a list like `[\"storeId\"]`.\n\nUsing a list is optional and typically used when joining on multiple columns. For a single key, `\"storeId\"` is valid syntax.","E. There is no DataFrame.join() method; DataFrame.merge() should be used instead.\n\nIncorrect. `join()` is the correct Spark DataFrame method. `merge()` is used in pandas, not in Spark."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"063ec7db8c204550969ab09a1067b9132509a20927ee6881d21e2cab5e7ad866","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"default-inner-vs-required-outer-join","decision_id":"default-inner-vs-required-outer-join"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-138","number":138,"stem":"A developer wants to create a DataFrame that includes a new column dayOfYear, which represents the numeric day of the year derived from the openDate column in the DataFrame storesDF.\n\nNote that openDate is stored as an integer representing a UNIX epoch timestamp (seconds since midnight, January 1, 1970).\n\nA sample of storesDF is shown below:\n\nstoreId | openDate\n0 | 1100746394\n1 | 1474410343\n2 | 1116610009\n3 | 1180035265\n4 | 1408024997\n... | ...\n\nLines of code:\n\n```\n1. storesDF.withColumn(\"openTimestamp\", col(\"openDate\").cast(\"Timestamp\"))\n2. storesDF.withColumn(\"dayOfYear\", dayofyear(col(\"openDate\")))\n3. storesDF.withColumn(\"openDateFormat\", col(\"openDate\").cast(\"Date\")) \\\n4. withColumn(\"dayOfYear\", dayofyear(col(\"openTimestamp\")))\n5. withColumn(\"dayOfYear\", dayofyear(col(\"openDateFormat\")))\n6. withColumn(\"dayOfYear\", get_dayofyear(col(\"openDateFormat\")))\n```\n\nWhich sequence of code lines should be executed to correctly create the dayOfYear column?","options":["A. 3, 6","B. 2","C. 3, 5","D. 1, 6","E. 1, 4"],"correct":4,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"epoch-date","concept":"epoch-date","concept_label":"epoch-date","explanation_ja":"epoch秒をtimestampにcastし、作成したtimestamp列からdayofyearを取得する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.dayofyear.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: E\n\n1, 4\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe column openDate contains a UNIX epoch timestamp stored as an integer (seconds since Jan 1, 1970). Spark date functions such as `dayofyear()` require the input column to be of type Timestamp or Date, not an integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct process is:\n\n- \nConvert the integer epoch value into a Timestamp\n\n- \nApply the `dayofyear()` function to the timestamp column\n\nCorrect sequence:\n\n```\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"openTimestamp\", col(\"openDate\").cast(\"Timestamp\"))\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"dayOfYear\", dayofyear(col(\"openTimestamp\")))\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n• `cast(\"Timestamp\")` converts the UNIX epoch integer into a timestamp.\n• `dayofyear()` extracts the numeric day of the year (1–366) from the timestamp.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.dayofyear.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.dayofyear.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark date functions operate on date or timestamp columns, not raw integers.\n\nCommon workflow when working with epoch timestamps:\n\nEpoch (integer) → Timestamp → Date functions\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf.withColumn(\"timestamp\", col(\"epochColumn\").cast(\"timestamp\"))\n  .withColumn(\"dayOfYear\", dayofyear(\"timestamp\"))\n```\n\nThe function `dayofyear()` extracts values from calendar-based date/time types, which is why conversion is required first.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA. 3, 6\n\nLine 3 converts to a Date, which is acceptable, but line 6 uses `get_dayofyear()`, which does not exist in the Spark API.\n\nB. 2\n\n```\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"dayOfYear\", dayofyear(col(\"openDate\")))\n```\n\nThis attempts to apply `dayofyear()` directly to an integer column, which is invalid because Spark expects a Date or Timestamp.\n\nC. 3, 5\n\nLine 3 attempts to cast the epoch integer directly to Date, which does not correctly convert UNIX epoch seconds. The proper conversion should be to Timestamp first.\n\nD. 1, 6\n\nAlthough line 1 correctly converts to timestamp, line 6 uses `get_dayofyear()`, which is not a valid Spark function.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nWhen working with UNIX epoch timestamps in Spark:\n\n- \nConvert the integer to Timestamp\n\n- \nApply date functions like `dayofyear()`, `month()`, `year()`, etc.\n\nTypical pattern:\n\n```\ndf.withColumn(\"timestamp\", col(\"epoch\").cast(\"timestamp\")) \\\n  .withColumn(\"dayOfYear\", dayofyear(\"timestamp\"))\n```","source_option_explanations":["A. 3, 6\n\nLine 3 converts to a Date, which is acceptable, but line 6 uses `get_dayofyear()`, which does not exist in the Spark API.","B. 2\n\n```\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"dayOfYear\", dayofyear(col(\"openDate\")))\n```\n\nThis attempts to apply `dayofyear()` directly to an integer column, which is invalid because Spark expects a Date or Timestamp.","C. 3, 5\n\nLine 3 attempts to cast the epoch integer directly to Date, which does not correctly convert UNIX epoch seconds. The proper conversion should be to Timestamp first.","D. 1, 6\n\nAlthough line 1 correctly converts to timestamp, line 6 uses `get_dayofyear()`, which is not a valid Spark function.","Correct Answer: E\n\n1, 4\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe column openDate contains a UNIX epoch timestamp stored as an integer (seconds since Jan 1, 1970). Spark date functions such as `dayofyear()` require the input column to be of type Timestamp or Date, not an integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct process is:\n\n- \nConvert the integer epoch value into a Timestamp\n\n- \nApply the `dayofyear()` function to the timestamp column\n\nCorrect sequence:\n\n```\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"openTimestamp\", col(\"openDate\").cast(\"Timestamp\"))\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"dayOfYear\", dayofyear(col(\"openTimestamp\")))\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n• `cast(\"Timestamp\")` converts the UNIX epoch integer into a timestamp.\n• `dayofyear()` extracts the numeric day of the year (1–366) from the timestamp."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.dayofyear.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"bb219803efb083d7f99f908fc24f0e4f78b53ac970a95298fe9267005251d271","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"epoch-date-part","decision_id":"epoch-date-part"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-139","number":139,"stem":"A developer wants to create a DataFrame that includes a new column month, which represents the numeric month extracted from the `openDate` column in the DataFrame storesDF.\n\nNote that the openDate column is stored as an integer representing a UNIX epoch timestamp, meaning the number of seconds since January 1, 1970.\n\nA sample of storesDF is shown below:\n\nstoreId | openDate\n0 | 1100746394\n1 | 1474410343\n2 | 1116610009\n3 | 1180035265\n4 | 1408024997\n... | ...\n\nWhich of the following code snippets correctly produces a DataFrame containing the month column derived from openDate?","options":["A.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"month\", getMonth(col(\"openDate\")))","B.\n(storesDF.withColumn(\"openTimestamp\", col(\"openDate\").cast(\"Timestamp\"))\n.withColumn(\"month\", month(col(\"openTimestamp\"))))","C.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"month\", month(col(\"openDate\")))","D.\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"month\", substr(col(\"openDate\"), 4, 2))","E.\n(storesDF.withColumn(\"openDateFormat\", col(\"openDate\").cast(\"Date\"))\n.withColumn(\"month\", month(col(\"openDateFormat\"))))"],"correct":1,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"epoch-date","concept":"epoch-date","concept_label":"epoch-date","explanation_ja":"epoch秒をtimestampへ変換してmonthを取得する。整数をDateTypeへ直接castする方法とは異なる。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.month.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B\n\n```\n(storesDF.withColumn(\"openTimestamp\", col(\"openDate\").cast(\"Timestamp\"))\n .withColumn(\"month\", month(col(\"openTimestamp\"))))\n```\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe column openDate stores a UNIX epoch timestamp as an integer (seconds since Jan 1, 1970). Spark date functions such as `month()` require the input column to be of type Timestamp or Date, not an integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct approach is:\n\n- \nConvert the UNIX epoch integer into a Timestamp.\n\n- \nExtract the month using the `month()` function.\n\nThe correct transformation:\n\n```\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"openTimestamp\", col(\"openDate\").cast(\"timestamp\")) \\\n        .withColumn(\"month\", month(col(\"openTimestamp\")))\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n• `cast(\"timestamp\")` converts the epoch seconds to a timestamp value.\n• `month()` extracts the numeric month (1–12) from that timestamp.\n\nSpark documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.month.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.month.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark date functions operate on date/time types, not raw integers.\n\nTypical workflow when working with epoch timestamps:\n\nEpoch integer → Timestamp → Date/time functions\n\nExample pattern:\n\n```\ndf.withColumn(\"timestamp\", col(\"epoch\").cast(\"timestamp\"))\n  .withColumn(\"month\", month(\"timestamp\"))\n```\n\nThis pattern is common when ingesting logs or event data where timestamps are stored in UNIX epoch format.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA.\n`storesDF.withColumn(\"month\", getMonth(col(\"openDate\")))`\n\nIncorrect because `getMonth()` is not a valid Spark SQL function.\n\nC.\n`storesDF.withColumn(\"month\", month(col(\"openDate\")))`\n\nIncorrect because `month()` expects a Date or Timestamp column, not an integer epoch value.\n\nD.\n`storesDF.withColumn(\"month\", substr(col(\"openDate\"), 4, 2))`\n\nIncorrect because `openDate` is a numeric epoch timestamp, not a formatted date string. String substring extraction would produce meaningless values.\n\nE.\n\n```\n(storesDF.withColumn(\"openDateFormat\", col(\"openDate\").cast(\"Date\"))\n .withColumn(\"month\", month(col(\"openDateFormat\"))))\n```\n\nIncorrect because casting an epoch integer directly to Date does not correctly convert UNIX epoch seconds. The proper conversion must first produce a Timestamp.\n\nSpark documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-datetime-pattern.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-datetime-pattern.html)\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nWhen working with UNIX epoch timestamps in Spark:\n\n- \nConvert the integer to Timestamp\n\n- \nApply date/time extraction functions like `month()`, `year()`, or `dayofyear()`\n\nTypical pattern:\n\n```\ndf.withColumn(\"timestamp\", col(\"epoch\").cast(\"timestamp\"))\n  .withColumn(\"month\", month(\"timestamp\"))\n```\n\nUnderstanding this conversion pattern is a frequently tested concept in the Databricks Certified Associate Developer for Apache Spark exam.","source_option_explanations":["A.\n`storesDF.withColumn(\"month\", getMonth(col(\"openDate\")))`\n\nIncorrect because `getMonth()` is not a valid Spark SQL function.","Correct Answer: B\n\n```\n(storesDF.withColumn(\"openTimestamp\", col(\"openDate\").cast(\"Timestamp\"))\n .withColumn(\"month\", month(col(\"openTimestamp\"))))\n```\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe column openDate stores a UNIX epoch timestamp as an integer (seconds since Jan 1, 1970). Spark date functions such as `month()` require the input column to be of type Timestamp or Date, not an integer.\n\nTherefore, the correct approach is:\n\n- \nConvert the UNIX epoch integer into a Timestamp.\n\n- \nExtract the month using the `month()` function.\n\nThe correct transformation:\n\n```\nstoresDF.withColumn(\"openTimestamp\", col(\"openDate\").cast(\"timestamp\")) \\\n        .withColumn(\"month\", month(col(\"openTimestamp\")))\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n• `cast(\"timestamp\")` converts the epoch seconds to a timestamp value.\n• `month()` extracts the numeric month (1–12) from that timestamp.","C.\n`storesDF.withColumn(\"month\", month(col(\"openDate\")))`\n\nIncorrect because `month()` expects a Date or Timestamp column, not an integer epoch value.","D.\n`storesDF.withColumn(\"month\", substr(col(\"openDate\"), 4, 2))`\n\nIncorrect because `openDate` is a numeric epoch timestamp, not a formatted date string. String substring extraction would produce meaningless values.","E.\n\n```\n(storesDF.withColumn(\"openDateFormat\", col(\"openDate\").cast(\"Date\"))\n .withColumn(\"month\", month(col(\"openDateFormat\"))))\n```\n\nIncorrect because casting an epoch integer directly to Date does not correctly convert UNIX epoch seconds. The proper conversion must first produce a Timestamp."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.month.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-ref-datetime-pattern.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"2a8c84e091f6a8a8998a1cfac60f2bddfa25fcbae4b6a2e41a66c695cff7af92","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"epoch-date-part","decision_id":"epoch-seconds-to-timestamp-month"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-140","number":140,"stem":"Consider the following code snippet from `my_spark_app.py`:\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql import SparkSession\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import lit\nspark = SparkSession.builder.appName(\"CoreComponentsExample\").getOrCreate()\ndata = [(\"Alice\", 34), (\"Bob\", 36), (\"Cathy\", 31)]\ncolumns = [\"Name\", \"Age\"]\n \ndf = spark.createDataFrame(data, columns).withColumn(\"Status\", lit(\"Pass\"))\ndf_filtered = df.filter(df.Age > 35)\ndf_filtered.show()\n \nspark.stop()\n```\n\nWhat is the primary role of the Spark driver node in this application?","options":["A.\nThe driver coordinates execution by converting actions into tasks and distributing them to worker nodes.","B.\nThe driver node only provides the user interface used to monitor the Spark application.","C.\nThe driver node stores the DataFrame data and performs all computations locally.","D.\nThe driver node stores the final output after worker nodes complete their computations."],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"driver-role","concept":"driver-role","concept_label":"driver-role","explanation_ja":"driverがアプリの制御・計画・task配布を担当する。withColumnの定数はlitで包む。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.lit.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/cluster-overview.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/web-ui.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"e32b93ecd6e51b9b471f3864759138bf5d70a865ae0260bc6cb11e176ccfb186","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"withColumnの第2引数はColumnが必要。litをimportして文字列リテラルをColumn化する。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.withColumn.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.lit.html"],"original_stem":"Consider the following code snippet from `my_spark_app.py`:\n\n```\nfrom pyspark.sql import SparkSession\nspark = SparkSession.builder.appName(\"CoreComponentsExample\").getOrCreate()\ndata = [(\"Alice\", 34), (\"Bob\", 36), (\"Cathy\", 31)]\ncolumns = [\"Name\", \"Age\"]\n \ndf = spark.createDataFrame(data, columns).withColumn(\"Status\", \"Pass\")\ndf_filtered = df.filter(df.Age > 35)\ndf_filtered.show()\n \nspark.stop()\n```\n\nWhat is the primary role of the Spark driver node in this application?","original_options":["A.\nThe driver coordinates execution by converting actions into tasks and distributing them to worker nodes.","B.\nThe driver node only provides the user interface used to monitor the Spark application.","C.\nThe driver node stores the DataFrame data and performs all computations locally.","D.\nThe driver node stores the final output after worker nodes complete their computations."],"original_correct":["A"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"driver-plans-schedules-coordinates","decision_id":"driver-plans-schedules-coordinates"}]},{"id":"EX4-S8","title":"原文セット 8","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-141","number":141,"stem":"A data engineer is working with a Structured Streaming DataFrame named streaming_df that continuously receives the following streaming data:\n\n![画像](https://img-c.udemycdn.com/redactor/raw/practice_test_question/2026-03-11_09-55-16-f6a9a6fea888b7c8117b90c8c8e0b523.png)\n\n![画像](https://img-c.udemycdn.com/redactor/raw/practice_test_question/2026-03-11_09-55-16-f6a9a6fea888b7c8117b90c8c8e0b523.png)\n\nWhich of the following operations is supported on the streaming DataFrame streaming_df?","options":["A.\nstreaming_df.select(countDistinct(\"Name\"))","B.\nstreaming_df.groupby(\"Id\").count()","C.\nstreaming_df.orderBy(\"timestamp\").limit(4)","D.\nstreaming_df.filter(col(\"count\") < 30).show()"],"correct":1,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"stream-supported-operation","concept":"stream-supported-operation","concept_label":"stream-supported-operation","explanation_ja":"streamingではgroupByのcountをqueryとして実行できる。streaming DataFrameのshowや一般のorderBy/limitには制約がある。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: B\n\n`streaming_df.groupby(\"Id\").count()`\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Structured Streaming, Spark supports stateful aggregations such as `groupBy().count()` on streaming DataFrames. These aggregations maintain state across incoming micro-batches and continuously update results as new records arrive in the stream.\n\nThe following operation is valid:\n\n```\nstreaming_df.groupBy(\"Id\").count()\n```\n\nThis performs a running aggregation, where Spark maintains a state store to keep track of counts per `Id` as new data arrives in the stream.\n\nStructured Streaming supports aggregations such as:\n\n- \n`groupBy().count()`\n\n- \n`groupBy().agg()`\n\n- \nwindow-based aggregations with event-time windows\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark Structured Streaming operates on unbounded data streams. Because the data is continuously arriving, certain operations must be incrementally computable.\n\nSupported operations generally include:\n\n• `select()`\n• `filter()`\n• `groupBy()` aggregations\n• window aggregations\n• joins (with constraints)\n\nSpark processes streaming data using a micro-batch execution model, where each batch updates the result table incrementally.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA.\n`streaming_df.select(countDistinct(\"Name\"))`\n\n`countDistinct()` requires a global distinct aggregation, which is not supported directly on streaming DataFrames without special handling because it requires unbounded state tracking.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#unsupported-operations](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#unsupported-operations)\n\nC.\n`streaming_df.orderBy(\"timestamp\").limit(4)`\n\nGlobal sorting operations such as `orderBy()` are not supported on streaming DataFrames because the dataset is unbounded and Spark cannot determine the global order of infinite incoming data.\n\nD.\n`streaming_df.filter(col(\"count\") < 30).show()`\n\nAlthough `filter()` is supported in streaming queries, `show()` is a batch DataFrame action and cannot be used on streaming DataFrames. Streaming queries must be started using `writeStream.start()`.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#starting-streaming-queries](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#starting-streaming-queries)\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nIn Structured Streaming:\n\nSupported operations include:\n\n• `filter()`\n• `select()`\n• `groupBy()` aggregations\n• window aggregations\n\nUnsupported operations generally include:\n\n• global sorting (`orderBy`)\n• global limits (`limit`)\n• batch actions like `show()`\n• unbounded distinct aggregations (`countDistinct`)\n\nTherefore, the correct supported operation is:\n\n```\nstreaming_df.groupBy(\"Id\").count()\n```","source_option_explanations":["A.\n`streaming_df.select(countDistinct(\"Name\"))`\n\n`countDistinct()` requires a global distinct aggregation, which is not supported directly on streaming DataFrames without special handling because it requires unbounded state tracking.","Correct Answer: B\n\n`streaming_df.groupby(\"Id\").count()`\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Structured Streaming, Spark supports stateful aggregations such as `groupBy().count()` on streaming DataFrames. These aggregations maintain state across incoming micro-batches and continuously update results as new records arrive in the stream.\n\nThe following operation is valid:\n\n```\nstreaming_df.groupBy(\"Id\").count()\n```\n\nThis performs a running aggregation, where Spark maintains a state store to keep track of counts per `Id` as new data arrives in the stream.\n\nStructured Streaming supports aggregations such as:\n\n- \n`groupBy().count()`\n\n- \n`groupBy().agg()`\n\n- \nwindow-based aggregations with event-time windows","C.\n`streaming_df.orderBy(\"timestamp\").limit(4)`\n\nGlobal sorting operations such as `orderBy()` are not supported on streaming DataFrames because the dataset is unbounded and Spark cannot determine the global order of infinite incoming data.","D.\n`streaming_df.filter(col(\"count\") < 30).show()`\n\nAlthough `filter()` is supported in streaming queries, `show()` is a batch DataFrame action and cannot be used on streaming DataFrames. Streaming queries must be started using `writeStream.start()`."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#unsupported-operations","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#starting-streaming-queries"],"images":[{"url":"https://img-c.udemycdn.com/redactor/raw/practice_test_question/2026-03-11_09-55-16-f6a9a6fea888b7c8117b90c8c8e0b523.png","local":"exam4-q141.png","alt":"設問141のデータ表"}],"source_hash":"767eb7ec33c7d5057dbc72b10c2b1da2041f46ebe75d26fe2080eb5495ae54dd","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"supported-streaming-dataframe-operations","decision_id":"supported-streaming-dataframe-operations"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-142","number":142,"stem":"An MLOps engineer is implementing a Pandas UDF that uses a language model to translate English text into Spanish. However, the current implementation loads the translation model every time the UDF is invoked, which significantly reduces the performance of the pipeline.\n\nThe current code is shown below:\n\n```\ndef in_spanish_inner(df: pd.Series) -> pd.Series:\n    model = get_translation_model(target_lang='es')\n    return df.apply(model)\nin_spanish = sf.pandas_udf(in_spanish_inner, StringType())\n```\n\nWhat modification should the engineer make to reduce the number of times the language model is loaded?","options":["A.\nConvert the Pandas UDF into a standard PySpark UDF.","B.\nChange the Pandas UDF from a Series → Series UDF to a Series → Scalar UDF.","C.\nExecute the `in_spanish_inner()` logic using a mapInPandas() function call.","D.\nModify the Pandas UDF from a Series → Series UDF to an Iterator[Series] → Iterator[Series] UDF."],"correct":3,"domain":"Pandas API","topic":"iterator-pandas-udf","concept":"iterator-pandas-udf","concept_label":"iterator-pandas-udf","explanation_ja":"Iterator型のPandas UDFで初期化をiteratorごとに行い、各Series batchでモデルを使い回す。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/sql/arrow_pandas.html#iterator-of-series-to-iterator-of-series-udf","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D\n\nModify the Pandas UDF from a Series → Series UDF to an Iterator[Series] → Iterator[Series] UDF.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn the current implementation, the translation model is loaded inside the UDF function:\n\n```\ndef in_spanish_inner(df: pd.Series) -> pd.Series:\n    model = get_translation_model(target_lang='es')\n    return df.apply(model)\n```\n\nWith a Series → Series Pandas UDF, Spark invokes the function once per batch of data, meaning the model may be loaded multiple times per executor, which causes significant performance overhead.\n\nUsing an Iterator Pandas UDF (`Iterator[pd.Series] -> Iterator[pd.Series]`) allows initialization logic (like loading the model) to be executed once per Python worker, and then reused across multiple batches processed by that worker.\n\nExample optimized implementation:\n\n```\ndef in_spanish_inner(iterator):\n    model = get_translation_model(target_lang=\"es\")\n    for series in iterator:\n        yield series.apply(model)\n```\n\nThen register the UDF:\n\n```\nin_spanish = sf.pandas_udf(in_spanish_inner, StringType())\n```\n\nThis approach ensures:\n\n• The model loads once per Python worker\n• Multiple batches reuse the same model instance\n• Significant improvement in performance and latency\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/sql/arrow_pandas.html#iterator-of-series-to-iterator-of-series-udf](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/sql/arrow_pandas.html#iterator-of-series-to-iterator-of-series-udf)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark provides several types of Pandas UDFs:\n\nSeries → Series UDF\n• Processes batches of column data\n• Function called once per batch\n\nIterator[Series] → Iterator[Series] UDF\n• Allows state initialization once per worker\n• Ideal for machine learning models, NLP models, or external resources\n\nThis pattern is commonly used in MLOps pipelines where loading models repeatedly is expensive.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA. Convert the Pandas UDF into a standard PySpark UDF.\n\nStandard PySpark UDFs operate row-by-row, do not use Apache Arrow, and are typically much slower than Pandas UDFs. This would worsen performance rather than improve it.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-pyspark-pandas-with-arrow.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-pyspark-pandas-with-arrow.html)\n\nB. Change the Pandas UDF from a Series → Series UDF to a Series → Scalar UDF.\n\nThis type of UDF does not exist in the Pandas UDF API. Pandas UDFs operate on vectorized batches, not scalar outputs.\n\nC. Execute the logic using `mapInPandas()`.\n\n`mapInPandas()` is used when the entire DataFrame schema may change or when performing complex transformations, but it does not directly address the model initialization issue as cleanly as Iterator Pandas UDFs designed for this purpose.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nWhen working with machine learning or large models in Pandas UDFs, use:\n\nIterator-based Pandas UDFs\n\nBecause they allow:\n\n• one-time initialization per worker\n• reuse of expensive resources\n• significantly improved performance\n\nThis pattern is widely used in Databricks MLOps pipelines and large-scale inference workloads.","source_option_explanations":["A. Convert the Pandas UDF into a standard PySpark UDF.\n\nStandard PySpark UDFs operate row-by-row, do not use Apache Arrow, and are typically much slower than Pandas UDFs. This would worsen performance rather than improve it.","B. Change the Pandas UDF from a Series → Series UDF to a Series → Scalar UDF.\n\nThis type of UDF does not exist in the Pandas UDF API. Pandas UDFs operate on vectorized batches, not scalar outputs.","C. Execute the logic using `mapInPandas()`.\n\n`mapInPandas()` is used when the entire DataFrame schema may change or when performing complex transformations, but it does not directly address the model initialization issue as cleanly as Iterator Pandas UDFs designed for this purpose.","Correct Answer: D\n\nModify the Pandas UDF from a Series → Series UDF to an Iterator[Series] → Iterator[Series] UDF.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn the current implementation, the translation model is loaded inside the UDF function:\n\n```\ndef in_spanish_inner(df: pd.Series) -> pd.Series:\n    model = get_translation_model(target_lang='es')\n    return df.apply(model)\n```\n\nWith a Series → Series Pandas UDF, Spark invokes the function once per batch of data, meaning the model may be loaded multiple times per executor, which causes significant performance overhead.\n\nUsing an Iterator Pandas UDF (`Iterator[pd.Series] -> Iterator[pd.Series]`) allows initialization logic (like loading the model) to be executed once per Python worker, and then reused across multiple batches processed by that worker.\n\nExample optimized implementation:\n\n```\ndef in_spanish_inner(iterator):\n    model = get_translation_model(target_lang=\"es\")\n    for series in iterator:\n        yield series.apply(model)\n```\n\nThen register the UDF:\n\n```\nin_spanish = sf.pandas_udf(in_spanish_inner, StringType())\n```"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/sql/arrow_pandas.html#iterator-of-series-to-iterator-of-series-udf","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-pyspark-pandas-with-arrow.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"1de413a8541f29883b2861d48f386900cb9ddea6c330116d488fd0bf0f43645e","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"iterator-pandas-udf-initialization","decision_id":"iterator-pandas-udf-initialization"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-143","number":143,"stem":"A developer needs to join two tables, `sales.purchases_fct` and `sales.customer_dim`, using the following code:\n\n```\nimport pyspark.sql.functions as F\npurch_df = spark.table(\"sales.purchases_fct\")\ncust_df = spark.table(\"sales.customer_dim\").dropDuplicates([\"cust_id\"])\nfact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"))\n```\n\nAfter reviewing the results, the developer notices that some customers present in the `purchases_fct` table are missing in the joined output because they do not exist in the `customer_dim` table.\n\nWhich modification should be made so that these customer records from `purchases_fct` are retained in the final dataset?","options":["A.\n`fact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"), \"left\")`","B.\n`fact_df = cust_df.join(purch_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"))`","C.\n`fact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"cust_id\") == F.col(\"customer_id\"))`","D.\n`fact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"), \"right_outer\")`"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"join-left","concept":"join-left","concept_label":"join-left","explanation_ja":"左表の不一致行も保持するにはleft join。defaultのinner joinでは失われる。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\n```\nfact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"), \"left\")\n```\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe current code performs a join without specifying a join type:\n\n```\nfact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"))\n```\n\nIn Apache Spark, when the join type is not specified, the default join type is `inner`. An inner join only returns rows where matching keys exist in both tables. Therefore, if a `customer_id` in `purchases_fct` does not exist in `customer_dim`, that record will be excluded from the result.\n\nTo retain all rows from the purchases_fct table, the developer should use a left join:\n\n```\nfact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"), \"left\")\n```\n\nA left join (left outer join) keeps:\n\n- \nall rows from the left DataFrame (`purch_df`)\n\n- \nmatching rows from the right DataFrame (`cust_df`)\n\n- \n`NULL` values where no match exists\n\nThis ensures that all purchase records remain in the final dataset, even if the customer does not exist in the dimension table.\n\nSpark documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark supports multiple join types:\n\n• inner – returns only matching rows from both tables\n• left / left_outer – keeps all rows from the left table\n• right / right_outer – keeps all rows from the right table\n• full / full_outer – keeps all rows from both tables\n\nIn data warehousing and star schema modeling, fact tables are commonly joined with dimension tables using left joins so that fact records are never lost even if dimension data is missing.\n\nExample pattern:\n\n```\nfact_df = fact_table.join(dim_table, condition, \"left\")\n```\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nB.\n\n```\nfact_df = cust_df.join(purch_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"))\n```\n\nThis still performs an inner join, which will continue to drop records without matching customers.\n\nC.\n\n```\nfact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"cust_id\") == F.col(\"customer_id\"))\n```\n\nThe join condition is logically equivalent to the original one. However, the join type remains inner, so unmatched purchase records will still be removed.\n\nD.\n\n```\nfact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"), \"right_outer\")\n```\n\nA right outer join keeps all rows from `cust_df`, not `purch_df`. This would ensure all customers appear, but it would not guarantee that all purchase records are retained, which is the requirement.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nWhen working with fact and dimension tables in Spark:\n\n• Fact table should usually be the left table\n• Use a left join to ensure fact records are preserved\n\nTypical pattern:\n\n```\nfact_df = fact_table.join(dim_table, condition, \"left\")\n```","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\n```\nfact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"), \"left\")\n```\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe current code performs a join without specifying a join type:\n\n```\nfact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"))\n```\n\nIn Apache Spark, when the join type is not specified, the default join type is `inner`. An inner join only returns rows where matching keys exist in both tables. Therefore, if a `customer_id` in `purchases_fct` does not exist in `customer_dim`, that record will be excluded from the result.\n\nTo retain all rows from the purchases_fct table, the developer should use a left join:\n\n```\nfact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"), \"left\")\n```\n\nA left join (left outer join) keeps:\n\n- \nall rows from the left DataFrame (`purch_df`)\n\n- \nmatching rows from the right DataFrame (`cust_df`)\n\n- \n`NULL` values where no match exists\n\nThis ensures that all purchase records remain in the final dataset, even if the customer does not exist in the dimension table.","B.\n\n```\nfact_df = cust_df.join(purch_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"))\n```\n\nThis still performs an inner join, which will continue to drop records without matching customers.","C.\n\n```\nfact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"cust_id\") == F.col(\"customer_id\"))\n```\n\nThe join condition is logically equivalent to the original one. However, the join type remains inner, so unmatched purchase records will still be removed.","D.\n\n```\nfact_df = purch_df.join(cust_df, F.col(\"customer_id\") == F.col(\"cust_id\"), \"right_outer\")\n```\n\nA right outer join keeps all rows from `cust_df`, not `purch_df`. This would ensure all customers appear, but it would not guarantee that all purchase records are retained, which is the requirement."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"aa1476d8a9687776154d81079f0a663b94888551e791989062455abdb7c85ab1","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"left-join-preserve","decision_id":"left-join-preserve-unmatched-facts"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-144","number":144,"stem":"A developer needs to generate a Python dictionary from a small Parquet table containing the following data:\n\n```\nregion_id | region\n----------+-----------\n1         | AMERICA\n3         | EUROPE\n0         | AFRICA\n4         | MIDDLE EAST\n2         | ASIA\n```\n\nThe resulting dictionary must map region → region_id, and it should include only the three smallest `region_id` values.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly satisfies these requirements?","options":["A.\n\n```\nregions = dict(\n    regions_df\n        .select('region', 'region_id')\n        .sort('region_id')\n        .take(3)\n)\n```","B.\n\n```\nregions = dict(\n    regions_df\n        .select('region_id', 'region')\n        .sort('region_id')\n        .take(3)\n)\n```","C.\n\n```\nregions = dict(\n    regions_df\n        .select('region_id', 'region')\n        .limit(3)\n        .collect()\n)\n```","D.\n\n```\nregions = dict(\n    regions_df\n        .select('region', 'region_id')\n        .sort(desc('region_id'))\n        .take(3)\n)\n```"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"collect-small-dictionary","concept":"collect-small-dictionary","concept_label":"collect-small-dictionary","explanation_ja":"region→region_idの順に2列を選び、id昇順の3行をtakeしてdictへ変換する。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.take.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\n```\nregions = dict(\n    regions_df\n        .select('region', 'region_id')\n        .sort('region_id')\n        .take(3)\n)\n```\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe requirement is:\n\n• Create a Python dictionary\n• Mapping region → region_id\n• Include only the three smallest region_id values\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n- \nSelect columns in the correct order\n\n```\n.select('region', 'region_id')\n```\n\nThis ensures the resulting rows are `(region, region_id)`, which is the required dictionary key-value structure.\n\n- \nSort by region_id\n\n```\n.sort('region_id')\n```\n\nSorting ensures that the smallest region_id values appear first.\n\n- \nTake the first three rows\n\n```\n.take(3)\n```\n\n`take(3)` returns the first three rows from the sorted dataset.\n\n- \nConvert to dictionary\n\nPassing the rows `(region, region_id)` to `dict()` produces:\n\n```\n{\n \"AFRICA\": 0,\n \"AMERICA\": 1,\n \"ASIA\": 2\n}\n```\n\nSpark documentation for `take()` and DataFrame operations:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.take.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.take.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nWhen converting Spark data to Python objects:\n\n• Use `collect()` or `take()` to bring data to the driver\n• Only do this for small datasets\n\nBecause Spark DataFrames are distributed, they must be materialized to the driver before Python structures (like dictionaries) can be created.\n\nTypical pattern:\n\n```\ndict(df.select(\"key\", \"value\").collect())\n```\n\nor\n\n```\ndict(df.select(\"key\", \"value\").take(n))\n```\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nB.\n\n```\n.select('region_id', 'region')\n```\n\nThis creates tuples `(region_id, region)`, which produces a dictionary mapping:\n\n```\nregion_id → region\n```\n\nBut the requirement is region → region_id, so the key/value order is incorrect.\n\nC.\n\n```\n.select('region_id', 'region')\n.limit(3)\n.collect()\n```\n\nTwo issues:\n\n- \nThe column order still produces region_id → region mapping.\n\n- \n`limit(3)` without sorting does not guarantee the three smallest region_id values, because Spark does not guarantee ordering unless explicitly sorted.\n\nSpark documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.limit.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.limit.html)\n\nD.\n\n```\n.sort(desc('region_id'))\n.take(3)\n```\n\nThis sorts in descending order, returning the largest region_id values, which contradicts the requirement to retrieve the smallest region_id values.\n\nSpark documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.desc.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.desc.html)\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nWhen converting Spark results to Python dictionaries:\n\n- \nEnsure correct column order for key/value mapping\n\n- \nSort the DataFrame when selecting top/bottom values\n\n- \nUse `take()` or `collect()` only for small datasets\n\nTypical pattern:\n\n```\ndict(df.select(\"key\", \"value\").sort(\"value\").take(n))\n```","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\n```\nregions = dict(\n    regions_df\n        .select('region', 'region_id')\n        .sort('region_id')\n        .take(3)\n)\n```\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe requirement is:\n\n• Create a Python dictionary\n• Mapping region → region_id\n• Include only the three smallest region_id values\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n- \nSelect columns in the correct order\n\n```\n.select('region', 'region_id')\n```\n\nThis ensures the resulting rows are `(region, region_id)`, which is the required dictionary key-value structure.\n\n- \nSort by region_id\n\n```\n.sort('region_id')\n```\n\nSorting ensures that the smallest region_id values appear first.\n\n- \nTake the first three rows\n\n```\n.take(3)\n```\n\n`take(3)` returns the first three rows from the sorted dataset.\n\n- \nConvert to dictionary\n\nPassing the rows `(region, region_id)` to `dict()` produces:\n\n```\n{\n \"AFRICA\": 0,\n \"AMERICA\": 1,\n \"ASIA\": 2\n}\n```\n\nSpark documentation for `take()` and DataFrame operations:","B.\n\n```\n.select('region_id', 'region')\n```\n\nThis creates tuples `(region_id, region)`, which produces a dictionary mapping:\n\n```\nregion_id → region\n```\n\nBut the requirement is region → region_id, so the key/value order is incorrect.","C.\n\n```\n.select('region_id', 'region')\n.limit(3)\n.collect()\n```\n\nTwo issues:\n\n- \nThe column order still produces region_id → region mapping.\n\n- \n`limit(3)` without sorting does not guarantee the three smallest region_id values, because Spark does not guarantee ordering unless explicitly sorted.","D.\n\n```\n.sort(desc('region_id'))\n.take(3)\n```\n\nThis sorts in descending order, returning the largest region_id values, which contradicts the requirement to retrieve the smallest region_id values."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.take.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.limit.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.desc.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"3b5b08b9635f48289e5e7baeabef8ece6a0c9b77ea345839a1eef53bbfa30a2d","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"small-dictionary-key-order-top-n","decision_id":"small-dictionary-key-order-top-n"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-145","number":145,"stem":"A developer needs to write data in Parquet format containing the name and platform columns from the file devices.json. The following code has been written, but it contains three placeholders: X, Y, and Z.\n\n```\ndevices_df = spark.read.json(\"devices.json\")\ndevices_df.createOrReplaceTempView(\"devices\")\n \nspark.sql(\"SELECT name, platform FROM X\").Y.Z(\"names.parquet\", format=\"parquet\")\n```\n\nWhat should replace X, Y, and Z, respectively, so that the query result is correctly written as a Parquet file?","options":["A.\ndevices_df, write, save","B.\ndevices, save, write","C.\ndevices, write, saveAsTable","D.\ndevices, write, save"],"correct":3,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"sql-query-write","concept":"sql-query-write","concept_label":"sql-query-write","explanation_ja":"SQLのFROMは登録したview名。結果DataFrameのwrite.saveでパスへParquet出力する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.save.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: D\n\ndevices, write, save\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe SQL query references a temporary view created earlier in the code:\n\n```\ndevices_df.createOrReplaceTempView(\"devices\")\n```\n\nTherefore, the table name used in the SQL query must be `devices`, not the DataFrame variable name.\n\nThe result of `spark.sql()` returns a DataFrame, and to write this DataFrame to storage, Spark uses the DataFrameWriter API:\n\n```\nDataFrame.write\n```\n\nFinally, the `save()` method writes the DataFrame to the specified path with the desired format.\n\nCorrect completed code:\n\n```\ndevices_df = spark.read.json(\"devices.json\")\ndevices_df.createOrReplaceTempView(\"devices\")\n \nspark.sql(\"SELECT name, platform FROM devices\") \\\n     .write \\\n     .save(\"names.parquet\", format=\"parquet\")\n```\n\nExplanation of each placeholder:\n\nX → `devices` (the SQL temporary view)\nY → `write` (DataFrameWriter interface)\nZ → `save` (method used to write data to a file path)\n\nSpark documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.save.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.save.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nWhen using Spark SQL with temporary views:\n\n- \nCreate a temporary view\n\n```\ndf.createOrReplaceTempView(\"view_name\")\n```\n\n- \nQuery it with SQL\n\n```\nspark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM view_name\")\n```\n\n- \nWrite the result using the DataFrameWriter API\n\n```\ndf.write.format(\"parquet\").save(\"path\")\n```\n\nThe `.write` property exposes methods such as:\n\n• `save()`\n• `parquet()`\n• `json()`\n• `csv()`\n• `saveAsTable()`\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA. `devices_df, write, save`\n\nIncorrect because SQL queries must reference the temporary view name, not the DataFrame variable.\n\nB. `devices, save, write`\n\nIncorrect order. `save()` is a method of `DataFrameWriter`, which is accessed through `.write`.\n\nCorrect order:\n\n```\nDataFrame.write.save()\n```\n\nC. `devices, write, saveAsTable`\n\n`saveAsTable()` writes to a managed Spark table in the metastore, not to a file path like `\"names.parquet\"`.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.saveAsTable.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.saveAsTable.html)\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nWhen writing query results in Spark:\n\n```\nspark.sql(\"SELECT ... FROM table\") \\\n     .write \\\n     .save(\"path\", format=\"parquet\")\n```\n\nPattern to remember:\n\nSQL Query → DataFrame → `.write` → `.save()`","source_option_explanations":["A. `devices_df, write, save`\n\nIncorrect because SQL queries must reference the temporary view name, not the DataFrame variable.","B. `devices, save, write`\n\nIncorrect order. `save()` is a method of `DataFrameWriter`, which is accessed through `.write`.\n\nCorrect order:\n\n```\nDataFrame.write.save()\n```","C. `devices, write, saveAsTable`\n\n`saveAsTable()` writes to a managed Spark table in the metastore, not to a file path like `\"names.parquet\"`.","Correct Answer: D\n\ndevices, write, save\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe SQL query references a temporary view created earlier in the code:\n\n```\ndevices_df.createOrReplaceTempView(\"devices\")\n```\n\nTherefore, the table name used in the SQL query must be `devices`, not the DataFrame variable name.\n\nThe result of `spark.sql()` returns a DataFrame, and to write this DataFrame to storage, Spark uses the DataFrameWriter API:\n\n```\nDataFrame.write\n```\n\nFinally, the `save()` method writes the DataFrame to the specified path with the desired format.\n\nCorrect completed code:\n\n```\ndevices_df = spark.read.json(\"devices.json\")\ndevices_df.createOrReplaceTempView(\"devices\")\n \nspark.sql(\"SELECT name, platform FROM devices\") \\\n     .write \\\n     .save(\"names.parquet\", format=\"parquet\")\n```\n\nExplanation of each placeholder:\n\nX → `devices` (the SQL temporary view)\nY → `write` (DataFrameWriter interface)\nZ → `save` (method used to write data to a file path)"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.save.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrameWriter.saveAsTable.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"13f83f291a0e65b0a57f0f452e9353d7b911f68ec8bd4e316601c672afd25ca7","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"sql-view-result-parquet-writer","decision_id":"sql-view-result-parquet-writer"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-146","number":146,"stem":"Consider the following code snippet:\n\n```\nimport pyspark.pandas as ps\npsdf = ps.DataFrame({'col': [1, 2], 'col2': [3, 4]})\n```\n\nWhich method should be used to convert a Pandas API on Spark DataFrame (`pyspark.pandas.DataFrame`) into a standard PySpark DataFrame (`pyspark.sql.DataFrame`)?","options":["A.\n`psdf.to_spark()`","B.\n`psdf.to_pyspark()`","C.\n`psdf.to_pandas()`","D.\n`psdf.to_dataframe()`"],"correct":0,"domain":"Pandas API","topic":"pandas-to-spark","concept":"pandas-to-spark","concept_label":"pandas-to-spark","explanation_ja":"Pandas API on Sparkのto_sparkで通常のSpark DataFrameへ変換する。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.pandas/api/pyspark.pandas.DataFrame.to_pandas.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\n`psdf.to_spark()`\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe Pandas API on Spark (`pyspark.pandas` or `pandas-on-Spark`) provides a method `to_spark()` that converts a pyspark.pandas.DataFrame into a PySpark DataFrame (`pyspark.sql.DataFrame`).\n\nExample:\n\n```\nimport pyspark.pandas as ps\n \npsdf = ps.DataFrame({'col': [1, 2], 'col2': [3, 4]})\n \nspark_df = psdf.to_spark()\n```\n\nAfter conversion:\n\n- \n`psdf` → Pandas API on Spark DataFrame\n\n- \n`spark_df` → Standard PySpark DataFrame\n\nThis conversion is useful when switching from pandas-style operations to Spark SQL/DataFrame operations.\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nThere are three related DataFrame types in the Spark ecosystem:\n\nPandas DataFrame\n• Runs locally in Python memory\n\nPandas API on Spark DataFrame (`pyspark.pandas`)\n• Provides pandas-like syntax\n• Executes operations in a distributed Spark environment\n\nPySpark DataFrame (`pyspark.sql.DataFrame`)\n• Native Spark SQL DataFrame\n• Optimized through Catalyst and Tungsten engines\n\nConversion flow often looks like:\n\n```\npandas DataFrame\n        ↓\npandas-on-Spark DataFrame\n        ↓\nPySpark DataFrame\n```\n\nThe `to_spark()` method performs the second conversion step.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nB. `psdf.to_pyspark()`\n\nThis method does not exist in the Pandas API on Spark.\n\nC. `psdf.to_pandas()`\n\nThis converts the DataFrame to a local pandas DataFrame, not a PySpark DataFrame. It collects all data to the driver, which can cause memory issues for large datasets.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.pandas/api/pyspark.pandas.DataFrame.to_pandas.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.pandas/api/pyspark.pandas.DataFrame.to_pandas.html)\n\nD. `psdf.to_dataframe()`\n\nThis method does not exist in the Pandas API on Spark.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nWhen converting between DataFrame types:\n\n• Pandas-on-Spark → PySpark\n\n```\ndf.to_spark()\n```\n\n• Pandas-on-Spark → pandas\n\n```\ndf.to_pandas()\n```\n\nUnderstanding these conversions is important when integrating pandas-style data manipulation with Spark's distributed processing engine.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\n`psdf.to_spark()`\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe Pandas API on Spark (`pyspark.pandas` or `pandas-on-Spark`) provides a method `to_spark()` that converts a pyspark.pandas.DataFrame into a PySpark DataFrame (`pyspark.sql.DataFrame`).\n\nExample:\n\n```\nimport pyspark.pandas as ps\n \npsdf = ps.DataFrame({'col': [1, 2], 'col2': [3, 4]})\n \nspark_df = psdf.to_spark()\n```\n\nAfter conversion:\n\n- \n`psdf` → Pandas API on Spark DataFrame\n\n- \n`spark_df` → Standard PySpark DataFrame\n\nThis conversion is useful when switching from pandas-style operations to Spark SQL/DataFrame operations.","B. `psdf.to_pyspark()`\n\nThis method does not exist in the Pandas API on Spark.","C. `psdf.to_pandas()`\n\nThis converts the DataFrame to a local pandas DataFrame, not a PySpark DataFrame. It collects all data to the driver, which can cause memory issues for large datasets.","D. `psdf.to_dataframe()`\n\nThis method does not exist in the Pandas API on Spark."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.pandas/api/pyspark.pandas.DataFrame.to_pandas.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"1dbefb9141f6e8a3a20c6b615fabafa4873031ad2585aa5f345a18fcfedf3064","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"pandas-api-to-pyspark-conversion","decision_id":"pandas-api-to-pyspark-conversion"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-147","number":147,"stem":"Consider the following two DataFrames df1 and df2:\n\n```\ndf1 = spark.createDataFrame([(1, 'Alice', 1000), (2, 'Bob', 1500)], ['id', 'name', 'salary'])\n \ndf2 = spark.createDataFrame([(1, 'Alice', 'HR'), (2, 'Charlie', 'Engineering')], \n                            ['id', 'name', 'dept'])\n \n# Perform the join\njoined_df = df1.join(df2, X)\n```\n\nWhich expression should replace X to perform an inner join where both `id` and `name` must match between the two DataFrames?","options":["A.\n`(df1.id == df2.id) & (df1.name == df2.name)`","B.\n`(df1.id == df2.id)`","C.\n`['id', 'dept']`","D.\n`['id']`"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"join-multikey","concept":"join-multikey","concept_label":"join-multikey","explanation_ja":"2つの等値条件を&で結び、両列の一致を要求する。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\n`(df1.id == df2.id) & (df1.name == df2.name)`\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe requirement states that the join should occur only when both `id` and `name` match between `df1` and `df2`. This means the join condition must include two equality conditions combined with a logical AND operator.\n\nThe correct join expression is:\n\n```\njoined_df = df1.join(df2, (df1.id == df2.id) & (df1.name == df2.name))\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n• `df1.id == df2.id` ensures matching IDs\n• `df1.name == df2.name` ensures matching names\n• `&` combines both conditions so that both must be true\n\nSince no join type is specified, Spark performs an inner join by default, meaning only rows satisfying both conditions will appear in the result.\n\nSpark documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark allows joins in two primary ways:\n\n- \nColumn name list (when column names match)\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, [\"id\"])\n```\n\n- \nExplicit join conditions\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, df1.id == df2.id)\n```\n\nWhen joining on multiple columns with conditions, Spark requires a boolean expression combining conditions with `&`.\n\nExample:\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, (df1.col1 == df2.col1) & (df1.col2 == df2.col2))\n```\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nB. `(df1.id == df2.id)`\n\nThis only checks the id column, but the requirement states that both id and name must match.\n\nC. `['id', 'dept']`\n\nThis is invalid because:\n\n• `dept` does not exist in `df1`\n• Spark requires column names to exist in both DataFrames when using list-based joins.\n\nD. `['id']`\n\nThis performs a join only on the id column, ignoring the name condition, which does not satisfy the requirement.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nTo join on multiple conditions in Spark, use a boolean expression:\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, (condition1) & (condition2))\n```\n\nExample pattern:\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, (df1.colA == df2.colA) & (df1.colB == df2.colB))\n```\n\nThis ensures that all specified conditions must match for the join to succeed.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\n`(df1.id == df2.id) & (df1.name == df2.name)`\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe requirement states that the join should occur only when both `id` and `name` match between `df1` and `df2`. This means the join condition must include two equality conditions combined with a logical AND operator.\n\nThe correct join expression is:\n\n```\njoined_df = df1.join(df2, (df1.id == df2.id) & (df1.name == df2.name))\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n• `df1.id == df2.id` ensures matching IDs\n• `df1.name == df2.name` ensures matching names\n• `&` combines both conditions so that both must be true\n\nSince no join type is specified, Spark performs an inner join by default, meaning only rows satisfying both conditions will appear in the result.","B. `(df1.id == df2.id)`\n\nThis only checks the id column, but the requirement states that both id and name must match.","C. `['id', 'dept']`\n\nThis is invalid because:\n\n• `dept` does not exist in `df1`\n• Spark requires column names to exist in both DataFrames when using list-based joins.","D. `['id']`\n\nThis performs a join only on the id column, ignoring the name condition, which does not satisfy the requirement.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nTo join on multiple conditions in Spark, use a boolean expression:\n\n```\ndf1.join(df2, (condition1) & (condition2))\n```"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"4227e5d72dff8723a96cdb767f039509d61236dd24d798f7e4cd7d0ac807b8bf","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"multi-key-equi-join","decision_id":"multi-key-inner-join-conjunction"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-148","number":148,"stem":"A developer needs to store the result of a complex Spark transformation pipeline into a Parquet table named `events.live_latest`. Downstream users typically query this table using Spark SQL filters directly on event_year and event_month, extracted from the `event_ts` column, which is of TIMESTAMP type.\n\nThe following implementation is currently used:\n\n```\nimport pyspark.sql.functions as F\n \nfinal_df \\\n  .withColumn(\"event_year\", F.year(\"event_ts\")) \\\n  .withColumn(\"event_month\", F.month(\"event_ts\")) \\\n  .write \\\n  .bucketBy(42, [\"event_year\", \"event_month\"]) \\\n  .format(\"parquet\") \\\n  .saveAsTable(\"events.live_latest\")\n```\n\nHowever, downstream users report poor query performance when filtering by year and month.\n\nWhich modification should be applied so that queries filtering on both year and month of `event_ts` perform more efficiently?","options":["A.\nReplace `.bucketBy()` with `.partitionBy(\"event_year\")`.","B.\nReplace `.bucketBy()` with `.partitionBy([\"event_year\", \"event_month\"])`.","C.\nReduce the bucket count by lowering the value in `.bucketBy(42, ...)`.","D.\nAdd `.sortBy(\"event_month\")` after `.bucketBy()`."],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"storage-partition","concept":"storage-partition","concept_label":"storage-partition","explanation_ja":"年・月のpartition列自体をfilterするとdirectory pruningを使える。bucketByとpartitionByは用途が違う。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-parquet.html#partition-discovery","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-parquet.html#partition-discovery","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html#partition-discovery"],"images":[],"source_hash":"b6b52315a7ee67af591ca71baa72f2e9beb0886c2b2ff1e0b86880d3d907eff7","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"partition pruningは派生元timestampへの任意の式のfilterで常に効くとは限らない。partition列自体をfilterする条件に明示。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-parquet.html#partition-discovery"],"original_stem":"A developer needs to store the result of a complex Spark transformation pipeline into a Parquet table named `events.live_latest`. Downstream users typically query this table using Spark SQL filters on both the year and month extracted from the `event_ts` column, which is of TIMESTAMP type.\n\nThe following implementation is currently used:\n\n```\nimport pyspark.sql.functions as F\n \nfinal_df \\\n  .withColumn(\"event_year\", F.year(\"event_ts\")) \\\n  .withColumn(\"event_month\", F.month(\"event_ts\")) \\\n  .write \\\n  .bucketBy(42, [\"event_year\", \"event_month\"]) \\\n  .format(\"parquet\") \\\n  .saveAsTable(\"events.live_latest\")\n```\n\nHowever, downstream users report poor query performance when filtering by year and month.\n\nWhich modification should be applied so that queries filtering on both year and month of `event_ts` perform more efficiently?","original_options":["A.\nReplace `.bucketBy()` with `.partitionBy(\"event_year\")`.","B.\nReplace `.bucketBy()` with `.partitionBy([\"event_year\", \"event_month\"])`.","C.\nReduce the bucket count by lowering the value in `.bucketBy(42, ...)`.","D.\nAdd `.sortBy(\"event_month\")` after `.bucketBy()`."],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"partition-pruning-layout","decision_id":"partition-vs-bucket-filter-pruning"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-149","number":149,"stem":"A data engineer must create an ingestion pipeline for a collection of Parquet files delivered nightly by an upstream team. The files are organized in a directory structure with the base path:\n\n/path/events/data\n\nDaily data is stored in subdirectories following the pattern:\n\nyear/month/day\n\nExample directory structure:\n\n/path/events/data/2024/01/01\n/path/events/data/2024/01/02\n/path/events/data/2024/01/03\n/path/events/data/2023/01/01\n/path/events/data/2023/01/02\n/path/events/data/2023/01/03\n\nWhich of the following code snippets will correctly read all the data within this directory hierarchy?","options":["A.\ndf = spark.read.option(\"inferSchema\", \"true\").parquet(\"/path/events/data/\")","B.\ndf = spark.read.option(\"recursiveFileLookup\", \"true\").parquet(\"/path/events/data/\")","C.\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"/path/events/data/*\")","D.\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"/path/events/data/\")"],"correct":1,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"recursive-parquet","concept":"recursive-parquet","concept_label":"recursive-parquet","explanation_ja":"Hive形式ではないyear/month/day階層の全ファイルを読むにはrecursiveFileLookup=Trueを明示する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-generic-options.html#recursive-file-lookup","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-generic-options.html#recursive-file-lookup","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-parquet.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-generic-options.html"],"images":[],"source_hash":"335d8ee8ac730f32c5ba030ab504016e0565643bc27af0f6c85aeed19d19b96b","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"通常ディレクトリ階層の再帰読取は明示recursiveFileLookupが確実。元キーDからBへ修正。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-generic-options.html#recursive-file-lookup"],"original_stem":"A data engineer must create an ingestion pipeline for a collection of Parquet files delivered nightly by an upstream team. The files are organized in a directory structure with the base path:\n\n/path/events/data\n\nDaily data is stored in subdirectories following the pattern:\n\nyear/month/day\n\nExample directory structure:\n\n/path/events/data/2024/01/01\n/path/events/data/2024/01/02\n/path/events/data/2024/01/03\n/path/events/data/2023/01/01\n/path/events/data/2023/01/02\n/path/events/data/2023/01/03\n\nWhich of the following code snippets will correctly read all the data within this directory hierarchy?","original_options":["A.\ndf = spark.read.option(\"inferSchema\", \"true\").parquet(\"/path/events/data/\")","B.\ndf = spark.read.option(\"recursiveFileLookup\", \"true\").parquet(\"/path/events/data/\")","C.\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"/path/events/data/*\")","D.\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\"/path/events/data/\")"],"original_correct":["D"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"recursive-file-lookup-nonhive-hierarchy","decision_id":"recursive-file-lookup-nonhive-hierarchy"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-150","number":150,"stem":"A Spark developer wants to optimize the performance of an existing PySpark UDF that computes a SHAKE-256 hash. This hashing functionality is not available in the built-in Spark SQL functions library.\n\nThe current UDF implementation is:\n\n```\nimport hashlib\nimport pyspark.sql.functions as sf\nfrom pyspark.sql.types import StringType\n \ndef shake_256(raw: str) -> str:\n    return hashlib.shake_256(raw.encode()).hexdigest(20)\n \nshake_256_udf = sf.udf(shake_256, StringType())\n```\n\nTo improve performance, the developer attempts to convert the UDF into a Pandas UDF using the following definition:\n\n```\nshake_256_udf = sf.pandas_udf(shake_256, StringType())\n```\n\nHowever, Spark raises the error:\n\nUNSUPPORTED_SIGNATURE: Unsupported signature: (raw: str) -> str\n\nAfter rewriting the body to hash every element in a pandas Series and return a Series of the same length, which signature should be used for a Series → Series Pandas UDF?","options":["A.\n\n```\ndef shake_256(df: pd.Series) -> str\n```","B.\n\n```\ndef shake_256(df: Iterator[pd.Series]) -> Iterator[pd.Series]\n```","C.\n\n```\ndef shake_256(raw: str) -> str\n```","D.\n\n```\ndef shake_256(df: pd.Series) -> pd.Series\n```"],"correct":3,"domain":"Pandas API","topic":"scalar-pandas-udf","concept":"scalar-pandas-udf","concept_label":"scalar-pandas-udf","explanation_ja":"Series→Series型なら入力と出力はpandas Series。同時に関数本体も各要素をhashする処理へ変更する。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.pandas_udf.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.pandas_udf.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/sql/arrow_pandas.html#pandas-udfs","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/user_guide/sql/arrow_pandas.html#iterator-of-series-to-iterator-of-series-udf"],"images":[],"source_hash":"98b957e66bacc32f09081719c6ce51de90ee4095100a1b9d6505e9a9d972e3fd","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"Pandas UDFはsignature変更だけでは動かない。Seriesを処理する本体も必要で、Iterator型も対応形なのでSeries→Seriesに要件を限定。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.pandas_udf.html"],"original_stem":"A Spark developer wants to optimize the performance of an existing PySpark UDF that computes a SHAKE-256 hash. This hashing functionality is not available in the built-in Spark SQL functions library.\n\nThe current UDF implementation is:\n\n```\nimport hashlib\nimport pyspark.sql.functions as sf\nfrom pyspark.sql.types import StringType\n \ndef shake_256(raw):\n    return hashlib.shake_256(raw.encode()).hexdigest(20)\n \nshake_256_udf = sf.udf(shake_256, StringType())\n```\n\nTo improve performance, the developer attempts to convert the UDF into a Pandas UDF using the following definition:\n\n```\nshake_256_udf = sf.pandas_udf(shake_256, StringType())\n```\n\nHowever, Spark raises the error:\n\nUNSUPPORTED_SIGNATURE: Unsupported signature: (raw: str) -> str\n\nWhat should the function signature of `shake_256()` be changed to so that it works correctly with a Pandas UDF?","original_options":["A.\n\n```\ndef shake_256(df: pd.Series) -> str\n```","B.\n\n```\ndef shake_256(df: Iterator[pd.Series]) -> Iterator[pd.Series]\n```","C.\n\n```\ndef shake_256(raw: str) -> str\n```","D.\n\n```\ndef shake_256(df: pd.Series) -> pd.Series\n```"],"original_correct":["D"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"scalar-pandas-udf-series-signature","decision_id":"scalar-pandas-udf-series-signature"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-151","number":151,"stem":"In the following code snippet, `aggDf` represents the result of aggregation operations on a streaming DataFrame.\n\n```\naggDf\n  .writeStream \\\n  .outputMode(\"________\") \\\n  .format(\"console\") \\\n  .start()\n```\n\nWhich output mode should be specified so that the entire aggregated result table is written to the console on every trigger execution?","options":["A. complete","B. append","C. replace","D. aggregate"],"correct":0,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"stream-output-complete","concept":"stream-output-complete","concept_label":"stream-output-complete","explanation_ja":"complete output modeはtriggerごとに集計結果表全体を書き出す。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#output-modes","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\ncomplete\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Spark Structured Streaming, the Complete output mode writes the entire result table to the sink on every trigger. This is particularly useful when working with aggregations, because the aggregated result may change as new data arrives.\n\nThe correct configuration is:\n\n```\naggDf \\\n  .writeStream \\\n  .outputMode(\"complete\") \\\n  .format(\"console\") \\\n  .start()\n```\n\nWith complete mode, Spark outputs the full aggregated table every time the streaming query processes a new micro-batch.\n\nFor example, if you have a streaming aggregation like:\n\n```\nstreaming_df.groupBy(\"Id\").count()\n```\n\nEach trigger will print the entire updated aggregation result.\n\nSpark documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#output-modes](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#output-modes)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nStructured Streaming supports three output modes:\n\nAppend mode\n\n• Only new rows added since the last trigger are written to the sink\n• Typically used for event streams without updates\n\nUpdate mode\n\n• Only rows that changed since the last trigger are written\n\nComplete mode\n\n• The entire result table is written every trigger\n\nAggregation queries often require complete mode because previously computed rows may change when new events arrive.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nB. append\n\nAppend mode only writes new rows that were added to the result table. It cannot output the entire aggregated result table, especially when aggregation results are continuously updated.\n\nC. replace\n\n`replace` is not a valid Structured Streaming output mode.\n\nD. aggregate\n\n`aggregate` is not an output mode in Spark Structured Streaming.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nStructured Streaming output modes:\n\nAppend → only new rows\nUpdate → only modified rows\nComplete → entire result table every trigger\n\nTherefore, when you need the full aggregated table printed every time, the correct mode is:\n\n```\noutputMode(\"complete\")\n```","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\ncomplete\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nIn Spark Structured Streaming, the Complete output mode writes the entire result table to the sink on every trigger. This is particularly useful when working with aggregations, because the aggregated result may change as new data arrives.\n\nThe correct configuration is:\n\n```\naggDf \\\n  .writeStream \\\n  .outputMode(\"complete\") \\\n  .format(\"console\") \\\n  .start()\n```\n\nWith complete mode, Spark outputs the full aggregated table every time the streaming query processes a new micro-batch.\n\nFor example, if you have a streaming aggregation like:\n\n```\nstreaming_df.groupBy(\"Id\").count()\n```\n\nEach trigger will print the entire updated aggregation result.","B. append\n\nAppend mode only writes new rows that were added to the result table. It cannot output the entire aggregated result table, especially when aggregation results are continuously updated.","C. replace\n\n`replace` is not a valid Structured Streaming output mode.","D. aggregate\n\n`aggregate` is not an output mode in Spark Structured Streaming."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#output-modes"],"images":[],"source_hash":"9ed0b62eca9330a39f733b570fdca3afc358b5cf96f0a150d39a9955eb536918","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"complete-stream-output-mode","decision_id":"complete-stream-output-mode"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-152","number":152,"stem":"A data engineer is analyzing the following DataFrame `df`:\n\n![画像](https://img-c.udemycdn.com/redactor/raw/practice_test_question/2026-03-12_09-50-34-2c964a84fb12b4a9b914e8cf4bbefe8a.png)\n\n![画像](https://img-c.udemycdn.com/redactor/raw/practice_test_question/2026-03-12_09-50-34-2c964a84fb12b4a9b914e8cf4bbefe8a.png)\n\nThe engineer wants the rows ordered so that the highest `count` values appear first, followed by the next highest values, and so on.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly sorts the DataFrame using the Name and count columns to achieve this ordering?","options":["A.\n`df.select(\"Name\", \"count\").orderBy(\"count\", \"Name\", ascending=False)`","B.\n`df.select(\"Name\", \"count\").orderBy(\"count\")`","C.\n`df.select(\"Name\", \"count\").sort(\"Name\", \"count\")`","D.\n`df.select(\"Name\", \"count\").sort(\"Name\")`"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"sort-direction","concept":"sort-direction","concept_label":"sort-direction","explanation_ja":"count降順を先に指定し、Nameは同count時の第2ソートキーになる。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html","source_explanation":"Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe requirement is to order rows so that the highest `count` values appear first. That means the DataFrame must be sorted in descending order by `count`.\n\nIn Apache Spark, the `orderBy()` or `sort()` transformation is used to sort rows in a DataFrame. When the parameter `ascending=False` is provided, Spark sorts the specified columns in descending order.\n\nOption A performs the following:\n\n- \nSelects the required columns: `Name` and `count`\n\n- \nSorts the DataFrame using `orderBy()`\n\n- \nSpecifies `ascending=False`, ensuring the largest count values appear first\n\nExample logic:\n\n- \nIf counts are `[50, 40, 30, 20, 10]`\n\n- \nUsing `ascending=False` results in the order\n`50 → 40 → 30 → 20 → 10`\n\nThis matches the requirement that highest values appear first.\n\nSpark documentation confirms that `orderBy()` sorts rows and supports specifying sort order through the `ascending` parameter.\n\nRelevant documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html)\n\nFoundational concept (good to know)\n\n- \n`orderBy()` and `sort()` are aliases in Spark DataFrames.\n\n- \nBy default, sorting is ascending.\n\n- \nTo place the largest values first, descending order must be explicitly specified.\n\n- \nSorting is a wide transformation, meaning it can trigger a shuffle across partitions.\n\nUnderstanding default ordering behavior is important because many Spark bugs come from assuming descending order when the default is ascending.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nOption B\n\n`df.select(\"Name\", \"count\").orderBy(\"count\")`\n\nThis sorts by `count`, but the default order is ascending, meaning the lowest values appear first. This directly contradicts the requirement to show the highest values first.\n\nOption C\n\n`df.select(\"Name\", \"count\").sort(\"Name\", \"count\")`\n\nThis sorts primarily by Name, not by `count`. The requirement focuses on ordering rows by count values, so prioritizing `Name` does not meet the requirement.\n\nOption D\n\n`df.select(\"Name\", \"count\").sort(\"Name\")`\n\nThis sorts only by Name alphabetically and completely ignores the `count` column. Therefore, it does not satisfy the requirement of ordering rows based on highest count values.\n\nKey learning takeaway\n\nIn Spark DataFrames:\n\n- \n`orderBy()` and `sort()` are equivalent.\n\n- \nDefault sorting is ascending.\n\n- \nTo get largest values first, you must explicitly specify descending order using `ascending=False` or `desc()`.\n\nCorrect solution: A.","source_option_explanations":["Correct answer: A\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe requirement is to order rows so that the highest `count` values appear first. That means the DataFrame must be sorted in descending order by `count`.\n\nIn Apache Spark, the `orderBy()` or `sort()` transformation is used to sort rows in a DataFrame. When the parameter `ascending=False` is provided, Spark sorts the specified columns in descending order.\n\nOption A performs the following:\n\n- \nSelects the required columns: `Name` and `count`\n\n- \nSorts the DataFrame using `orderBy()`\n\n- \nSpecifies `ascending=False`, ensuring the largest count values appear first\n\nExample logic:\n\n- \nIf counts are `[50, 40, 30, 20, 10]`\n\n- \nUsing `ascending=False` results in the order\n`50 → 40 → 30 → 20 → 10`\n\nThis matches the requirement that highest values appear first.\n\nSpark documentation confirms that `orderBy()` sorts rows and supports specifying sort order through the `ascending` parameter.","Option B\n\n`df.select(\"Name\", \"count\").orderBy(\"count\")`\n\nThis sorts by `count`, but the default order is ascending, meaning the lowest values appear first. This directly contradicts the requirement to show the highest values first.","Option C\n\n`df.select(\"Name\", \"count\").sort(\"Name\", \"count\")`\n\nThis sorts primarily by Name, not by `count`. The requirement focuses on ordering rows by count values, so prioritizing `Name` does not meet the requirement.","Option D\n\n`df.select(\"Name\", \"count\").sort(\"Name\")`\n\nThis sorts only by Name alphabetically and completely ignores the `count` column. Therefore, it does not satisfy the requirement of ordering rows based on highest count values."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html"],"images":[{"url":"https://img-c.udemycdn.com/redactor/raw/practice_test_question/2026-03-12_09-50-34-2c964a84fb12b4a9b914e8cf4bbefe8a.png","local":"exam4-q152.png","alt":"設問152のデータ表"}],"source_hash":"d9280b445b09d8abb7097aca09ddf237745d5ba5ae8d03e61575871dc72a7a3c","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"sort-direction","decision_id":"sort-direction"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-153","number":153,"stem":"A data engineer is analyzing the following Spark DataFrame:\n\n![画像](https://img-c.udemycdn.com/redactor/raw/practice_test_question/2026-03-12_07-53-18-f3d8ee122167985c66bdb9bd846df2ca.png)\n\n![画像](https://img-c.udemycdn.com/redactor/raw/practice_test_question/2026-03-12_07-53-18-f3d8ee122167985c66bdb9bd846df2ca.png)\n\nThe engineer needs to retrieve the distinct values from the Name column and return them as a list sorted alphabetically.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly achieves this?","options":["A.\ndf.select(\"Name\").orderBy(df[\"Name\"].asc())","B.\n[row[\"Name\"] for row in df.select(\"Name\").distinct().orderBy(\"Name\").collect()]","C.\ndf.select(\"Name\").distinct()","D.\ndf.select(\"Name\").distinct().orderBy(df[\"Name\"].desc())"],"correct":1,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"distinct-sort-list","concept":"distinct-sort-list","concept_label":"distinct-sort-list","explanation_ja":"distinctしたNameを昇順にした後collectし、Rowから文字列を取り出してlistを作る。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.collect.html","source_explanation":null,"source_option_explanations":null,"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.collect.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.distinct.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.distinct.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.orderBy.html"],"images":[{"url":"https://img-c.udemycdn.com/redactor/raw/practice_test_question/2026-03-12_07-53-18-f3d8ee122167985c66bdb9bd846df2ca.png","local":"exam4-q153.png","alt":"設問153のデータ表"}],"source_hash":"88076d94e98528779aca50254936fc3f77a3d108e8d2d0f3c91652cd7dacfb98","revision":{"id":"20261025-r1","reason":"distinct/orderByはDataFrameのまま。要求どおりPython listを返す処理を正解Bに追加。","sources":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.collect.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.distinct.html"],"original_stem":"A data engineer is analyzing the following Spark DataFrame:\n\n![画像](https://img-c.udemycdn.com/redactor/raw/practice_test_question/2026-03-12_07-53-18-f3d8ee122167985c66bdb9bd846df2ca.png)\n\n![画像](https://img-c.udemycdn.com/redactor/raw/practice_test_question/2026-03-12_07-53-18-f3d8ee122167985c66bdb9bd846df2ca.png)\n\nThe engineer needs to retrieve the distinct values from the Name column and return them as a list sorted alphabetically.\n\nWhich code snippet correctly achieves this?","original_options":["A.\ndf.select(\"Name\").orderBy(df[\"Name\"].asc())","B.\ndf.select(\"Name\").distinct().orderBy(df[\"Name\"])","C.\ndf.select(\"Name\").distinct()","D.\ndf.select(\"Name\").distinct().orderBy(df[\"Name\"].desc())"],"original_correct":["B"]},"grading_hold":null,"validation":"公式仕様に基づき訂正。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"distinct-sorted-python-value-list","decision_id":"distinct-sorted-python-value-list"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-154","number":154,"stem":"The database administration team reports that a Spark application is creating too many connections to a PostgreSQL database. While reviewing the Spark job, an engineer finds the following code used to read data through JDBC:\n\n```\nSET spark.sql.shuffle.partitions=1007;\n \nCREATE TEMPORARY VIEW sales_fct\nUSING org.apache.spark.sql.jdbc\nOPTIONS (\n  url \"jdbc:postgresql://pgdb1:5432/dw\",\n  dbtable \"dw.sales_fct\",\n  user \"sparkrep01\",\n  password \"G7xPg9vL2M\",\n  numPartitions \"25\",\n  partitionColumn \"sales_id\",\n  lowerBound \"1\",\n  upperBound \"10000\"\n)\n```\n\nWhich modification would reduce the number of database connections opened by the Spark application?","options":["A.\nDecrease the numPartitions value from 25 to 8.","B.\nIncrease the upperBound value from 10,000 to 100,000 to widen the partition stride.","C.\nReduce spark.sql.shuffle.partitions from 100 to 8.","D.\nChange partitionColumn from `sales_id` to another PostgreSQL column that has an index available."],"correct":0,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"jdbc-connections","concept":"jdbc-connections","concept_label":"jdbc-connections","explanation_ja":"JDBCのnumPartitionsは同時接続数の上限に関係する。shuffle partitionsやupperBoundを変えるだけでは同じ制限にならない。","distractors_ja":["正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-jdbc.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: A\n\nDecrease the `numPartitions` value from 25 to 8.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nWhen Spark reads data using JDBC parallelization, the parameter `numPartitions` controls how many parallel JDBC connections Spark opens to the database.\n\nIn the given configuration:\n\n```\nnumPartitions \"25\"\n```\n\nSpark will create 25 parallel JDBC connections, each reading a portion of the table defined by the `partitionColumn`, `lowerBound`, and `upperBound`.\n\nReducing this value directly reduces the number of simultaneous database connections opened by Spark.\n\nExample modification:\n\n```\nnumPartitions \"8\"\n```\n\nThis change limits Spark to 8 parallel JDBC reads, thereby lowering the load on the PostgreSQL database.\n\nSpark JDBC documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-jdbc.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-jdbc.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark parallelizes JDBC reads using these parameters:\n\n• `partitionColumn` – column used to divide the data\n• `lowerBound` – minimum value of the partition column\n• `upperBound` – maximum value of the partition column\n• `numPartitions` – number of parallel tasks / database connections\n\nSpark splits the data range into numPartitions segments, and each executor task opens a separate JDBC connection.\n\nExample concept:\n\nIf:\n\n```\nnumPartitions = 25\n```\n\nSpark will create 25 parallel queries, meaning 25 database connections.\n\nReducing `numPartitions` reduces the number of parallel database connections.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nB. Increase the upperBound value from 10,000 to 100,000 to widen the partition stride.\n\nChanging `upperBound` only affects the range used for partition splitting, not the number of partitions or connections.\n\nSpark will still create 25 connections because `numPartitions` remains unchanged.\n\nC. Reduce `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` from 100 to 8.\n\n`spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` controls shuffle operations inside Spark, not JDBC reads. It does not affect the number of JDBC connections opened.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#other-configuration-options](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#other-configuration-options)\n\nD. Change `partitionColumn` to another PostgreSQL column that has an index available.\n\nUsing an indexed column may improve query performance, but it does not change the number of database connections Spark opens.\n\nThe connection count is still determined by `numPartitions`.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nWhen reading from JDBC in Spark:\n\n• `numPartitions` controls the number of parallel JDBC connections\n\nRule to remember:\n\n```\nNumber of JDBC connections ≈ numPartitions\n```\n\nTherefore, to reduce database load, lower the `numPartitions` value.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer: A\n\nDecrease the `numPartitions` value from 25 to 8.\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nWhen Spark reads data using JDBC parallelization, the parameter `numPartitions` controls how many parallel JDBC connections Spark opens to the database.\n\nIn the given configuration:\n\n```\nnumPartitions \"25\"\n```\n\nSpark will create 25 parallel JDBC connections, each reading a portion of the table defined by the `partitionColumn`, `lowerBound`, and `upperBound`.\n\nReducing this value directly reduces the number of simultaneous database connections opened by Spark.\n\nExample modification:\n\n```\nnumPartitions \"8\"\n```\n\nThis change limits Spark to 8 parallel JDBC reads, thereby lowering the load on the PostgreSQL database.","B. Increase the upperBound value from 10,000 to 100,000 to widen the partition stride.\n\nChanging `upperBound` only affects the range used for partition splitting, not the number of partitions or connections.\n\nSpark will still create 25 connections because `numPartitions` remains unchanged.","C. Reduce `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` from 100 to 8.\n\n`spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` controls shuffle operations inside Spark, not JDBC reads. It does not affect the number of JDBC connections opened.","D. Change `partitionColumn` to another PostgreSQL column that has an index available.\n\nUsing an indexed column may improve query performance, but it does not change the number of database connections Spark opens.\n\nThe connection count is still determined by `numPartitions`."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-jdbc.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-performance-tuning.html#other-configuration-options"],"images":[],"source_hash":"d129006b01c259cc62d69033ceeda67346df97b77f71d5f83a1db1e4270c2400","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"jdbc-num-partitions-connections","decision_id":"jdbc-num-partitions-connections"},{"id":"PASTED-SPARK-20261024-155","number":155,"stem":"A developer is troubleshooting a Spark application where data from a CSV file is being incorrectly loaded into a DataFrame.\n\nThe CSV file was read using the following Spark SQL statement:\n\n```\nCREATE TABLE default.air_ref\n(\n  iata STRING,\n  iso_country STRING,\n  city STRING,\n  lat DOUBLE,\n  long DOUBLE\n)\nUSING CSV\nOPTIONS ('path' = '/landing/air_ref_20241001.csv')\n```\n\nHowever, when running:\n\n```\nSELECT * FROM default.air_ref\n```\n\nthe output appears incorrectly parsed, for example:\n\n```\n+-------------------------+------------+------+-----+------+\n| iata                    | iso_country| city | lat | long |\n+-------------------------+------------+------+-----+------+\n| iata | iso_country | ...| NULL       | NULL | NULL| NULL |\n| SYD | AU | Sydney | -33 | ...        | NULL | NULL| NULL |\n+-------------------------+------------+------+-----+------+\n```\n\nThis indicates that multiple values are being read into a single column, causing the remaining columns to be NULL.\n\nWhich parameter should be added to the OPTIONS clause in the `CREATE TABLE` statement to correctly parse the CSV file?","options":["A.\n`quote = '|'`","B.\n`inferSchema = 'true'`","C.\n`header = 'true', sep = '|'`","D.\n`header = 'true'`"],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"csv-delimiter","concept":"csv-delimiter","concept_label":"csv-delimiter","explanation_ja":"列区切りsepと先頭行headerの両方を指定する。inferSchemaは区切り文字を直さない。","distractors_ja":["この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。","正答に含まれます。上の要点と公式参照で根拠を確認してください。","この選択肢は要件を満たしません。上の要点と公式参照で違いを確認してください。"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-csv.html","source_explanation":"Correct Answer: C\n\nheader = 'true', sep = '|'\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe incorrect output indicates that multiple values are being loaded into a single column, which typically happens when Spark uses the wrong delimiter to parse the CSV file.\n\nBy default, Spark expects CSV files to be comma-separated. However, the sample rows clearly show that the file uses the pipe character (`|`) as the delimiter:\n\nExample row:\n\n```\nSYD | AU | Sydney | -33 | ...\n```\n\nTo correctly parse such a file, Spark must be told:\n\n• the separator character (`|`)\n• that the file contains a header row\n\nCorrect configuration:\n\n```\nOPTIONS (\n  path '/landing/air_ref_20241001.csv',\n  header 'true',\n  sep '|'\n)\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n• `sep='|'` tells Spark to split fields using the pipe delimiter\n• `header='true'` instructs Spark to treat the first row as column names\n\nWithout the correct separator, Spark reads the entire line as a single column, leaving the remaining columns as `NULL`.\n\nSpark documentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-csv.html](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-csv.html)\n\nNeed-to-know foundational concept\n\nSpark CSV reader parameters commonly used in ingestion pipelines:\n\ndelimiter / sep\nSpecifies the character used to separate fields.\n\nheader\nIndicates whether the first row contains column names.\n\ninferSchema\nAllows Spark to automatically determine column data types.\n\nExample pattern:\n\n```\nCREATE TABLE table_name\nUSING CSV\nOPTIONS (\n  path '/data/file.csv',\n  header 'true',\n  sep '|'\n)\n```\n\nCorrect delimiter configuration is critical for correct parsing.\n\nWhy the other options are not the best choice\n\nA. `quote = '|'`\n\nThe `quote` option specifies the character used to enclose fields that may contain delimiters, not the delimiter itself. Using `|` as a quote character would not fix the parsing issue.\n\nDocumentation:\n[https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-csv.html#data-source-option](https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-csv.html#data-source-option)\n\nB. `inferSchema = 'true'`\n\nThis only tells Spark to detect column data types automatically. It does not affect how columns are split.\n\nD. `header = 'true'`\n\nWhile this ensures the first row is treated as column names, it does not fix the delimiter issue, so the file would still be parsed incorrectly.\n\nKey takeaway for the exam\n\nWhen Spark loads CSV data incorrectly with multiple fields appearing in a single column, the most common cause is an incorrect delimiter configuration.\n\nAlways specify the correct delimiter:\n\n```\nsep '|'\n```\n\nThis is a frequently tested ingestion configuration concept in Spark and Databricks exams.","source_option_explanations":["A. `quote = '|'`\n\nThe `quote` option specifies the character used to enclose fields that may contain delimiters, not the delimiter itself. Using `|` as a quote character would not fix the parsing issue.","B. `inferSchema = 'true'`\n\nThis only tells Spark to detect column data types automatically. It does not affect how columns are split.","Correct Answer: C\n\nheader = 'true', sep = '|'\n\nWhy this option is correct\n\nThe incorrect output indicates that multiple values are being loaded into a single column, which typically happens when Spark uses the wrong delimiter to parse the CSV file.\n\nBy default, Spark expects CSV files to be comma-separated. However, the sample rows clearly show that the file uses the pipe character (`|`) as the delimiter:\n\nExample row:\n\n```\nSYD | AU | Sydney | -33 | ...\n```\n\nTo correctly parse such a file, Spark must be told:\n\n• the separator character (`|`)\n• that the file contains a header row\n\nCorrect configuration:\n\n```\nOPTIONS (\n  path '/landing/air_ref_20241001.csv',\n  header 'true',\n  sep '|'\n)\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n• `sep='|'` tells Spark to split fields using the pipe delimiter\n• `header='true'` instructs Spark to treat the first row as column names\n\nWithout the correct separator, Spark reads the entire line as a single column, leaving the remaining columns as `NULL`.","D. `header = 'true'`\n\nWhile this ensures the first row is treated as column names, it does not fix the delimiter issue, so the file would still be parsed incorrectly."],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-csv.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-data-sources-csv.html#data-source-option"],"images":[],"source_hash":"e872339e4797f226fc21e75472803d10685d2a882f6bb6fdb073c33f38caf1fc","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"validation":"設問・全選択肢を静的確認。Spark実行未検証。","source_kind":"provided","content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","family_id":"csv-header-separator-options","decision_id":"csv-header-separator-options"}]},{"id":"AUTHORED-Architecture","title":"自作問題 · Architecture","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-001","number":null,"stem":"A batch application has materialized a persisted RDD. One executor fails and the only cached copies of several partitions are lost. The driver and sufficient executors remain healthy. The original input is still accessible, and all transformations are deterministic. What should Spark normally do when a later action needs the lost partitions?","options":["Recompute every partition of every RDD in the application from the beginning.","Return NULL for each record that was stored on the failed executor.","Recompute the missing partitions from their lineage and reuse surviving cached partitions where possible.","Stop permanently because persistence disables fault recovery."],"correct":2,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"キャッシュ喪失とlineage","concept":"cached-partition-loss-lineage-recompute","decision_id":"cached-partition-loss-lineage-recompute","family_id":"cached-partition-loss-lineage-recompute","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A batch application has materialized a persisted RDD. One executor fails and the only cached copies of several partitions are lost. The driver and sufficient executors remain healthy. The original input is still accessible, and all transformations are deterministic. What should Spark normally do when a later action needs the lost partitions?","options_ja":["Recompute every partition of every RDD in the application from the beginning.","Return NULL for each record that was stored on the failed executor.","Recompute the missing partitions from their lineage and reuse surviving cached partitions where possible.","Stop permanently because persistence disables fault recovery."],"explanation_ja":"失われたcache partitionは元の変換から再計算できる。cacheはlineageを消さず、残るpartitionまで必ず全て捨てる必要はない。","distractors_ja":["障害で全applicationの全partitionを無条件に再計算する規則ではない。","cache喪失をレコードのNULL置換で埋めない。","正答。lineageと元入力が利用可能なら失われたpartitionを再構築する。","persistenceしてもlineageによる障害回復が可能。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/rdd-programming-guide.html#rdd-persistence"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/rdd-programming-guide.html#rdd-persistence","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"dbfa58670312f5daf0a2662c132c0e115ef1a1029aebf43e2419b8727fd61b6c"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-002","number":null,"stem":"A PySpark application runs on a remote cluster. A driver-side Python list named seen is captured by a function passed to an RDD foreach action. Each task appends identifiers to that list. After foreach finishes successfully, the driver list is still empty. Which explanation is correct?","options":["foreach is a transformation, so no tasks have executed yet.","Spark only copies mutations back when the list contains fewer than 1,000 elements.","Executor tasks modify serialized copies of the closure state, not the original list in the driver process.","The driver must enable broadcast joins before Python lists can be modified."],"correct":2,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"Python closureの変数","concept":"executor-closure-cannot-mutate-driver-state","decision_id":"executor-closure-cannot-mutate-driver-state","family_id":"executor-closure-cannot-mutate-driver-state","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A PySpark application runs on a remote cluster. A driver-side Python list named seen is captured by a function passed to an RDD foreach action. Each task appends identifiers to that list. After foreach finishes successfully, the driver list is still empty. Which explanation is correct?","options_ja":["foreach is a transformation, so no tasks have executed yet.","Spark only copies mutations back when the list contains fewer than 1,000 elements.","Executor tasks modify serialized copies of the closure state, not the original list in the driver process.","The driver must enable broadcast joins before Python lists can be modified."],"explanation_ja":"executorで実行するclosureはdriverとは別の変数コピーを使う。外部listへの変更をdriver共有メモリへの書込みとして扱えない。","distractors_ja":["foreachはactionであり、成功終了までにtaskは実行される。","listの要素数で変更がdriverへ反映される仕組みはない。","正答。driverとexecutorは別processのためコピーの変更は戻らない。","broadcast joinはPython closureの可変状態共有を有効化しない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/rdd-programming-guide.html#understanding-closures"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/rdd-programming-guide.html#understanding-closures","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"09a0ea503f8f3e26933c434207a03c990b111d5ddc58c0bf012b7e26b2b87bc5"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-003","number":null,"stem":"A team is porting a Scala pipeline that represents business objects as Dataset[Order] using an encoder. The replacement application must be written in Python and should retain Spark SQL optimized structured-data processing. Which migration plan matches the public language APIs in Apache Spark 3.5.6?","options":["Represent the records with a PySpark DataFrame; the strongly typed Dataset API is available in Scala and Java, not Python.","Add Python type hints to Dataset[Order] so the Python compiler enforces the same typed Dataset operations.","Replace all structured processing with RDDs because Spark does not provide a Python DataFrame API.","Import the Scala encoder into Python and call DataFrame.as(Order) to obtain the same typed Dataset API."],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"言語APIとtyped Dataset","concept":"python-dataframe-without-typed-dataset-api","decision_id":"python-dataframe-without-typed-dataset-api","family_id":"python-dataframe-without-typed-dataset-api","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A team is porting a Scala pipeline that represents business objects as Dataset[Order] using an encoder. The replacement application must be written in Python and should retain Spark SQL optimized structured-data processing. Which migration plan matches the public language APIs in Apache Spark 3.5.6?","options_ja":["Represent the records with a PySpark DataFrame; the strongly typed Dataset API is available in Scala and Java, not Python.","Add Python type hints to Dataset[Order] so the Python compiler enforces the same typed Dataset operations.","Replace all structured processing with RDDs because Spark does not provide a Python DataFrame API.","Import the Scala encoder into Python and call DataFrame.as(Order) to obtain the same typed Dataset API."],"explanation_ja":"Sparkのtyped Dataset APIはScalaとJavaに提供され、Pythonには同じAPIがない。Pythonではschema付きDataFrameを使いSpark SQLの最適化されたstructured data処理を行える。","distractors_ja":["正答。Python DataFrameとScala/Javaのtyped Datasetという言語API境界を区別する。","Python型注釈を付けても未提供のtyped Dataset APIや静的compiler検査が追加されない。","PySparkにはDataFrame APIがあるため全処理をRDDへ戻す必要はない。","ScalaのencoderやDataFrame.asをPythonへimportして同じtyped Dataset APIを利用することはできない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-programming-guide.html#datasets-and-dataframes"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-programming-guide.html#datasets-and-dataframes","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"316e2eae052aff0eff33da56b466253ed5bcdade429e9774e0ec7edca5deb42f"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-004","number":null,"stem":"Several stages of a PySpark application need the same Python dictionary as a lookup. The dictionary fits comfortably in each worker process and must remain read-only during the application. The engineer wants to distribute it efficiently rather than capture its full value repeatedly in task closures. Which approach is appropriate?","options":["Put the dictionary in an accumulator and read the accumulated value from every task.","Create a SparkContext broadcast variable and read its value inside the task function.","Use the DataFrame broadcast join hint directly on the Python dictionary.","Create a separate SparkSession for every dictionary key."],"correct":1,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"共有lookupとbroadcast変数","concept":"python-readonly-lookup-broadcast-value","decision_id":"python-readonly-lookup-broadcast-value","family_id":"python-readonly-lookup-broadcast-value","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"Several stages of a PySpark application need the same Python dictionary as a lookup. The dictionary fits comfortably in each worker process and must remain read-only during the application. The engineer wants to distribute it efficiently rather than capture its full value repeatedly in task closures. Which approach is appropriate?","options_ja":["Put the dictionary in an accumulator and read the accumulated value from every task.","Create a SparkContext broadcast variable and read its value inside the task function.","Use the DataFrame broadcast join hint directly on the Python dictionary.","Create a separate SparkSession for every dictionary key."],"explanation_ja":"read-onlyなPython lookupの共有にはSparkContext.broadcastを使い、task内でbroadcast変数のvalueを読む。DataFrame join用のbroadcast hintとはAPIが異なる。","distractors_ja":["accumulatorの値を読めるのはdriverでありlookup共有用ではない。","正答。Pythonの値をbroadcast変数として配布する。","DataFrame join hintはPython辞書をbroadcast変数に変換しない。","sessionをkeyごとに作ってもlookup配布の効率化にならない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/api/pyspark.Broadcast.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/rdd-programming-guide.html#broadcast-variables"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/api/pyspark.Broadcast.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"165c53e43334870d68e76c49f8284006ebc05e688a3b075c062a87b9647ce974"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-005","number":null,"stem":"A driver creates a broadcast variable called lookup_bc, uses it in an action, and then calls lookup_bc.unpersist(blocking=True). The driver does not call destroy and keeps the broadcast object. A later action uses lookup_bc.value again. Which behavior is expected?","options":["The value becomes a writable shared variable for all executors.","The later action must fail because unpersist permanently invalidates the broadcast object.","unpersist changes the broadcast value to an empty dictionary.","Executor cached copies are removed, and the broadcast can be sent again when needed."],"correct":3,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"broadcastのunpersist","concept":"broadcast-unpersist-allows-resend","decision_id":"broadcast-unpersist-allows-resend","family_id":"broadcast-unpersist-allows-resend","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A driver creates a broadcast variable called lookup_bc, uses it in an action, and then calls lookup_bc.unpersist(blocking=True). The driver does not call destroy and keeps the broadcast object. A later action uses lookup_bc.value again. Which behavior is expected?","options_ja":["The value becomes a writable shared variable for all executors.","The later action must fail because unpersist permanently invalidates the broadcast object.","unpersist changes the broadcast value to an empty dictionary.","Executor cached copies are removed, and the broadcast can be sent again when needed."],"explanation_ja":"Broadcast.unpersistはexecutor上のcache copyを削除する。destroyとは異なり、そのbroadcastを後で使えば再転送される。","distractors_ja":["read-writeの共有状態に変わることはない。","永久無効化の説明はunpersistではない。","unpersistは論理的なbroadcast valueを書き換えない。","正答。削除後の利用時にはexecutorへ再送される。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/api/pyspark.Broadcast.unpersist.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/api/pyspark.Broadcast.unpersist.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"5a90f85c72d785e61446e3570028f3384afc5ea91a6707cd7d819fc3a7616e20"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-006","number":null,"stem":"An engineer uses a numeric accumulator to count invalid rows during a batch action. The engineer also wants each executor task to read the accumulator current total and stop when a global threshold is reached. Which statement accurately describes the accumulator contract?","options":["Tasks can add updates, while the driver reads the accumulated value; it is not a shared readable global threshold for tasks.","Reading an accumulator in a task automatically triggers a shuffle that synchronizes every task.","Only the cluster manager can update an accumulator.","All tasks can read and write the same globally synchronized accumulator value."],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"accumulatorの読取り範囲","concept":"accumulator-updates-workers-read-driver","decision_id":"accumulator-updates-workers-read-driver","family_id":"accumulator-updates-workers-read-driver","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"An engineer uses a numeric accumulator to count invalid rows during a batch action. The engineer also wants each executor task to read the accumulator current total and stop when a global threshold is reached. Which statement accurately describes the accumulator contract?","options_ja":["Tasks can add updates, while the driver reads the accumulated value; it is not a shared readable global threshold for tasks.","Reading an accumulator in a task automatically triggers a shuffle that synchronizes every task.","Only the cluster manager can update an accumulator.","All tasks can read and write the same globally synchronized accumulator value."],"explanation_ja":"accumulatorはtaskから加算しdriverで値を読む契約。task全体が読み書きする共有カウンタではなく、分散taskを止める同期しきい値として使えない。","distractors_ja":["正答。加算の集約とdriver側の読取りを分ける。","task内読取りをshuffle同期へ自動変換しない。","worker taskから加算できる。","taskで共通の現在値を同期読取りする契約ではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/api/pyspark.Accumulator.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/api/pyspark.Accumulator.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"58aa8e8927810f6c0209cae221a4f04b01022f91ba6e8d4cb4e753d40520663c"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-007","number":null,"stem":"Two independent Spark applications request executor resources from the same YARN cluster. Each application already has a driver that can schedule its own tasks. Which component is responsible for allocating cluster resources across these applications?","options":["The cluster manager, such as YARN.","The executor that currently has the most free heap memory.","The DataFrameReader of the application submitted first.","The Catalyst optimizer of either application."],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"cluster managerの役割","concept":"cluster-manager-allocates-application-resources","decision_id":"cluster-manager-allocates-application-resources","family_id":"cluster-manager-allocates-application-resources","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"Two independent Spark applications request executor resources from the same YARN cluster. Each application already has a driver that can schedule its own tasks. Which component is responsible for allocating cluster resources across these applications?","options_ja":["The cluster manager, such as YARN.","The executor that currently has the most free heap memory.","The DataFrameReader of the application submitted first.","The Catalyst optimizer of either application."],"explanation_ja":"application間のresource割当はcluster managerが担う。各driver内のtask scheduling、CatalystのSQL最適化とは範囲が異なる。","distractors_ja":["正答。YARN等のcluster managerがresourceを配分する。","executorが他applicationへresourceを割り当てる構成ではない。","readerは外部データの読取りAPIでありcluster resourceを割り当てない。","CatalystはSQL plan最適化を担いcluster全体のresource配分をしない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/cluster-overview.html#components"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/cluster-overview.html#components","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"584db127a1da4319ffa762bf2a346e29ff087d08015f9a268cc8db32551f27f6"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-008","number":null,"stem":"Application A caches a large DataFrame on a cluster. Application B starts with a different SparkContext on the same worker machines and reads the same source path. No external cache service is configured. Can B directly reuse A executor cache just because the paths and worker machines match?","options":["No, because a cluster can run only one Spark application at a time.","Yes. DataFrame cache is automatically global to all SparkContexts on a worker.","Yes, but only when both applications use the same DataFrame variable name.","No. The applications have separate executor processes; cross-application data sharing requires an external mechanism."],"correct":3,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"application間のcache隔離","concept":"separate-applications-do-not-share-executor-cache","decision_id":"separate-applications-do-not-share-executor-cache","family_id":"separate-applications-do-not-share-executor-cache","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"Application A caches a large DataFrame on a cluster. Application B starts with a different SparkContext on the same worker machines and reads the same source path. No external cache service is configured. Can B directly reuse A executor cache just because the paths and worker machines match?","options_ja":["No, because a cluster can run only one Spark application at a time.","Yes. DataFrame cache is automatically global to all SparkContexts on a worker.","Yes, but only when both applications use the same DataFrame variable name.","No. The applications have separate executor processes; cross-application data sharing requires an external mechanism."],"explanation_ja":"Spark applicationごとに独立したexecutor processを持ち、通常のexecutor cacheは別applicationへそのまま共有されない。同じpathや変数名では共有されない。","distractors_ja":["複数applicationの実行自体は可能。","通常のDataFrame cacheが全SparkContextに公開されるわけではない。","Python変数名は別application間のcache共有keyではない。","正答。cacheの隔離はapplicationのprocess境界に従う。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/cluster-overview.html#components"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/cluster-overview.html#components","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"6de57d527b2af70a911fc6f400233b658f9555957bd7e725b10e09e1fce9651c"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-009","number":null,"stem":"A developer submits a client-mode application from a laptop. The laptop can reach the cluster manager, but a firewall prevents every executor from opening a connection back to the driver. Executors are allocated successfully, yet the application cannot run tasks normally. Which requirement is missing?","options":["All executors must run on the same laptop as the driver.","The driver must be able to read every executor local disk directly.","The driver address and listening port must be reachable from the executors throughout the application.","The SQL warehouse directory must be a local folder on the laptop."],"correct":2,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"client driverとネットワーク","concept":"client-driver-must-accept-executor-connections","decision_id":"client-driver-must-accept-executor-connections","family_id":"client-driver-must-accept-executor-connections","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A developer submits a client-mode application from a laptop. The laptop can reach the cluster manager, but a firewall prevents every executor from opening a connection back to the driver. Executors are allocated successfully, yet the application cannot run tasks normally. Which requirement is missing?","options_ja":["All executors must run on the same laptop as the driver.","The driver must be able to read every executor local disk directly.","The driver address and listening port must be reachable from the executors throughout the application.","The SQL warehouse directory must be a local folder on the laptop."],"explanation_ja":"driverはexecutorからのconnectionを受け付ける必要がある。cluster managerへsubmitできることだけでは、driverとexecutorの双方向の必要通信を満たさない。","distractors_ja":["client modeでもexecutorはremote cluster上で実行できる。","driverがexecutor local diskを直接mountする要件ではない。","正答。driverはworker側からnetwork上で到達可能でなければならない。","warehouse pathの配置ではdriver接続拒否を解決しない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/cluster-overview.html#components"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/cluster-overview.html#components","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"33ec28ffdf664c3586d7b2f41b0b1f43ed5d77f37dd61fb128340db86cdba0bf"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-010","number":null,"stem":"A client-mode PySpark application starts with a 1 GB driver JVM heap. Inside the running Python program, the engineer sets spark.driver.memory to 4g and expects the existing driver heap to grow. Which change correctly sets a 4 GB driver heap for the next run?","options":["Call cache on the input DataFrame before setting the property.","Set spark.sql.shuffle.partitions to 4g in SparkSession.conf.","Pass --driver-memory 4g to spark-submit before the driver JVM starts.","Set spark.executor.memory to 4g after the first action."],"correct":2,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"driver JVMの起動時設定","concept":"client-driver-memory-set-before-launch","decision_id":"client-driver-memory-set-before-launch","family_id":"client-driver-memory-set-before-launch","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A client-mode PySpark application starts with a 1 GB driver JVM heap. Inside the running Python program, the engineer sets spark.driver.memory to 4g and expects the existing driver heap to grow. Which change correctly sets a 4 GB driver heap for the next run?","options_ja":["Call cache on the input DataFrame before setting the property.","Set spark.sql.shuffle.partitions to 4g in SparkSession.conf.","Pass --driver-memory 4g to spark-submit before the driver JVM starts.","Set spark.executor.memory to 4g after the first action."],"explanation_ja":"client modeではapplication内コードを実行する時点でdriver JVMが起動済み。driver heapは起動前に--driver-memory等で指定する。","distractors_ja":["cacheは既存JVMの最大heapを拡張しない。","shuffle partition数はmemory量の設定ではない。","正答。driver起動時のheap量をcommand lineから渡す。","executor memoryはdriver heapとは別の設定。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html#application-properties"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html#application-properties","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"955191cfafe8fa00773979810d0fd9810f1670ac06f2d67708a37b78dcf6e6cb"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-011","number":null,"stem":"An application starts with spark.task.maxFailures set to 2 in spark-defaults.conf. The spark-submit command sets the same property to 3. Before constructing SparkContext, the program explicitly sets the property to 5 in the SparkConf passed to SparkContext. No renamed keys or later modifications are involved. Which value takes precedence?","options":["3, because command-line settings always override application code.","5, because values explicitly set in the supplied SparkConf have highest precedence.","The three values are added, producing 10.","2, because the configuration file is loaded first."],"correct":1,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"Spark設定の優先順位","concept":"sparkconf-overrides-submit-defaults","decision_id":"sparkconf-overrides-submit-defaults","family_id":"sparkconf-overrides-submit-defaults","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"An application starts with spark.task.maxFailures set to 2 in spark-defaults.conf. The spark-submit command sets the same property to 3. Before constructing SparkContext, the program explicitly sets the property to 5 in the SparkConf passed to SparkContext. No renamed keys or later modifications are involved. Which value takes precedence?","options_ja":["3, because command-line settings always override application code.","5, because values explicitly set in the supplied SparkConf have highest precedence.","The three values are added, producing 10.","2, because the configuration file is loaded first."],"explanation_ja":"SparkConf内の明示設定はsubmit flagやdefaults fileより優先される。ここでは起動時heapのような例外ではなくtask retryのruntime propertyを指定している。","distractors_ja":["command lineより明示SparkConfの値が優先される。","正答。SparkConf、submit flag、defaultsの順に優先される。","同じpropertyの値を加算しない。","最初に読み込んだ値が最優先になる規則ではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html#dynamically-loading-spark-properties"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html#dynamically-loading-spark-properties","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"78d5ff8ec1714d78bb6c82a663a738e85c6ecfc0a23838c0fb7b5be91cf92f77"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-012","number":null,"stem":"A shared cluster runs an application with dynamic allocation enabled and its required shuffle-preservation mechanism configured. One phase has a sustained backlog of pending tasks. A later phase leaves several uncached executors idle beyond the configured timeout. What behavior is dynamic allocation designed to provide?","options":["Increase the number of rows in each DataFrame until all cores are busy.","Keep the original executor count fixed and change only Python thread counts.","Move the driver between executors whenever a stage finishes.","Request more executors for the backlog and release eligible idle executors later."],"correct":3,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"dynamic allocationのresource伸縮","concept":"dynamic-allocation-pending-tasks-idle-release","decision_id":"dynamic-allocation-pending-tasks-idle-release","family_id":"dynamic-allocation-pending-tasks-idle-release","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A shared cluster runs an application with dynamic allocation enabled and its required shuffle-preservation mechanism configured. One phase has a sustained backlog of pending tasks. A later phase leaves several uncached executors idle beyond the configured timeout. What behavior is dynamic allocation designed to provide?","options_ja":["Increase the number of rows in each DataFrame until all cores are busy.","Keep the original executor count fixed and change only Python thread counts.","Move the driver between executors whenever a stage finishes.","Request more executors for the backlog and release eligible idle executors later."],"explanation_ja":"dynamic allocationは待機taskのbacklogに応じexecutorを増やし、条件を満たすidle executorを解放する。partition数やPython thread数の自動調整そのものではない。","distractors_ja":["入力の行数を変える機能ではない。","固定executor数の説明はdynamic allocationの目的と逆。","driverの移動機能ではない。","正答。applicationが占有するexecutor resourceを負荷に合わせて伸縮する。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/job-scheduling.html#dynamic-resource-allocation"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/job-scheduling.html#dynamic-resource-allocation","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"3c2c5b758ecbc73222cdfe2cb5f3a44931d015949ac3089afd7e7725121b5917"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-013","number":null,"stem":"An application uses dynamic allocation with an external shuffle service. An executor has finished writing shuffle map-output files and is later removed while other tasks may still need those files. What is the key architectural purpose of the external shuffle service in this situation?","options":["It makes all cached Python objects permanently available after the application ends.","It can continue serving the executor shuffle files independently of that executor process.","It replaces the driver and schedules every downstream task.","It copies every output record into the metastore before the executor exits."],"correct":1,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"external shuffle service","concept":"external-shuffle-service-outlives-executor","decision_id":"external-shuffle-service-outlives-executor","family_id":"external-shuffle-service-outlives-executor","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"An application uses dynamic allocation with an external shuffle service. An executor has finished writing shuffle map-output files and is later removed while other tasks may still need those files. What is the key architectural purpose of the external shuffle service in this situation?","options_ja":["It makes all cached Python objects permanently available after the application ends.","It can continue serving the executor shuffle files independently of that executor process.","It replaces the driver and schedules every downstream task.","It copies every output record into the metastore before the executor exits."],"explanation_ja":"external shuffle serviceはexecutorの寿命と独立したprocessでshuffle fileを提供する。通常の任意のcacheやapplication全体を永続化する機能ではない。","distractors_ja":["全てのPython objectやapplication終了後cacheを永久保持するものではない。","正答。executor削除後もshuffle fileをserveできる構成にする。","task schedulingはdriverの役割。","metastoreへ全レコードを保存する方式ではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/job-scheduling.html#graceful-decommission-of-executors"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/job-scheduling.html#graceful-decommission-of-executors","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"b684e8ac612e4388f4394293b34f636e2a8d04391b52af3692faf0d0f25f4e74"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-014","number":null,"stem":"Most tasks in a stage have completed, but one task is much slower than its peers. Speculation is enabled, and the slow task satisfies all configured speculation criteria. No task failure has been reported. Which action can Spark take?","options":["Restart every completed task in the application.","Move the driver process to the slow executor.","Change the slow task input records to NULL and finish the stage.","Launch another attempt of the slow task on another available executor."],"correct":3,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"speculative execution","concept":"speculation-runs-copy-of-straggler-task","decision_id":"speculation-runs-copy-of-straggler-task","family_id":"speculation-runs-copy-of-straggler-task","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"Most tasks in a stage have completed, but one task is much slower than its peers. Speculation is enabled, and the slow task satisfies all configured speculation criteria. No task failure has been reported. Which action can Spark take?","options_ja":["Restart every completed task in the application.","Move the driver process to the slow executor.","Change the slow task input records to NULL and finish the stage.","Launch another attempt of the slow task on another available executor."],"explanation_ja":"speculationは失敗が未報告でも遅いtaskの別attemptを起動し、stragglerの影響を減らす。全taskの一括再実行やdriver移動ではない。","distractors_ja":["完了済み全taskの再実行はspeculationの動作ではない。","driver移動の機能ではない。","入力のデータ内容をNULLへ変えて完了扱いにしない。","正答。条件を満たす遅いtaskのコピーを別attemptとして起動できる。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html#scheduling"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html#scheduling","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"e4c558580bf5bd01618465b8b0ca0fcb2556997b2a5f9a8a916f1971a9d70d6e"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-015","number":null,"stem":"A batch application sets spark.task.maxFailures to 3. One particular task fails three consecutive attempts, with no successful attempt between them. Failures are normal counted task failures, and no other retry policy overrides this setting. What does this configuration mean?","options":["Spark retries the task forever as long as the driver is alive.","Three consecutive failed attempts reach the configured failure limit for that task, and Spark gives up on the job.","Spark sums failures from every task and only checks whether the global sum is divisible by three.","Spark allows three retries after the initial attempt, so a fourth attempt is always launched."],"correct":1,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"taskの連続失敗上限","concept":"task-max-failures-per-task-not-global-sum","decision_id":"task-max-failures-per-task-not-global-sum","family_id":"task-max-failures-per-task-not-global-sum","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A batch application sets spark.task.maxFailures to 3. One particular task fails three consecutive attempts, with no successful attempt between them. Failures are normal counted task failures, and no other retry policy overrides this setting. What does this configuration mean?","options_ja":["Spark retries the task forever as long as the driver is alive.","Three consecutive failed attempts reach the configured failure limit for that task, and Spark gives up on the job.","Spark sums failures from every task and only checks whether the global sum is divisible by three.","Spark allows three retries after the initial attempt, so a fourth attempt is always launched."],"explanation_ja":"task.maxFailuresは特定taskの連続失敗attempt数の上限。3なら初回を含む3失敗で上限に達し、追加retry数は2。application全taskの失敗数の合算ではない。","distractors_ja":["有限の失敗上限が設定されている。","正答。対象taskが3回連続失敗すると上限となる。","別々のtaskの失敗合計をこの設定で判定しない。","設定値は初回を除くretry数ではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html#scheduling"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html#scheduling","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"0423a9f991121a45d9c3701371b24af8d6899022637e9b8985987f18425b0ecf"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-016","number":null,"stem":"A service submits long batch actions and short interactive actions from separate threads into the same SparkContext. Under the default FIFO scheduler, a long job can occupy all available slots while short jobs wait. The team wants concurrent jobs to receive a share of the application resources. Which scheduling mode addresses this requirement?","options":["Setting spark.task.maxFailures to 1.","Changing every short job to a separate stage in the long job.","Disabling all actions and running only transformations.","FAIR scheduling within the SparkContext."],"correct":3,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"同一application内のFAIR scheduler","concept":"fair-scheduler-shares-between-concurrent-jobs","decision_id":"fair-scheduler-shares-between-concurrent-jobs","family_id":"fair-scheduler-shares-between-concurrent-jobs","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A service submits long batch actions and short interactive actions from separate threads into the same SparkContext. Under the default FIFO scheduler, a long job can occupy all available slots while short jobs wait. The team wants concurrent jobs to receive a share of the application resources. Which scheduling mode addresses this requirement?","options_ja":["Setting spark.task.maxFailures to 1.","Changing every short job to a separate stage in the long job.","Disabling all actions and running only transformations.","FAIR scheduling within the SparkContext."],"explanation_ja":"同一SparkContext内の複数jobへresourceを分ける目的ではFAIR schedulerを使う。cluster managerによるapplication間の配分とは階層が違う。","distractors_ja":["task failure retry上限はjob間の公平配分と無関係。","別のjobを任意に別jobのstageへ組み込む設定ではない。","actionをなくすと計算結果を要求できない。","正答。FAIRは同一application内の並行jobへのresource分配を行う。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/job-scheduling.html#scheduling-within-an-application"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/job-scheduling.html#scheduling-within-an-application","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"665a838f2783ca106eea4d15d3120f35bb63b2a2e1cc07f6a5c534c65ef35190"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-017","number":null,"stem":"An engineer sees two executor process identifiers on a single worker machine. Both executors belong to the same application, and the cluster manager has allocated enough CPU and memory for both. Which conclusion is valid?","options":["This can be valid: a worker is a machine that can host executor processes, and an executor is not the same thing as a worker node.","Spark must merge the two executor heaps into a single shared JVM heap.","Both process identifiers must refer to the driver.","The application must be in local mode because a worker can never host multiple executors."],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"workerとexecutorの関係","concept":"worker-node-can-host-multiple-executors","decision_id":"worker-node-can-host-multiple-executors","family_id":"worker-node-can-host-multiple-executors","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"An engineer sees two executor process identifiers on a single worker machine. Both executors belong to the same application, and the cluster manager has allocated enough CPU and memory for both. Which conclusion is valid?","options_ja":["This can be valid: a worker is a machine that can host executor processes, and an executor is not the same thing as a worker node.","Spark must merge the two executor heaps into a single shared JVM heap.","Both process identifiers must refer to the driver.","The application must be in local mode because a worker can never host multiple executors."],"explanation_ja":"workerはcodeを実行できるnode、executorはapplication用に起動するprocess。適切なresourceと設定があれば同一worker上の複数executorは成立する。","distractors_ja":["正答。nodeとprocessを区別する。","独立JVM processのheapが自動的に一つになるわけではない。","executor IDがdriver process IDを表すわけではない。","複数executorが同じworkerにあることはlocal modeの証拠ではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/cluster-overview.html#glossary","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/job-scheduling.html#scheduling-across-applications"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/cluster-overview.html#glossary","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"8de9854cf149b752a3f9b03b39a54536d4454378727f72f1f90777e329120bb4"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-018","number":null,"stem":"A long-running application keeps one SparkContext alive and submits several independent actions over an hour. Dynamic allocation is disabled, and no executor failures occur. Must Spark create a new application and a new set of executors after every action completes?","options":["No. The same application and its executor processes can execute tasks for multiple actions while SparkContext remains alive.","No. Spark executes all future actions entirely inside the cluster manager process.","Yes. Every action requires a different SparkContext.","Yes. Executors exist only for a single task and are always destroyed after it."],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"applicationとjobの寿命","concept":"multiple-actions-same-application-executor-lifetime","decision_id":"multiple-actions-same-application-executor-lifetime","family_id":"multiple-actions-same-application-executor-lifetime","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A long-running application keeps one SparkContext alive and submits several independent actions over an hour. Dynamic allocation is disabled, and no executor failures occur. Must Spark create a new application and a new set of executors after every action completes?","options_ja":["No. The same application and its executor processes can execute tasks for multiple actions while SparkContext remains alive.","No. Spark executes all future actions entirely inside the cluster manager process.","Yes. Every action requires a different SparkContext.","Yes. Executors exist only for a single task and are always destroyed after it."],"explanation_ja":"applicationはdriverとexecutorからなり、同じapplicationのexecutorが複数actionに由来するtaskを処理できる。action、job、applicationを同一視しない。","distractors_ja":["正答。条件を満たす限り同じexecutorを複数actionで使える。","cluster manager内でデータ計算全体を実行する構成ではない。","actionごとにSparkContextを作成する要件はない。","executorはtask1個ごとの使い捨てprocessではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/cluster-overview.html#components"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/cluster-overview.html#components","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"4510e01264a2f18ffc128033b57bdfe5829e5169e9934f44871fc0872907039a"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-019","number":null,"stem":"A developer uses rdd.foreach(print) in a remotely distributed PySpark application to inspect records. The action succeeds, but the records do not appear in the driver console. Assume worker output is not automatically aggregated into that console. What is the most direct explanation?","options":["The function runs in executor tasks, so its output goes to executor-side stdout rather than directly to driver stdout.","Driver stdout is disabled whenever more than one partition exists.","foreach silently discards all function calls when data is cached.","The print function is converted into a SQL query and its result is stored in the metastore."],"correct":0,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"executor標準出力","concept":"task-print-writes-executor-stdout","decision_id":"task-print-writes-executor-stdout","family_id":"task-print-writes-executor-stdout","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A developer uses rdd.foreach(print) in a remotely distributed PySpark application to inspect records. The action succeeds, but the records do not appear in the driver console. Assume worker output is not automatically aggregated into that console. What is the most direct explanation?","options_ja":["The function runs in executor tasks, so its output goes to executor-side stdout rather than directly to driver stdout.","Driver stdout is disabled whenever more than one partition exists.","foreach silently discards all function calls when data is cached.","The print function is converted into a SQL query and its result is stored in the metastore."],"explanation_ja":"task内のprintはそのtaskを実行するexecutor側へ出力する。driver consoleにないことだけでaction未実行と判断しない。","distractors_ja":["正答。driverとexecutorで標準出力の場所が異なる。","partition数によってdriver stdoutを無効にする規則ではない。","cacheの有無でforeachの関数呼出しを省略する規則ではない。","printをSQLへ変換してmetastoreへ保存しない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/rdd-programming-guide.html#printing-elements-of-an-rdd"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/rdd-programming-guide.html#printing-elements-of-an-rdd","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"52aa5787e77786f1de3dd8e2a4659b3770728e4228cc33f3cd041238e61dad32"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-020","number":null,"stem":"A PySpark application imports a custom pure-Python package named pricing_rules inside task functions. The package is installed on the submission machine but is absent on every executor. The engineer has pricing_rules.zip containing the package and wants to distribute it with the application. Which spark-submit option is appropriate?","options":["--executor-cores pricing_rules.zip","--py-files pricing_rules.zip","--driver-memory pricing_rules.zip","--name pricing_rules.zip"],"correct":1,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"Pythonコードの配布","concept":"submit-py-files-distributes-python-module","decision_id":"submit-py-files-distributes-python-module","family_id":"submit-py-files-distributes-python-module","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A PySpark application imports a custom pure-Python package named pricing_rules inside task functions. The package is installed on the submission machine but is absent on every executor. The engineer has pricing_rules.zip containing the package and wants to distribute it with the application. Which spark-submit option is appropriate?","options_ja":["--executor-cores pricing_rules.zip","--py-files pricing_rules.zip","--driver-memory pricing_rules.zip","--name pricing_rules.zip"],"explanation_ja":"pure-Pythonの.py/.zip/.eggは--py-filesでapplicationと一緒に配布できる。driverにだけ存在するpackageが全executorへ自動installされるわけではない。","distractors_ja":["executor-coresはCPU数を指定する。","正答。Python code archiveをexecutorへ配布する指定。","driver-memoryはheap量を指定する。","nameはapplication表示名でありcodeを配布しない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/submitting-applications.html#bundling-your-applications-dependencies"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/submitting-applications.html#bundling-your-applications-dependencies","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"827f4a3c6443b66c29402b2ef3b29d3518f2367840ead6770bd21610228a9dff"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-ARC-021","number":null,"stem":"A server wants two Spark SQL sessions with separate temporary views and session SQL configuration while keeping the same underlying SparkContext. It calls second = spark.newSession(). Which statement describes second?","options":["It permanently stops spark and transfers its driver process to an executor.","It shares every temporary view and all SQL configuration changes with spark.","It has a new independent SparkContext and a completely separate cluster allocation.","It has separate SQL configuration, temporary views, and UDF registration state, while sharing SparkContext and the table cache."],"correct":3,"domain":"Architecture","topic":"newSessionの分離と共有","concept":"new-session-isolates-sql-state-shares-context","decision_id":"new-session-isolates-sql-state-shares-context","family_id":"new-session-isolates-sql-state-shares-context","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A server wants two Spark SQL sessions with separate temporary views and session SQL configuration while keeping the same underlying SparkContext. It calls second = spark.newSession(). Which statement describes second?","options_ja":["It permanently stops spark and transfers its driver process to an executor.","It shares every temporary view and all SQL configuration changes with spark.","It has a new independent SparkContext and a completely separate cluster allocation.","It has separate SQL configuration, temporary views, and UDF registration state, while sharing SparkContext and the table cache."],"explanation_ja":"newSessionはSQLConf、temporary view、登録UDFを分離しつつSparkContextとtable cacheを共有する。getOrCreateの既存session再利用とは別のAPI契約。","distractors_ja":["元sessionの停止やdriver移転を行わない。","temporary view等は分離するため全て共有するわけではない。","newSessionは別SparkContextを作らない。","正答。sessionのSQL状態を分離し実行基盤を共有する。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.SparkSession.newSession.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.SparkSession.newSession.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"39c2f68aaf85e53e12ee385f96629531eceec6de09cf39e49d1f275f2b61c9a9"}]},{"id":"AUTHORED-Spark-SQL","title":"自作問題 · Spark SQL","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-001","number":null,"stem":"A reporting job selects customers who are not blocked. Every customers.id is non-null, but blocked.id may contain NULL. The current predicate is id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM blocked), and the job unexpectedly returns no rows. The engineer must retain every non-blocked customer using NOT IN. Which replacement is correct in Spark SQL?","options":["id NOT IN (SELECT COALESCE(id, customers.id) FROM blocked)","id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM blocked WHERE id IS NOT NULL)","id IN (SELECT id FROM blocked WHERE id IS NOT NULL)","id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM blocked) OR id IS NULL"],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"NOT INとNULL","concept":"sql-not-in-null-subquery-guard","family_id":"sql-not-in-null-subquery-guard","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A reporting job selects customers who are not blocked. Every customers.id is non-null, but blocked.id may contain NULL. The current predicate is id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM blocked), and the job unexpectedly returns no rows. The engineer must retain every non-blocked customer using NOT IN. Which replacement is correct in Spark SQL?","options_ja":["id NOT IN (SELECT COALESCE(id, customers.id) FROM blocked)","id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM blocked WHERE id IS NOT NULL)","id IN (SELECT id FROM blocked WHERE id IS NOT NULL)","id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM blocked) OR id IS NULL"],"explanation_ja":"正解は右側のNULLを副問い合わせ内で除く方法。左側idは非NULLという条件がある。NOT INの比較集合にNULLがあると、不一致の値でもUNKNOWNになりWHEREを通らない。","distractors_ja":["右側NULLを各顧客自身のidに置き換えると、その顧客を除外してしまう。","正解。非NULLのブロック済みidだけを比較集合にする。","ブロック済み顧客を選ぶ逆の条件。","左側idは非NULLなのでORは救済にならず、右側NULLも残る。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-null-semantics.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-null-semantics.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-not-in-null-subquery-guard","images":[],"source_hash":"8b4110c0db677cb9dddfc2bd997583dbcccb601b5d9272a190c328569d2a313b"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-002","number":null,"stem":"An orders table contains region, status, and amount. An analyst needs one row per region whose total amount from completed orders exceeds 50000. Pending orders must not contribute to the total. Which query applies both filters at the correct stage in Spark SQL?","options":["SELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total FROM orders WHERE status = 'complete' GROUP BY region HAVING SUM(amount) > 50000","SELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total FROM orders WHERE SUM(amount) > 50000 GROUP BY region HAVING status = 'complete'","SELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total FROM orders WHERE status = 'complete' AND amount > 50000 GROUP BY region","SELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total FROM orders GROUP BY region, status HAVING SUM(amount) > 50000"],"correct":0,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"WHEREとHAVING","concept":"sql-row-filter-before-group-having-after","family_id":"sql-row-filter-before-group-having-after","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"An orders table contains region, status, and amount. An analyst needs one row per region whose total amount from completed orders exceeds 50000. Pending orders must not contribute to the total. Which query applies both filters at the correct stage in Spark SQL?","options_ja":["SELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total FROM orders WHERE status = 'complete' GROUP BY region HAVING SUM(amount) > 50000","SELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total FROM orders WHERE SUM(amount) > 50000 GROUP BY region HAVING status = 'complete'","SELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total FROM orders WHERE status = 'complete' AND amount > 50000 GROUP BY region","SELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total FROM orders GROUP BY region, status HAVING SUM(amount) > 50000"],"explanation_ja":"WHEREでcompleteの明細を絞り、その後regionで集計し、HAVINGで集計値を判定する。個々の明細が50000を超える必要はない。","distractors_ja":["正解。行の状態条件と集約後の合計条件が正しい段階にある。","WHEREに集約関数は置けない。statusもregionごとの集約結果の単一値として定義されていない。","明細金額を先に除外し、合計が条件を満たす小額明細の集合を失う。","pendingも集計対象になり、regionとstatusごとに複数行を返す。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-having.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-having.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-row-filter-before-group-having-after","images":[],"source_hash":"ee0dfd4c47b2997963866745d0bfb42b04f6727681444200c3085900092c4155"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-003","number":null,"stem":"A batch query calculates a running total with SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY account ORDER BY day RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW). For one account the rows are (day=1, amount=10), (day=1, amount=20), and (day=2, amount=5). All values are non-null. Which totals are attached to these three rows, respectively?","options":["10, 30, 35","10, 20, 5","30, 30, 35","35, 35, 35"],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"RANGEの同順位行","concept":"sql-range-current-row-includes-order-peers","family_id":"sql-range-current-row-includes-order-peers","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A batch query calculates a running total with SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY account ORDER BY day RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW). For one account the rows are (day=1, amount=10), (day=1, amount=20), and (day=2, amount=5). All values are non-null. Which totals are attached to these three rows, respectively?","options_ja":["10, 30, 35","10, 20, 5","30, 30, 35","35, 35, 35"],"explanation_ja":"RANGEのCURRENT ROWはORDER BY値が同じpeerを含む。day=1の2行はともに10+20=30。day=2では35になる。ROWSの物理行数のframeとは区別する。","distractors_ja":["最初の行だけ10にするのは、同順位の扱いが異なるROWSの累積を想定した値。","windowの集約を行わず元の金額を返している。","正解。day=1のpeer全体を含め、その後day=2まで含める。","UNBOUNDED FOLLOWINGは指定されていない。day=1にはday=2を含めない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-window.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-window.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-range-current-row-includes-order-peers","images":[],"source_hash":"dd454ffbc5e1d8fd1faf839a5268e913d1bc7abeaf61597231c40720007bb614"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-004","number":null,"stem":"A compensation report must retain all employees in the two highest distinct salary levels within each department. Ties must be retained, so a department may return more than two employees. All salaries are non-null. Which expression should compute level_no in a subquery before the outer query filters level_no <= 2?","options":["ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC)","NTILE(2) OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC)","DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC)","RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC)"],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"同額を保持する順位","concept":"sql-dense-rank-top-distinct-salary-levels","family_id":"sql-dense-rank-top-distinct-salary-levels","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A compensation report must retain all employees in the two highest distinct salary levels within each department. Ties must be retained, so a department may return more than two employees. All salaries are non-null. Which expression should compute level_no in a subquery before the outer query filters level_no <= 2?","options_ja":["ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC)","NTILE(2) OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC)","DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC)","RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC)"],"explanation_ja":"DENSE_RANKは同額を同順位にし、次の異なる金額へ欠番なしで進む。したがって上位2つの異なる給与水準を同額者も含めて残せる。","distractors_ja":["必ず各行に別番号を振るため同額者の一部を落とす。","行を2つのバケットに分ける機能であり、異なる給与水準の上位2つを表さない。","正解。同額と欠番なしの両要件を満たす。","同額が複数あると次順位に欠番が生じ、2番目の給与水準が順位3以上になることがある。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-window.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-window.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-dense-rank-top-distinct-salary-levels","images":[],"source_hash":"b1802b01775fc3e8462ef2f5fd466c335f5086db9b80bfc120da2992f4b12f9a"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-005","number":null,"stem":"A ledger contains account, seq, and balance. Within each account, seq is unique and all columns are non-null. Every output row must include the balance from the row with the greatest seq in that account. Which window expression guarantees that LAST_VALUE examines the entire account rather than stopping at the current row?","options":["LAST_VALUE(balance) OVER (PARTITION BY account ORDER BY seq ROWS BETWEEN CURRENT ROW AND CURRENT ROW)","LAST_VALUE(balance) OVER (ORDER BY seq ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING)","LAST_VALUE(balance) OVER (PARTITION BY account ORDER BY seq ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)","LAST_VALUE(balance) OVER (PARTITION BY account ORDER BY seq ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING)"],"correct":3,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"LAST_VALUEとフレーム終端","concept":"sql-last-value-whole-partition-frame","family_id":"sql-last-value-whole-partition-frame","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A ledger contains account, seq, and balance. Within each account, seq is unique and all columns are non-null. Every output row must include the balance from the row with the greatest seq in that account. Which window expression guarantees that LAST_VALUE examines the entire account rather than stopping at the current row?","options_ja":["LAST_VALUE(balance) OVER (PARTITION BY account ORDER BY seq ROWS BETWEEN CURRENT ROW AND CURRENT ROW)","LAST_VALUE(balance) OVER (ORDER BY seq ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING)","LAST_VALUE(balance) OVER (PARTITION BY account ORDER BY seq ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)","LAST_VALUE(balance) OVER (PARTITION BY account ORDER BY seq ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING)"],"explanation_ja":"UNBOUNDED FOLLOWINGまでframeを広げ、accountごとのseq最大行を含める。seqの一意性により終端行は明確。balanceの最大値を求める問題ではない。","distractors_ja":["現在行1行だけを見る。","accountによる分割がなく、別accountを含む全体の終端を参照する。","frameの末尾が現在行なので、その行のbalanceが返る。","正解。accountごとの全行をseq順で評価し、最後の値を全行へ付ける。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-window.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-window.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-last-value-whole-partition-frame","images":[],"source_hash":"79e63bb7fcab6aaadd781afcc08488a8c7ddaddb161e174fbdbff826a93f9e1e"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-006","number":null,"stem":"A sensor report uses LAG(reading, 1, -1) OVER (PARTITION BY sensor ORDER BY seq). For one sensor, the unique seq values are 1, 2, 3 and their readings are 8, NULL, 12. The default RESPECT NULLS behavior is used. Which values does the expression return in seq order?","options":["-1, 8, NULL","-1, 8, 8","-1, 8, -1","NULL, 8, NULL"],"correct":0,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"LAGの既定値とNULL","concept":"sql-lag-default-only-missing-offset-row","family_id":"sql-lag-default-only-missing-offset-row","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A sensor report uses LAG(reading, 1, -1) OVER (PARTITION BY sensor ORDER BY seq). For one sensor, the unique seq values are 1, 2, 3 and their readings are 8, NULL, 12. The default RESPECT NULLS behavior is used. Which values does the expression return in seq order?","options_ja":["-1, 8, NULL","-1, 8, 8","-1, 8, -1","NULL, 8, NULL"],"explanation_ja":"第3引数の-1は参照する前行が存在しないときに使う。seq=3には前行が存在し、そのreadingがNULLなのでNULLを返す。既定ではNULLを飛ばさない。","distractors_ja":["正解。前行なし、8、既存前行のNULLの順。","IGNORE NULLSを指定していないため、8まで遡らない。","既存の前行のNULLをdefaultで置き換える動作ではない。","最初の行には明示defaultの-1が使われる。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-window.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-lag-default-only-missing-offset-row","images":[],"source_hash":"1078df75e737117dc2a4c2700870d982c0e6668d566d54e183a2aab2852b3ef5"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-007","number":null,"stem":"A dashboard needs one row per shop, the count of all orders, and the sum of amount for paid orders only. The orders table may also contain unpaid orders. Which query preserves unpaid rows in the overall count while excluding them only from the paid sum? Assume every shop has at least one paid order.","options":["SELECT shop, COUNT(*) AS all_n, SUM(amount) AS paid_total FROM orders WHERE status = 'paid' GROUP BY shop","SELECT shop, COUNT(status = 'paid') AS all_n, SUM(amount) AS paid_total FROM orders GROUP BY shop","SELECT shop, COUNT(*) AS all_n, SUM(amount) FILTER (WHERE status = 'paid') AS paid_total FROM orders GROUP BY shop","SELECT shop, COUNT(*) AS all_n, SUM(amount) AS paid_total FROM orders GROUP BY shop HAVING status = 'paid'"],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"集約ごとのFILTER","concept":"sql-aggregate-filter-preserve-other-aggregate-input","family_id":"sql-aggregate-filter-preserve-other-aggregate-input","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A dashboard needs one row per shop, the count of all orders, and the sum of amount for paid orders only. The orders table may also contain unpaid orders. Which query preserves unpaid rows in the overall count while excluding them only from the paid sum? Assume every shop has at least one paid order.","options_ja":["SELECT shop, COUNT(*) AS all_n, SUM(amount) AS paid_total FROM orders WHERE status = 'paid' GROUP BY shop","SELECT shop, COUNT(status = 'paid') AS all_n, SUM(amount) AS paid_total FROM orders GROUP BY shop","SELECT shop, COUNT(*) AS all_n, SUM(amount) FILTER (WHERE status = 'paid') AS paid_total FROM orders GROUP BY shop","SELECT shop, COUNT(*) AS all_n, SUM(amount) AS paid_total FROM orders GROUP BY shop HAVING status = 'paid'"],"explanation_ja":"aggregate FILTERは指定した集約だけの入力を絞る。COUNT(*)は全注文を数え、SUM(amount)だけpaidを集計する。WHEREでpaidを絞ると全注文数も減る。","distractors_ja":["WHEREで未払い注文を除くためall_nもpaidのみになる。","COUNT(boolean)はTRUEだけでなくFALSEも非NULLなら数え、SUMは全statusを合算する。","正解。全行のCOUNTとpaidのみのSUMを同時に計算する。","statusはGROUP BY項目でも集約でもなく、このHAVINGではショップ単位の条件を定義できない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-groupby.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-groupby.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-aggregate-filter-preserve-other-aggregate-input","images":[],"source_hash":"6c40304de74365c93f51970cba737b9264a502ebe99e128346d4ae5008abc512"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-008","number":null,"stem":"An analyst runs SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id, channel) AS n FROM touchpoints. The input pairs are (1,'web'), (1,'web'), (1,NULL), (2,'app'), and (NULL,'app'). In Apache Spark 3.5.6, what value is returned? The question concerns the two-argument COUNT expression, not COUNT over a struct.","options":["3","4","5","2"],"correct":3,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"複合COUNT DISTINCTとNULL","concept":"sql-count-distinct-multiple-non-null-columns","family_id":"sql-count-distinct-multiple-non-null-columns","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"An analyst runs SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id, channel) AS n FROM touchpoints. The input pairs are (1,'web'), (1,'web'), (1,NULL), (2,'app'), and (NULL,'app'). In Apache Spark 3.5.6, what value is returned? The question concerns the two-argument COUNT expression, not COUNT over a struct.","options_ja":["3","4","5","2"],"explanation_ja":"複数引数のCOUNT(DISTINCT ...)は全引数が非NULLの一意な組合せを数える。有効な組は(1,web)と(2,app)の2つ。structを1引数にする場合と混同しない。","distractors_ja":["NULLを含む2組のどちらか一方だけを誤って数えた値。","NULLを含む一意な組も数えた値。","重複とNULLのいずれも除外していない。","正解。全引数が非NULLである一意な組は2つ。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-count-distinct-multiple-non-null-columns","images":[],"source_hash":"34a7490116e1e5ad8c61837053851dc2d0bce121d845b52306a3ffebc1da4e23"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-009","number":null,"stem":"A sales table contains real NULL values in region. A report uses GROUP BY ROLLUP(region), producing both a group for the real NULL region and a grand-total row whose region is also NULL. The engineer must label only the grand total as 'ALL' and retain real NULL values. Which expression should replace region in the SELECT list?","options":["CASE WHEN GROUPING(region) = 1 THEN 'ALL' ELSE region END","COALESCE(region, 'ALL')","CASE WHEN region IS NULL THEN 'ALL' ELSE region END","CASE WHEN GROUPING(region) = 0 THEN 'ALL' ELSE region END"],"correct":0,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"ROLLUPの集計NULL識別","concept":"sql-grouping-distinguish-real-null-rollup","family_id":"sql-grouping-distinguish-real-null-rollup","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A sales table contains real NULL values in region. A report uses GROUP BY ROLLUP(region), producing both a group for the real NULL region and a grand-total row whose region is also NULL. The engineer must label only the grand total as 'ALL' and retain real NULL values. Which expression should replace region in the SELECT list?","options_ja":["CASE WHEN GROUPING(region) = 1 THEN 'ALL' ELSE region END","COALESCE(region, 'ALL')","CASE WHEN region IS NULL THEN 'ALL' ELSE region END","CASE WHEN GROUPING(region) = 0 THEN 'ALL' ELSE region END"],"explanation_ja":"GROUPING(region)=1は、その行でregionが集計によって省略されたことを示す。実データのNULLをまとめたgroupでは0なので、実NULLと総計用NULLを区別できる。","distractors_ja":["正解。集計によって省略された総計行だけをALLにする。","実データのNULLもALLへ変えるので区別できない。","NULLである理由を区別せず、どちらの行もALLにする。","条件が逆であり、詳細groupをALLにしてしまう。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-groupby.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-groupby.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-grouping-distinguish-real-null-rollup","images":[],"source_hash":"2ff590f46fe7c80b135c8dac5427b3a36a636d69349217761b5f2d6afde9e214"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-010","number":null,"stem":"Two audit extracts have identical schemas. A row may occur repeatedly within either extract and may also occur in both. The combined audit must preserve every occurrence because repeated events are being investigated. No column reconciliation is required. Which Spark SQL operation satisfies this requirement without removing duplicate occurrences?","options":["SELECT * FROM audit_a UNION SELECT * FROM audit_b","SELECT * FROM audit_a UNION ALL SELECT * FROM audit_b","SELECT DISTINCT * FROM (SELECT * FROM audit_a UNION ALL SELECT * FROM audit_b) t","SELECT * FROM audit_a INTERSECT ALL SELECT * FROM audit_b"],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"UNION ALLの出現回数保持","concept":"sql-union-all-preserve-bag-occurrences","family_id":"sql-union-all-preserve-bag-occurrences","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"Two audit extracts have identical schemas. A row may occur repeatedly within either extract and may also occur in both. The combined audit must preserve every occurrence because repeated events are being investigated. No column reconciliation is required. Which Spark SQL operation satisfies this requirement without removing duplicate occurrences?","options_ja":["SELECT * FROM audit_a UNION SELECT * FROM audit_b","SELECT * FROM audit_a UNION ALL SELECT * FROM audit_b","SELECT DISTINCT * FROM (SELECT * FROM audit_a UNION ALL SELECT * FROM audit_b) t","SELECT * FROM audit_a INTERSECT ALL SELECT * FROM audit_b"],"explanation_ja":"UNION ALLは両入力のすべての出現回数を保持する。UNIONはDISTINCTが既定で、同じ行を1行にまとめる。スキーマ不一致の解決ではなく重複の意味を問う。","distractors_ja":["UNIONは同一行を重複排除する。","正解。両extract内とextract間の重複出現をすべて保持する。","外側のDISTINCTで出現回数を失う。","両方にある行だけを残し、入力の単純連結にならない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-setops.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-setops.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-union-all-preserve-bag-occurrences","images":[],"source_hash":"4245410b2ebd155ffd5c2a5cd71e251e2fd905b6492299e748bad66d68f76b46"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-011","number":null,"stem":"An inventory reconciliation uses bag semantics: each occurrence in the right input cancels one equal occurrence in the left input. The left single-column input contains x, x, x, y, and the right input contains x, x. What rows remain after SELECT item FROM left_input EXCEPT ALL SELECT item FROM right_input in Spark SQL?","options":["Three x rows and one y row","One x row and one y row","Only y","Two x rows and one y row"],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"EXCEPT ALLの重複差分","concept":"sql-except-all-subtract-occurrence-counts","family_id":"sql-except-all-subtract-occurrence-counts","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"An inventory reconciliation uses bag semantics: each occurrence in the right input cancels one equal occurrence in the left input. The left single-column input contains x, x, x, y, and the right input contains x, x. What rows remain after SELECT item FROM left_input EXCEPT ALL SELECT item FROM right_input in Spark SQL?","options_ja":["Three x rows and one y row","One x row and one y row","Only y","Two x rows and one y row"],"explanation_ja":"EXCEPT ALLは重複の出現回数を差し引く。xは3−2で1つ、yは右にないので1つ残る。EXCEPT DISTINCTとは違う。","distractors_ja":["右入力を差し引いていない。","正解。xが1出現とyが1出現残る。","EXCEPT DISTINCTのように、右にあるxをすべて除いた結果。","右のxを1出現しか差し引いていない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-setops.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-setops.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-except-all-subtract-occurrence-counts","images":[],"source_hash":"312c69e17dce4ffefb27fb46391a65566b84ad7160a7614f6978bc584301396f"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-012","number":null,"stem":"A reconciliation joins incoming and reference on nullable key. The business defines two NULL keys as equal, but a NULL key must never match a non-null key. Duplicate keys are allowed and ordinary join multiplicity is acceptable. Which Spark SQL ON condition implements exactly that equality rule without replacing NULL with a sentinel value?","options":["incoming.key IS NULL OR reference.key IS NULL","incoming.key = reference.key","incoming.key <=> reference.key","COALESCE(incoming.key, 0) = COALESCE(reference.key, 0)"],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"NULL安全等価の結合条件","concept":"sql-null-safe-equality-without-sentinel-collision","family_id":"sql-null-safe-equality-without-sentinel-collision","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A reconciliation joins incoming and reference on nullable key. The business defines two NULL keys as equal, but a NULL key must never match a non-null key. Duplicate keys are allowed and ordinary join multiplicity is acceptable. Which Spark SQL ON condition implements exactly that equality rule without replacing NULL with a sentinel value?","options_ja":["incoming.key IS NULL OR reference.key IS NULL","incoming.key = reference.key","incoming.key <=> reference.key","COALESCE(incoming.key, 0) = COALESCE(reference.key, 0)"],"explanation_ja":"<=>は両方NULLならTRUE、片方だけNULLならFALSE、非NULL同士は通常の等値比較になる。0等への置換は実際の0キーとの衝突を招く。","distractors_ja":["片方だけNULLでもTRUEとなり、非NULLキーと一致させてしまう。","通常の=はNULL同士でもUNKNOWNなので一致しない。","正解。NULL同士だけを等値として扱う。","実際の0とNULLを同じ値に変えてしまい、要求に反する一致が発生する。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-null-semantics.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-null-semantics.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-null-safe-equality-without-sentinel-collision","images":[],"source_hash":"a45a4867f09b0c214e3aaea8e6e95cdd8dbd065512274581808525f1988a5172"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-013","number":null,"stem":"A report must list every customer, with any paid invoice amount beside the customer. Customers with no paid invoices must still appear with a NULL invoice amount, even when they have unpaid invoices. Which Spark SQL query preserves those customers? Assume invoices.customer_id refers to customers.id, and multiple paid invoices may produce multiple rows.","options":["SELECT c.id, i.amount FROM customers c INNER JOIN invoices i ON c.id = i.customer_id AND i.status = 'paid'","SELECT c.id, i.amount FROM customers c LEFT JOIN invoices i ON c.id = i.customer_id WHERE i.status = 'paid' OR i.customer_id IS NULL","SELECT c.id, i.amount FROM customers c LEFT JOIN invoices i ON c.id = i.customer_id AND i.status = 'paid'","SELECT c.id, i.amount FROM customers c LEFT JOIN invoices i ON c.id = i.customer_id WHERE i.status = 'paid'"],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"LEFT JOINのONとWHERE","concept":"sql-left-join-filter-right-side-in-on","family_id":"sql-left-join-filter-right-side-in-on","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A report must list every customer, with any paid invoice amount beside the customer. Customers with no paid invoices must still appear with a NULL invoice amount, even when they have unpaid invoices. Which Spark SQL query preserves those customers? Assume invoices.customer_id refers to customers.id, and multiple paid invoices may produce multiple rows.","options_ja":["SELECT c.id, i.amount FROM customers c INNER JOIN invoices i ON c.id = i.customer_id AND i.status = 'paid'","SELECT c.id, i.amount FROM customers c LEFT JOIN invoices i ON c.id = i.customer_id WHERE i.status = 'paid' OR i.customer_id IS NULL","SELECT c.id, i.amount FROM customers c LEFT JOIN invoices i ON c.id = i.customer_id AND i.status = 'paid'","SELECT c.id, i.amount FROM customers c LEFT JOIN invoices i ON c.id = i.customer_id WHERE i.status = 'paid'"],"explanation_ja":"paid条件をONへ置くと、paidと一致しないcustomerも左側の保持によりNULL補完される。WHEREは結合後の行を削除するため、不払い請求だけあるcustomerが失われる。","distractors_ja":["INNER JOINなのでpaid一致のないcustomerを保持しない。","未払いだけあるcustomerは実際に右行と一致済みなのでNULL補完行が作られず、WHEREで全行を落とす。","正解。paidに一致しなくてもcustomerをNULL補完で残す。","未一致行のstatusもNULLなのでWHEREを通らず、customerを失う。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-join.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-null-semantics.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-join.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-left-join-filter-right-side-in-on","images":[],"source_hash":"6704623a67b2ec2ba5fd2f203bb71b00ccc4f44c49e05c46aa4fdc2d5f2ec922"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-014","number":null,"stem":"A CSV schema is explicitly defined as id INT, name STRING. A supplier accidentally reverses the header to name,id while keeping a comma delimiter. The pipeline uses header=true and spark.sql.caseSensitive=true. It must reject the mismatched column positions rather than silently applying the schema by position. Which CSV option should the engineer set in Spark 3.5.6?","options":["enforceSchema=true","inferSchema=true","header=false","enforceSchema=false"],"correct":3,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"CSVヘッダー位置検証","concept":"sql-csv-enforce-schema-false-header-validation","family_id":"sql-csv-enforce-schema-false-header-validation","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A CSV schema is explicitly defined as id INT, name STRING. A supplier accidentally reverses the header to name,id while keeping a comma delimiter. The pipeline uses header=true and spark.sql.caseSensitive=true. It must reject the mismatched column positions rather than silently applying the schema by position. Which CSV option should the engineer set in Spark 3.5.6?","options_ja":["enforceSchema=true","inferSchema=true","header=false","enforceSchema=false"],"explanation_ja":"header=trueかつenforceSchema=falseでは、CSV header名とschema名を位置ごとに比較する。headerの順序が違うと検証で失敗する。名前から自動で列を並べ替える機能ではない。","distractors_ja":["明示schemaを位置で強制適用し、header名を無視する。","schemaは明示済みであり、schema推論を有効化することが位置検証の指定にはならない。","headerをデータ行として扱い、列位置の検証を行わない。","正解。明示schemaとheaderの位置・名前を検証する。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-csv.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-csv.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-csv-enforce-schema-false-header-validation","images":[],"source_hash":"a8971f3c2ed3a0a231e97d2b6076c54550ee23757c45a59336bd824805623871"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-015","number":null,"stem":"A notebook executes spark.sql(\"WITH recent AS (SELECT * FROM events WHERE event_date = DATE '2026-10-01') SELECT COUNT(*) FROM recent\") successfully. In a separate spark.sql call, SELECT * FROM recent fails because no table or view named recent exists. The SparkSession remains open. Which statement correctly explains this behavior?","options":["The CTE name is scoped to its SQL statement and does not register a session view.","The first query must run collect() before the CTE becomes a permanent table.","Every CTE is stored in the global_temp database and needs that prefix.","A CTE is available only after the SparkSession is stopped and restarted."],"correct":0,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"CTEの文単位スコープ","concept":"sql-cte-statement-scope-not-session-view","family_id":"sql-cte-statement-scope-not-session-view","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A notebook executes spark.sql(\"WITH recent AS (SELECT * FROM events WHERE event_date = DATE '2026-10-01') SELECT COUNT(*) FROM recent\") successfully. In a separate spark.sql call, SELECT * FROM recent fails because no table or view named recent exists. The SparkSession remains open. Which statement correctly explains this behavior?","options_ja":["The CTE name is scoped to its SQL statement and does not register a session view.","The first query must run collect() before the CTE becomes a permanent table.","Every CTE is stored in the global_temp database and needs that prefix.","A CTE is available only after the SparkSession is stopped and restarted."],"explanation_ja":"WITHで定義したCTEはそのSQL文の中だけで参照できる。SparkSessionが継続していてもカタログへ一時viewを作ったことにはならない。","distractors_ja":["正解。別のSQL文から同じCTE名は直接参照できない。","actionの実行はCTEを永続tableへ登録しない。","CTEはglobal temporary viewではない。","sessionの再起動でCTEの範囲は拡張されない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-cte.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-cte.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-cte-statement-scope-not-session-view","images":[],"source_hash":"8a8bdc595ee3c2b26793e9310b9dc734c05510c40731a9db88f2780862213eac"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-016","number":null,"stem":"A Parquet table named analytics.shipments is cached in Spark. An external process replaces files at the same existing table location without changing the schema or adding catalog partitions. Subsequent queries must invalidate stale cached data and metadata, while keeping the table definition. Which Spark SQL statement directly requests this refresh?","options":["REFRESH TABLE analytics.shipments","ANALYZE TABLE analytics.shipments COMPUTE STATISTICS","DESCRIBE TABLE analytics.shipments","DROP TABLE analytics.shipments"],"correct":0,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"外部更新後のキャッシュ更新","concept":"sql-refresh-table-invalidate-data-metadata","family_id":"sql-refresh-table-invalidate-data-metadata","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A Parquet table named analytics.shipments is cached in Spark. An external process replaces files at the same existing table location without changing the schema or adding catalog partitions. Subsequent queries must invalidate stale cached data and metadata, while keeping the table definition. Which Spark SQL statement directly requests this refresh?","options_ja":["REFRESH TABLE analytics.shipments","ANALYZE TABLE analytics.shipments COMPUTE STATISTICS","DESCRIBE TABLE analytics.shipments","DROP TABLE analytics.shipments"],"explanation_ja":"REFRESH TABLEは対象table/viewのcached dataとmetadataを無効化し、次の実行で遅延的に再構築する。table定義の削除や統計計算とは用途が異なる。新しいpartition登録はこの問題の条件に含まない。","distractors_ja":["正解。table定義を保持し、staleなcacheを無効化する。","optimizer用の統計を収集する命令であり、cached dataとmetadataの無効化を直接指示するものではない。","metadataを表示するだけで、refreshを要求しない。","table定義を保持する条件に反する。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-aux-cache-refresh-table.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-aux-cache-refresh-table.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-refresh-table-invalidate-data-metadata","images":[],"source_hash":"daa13ffd39ff5def5ea7dec5d589e94dd568cf0577d43040b995c0c469ad0517"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-017","number":null,"stem":"An engineer inherits a catalog-registered data source table, analytics.clicks. Before changing the ingestion job, the engineer needs to inspect the table's storage location and provider as well as its columns. The command must inspect the existing catalog object without scanning all table rows or changing data. Which statement should the engineer use?","options":["REFRESH TABLE analytics.clicks","DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED analytics.clicks","SHOW TABLES IN analytics","SELECT * FROM analytics.clicks LIMIT 0"],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"テーブルの詳細メタデータ","concept":"sql-describe-extended-provider-location","family_id":"sql-describe-extended-provider-location","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"An engineer inherits a catalog-registered data source table, analytics.clicks. Before changing the ingestion job, the engineer needs to inspect the table's storage location and provider as well as its columns. The command must inspect the existing catalog object without scanning all table rows or changing data. Which statement should the engineer use?","options_ja":["REFRESH TABLE analytics.clicks","DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED analytics.clicks","SHOW TABLES IN analytics","SELECT * FROM analytics.clicks LIMIT 0"],"explanation_ja":"DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDEDは列情報に加えprovider、location等の詳細table metadataを返す。データ行の読取や定義の変更をせず既存tableを確認できる。","distractors_ja":["cacheの無効化を行う命令であり、詳細metadataの表示ではない。","正解。columnsと詳細metadataを同時に確認する。","tableの一覧であり、個々のstorage locationやproviderを返さない。","結果のschemaは得られるが、tableのproviderやlocationを表示しない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-aux-describe-table.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-aux-describe-table.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-describe-extended-provider-location","images":[],"source_hash":"4272165d37464a727c878546b918c797ae1c7426a79a39217038726bf69b0ca2"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-018","number":null,"stem":"A database named reporting contains permanent tables sales_daily, sales_monthly, archive_2025, and users. There are no temporary views. An engineer wants a catalog listing of table names that start with sales or archive, without querying their data. Which Spark SQL command uses the SHOW TABLES matching syntax correctly?","options":["DESCRIBE TABLE reporting.sales*","SHOW TABLES IN reporting WHERE tableName LIKE 'sales%' OR tableName LIKE 'archive%'","SHOW TABLES IN reporting LIKE 'sales%|archive%'","SHOW TABLES IN reporting LIKE 'sales*|archive*'"],"correct":3,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"SHOW TABLESの名前検索","concept":"sql-show-tables-alternative-prefix-pattern","family_id":"sql-show-tables-alternative-prefix-pattern","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A database named reporting contains permanent tables sales_daily, sales_monthly, archive_2025, and users. There are no temporary views. An engineer wants a catalog listing of table names that start with sales or archive, without querying their data. Which Spark SQL command uses the SHOW TABLES matching syntax correctly?","options_ja":["DESCRIBE TABLE reporting.sales*","SHOW TABLES IN reporting WHERE tableName LIKE 'sales%' OR tableName LIKE 'archive%'","SHOW TABLES IN reporting LIKE 'sales%|archive%'","SHOW TABLES IN reporting LIKE 'sales*|archive*'"],"explanation_ja":"SHOW TABLESのLIKE patternは通常のSQL LIKEと異なる。*は任意長の文字、|は複数patternの代替を表すのでsales*|archive*で両prefixを列挙できる。","distractors_ja":["DESCRIBEは単一tableの説明であり、このwildcardによる一覧を取得する構文ではない。","SHOW TABLES文にこのWHERE句は追加できない。","SHOW TABLESのpatternで%はSQL LIKEのワイルドカードとして働かない。","正解。*と|を使うSHOW TABLESの構文に合う。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-aux-show-tables.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-aux-show-tables.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-show-tables-alternative-prefix-pattern","images":[],"source_hash":"fdb6a0a810a2f379f9267fc199cae7cf514343eb97cca40fac82806966d73ba6"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-019","number":null,"stem":"A supplier sends comma-separated files with a header and correctly quoted text fields. A single quoted comment may contain a newline, so one logical record can span two physical lines. The existing delimiter, quote character, and schema are already correct. Which CSV reader option addresses split records without treating the entire file as one text value?","options":["wholetext=true","lineSep=','","inferSchema=true","multiLine=true"],"correct":3,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"CSVの引用符内改行","concept":"sql-csv-multiline-quoted-records","family_id":"sql-csv-multiline-quoted-records","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A supplier sends comma-separated files with a header and correctly quoted text fields. A single quoted comment may contain a newline, so one logical record can span two physical lines. The existing delimiter, quote character, and schema are already correct. Which CSV reader option addresses split records without treating the entire file as one text value?","options_ja":["wholetext=true","lineSep=','","inferSchema=true","multiLine=true"],"explanation_ja":"CSVのmultiLine=trueは引用符で囲まれたfield内の改行を含むrecordを解析する。text sourceのwholetextとは違い、CSVの列構造を維持する。","distractors_ja":["wholetextはtext sourceでfile全体を1値に読む用途であり、CSV record解析の指定ではない。","commaは列区切りであり、行区切りにするとrecordを正しく分割できない。","型推論の有効化ではrecord境界の扱いを変更しない。","正解。複数の物理行にまたがるCSV recordを扱える。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-csv.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-csv.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-csv-multiline-quoted-records","images":[],"source_hash":"b711e07860e3e1361e72849066c1f7d9416a5afeefe33479599f4dff391a9194"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-020","number":null,"stem":"In Apache Spark 3.5.6 using the built-in session catalog, an engineer registers existing Parquet files with CREATE TABLE raw.orders USING PARQUET LOCATION '/shared/orders'. Catalog metadata confirms that the table type is EXTERNAL. The engineer then executes DROP TABLE raw.orders without PURGE or custom cleanup hooks. What happens to the table's catalog entry and the Parquet files?","options":["The catalog entry remains, but the Parquet files are removed.","The catalog entry is removed, but the Parquet files remain at /shared/orders.","Both the catalog entry and the Parquet files are removed.","DROP TABLE fails because an external table cannot be dropped."],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"外部テーブルのDROPとデータ保持","concept":"sql-drop-external-table-preserve-location-data","family_id":"sql-drop-external-table-preserve-location-data","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"In Apache Spark 3.5.6 using the built-in session catalog, an engineer registers existing Parquet files with CREATE TABLE raw.orders USING PARQUET LOCATION '/shared/orders'. Catalog metadata confirms that the table type is EXTERNAL. The engineer then executes DROP TABLE raw.orders without PURGE or custom cleanup hooks. What happens to the table's catalog entry and the Parquet files?","options_ja":["The catalog entry remains, but the Parquet files are removed.","The catalog entry is removed, but the Parquet files remain at /shared/orders.","Both the catalog entry and the Parquet files are removed.","DROP TABLE fails because an external table cannot be dropped."],"explanation_ja":"EXTERNAL tableのDROPはmetastoreのmetadataを削除し、外部locationの実データは保持する。catalog entryの有無とParquet fileの寿命を分ける。managed tableの既定warehouse path削除や外部の独自cleanup hookへ一般化しない。","distractors_ja":["DROPはtableのcatalog entryを除く。metadataを残してfileだけを消す操作ではない。","正解。table定義だけが除かれ、外部locationのParquet fileは残る。","EXTERNAL tableでは実データを所有するmanaged tableと異なり、通常のDROPで外部fileは削除しない。","EXTERNAL tableもDROPできる。違いは外部locationのfileを保持すること。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-ddl-drop-table.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-load-save-functions.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-ddl-drop-table.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-drop-external-table-preserve-location-data","images":[],"source_hash":"4d934617ca4152b108e4ffae3bc156e93ebfee66cd6c9c9fab8cd30135edb65b"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-021","number":null,"stem":"A PostgreSQL JDBC reader already has URL, credentials, numPartitions, lowerBound, and upperBound configured. It also sets query to SELECT order_id, amount FROM source_orders WHERE status = 'paid' and partitionColumn to order_id. Spark rejects this combination. The engineer must keep both source-side filtering and partitioned reading. Which change is supported by Spark 3.5.6?","options":["Keep query and add dbtable='source_orders'; retain partitionColumn='order_id'.","Keep query; replace partitionColumn with 'p.order_id' without adding a dbtable subquery.","Remove query; set dbtable to (SELECT order_id, amount FROM source_orders WHERE status = 'paid') p and partitionColumn to p.order_id.","Remove partitionColumn, lowerBound, and upperBound; retain only query."],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"JDBC副問い合わせの並列読取","concept":"sql-jdbc-dbtable-subquery-with-partition-column","family_id":"sql-jdbc-dbtable-subquery-with-partition-column","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A PostgreSQL JDBC reader already has URL, credentials, numPartitions, lowerBound, and upperBound configured. It also sets query to SELECT order_id, amount FROM source_orders WHERE status = 'paid' and partitionColumn to order_id. Spark rejects this combination. The engineer must keep both source-side filtering and partitioned reading. Which change is supported by Spark 3.5.6?","options_ja":["Keep query and add dbtable='source_orders'; retain partitionColumn='order_id'.","Keep query; replace partitionColumn with 'p.order_id' without adding a dbtable subquery.","Remove query; set dbtable to (SELECT order_id, amount FROM source_orders WHERE status = 'paid') p and partitionColumn to p.order_id.","Remove partitionColumn, lowerBound, and upperBound; retain only query."],"explanation_ja":"queryとpartitionColumnは同時に指定できない。括弧付きsubqueryとaliasをdbtableへ指定すればsource-side filterを保ち、aliasで修飾したpartitionColumnで並列読取を構成できる。","distractors_ja":["queryとdbtableの同時指定も禁止されている。","列名の修飾だけではqueryとpartitionColumnの併用禁止を解決しない。","正解。query optionを使わずdbtable subqueryへ移す。","query単独は有効だが、要求されたpartitioned readingを維持しない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-jdbc.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-jdbc.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-jdbc-dbtable-subquery-with-partition-column","images":[],"source_hash":"76b5582e2c4dcbfea08fc3a56c43461de1a2d0336b3181c00352f8923e6f1cd8"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-022","number":null,"stem":"A JDBC read already uses an acceptable fixed number of database connections. Monitoring shows many network round trips because the JDBC driver fetches only a few rows at a time. The database and executors can handle larger result batches. The engineer wants more rows per fetch without increasing parallel connections. Which Spark JDBC read option directly controls this behavior?","options":["spark.sql.shuffle.partitions","numPartitions","fetchsize","batchsize"],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"JDBC読取の往復回数","concept":"sql-jdbc-fetchsize-read-roundtrip","family_id":"sql-jdbc-fetchsize-read-roundtrip","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A JDBC read already uses an acceptable fixed number of database connections. Monitoring shows many network round trips because the JDBC driver fetches only a few rows at a time. The database and executors can handle larger result batches. The engineer wants more rows per fetch without increasing parallel connections. Which Spark JDBC read option directly controls this behavior?","options_ja":["spark.sql.shuffle.partitions","numPartitions","fetchsize","batchsize"],"explanation_ja":"fetchsizeはJDBC readで1回のround tripに取得する行数を指定する。batchsizeはwriteの挿入batch、numPartitionsは並列度・接続数であり目的が異なる。","distractors_ja":["Spark内のshuffle partition数であり、JDBC driverのfetch行数ではない。","接続の並列度を変えるので、接続数維持の要求と異なる。","正解。対応するJDBC driverで読取batchの行数を調整する。","JDBC writeで1回に挿入する行数の設定。readのfetchには効かない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-jdbc.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-jdbc.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-jdbc-fetchsize-read-roundtrip","images":[],"source_hash":"0a2daf0bc839d606b9e1efe0fd61bb5958f645c3df7b629861a24b366cc324b7"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-SQL-023","number":null,"stem":"Parquet files under /warehouse/clicks/day=2026-10-01 contain only user_id and event_type. The day value is encoded only in the directory name. An engineer reads just that day's directory but also needs day as a discovered partition column. Recursive file lookup is disabled. Which reader option preserves the partition column while restricting the input path to that directory?","options":["option('mergeSchema', 'true')","option('basePath', '/warehouse/clicks')","option('recursiveFileLookup', 'true')","option('inferSchema', 'true')"],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark SQL","topic":"部分パスとpartition discovery","concept":"sql-parquet-base-path-retain-partition-column","family_id":"sql-parquet-base-path-retain-partition-column","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"Parquet files under /warehouse/clicks/day=2026-10-01 contain only user_id and event_type. The day value is encoded only in the directory name. An engineer reads just that day's directory but also needs day as a discovered partition column. Recursive file lookup is disabled. Which reader option preserves the partition column while restricting the input path to that directory?","options_ja":["option('mergeSchema', 'true')","option('basePath', '/warehouse/clicks')","option('recursiveFileLookup', 'true')","option('inferSchema', 'true')"],"explanation_ja":"partition discoveryは既定では指定された入力pathより下だけを見る。basePathをtableのrootにすると、入力day directoryの値もpartition columnとして認識する。入力path自体は当日のままなので他の日は読まない。","distractors_ja":["file内schemaの統合であり、入力pathの上位にあるpartition directoryの認識範囲を指定しない。","正解。partition discoveryを開始するrootを指定する。","recursiveFileLookupはpartition inferenceを無効にするため目的と逆。","Parquetのpartition discoveryのbaseを指定するoptionではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-parquet.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-data-sources-parquet.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"sql-parquet-base-path-retain-partition-column","images":[],"source_hash":"05cd5d223bbfb9e60642eac67bd9dec20c2d00e5dfc4104f9f54bdb8d2bad24f"}]},{"id":"AUTHORED-DataFrame","title":"自作問題 · DataFrame","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-001","number":null,"stem":"A developer receives two DataFrames whose columns have the same names and integer types but arrive in different orders. The developer runs the following code in Spark 3.5.6:\n\n```python\nleft = spark.createDataFrame([(1, 10)], \"a INT, b INT\")\nright = spark.createDataFrame([(20, 2)], \"b INT, a INT\")\np = left.union(right)\nq = left.unionByName(right)\n```\n\nIgnoring row order, which row contributed by `right` appears in `p` and `q`, respectively, when both results are interpreted using columns `(a, b)`?","options":["Both operations fail because the column orders differ.","p: (20, 2); q: (2, 20)","p: (2, 20); q: (20, 2)","p: (2, 20); q: (2, 20)"],"correct":1,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"同じ列数で列順が異なるunion","concept":"union-silent-position-vs-name","family_id":"authored-union-silent-position-vs-name","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A developer receives two DataFrames whose columns have the same names and integer types but arrive in different orders. The developer runs the following code in Spark 3.5.6:\n\n```python\nleft = spark.createDataFrame([(1, 10)], \"a INT, b INT\")\nright = spark.createDataFrame([(20, 2)], \"b INT, a INT\")\np = left.union(right)\nq = left.unionByName(right)\n```\n\nIgnoring row order, which row contributed by `right` appears in `p` and `q`, respectively, when both results are interpreted using columns `(a, b)`?","options_ja":["Both operations fail because the column orders differ.","p: (20, 2); q: (2, 20)","p: (2, 20); q: (20, 2)","p: (2, 20); q: (2, 20)"],"explanation_ja":"unionは列の位置で結合し、左側のschema順(a,b)に右側の値(20,2)を対応させる。unionByNameは名前で対応し直すため右側行は(a=2,b=20)。欠落列ではなく同数・同型列で静かに値がずれる判断を問う。","distractors_ja":["列数と互換型が揃っているため、単に順序が異なるだけでは両方の失敗にはならない。","正答。unionは位置、unionByNameは名前を使う。","位置と名前の対応が逆。","unionは列名を見て自動並べ替えしない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.unionByName.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.union.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.unionByName.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"union-silent-position-vs-name","images":[],"source_hash":"5f7b3ae715b96537dc0dba7f1d7d868238332b030e58afb6cd55e3eaa798adaf"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-002","number":null,"stem":"A batch DataFrame contains one row per order. Some orders have no item IDs, but downstream auditing must retain those orders as rows with a NULL item. The input and transformation are:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.createDataFrame(\n    [(101, [7, 8]), (102, []), (103, None)],\n    \"order_id INT, items ARRAY<INT>\"\n)\nresult = df.select(\"order_id\", F.explode_outer(\"items\").alias(\"item\"))\n```\n\nAssuming `F` is `pyspark.sql.functions`, which description of `result` is correct?","options":["The transformation fails because empty arrays and NULL arrays cannot be processed together.","Two rows: orders 102 and 103 are removed.","Three rows: order 102 is removed, but order 103 produces a NULL item.","Four rows: order 101 produces two rows, and orders 102 and 103 each produce a NULL item."],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"空配列とNULLの親行保持","concept":"explode-outer-empty-parent-preservation","family_id":"authored-explode-outer-empty-parent-preservation","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A batch DataFrame contains one row per order. Some orders have no item IDs, but downstream auditing must retain those orders as rows with a NULL item. The input and transformation are:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.createDataFrame(\n    [(101, [7, 8]), (102, []), (103, None)],\n    \"order_id INT, items ARRAY<INT>\"\n)\nresult = df.select(\"order_id\", F.explode_outer(\"items\").alias(\"item\"))\n```\n\nAssuming `F` is `pyspark.sql.functions`, which description of `result` is correct?","options_ja":["The transformation fails because empty arrays and NULL arrays cannot be processed together.","Two rows: orders 102 and 103 are removed.","Three rows: order 102 is removed, but order 103 produces a NULL item.","Four rows: order 101 produces two rows, and orders 102 and 103 each produce a NULL item."],"explanation_ja":"explode_outerは配列要素を行に展開し、空配列もNULL配列もNULLを持つ1行として残す。101に2行、102と103に各1行の合計4行になる。","distractors_ja":["明示schemaによりARRAY<INT>のNULL値も扱える。","通常のexplodeに近い挙動であり、outer指定による親行保持を無視している。","空配列もouterでは残る。","正答。空/NULL配列を両方保持する。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.explode_outer.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.explode_outer.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"explode-outer-empty-parent-preservation","images":[],"source_hash":"91fe409f8a2b4415081f59e474da32a10d9961045aa660a46427e47b406f6c68"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-003","number":null,"stem":"An application must extract the first and last labels from a nonempty array without exploding it into rows. A developer writes:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.createDataFrame([([\"red\", \"blue\", \"green\"],)], \"labels ARRAY<STRING>\")\nresult = df.select(\n    F.element_at(\"labels\", 1).alias(\"first\"),\n    F.element_at(\"labels\", -1).alias(\"last\")\n)\n```\n\nAssume Spark 3.5.6 and that `F` is `pyspark.sql.functions`. What values will the single output row contain in `(first, last)`?","options":["The query fails because element_at does not allow negative array indexes.","(\"red\", NULL)","(\"blue\", \"green\")","(\"red\", \"green\")"],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"element_atの配列添字","concept":"element-at-array-one-based-negative-index","family_id":"authored-element-at-array-one-based-negative-index","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"An application must extract the first and last labels from a nonempty array without exploding it into rows. A developer writes:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.createDataFrame([([\"red\", \"blue\", \"green\"],)], \"labels ARRAY<STRING>\")\nresult = df.select(\n    F.element_at(\"labels\", 1).alias(\"first\"),\n    F.element_at(\"labels\", -1).alias(\"last\")\n)\n```\n\nAssume Spark 3.5.6 and that `F` is `pyspark.sql.functions`. What values will the single output row contain in `(first, last)`?","options_ja":["The query fails because element_at does not allow negative array indexes.","(\"red\", NULL)","(\"blue\", \"green\")","(\"red\", \"green\")"],"explanation_ja":"element_atの配列添字は1始まりで、負数は末尾から数える。1はred、-1はgreen。0始まりのgetItemなどとは異なる。","distractors_ja":["負の添字は正式にサポートされる。","-1は有効な添字でNULLにならない。","1を0始まりの添字として解釈している。","正答。1が先頭、-1が末尾。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.element_at.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.element_at.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"element-at-array-one-based-negative-index","images":[],"source_hash":"fa8ff2e4ab8556829f2a103ff04eb857a74d2f03dcc5470a6737d89b3523792d"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-004","number":null,"stem":"A DataFrame stores status codes in a map column. A developer needs to distinguish a stored zero from a key that is absent. The map contains only the two entries shown below:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.createDataFrame(\n    [({\"active\": 0, \"archived\": 1},)],\n    \"flags MAP<STRING, INT>\"\n)\nresult = df.select(\n    F.element_at(\"flags\", F.lit(\"active\")).alias(\"a\"),\n    F.element_at(\"flags\", F.lit(\"missing\")).alias(\"b\")\n)\n```\n\nWhat is the value of the output row `(a, b)` in Spark 3.5.6?","options":["(0, NULL)","(NULL, NULL)","The query fails because the second key is absent.","(0, 0)"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"mapの未登録キー取得","concept":"element-at-map-missing-key-null","family_id":"authored-element-at-map-missing-key-null","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A DataFrame stores status codes in a map column. A developer needs to distinguish a stored zero from a key that is absent. The map contains only the two entries shown below:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.createDataFrame(\n    [({\"active\": 0, \"archived\": 1},)],\n    \"flags MAP<STRING, INT>\"\n)\nresult = df.select(\n    F.element_at(\"flags\", F.lit(\"active\")).alias(\"a\"),\n    F.element_at(\"flags\", F.lit(\"missing\")).alias(\"b\")\n)\n```\n\nWhat is the value of the output row `(a, b)` in Spark 3.5.6?","options_ja":["(0, NULL)","(NULL, NULL)","The query fails because the second key is absent.","(0, 0)"],"explanation_ja":"mapのelement_atは存在するキーの値を返し、存在しないキーではNULLを返す。格納値0はNULLと異なる。配列の添字エラー規則と混同しない。","distractors_ja":["正答。存在する0と欠落によるNULLを区別する。","activeには0が明示的に格納されている。","mapの未登録キー取得はNULLを返す。","欠落キーは0で自動補完されない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.element_at.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.element_at.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"element-at-map-missing-key-null","images":[],"source_hash":"9a3bf84a29136f2780b28883392fd6dbb7e3600c653f4d7d2d2367d754b16243"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-005","number":null,"stem":"A developer must increase every measurement in an array by two while preserving one output row per input row and preserving the position of missing measurements. The developer uses a built-in higher-order function:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.createDataFrame([([10, None, 30],)], \"values ARRAY<INT>\")\nresult = df.select(F.transform(\"values\", lambda x: x + F.lit(2)).alias(\"adjusted\"))\n```\n\nAssume `F` is `pyspark.sql.functions` and no Python UDF is used. Which output is produced?","options":["Two rows containing the scalar values 12 and 32.","One row containing [12, 2, 32].","One row containing [12, NULL, 32].","One row containing [12, 32]."],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"配列要素の高階関数変換","concept":"array-transform-preserves-cardinality","family_id":"authored-array-transform-preserves-cardinality","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A developer must increase every measurement in an array by two while preserving one output row per input row and preserving the position of missing measurements. The developer uses a built-in higher-order function:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.createDataFrame([([10, None, 30],)], \"values ARRAY<INT>\")\nresult = df.select(F.transform(\"values\", lambda x: x + F.lit(2)).alias(\"adjusted\"))\n```\n\nAssume `F` is `pyspark.sql.functions` and no Python UDF is used. Which output is produced?","options_ja":["Two rows containing the scalar values 12 and 32.","One row containing [12, 2, 32].","One row containing [12, NULL, 32].","One row containing [12, 32]."],"explanation_ja":"transformは配列の各要素へColumn式を適用し配列を返す。行にexplodeせず、NULL+2はNULLなので3要素の位置が保持される。","distractors_ja":["transformは行展開を行わない。","NULLを0とする処理は指定されていない。","正答。配列長とNULL位置を維持する。","NULL要素を削除するfilterは指定されていない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.transform.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.transform.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"array-transform-preserves-cardinality","images":[],"source_hash":"0eb7f636ae4fe339b3710096bb8c0ebf64ebc45333859b806a985dd5372a79ee"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-006","number":null,"stem":"An orders DataFrame has a struct column named `shipping` with fields `city` and `postal_code`. Every input struct is non-NULL. A correction should replace only the postal code and preserve each row's existing city. The developer runs:\n\n```python\nresult = orders.withColumn(\n    \"shipping\",\n    F.col(\"shipping\").withField(\"postal_code\", F.lit(\"104-0001\"))\n)\n```\n\nFor an input shipping value `{city: \"Tokyo\", postal_code: \"OLD\"}`, which output structure is correct in Spark 3.5.6?","options":["shipping becomes {city: NULL, postal_code: \"104-0001\"}.","A top-level postal_code column is added while shipping remains unchanged.","shipping becomes {city: \"Tokyo\", postal_code: \"104-0001\"}.","shipping becomes the string \"104-0001\" and city is removed."],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"struct内のフィールド置換","concept":"struct-withfield-preserves-siblings","family_id":"authored-struct-withfield-preserves-siblings","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"An orders DataFrame has a struct column named `shipping` with fields `city` and `postal_code`. Every input struct is non-NULL. A correction should replace only the postal code and preserve each row's existing city. The developer runs:\n\n```python\nresult = orders.withColumn(\n    \"shipping\",\n    F.col(\"shipping\").withField(\"postal_code\", F.lit(\"104-0001\"))\n)\n```\n\nFor an input shipping value `{city: \"Tokyo\", postal_code: \"OLD\"}`, which output structure is correct in Spark 3.5.6?","options_ja":["shipping becomes {city: NULL, postal_code: \"104-0001\"}.","A top-level postal_code column is added while shipping remains unchanged.","shipping becomes {city: \"Tokyo\", postal_code: \"104-0001\"}.","shipping becomes the string \"104-0001\" and city is removed."],"explanation_ja":"Column.withFieldはstruct型の指定フィールドを追加または置換し、他フィールドを保持する。withColumnでshipping全体にその更新済みstructを代入している。NULL structの挙動を問わないよう前提を固定した。","distractors_ja":["cityをNULLへ変える式は存在しない。","代入先はトップレベルのpostal_codeではなくshipping。","正答。postal_codeだけ置換しcityは保持。","withFieldの戻り値は更新されたstructで、単なる文字列ではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.Column.withField.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.Column.withField.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"struct-withfield-preserves-siblings","images":[],"source_hash":"7667043ef4696d24bb5666dc451b79838fb696bbe9688fbb06d3149d06e3fff9"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-007","number":null,"stem":"Two DataFrames have schemas `left(id INT, amount INT)` and `right(id INT, category STRING)`. Both contain a matching, non-NULL ID. A developer compares these inner joins:\n\n```python\nby_name = left.join(right, \"id\", \"inner\")\nby_expr = left.join(right, left.id == right.id, \"inner\")\n```\n\nNo projection or column removal is applied afterward. In Spark 3.5.6, how many output columns are named `id` in `by_name` and `by_expr`, respectively?","options":["One in each result.","Two in by_name; one in by_expr.","One in by_name; two in by_expr.","Two in each result."],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"joinキーの出力schema","concept":"join-using-column-merges-output-key","family_id":"authored-join-using-column-merges-output-key","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"Two DataFrames have schemas `left(id INT, amount INT)` and `right(id INT, category STRING)`. Both contain a matching, non-NULL ID. A developer compares these inner joins:\n\n```python\nby_name = left.join(right, \"id\", \"inner\")\nby_expr = left.join(right, left.id == right.id, \"inner\")\n```\n\nNo projection or column removal is applied afterward. In Spark 3.5.6, how many output columns are named `id` in `by_name` and `by_expr`, respectively?","options_ja":["One in each result.","Two in by_name; one in by_expr.","One in by_name; two in by_expr.","Two in each result."],"explanation_ja":"joinに列名文字列を渡すequi-joinでは両側のkey列を1列として出す。Column条件を渡すjoinでは両側のidが残るため、後続selectで曖昧参照に注意する。","distractors_ja":["式joinでは明示的なprojectionなしにkey列を1本化しない。","列名joinと式joinのschema規則が逆。","正答。列名joinは1つ、式joinは両側の2つ。","列名指定joinは共通keyを1列に統合する。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"join-using-column-merges-output-key","images":[],"source_hash":"a5708df8cfe4d65fbd98ae229adb28de53ae707e2ec81abae88878abeebec3ac"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-008","number":null,"stem":"A developer needs to retain every source row whose ID appears in a reference DataFrame, without adding reference columns or multiplying rows because the reference contains duplicates:\n\n```python\nsource = spark.createDataFrame([(1, \"a\"), (1, \"b\"), (2, \"c\")], \"id INT, tag STRING\")\nreference = spark.createDataFrame([(1,), (1,)], \"id INT\")\nresult = source.join(reference, \"id\", \"left_semi\")\n```\n\nIgnoring row order, which rows and columns does `result` contain in Spark 3.5.6?","options":["Two rows: (1, \"a\") and (1, \"b\"); columns id and tag.","Three rows: (1, \"a\"), (1, \"b\"), and (2, \"c\"); columns id and tag.","One row: (1, \"a\"); columns id and tag.","Four rows: (1, \"a\") twice and (1, \"b\") twice; columns id and tag."],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"左半結合と右側重複","concept":"left-semi-existence-not-right-multiplicity","family_id":"authored-left-semi-existence-not-right-multiplicity","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A developer needs to retain every source row whose ID appears in a reference DataFrame, without adding reference columns or multiplying rows because the reference contains duplicates:\n\n```python\nsource = spark.createDataFrame([(1, \"a\"), (1, \"b\"), (2, \"c\")], \"id INT, tag STRING\")\nreference = spark.createDataFrame([(1,), (1,)], \"id INT\")\nresult = source.join(reference, \"id\", \"left_semi\")\n```\n\nIgnoring row order, which rows and columns does `result` contain in Spark 3.5.6?","options_ja":["Two rows: (1, \"a\") and (1, \"b\"); columns id and tag.","Three rows: (1, \"a\"), (1, \"b\"), and (2, \"c\"); columns id and tag.","One row: (1, \"a\"); columns id and tag.","Four rows: (1, \"a\") twice and (1, \"b\") twice; columns id and tag."],"explanation_ja":"left_semiは右側の一致存在を判定し、左側行と左側列だけを返す。右側の重複による出力増殖はなく、左側でtagの異なる2行は両方保持される。","distractors_ja":["正答。一致がある左側2行を各1回保持する。","id=2は右側に一致がなく削除される。","左側行をid単位でdropDuplicatesする操作ではない。","通常inner joinの多対多増殖に近い結果でsemiとは異なる。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.join.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"left-semi-existence-not-right-multiplicity","images":[],"source_hash":"8f819c249b8c71b04cd715a52afc523e7a0b7c208366f3f3d61f2f00bdee5a5d"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-009","number":null,"stem":"A daily ingestion job sometimes receives no records. The reporting code must still produce a single summary row, and the developer wants to predict its values before deciding how to display missing totals:\n\n```python\nempty = spark.createDataFrame([], \"amount DOUBLE\")\nsummary = empty.agg(\n    F.count(\"*\").alias(\"row_count\"),\n    F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"total\")\n)\n```\n\nNo `groupBy` keys or NULL replacement functions are used. What does `summary` contain in Spark 3.5.6?","options":["One row with (row_count=0, total=NULL).","One row with (row_count=NULL, total=NULL).","No rows, because the input is empty.","One row with (row_count=0, total=0.0)."],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"空入力に対する全体集約","concept":"empty-global-aggregate-count-sum","family_id":"authored-empty-global-aggregate-count-sum","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A daily ingestion job sometimes receives no records. The reporting code must still produce a single summary row, and the developer wants to predict its values before deciding how to display missing totals:\n\n```python\nempty = spark.createDataFrame([], \"amount DOUBLE\")\nsummary = empty.agg(\n    F.count(\"*\").alias(\"row_count\"),\n    F.sum(\"amount\").alias(\"total\")\n)\n```\n\nNo `groupBy` keys or NULL replacement functions are used. What does `summary` contain in Spark 3.5.6?","options_ja":["One row with (row_count=0, total=NULL).","One row with (row_count=NULL, total=NULL).","No rows, because the input is empty.","One row with (row_count=0, total=0.0)."],"explanation_ja":"group keyなしの全体集約は空入力でも集約結果1行を返す。COUNT(*)は0、SUMは空集合に対してNULL。業務表示上0が必要なら別途補完する。","distractors_ja":["正答。行数0と集計対象なしのNULLは異なる。","COUNT(*)は空集合でNULLではなく0。","全体集約は空入力でも1行の結果を返す。","SUMの空集合結果は自動的に0にはならない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-null-semantics.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-null-semantics.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"empty-global-aggregate-count-sum","images":[],"source_hash":"04027ecc5bce94e9733abd42f1d1ab1d10adfb01975c4d607b6e25c562948134"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-010","number":null,"stem":"A source stores positionally related codes and labels in separate arrays. Occasionally, the labels array is shorter, but every code must retain its position in the combined array. The developer runs:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.createDataFrame([([4, 5, 6], [\"x\"],)], \"codes ARRAY<INT>, labels ARRAY<STRING>\")\nresult = df.select(F.arrays_zip(\"codes\", \"labels\").alias(\"pairs\"))\n```\n\nWriting each resulting struct as `(codes, labels)`, which value does `pairs` contain in Spark 3.5.6?","options":["NULL, because the input arrays have different lengths.","[(4, \"x\")]","[(4, \"x\"), (5, \"x\"), (6, \"x\")]","[(4, \"x\"), (5, NULL), (6, NULL)]"],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"長さの違う配列のzip","concept":"arrays-zip-pad-shorter-input","family_id":"authored-arrays-zip-pad-shorter-input","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A source stores positionally related codes and labels in separate arrays. Occasionally, the labels array is shorter, but every code must retain its position in the combined array. The developer runs:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.createDataFrame([([4, 5, 6], [\"x\"],)], \"codes ARRAY<INT>, labels ARRAY<STRING>\")\nresult = df.select(F.arrays_zip(\"codes\", \"labels\").alias(\"pairs\"))\n```\n\nWriting each resulting struct as `(codes, labels)`, which value does `pairs` contain in Spark 3.5.6?","options_ja":["NULL, because the input arrays have different lengths.","[(4, \"x\")]","[(4, \"x\"), (5, \"x\"), (6, \"x\")]","[(4, \"x\"), (5, NULL), (6, NULL)]"],"explanation_ja":"arrays_zipは位置ごとの値をstructにまとめ、短い配列側の不足要素はNULLで埋める。最短配列に切り詰めず、ラベルの最後の値を繰り返すこともしない。","distractors_ja":["長さが違うこと自体は全体NULLの条件ではない。","Pythonの通常zipの最短長停止と混同している。","最後のラベル値でforward fillしない。","正答。最大長に揃え不足分をNULLで補う。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.arrays_zip.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.arrays_zip.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"arrays-zip-pad-shorter-input","images":[],"source_hash":"b66d2096caa20d519ab36905738873dbd458063a09a5dc5788ea77af9dbd69d4"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-011","number":null,"stem":"A pricing DataFrame has integer columns `preferred` and `backup`. A zero preferred price is valid and must not be treated as missing. The developer computes:\n\n```python\nresult = df.select(\n    \"id\",\n    F.coalesce(\"preferred\", \"backup\", F.lit(-1)).alias(\"price\")\n)\n```\n\nThe input rows, written as `(id, preferred, backup)`, are `(1, NULL, 80)`, `(2, 0, 90)`, and `(3, NULL, NULL)`. After ordering by ID, which sequence of price values is produced?","options":["80, 90, 0","NULL, 0, NULL","80, 90, -1","80, 0, -1"],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"列式coalesceの優先順","concept":"column-coalesce-first-nonnull-keeps-zero","family_id":"authored-column-coalesce-first-nonnull-keeps-zero","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A pricing DataFrame has integer columns `preferred` and `backup`. A zero preferred price is valid and must not be treated as missing. The developer computes:\n\n```python\nresult = df.select(\n    \"id\",\n    F.coalesce(\"preferred\", \"backup\", F.lit(-1)).alias(\"price\")\n)\n```\n\nThe input rows, written as `(id, preferred, backup)`, are `(1, NULL, 80)`, `(2, 0, 90)`, and `(3, NULL, NULL)`. After ordering by ID, which sequence of price values is produced?","options_ja":["80, 90, 0","NULL, 0, NULL","80, 90, -1","80, 0, -1"],"explanation_ja":"functions.coalesceは引数順で最初の非NULLを選ぶ。0は有効な非NULL値なのでbackupの90へ置換しない。全列NULLなら最後のliteral -1になる。DataFrame.coalesceによるpartition数変更とは別API。","distractors_ja":["0の扱いも、全NULL時のliteral -1も誤り。","backupとliteralのfallbackを無視している。","0はNULLでないため90に置換しない。","正答。最初の非NULL値を順番に採用する。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.coalesce.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.coalesce.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"column-coalesce-first-nonnull-keeps-zero","images":[],"source_hash":"113f0c076d9bb42290991d55fbe0b963a658ddb114f3c2fea4be5ce6975d7806"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-012","number":null,"stem":"A batch DataFrame events contains account_id, event_ts, event_id, and payload. All key and ordering values are non-null. Within each account, event_id is unique. The result must contain exactly one complete row per account: the latest event_ts, breaking timestamp ties by the greatest event_id. Assume F and Window are imported. Which expression satisfies this requirement?","options":["events.withColumn(\"rn\", F.row_number().over(Window.partitionBy(\"account_id\").orderBy(F.col(\"event_ts\").desc(), F.col(\"event_id\").desc()))).filter(\"rn = 1\").drop(\"rn\")","events.groupBy(\"account_id\").agg(F.max(\"event_ts\"))","events.dropDuplicates([\"account_id\"])","events.withColumn(\"rn\", F.row_number().over(Window.partitionBy(\"account_id\").orderBy(F.col(\"event_ts\").asc(), F.col(\"event_id\").asc()))).filter(\"rn = 1\").drop(\"rn\")"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"最新レコードの決定的な選択","concept":"row-number-latest-event-tiebreak","decision_id":"row-number-latest-event-tiebreak","family_id":"row-number-latest-event-tiebreak","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A batch DataFrame events contains account_id, event_ts, event_id, and payload. All key and ordering values are non-null. Within each account, event_id is unique. The result must contain exactly one complete row per account: the latest event_ts, breaking timestamp ties by the greatest event_id. Assume F and Window are imported. Which expression satisfies this requirement?","options_ja":["events.withColumn(\"rn\", F.row_number().over(Window.partitionBy(\"account_id\").orderBy(F.col(\"event_ts\").desc(), F.col(\"event_id\").desc()))).filter(\"rn = 1\").drop(\"rn\")","events.groupBy(\"account_id\").agg(F.max(\"event_ts\"))","events.dropDuplicates([\"account_id\"])","events.withColumn(\"rn\", F.row_number().over(Window.partitionBy(\"account_id\").orderBy(F.col(\"event_ts\").asc(), F.col(\"event_id\").asc()))).filter(\"rn = 1\").drop(\"rn\")"],"explanation_ja":"row_numberのpartitionをaccountにし、event時刻と一意event IDを降順に並べれば最新かつtie-breakが決まった1行を残せる。dropDuplicatesはどのpayloadを残すか指定できない。","distractors_ja":["正答。降順の全順序に対するrow_number=1が要件を満たす。","最大時刻だけを返し、対応する完全な元rowとevent IDによるtie-breakを満たさない。","dropDuplicatesは最新行や同時刻tie-breakを保証しない。","昇順なので最古行を選ぶ。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.row_number.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.row_number.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"d1598b063b2781069b7660b0a21f85b1556cfe130d1f3288e81d5131ee17d468"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-013","number":null,"stem":"A quality check examines a DoubleType column reading that can contain ordinary numbers, IEEE NaN, or SQL NULL. The engineer needs a Boolean flag that is true only for NaN. It must not flag an ordinary number or SQL NULL as NaN. Assume pyspark.sql.functions is imported as F. Which expression should be selected?","options":["F.col(\"reading\").isNull()","F.col(\"reading\").isNotNull()","F.isnan(\"reading\")","~F.isnan(\"reading\")"],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"NaNとNULLの区別","concept":"isnan-distinguishes-floating-nan-from-null","decision_id":"isnan-distinguishes-floating-nan-from-null","family_id":"isnan-distinguishes-floating-nan-from-null","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A quality check examines a DoubleType column reading that can contain ordinary numbers, IEEE NaN, or SQL NULL. The engineer needs a Boolean flag that is true only for NaN. It must not flag an ordinary number or SQL NULL as NaN. Assume pyspark.sql.functions is imported as F. Which expression should be selected?","options_ja":["F.col(\"reading\").isNull()","F.col(\"reading\").isNotNull()","F.isnan(\"reading\")","~F.isnan(\"reading\")"],"explanation_ja":"浮動小数のNaN判定にはisnanを使う。SQL NULLかどうかを判定するisNullとは別の条件。","distractors_ja":["isNullはSQL NULLを判定しNaNのflagではない。","isNotNullは通常の数値もtrueにする。","正答。isnanがNaNのみをtrueにする。","否定なのでNaN以外がtrueになる。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.isnan.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.isnan.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"761e02f32c8a4492b58b69424ee457062fe0500644ce26018fba94fc19a8763b"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-014","number":null,"stem":"A StringType column phone contains values such as \"+44 (20) 1234-AB56\". A downstream system requires every ASCII digit to be retained in its original order and every other character to be removed, including letters. A UDF must not be used. Assume pyspark.sql.functions is imported as F. Which expression meets the requirement for arbitrary such strings?","options":["F.translate(\"phone\", \"+ ()-\", \"\")","F.trim(\"phone\")","F.regexp_extract(\"phone\", \"[0-9]+\", 0)","F.regexp_replace(\"phone\", \"[^0-9]\", \"\")"],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"正規表現による文字列正規化","concept":"regexp-replace-remove-all-nondigits","decision_id":"regexp-replace-remove-all-nondigits","family_id":"regexp-replace-remove-all-nondigits","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A StringType column phone contains values such as \"+44 (20) 1234-AB56\". A downstream system requires every ASCII digit to be retained in its original order and every other character to be removed, including letters. A UDF must not be used. Assume pyspark.sql.functions is imported as F. Which expression meets the requirement for arbitrary such strings?","options_ja":["F.translate(\"phone\", \"+ ()-\", \"\")","F.trim(\"phone\")","F.regexp_extract(\"phone\", \"[0-9]+\", 0)","F.regexp_replace(\"phone\", \"[^0-9]\", \"\")"],"explanation_ja":"regexp_replaceで数字以外に一致する全substringを空文字へ置換する。regexp_extractは最初に一致する部分を取り出すため、分断された全数字を連結しない。","distractors_ja":["列挙した記号だけを除去し、任意の英字等が残る。","前後の空白処理では内部の記号や英字を除去できない。","最初の連続数字だけを抽出し後続数字を失う。","正答。全ての非ASCII数字文字を除去し数字の順序を保つ。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.regexp_replace.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.regexp_replace.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"72fe3ceb66a755ff2141f8230d73b171c54246ee59a450d4bf8995325aea4f3f"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-015","number":null,"stem":"A DataFrame has three StringType columns site, zone, and rack. A label must join the non-null values with a single hyphen, omitting null fields and their separators. For example, site=\"EU\", zone=NULL, rack=\"R7\" must produce \"EU-R7\". Assume F is imported. Which expression implements this behavior without a Python UDF?","options":["F.concat_ws(\"-\", \"site\", \"zone\", \"rack\")","F.concat_ws(\"-\", \"site\", F.coalesce(\"zone\", F.lit(\"\")), \"rack\")","F.concat_ws(\"\", \"site\", \"zone\", \"rack\")","F.concat(\"site\", F.lit(\"-\"), \"zone\", F.lit(\"-\"), \"rack\")"],"correct":0,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"NULLを除いた文字列連結","concept":"concat-ws-skips-null-input-fields","decision_id":"concat-ws-skips-null-input-fields","family_id":"concat-ws-skips-null-input-fields","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A DataFrame has three StringType columns site, zone, and rack. A label must join the non-null values with a single hyphen, omitting null fields and their separators. For example, site=\"EU\", zone=NULL, rack=\"R7\" must produce \"EU-R7\". Assume F is imported. Which expression implements this behavior without a Python UDF?","options_ja":["F.concat_ws(\"-\", \"site\", \"zone\", \"rack\")","F.concat_ws(\"-\", \"site\", F.coalesce(\"zone\", F.lit(\"\")), \"rack\")","F.concat_ws(\"\", \"site\", \"zone\", \"rack\")","F.concat(\"site\", F.lit(\"-\"), \"zone\", F.lit(\"-\"), \"rack\")"],"explanation_ja":"concat_wsはNULL引数を飛ばして非NULLの値をseparatorで連結する。NULLを空文字へ置換してから連結すると空fieldのためseparatorが増える。","distractors_ja":["正答。NULL fieldを省略してEU-R7を作る。","NULLを空文字に変えるとEU--R7となり余分なseparatorが残る。","separatorが空文字のためEUとR7の間にhyphenがない。","concatはNULLを含むとこの要件のNULL省略連結にならない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.concat_ws.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-functions-builtin.html#string-functions"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.concat_ws.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"33291970e5661a4db97e229c08cd4f1b0e5c8fa345699933b0fcb6273a7881ab"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-016","number":null,"stem":"A batch DataFrame inventory contains non-null numeric price and stock columns. The engineer must keep rows where price is at least 100 and stock is greater than zero. The code must build a PySpark Column predicate rather than evaluate a Python Boolean. Assume F is imported. Which expression correctly applies both conditions?","options":["inventory.filter((F.col(\"price\") >= 100) or (F.col(\"stock\") > 0))","inventory.filter((F.col(\"price\") >= 100) | (F.col(\"stock\") > 0))","inventory.filter((F.col(\"price\") > 100) & (F.col(\"stock\") >= 0))","inventory.filter((F.col(\"price\") >= 100) & (F.col(\"stock\") > 0))"],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"Columnの複数条件","concept":"column-boolean-and-needs-parentheses","decision_id":"column-boolean-and-needs-parentheses","family_id":"column-boolean-and-needs-parentheses","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A batch DataFrame inventory contains non-null numeric price and stock columns. The engineer must keep rows where price is at least 100 and stock is greater than zero. The code must build a PySpark Column predicate rather than evaluate a Python Boolean. Assume F is imported. Which expression correctly applies both conditions?","options_ja":["inventory.filter((F.col(\"price\") >= 100) or (F.col(\"stock\") > 0))","inventory.filter((F.col(\"price\") >= 100) | (F.col(\"stock\") > 0))","inventory.filter((F.col(\"price\") > 100) & (F.col(\"stock\") >= 0))","inventory.filter((F.col(\"price\") >= 100) & (F.col(\"stock\") > 0))"],"explanation_ja":"Column条件をANDするには各比較を括弧で囲み&で結ぶ。Pythonのand/orはColumnをPython boolとして評価しようとするため使わない。","distractors_ja":["PythonのorはColumn predicateの演算子ではない。","|はORとなり片方だけを満たす行も残す。","price=100を除外しstock=0を残すため境界が異なる。","正答。両比較を&で結び境界条件も要件通り。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/_modules/pyspark/sql/column.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.filter.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"7a1ce0b22be631218eac445f3001039abf59dc576c914f3c7600fc05eac939d0"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-017","number":null,"stem":"A StringType column raw_time contains valid timestamps formatted exactly as \"25/10/2026 14:05:09\". The result must be a TimestampType column, preserving both the date and time. The session uses UTC, and no time-zone conversion is needed. Assume pyspark.sql.functions is imported as F. Which expression specifies the correct input format?","options":["F.date_format(\"raw_time\", \"dd/MM/yyyy HH:mm:ss\")","F.to_date(\"raw_time\", \"dd/MM/yyyy\")","F.to_timestamp(\"raw_time\", \"MM/dd/yyyy HH:mm:ss\")","F.to_timestamp(\"raw_time\", \"dd/MM/yyyy HH:mm:ss\")"],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"書式付き日時文字列のparse","concept":"to-timestamp-explicit-source-format","decision_id":"to-timestamp-explicit-source-format","family_id":"to-timestamp-explicit-source-format","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A StringType column raw_time contains valid timestamps formatted exactly as \"25/10/2026 14:05:09\". The result must be a TimestampType column, preserving both the date and time. The session uses UTC, and no time-zone conversion is needed. Assume pyspark.sql.functions is imported as F. Which expression specifies the correct input format?","options_ja":["F.date_format(\"raw_time\", \"dd/MM/yyyy HH:mm:ss\")","F.to_date(\"raw_time\", \"dd/MM/yyyy\")","F.to_timestamp(\"raw_time\", \"MM/dd/yyyy HH:mm:ss\")","F.to_timestamp(\"raw_time\", \"dd/MM/yyyy HH:mm:ss\")"],"explanation_ja":"to_timestampに入力文字列と一致するformatを渡して日時をTimestampTypeへparseする。date_formatは文字列のformat、to_dateはDateTypeで時刻を落とす。","distractors_ja":["date_formatはTimestampTypeへparseするAPIではない。","DateTypeとなり時刻を保持しない。","月と日のpatternが逆で25を月として解釈しようとする。","正答。日/月/年と時分秒が入力書式に一致する。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.to_timestamp.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.to_timestamp.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"3131d38f809b03836f5d99c0955ed47452c673c9ce562f263085cd24c0e7cbce"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-018","number":null,"stem":"A DataFrame has a column chunks of type array<array<int>>. For one row, chunks is [[7, 8], NULL, [9]], where NULL is a missing subarray rather than an integer element. The engineer evaluates F.flatten(\"chunks\") without first replacing or removing the null subarray. What is the value produced for this row?","options":["[7, 8, 9]","[7, 8, NULL, 9]","NULL","[[7, 8], [9]]"],"correct":2,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"入れ子配列のflattenとNULL","concept":"flatten-null-subarray-propagates-null","decision_id":"flatten-null-subarray-propagates-null","family_id":"flatten-null-subarray-propagates-null","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A DataFrame has a column chunks of type array<array<int>>. For one row, chunks is [[7, 8], NULL, [9]], where NULL is a missing subarray rather than an integer element. The engineer evaluates F.flatten(\"chunks\") without first replacing or removing the null subarray. What is the value produced for this row?","options_ja":["[7, 8, 9]","[7, 8, NULL, 9]","NULL","[[7, 8], [9]]"],"explanation_ja":"flattenはarray of arraysを1段平坦化するが、subarray自体がNULLなら結果全体がNULLになる。NULL integer要素を含む配列とは区別する。","distractors_ja":["NULL subarrayを自動で空配列として無視しない。","NULL subarrayを単一NULL要素へ自動変換する動作ではない。","正答。NULL subarrayを含むためflatten結果はNULL。","NULLを除去して入れ子を残す関数ではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.flatten.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.flatten.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"634067b98a870bef3b9c8a2fb27676279f88e62a982a751db7e2f04cb7b41101"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-019","number":null,"stem":"A sales DataFrame contains store, channel, and amount. The only required channels are \"web\" and \"shop\". The result must have one row per store and separate web and shop columns containing summed amounts. The engineer also wants to supply the known pivot values explicitly. Which expression creates the required result?","options":["sales.groupBy(\"channel\").pivot(\"store\", [\"web\", \"shop\"]).sum(\"amount\")","sales.groupBy(\"store\").pivot(\"channel\", [\"web\", \"shop\"]).sum(\"amount\")","sales.groupBy(\"store\", \"channel\").sum(\"amount\")","sales.select(\"store\", \"web\", \"shop\").groupBy(\"store\").sum()"],"correct":1,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"pivotで既知categoryを列化","concept":"pivot-known-category-values-before-aggregate","decision_id":"pivot-known-category-values-before-aggregate","family_id":"pivot-known-category-values-before-aggregate","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A sales DataFrame contains store, channel, and amount. The only required channels are \"web\" and \"shop\". The result must have one row per store and separate web and shop columns containing summed amounts. The engineer also wants to supply the known pivot values explicitly. Which expression creates the required result?","options_ja":["sales.groupBy(\"channel\").pivot(\"store\", [\"web\", \"shop\"]).sum(\"amount\")","sales.groupBy(\"store\").pivot(\"channel\", [\"web\", \"shop\"]).sum(\"amount\")","sales.groupBy(\"store\", \"channel\").sum(\"amount\")","sales.select(\"store\", \"web\", \"shop\").groupBy(\"store\").sum()"],"explanation_ja":"storeをgroup key、channelをpivot列、既知のchannel値をpivot valuesとしてsumする。通常のgroupByだけではchannelが行のままになる。","distractors_ja":["group列とpivot列を逆にしておりstoreごとの結果にならない。","正答。channelの既知値を列へ変換してstore単位で集計する。","store×channelの行を作りweb/shopを別列にしない。","元DataFrameにwebとshopという列はなくselectできない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.GroupedData.pivot.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.GroupedData.pivot.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"09ba6b0e1b284d519566090fcd6af9478eea4144153b75cc81ca7cfe2d9a14df"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-020","number":null,"stem":"A DataFrame events has an event_id column and a payload StringType column. A payload value is {\"sku\":\"P8\",\"qty\":3}. The engineer must keep event_id and add a parsed struct column containing sku as STRING and qty as INT. The input is already a DataFrame, not a path to JSON files. Assume F is imported. Which expression is appropriate?","options":["events.withColumn(\"parsed\", F.col(\"payload\").cast(\"STRUCT<sku:STRING,qty:INT>\"))","events.withColumn(\"parsed\", F.to_json(\"payload\"))","spark.read.schema(\"sku STRING, qty INT\").json(\"payload\")","events.withColumn(\"parsed\", F.from_json(\"payload\", \"sku STRING, qty INT\"))"],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"JSON文字列列の構造化","concept":"from-json-parse-string-column-to-struct","decision_id":"from-json-parse-string-column-to-struct","family_id":"from-json-parse-string-column-to-struct","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A DataFrame events has an event_id column and a payload StringType column. A payload value is {\"sku\":\"P8\",\"qty\":3}. The engineer must keep event_id and add a parsed struct column containing sku as STRING and qty as INT. The input is already a DataFrame, not a path to JSON files. Assume F is imported. Which expression is appropriate?","options_ja":["events.withColumn(\"parsed\", F.col(\"payload\").cast(\"STRUCT<sku:STRING,qty:INT>\"))","events.withColumn(\"parsed\", F.to_json(\"payload\"))","spark.read.schema(\"sku STRING, qty INT\").json(\"payload\")","events.withColumn(\"parsed\", F.from_json(\"payload\", \"sku STRING, qty INT\"))"],"explanation_ja":"既存の文字列ColumnをJSON構造へparseするのはfrom_json。DataFrameReader.jsonはfile読取りであり、to_jsonは逆に構造を文字列化する。","distractors_ja":["StringTypeのJSONをstructへparseするにはcastではなくfrom_jsonを使う。","to_jsonはstruct等からJSON文字列を作る逆向きの関数。","payloadというpathを読もうとし元DataFrameのevent_idを保持する列変換ではない。","正答。payloadを指定schemaのstructへparseし元の列も保持する。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.from_json.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.from_json.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"a8341dc66fba6afccd2745c5ccc5107824cb0586af4cb1694d16b47abbe03df4"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-DF-021","number":null,"stem":"A recommendation DataFrame has two non-null array<string> columns preferred and available. Each array may contain repeated values. The result needs a new array containing only values present in both arrays, with duplicates removed. The order of the resulting array is irrelevant. Assume pyspark.sql.functions is imported as F. Which expression meets this requirement?","options":["F.array_except(\"preferred\", \"available\")","F.concat(\"preferred\", \"available\")","F.array_union(\"preferred\", \"available\")","F.array_intersect(\"preferred\", \"available\")"],"correct":3,"domain":"DataFrame","topic":"配列の集合積","concept":"array-intersect-common-values-without-duplicates","decision_id":"array-intersect-common-values-without-duplicates","family_id":"array-intersect-common-values-without-duplicates","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A recommendation DataFrame has two non-null array<string> columns preferred and available. Each array may contain repeated values. The result needs a new array containing only values present in both arrays, with duplicates removed. The order of the resulting array is irrelevant. Assume pyspark.sql.functions is imported as F. Which expression meets this requirement?","options_ja":["F.array_except(\"preferred\", \"available\")","F.concat(\"preferred\", \"available\")","F.array_union(\"preferred\", \"available\")","F.array_intersect(\"preferred\", \"available\")"],"explanation_ja":"array_intersectは両方の配列に存在する要素を重複なしで返す。出力順を条件に含めず集合積として判断する。","distractors_ja":["exceptはpreferredからavailableの要素を除く差集合。","concatは配列をつなぎ重複も残す。","unionはどちらかに存在する全要素を返し共通部分に限定しない。","正答。共通部分を重複なしで返す。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.array_intersect.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.functions.array_intersect.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"grading_hold":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","images":[],"source_hash":"f6fb1226de8b4e11a4563847706c0924e5e00a72a17b0b22d20ee1ba1d8de713"}]},{"id":"AUTHORED-Tuning","title":"自作問題 · Tuning","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-TUN-001","number":null,"stem":"A batch job aborts with a message that serialized task results exceed spark.driver.maxResultSize during collect(). The detailed result is too large for the driver, but the application only needs a small summary locally; detailed rows may be stored in distributed storage. Which change best meets the requirement while retaining the result-size guard?","options":["Write detailed rows with a distributed DataFrame writer and collect only the small aggregate summary.","Increase spark.executor.memory and leave collect() unchanged.","Cache the complete result before collecting every detailed row.","Set spark.driver.maxResultSize to 0 and continue collecting every detailed row."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"driver結果サイズ上限の診断","concept":"tuning-driver-result-size-guard-reduce-local-result","family_id":"tuning-driver-result-size-guard-reduce-local-result","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A batch job aborts with a message that serialized task results exceed spark.driver.maxResultSize during collect(). The detailed result is too large for the driver, but the application only needs a small summary locally; detailed rows may be stored in distributed storage. Which change best meets the requirement while retaining the result-size guard?","options_ja":["Write detailed rows with a distributed DataFrame writer and collect only the small aggregate summary.","Increase spark.executor.memory and leave collect() unchanged.","Cache the complete result before collecting every detailed row.","Set spark.driver.maxResultSize to 0 and continue collecting every detailed row."],"explanation_ja":"maxResultSizeはactionでdriverへ返るserialized resultの合計上限。0は無制限だがdriverで全結果を処理できる保証はない。詳細は分散出力し、必要な小さなsummaryだけ返せばguardを維持できる。","distractors_ja":["正解。driverに必要な結果量を要件に合わせて小さくする。","executor heapを増やしてもdriverへ返る合計結果サイズは減らない。","cacheはdriverへ収集する行数・結果サイズの上限を減らさない。","guardを無効にするうえ、driver容量の条件を解決しない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"tuning-driver-result-size-guard-reduce-local-result","images":[],"source_hash":"bd6775f5510817c4db6231525abeb2025d3ef1a4c41684d5e009f338c2161816"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-TUN-002","number":null,"stem":"A PySpark application runs in YARN containers. Executors are killed for exceeding container physical memory while JVM heap usage remains well below its limit. Logs show that Python worker processes consume most of the extra memory. spark.executor.pyspark.memory is not set. Which setting most directly increases the container allowance covering this non-JVM memory?","options":["spark.driver.memory","spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold","spark.executor.memoryOverhead","spark.sql.shuffle.partitions"],"correct":2,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"Python workerとコンテナメモリ","concept":"tuning-yarn-python-worker-memory-overhead","family_id":"tuning-yarn-python-worker-memory-overhead","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A PySpark application runs in YARN containers. Executors are killed for exceeding container physical memory while JVM heap usage remains well below its limit. Logs show that Python worker processes consume most of the extra memory. spark.executor.pyspark.memory is not set. Which setting most directly increases the container allowance covering this non-JVM memory?","options_ja":["spark.driver.memory","spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold","spark.executor.memoryOverhead","spark.sql.shuffle.partitions"],"explanation_ja":"YARNではexecutor memoryOverheadがJVM heap以外の追加メモリを確保する。executor.pyspark.memory未設定ならPython workerのメモリもこの枠を使う。JVM heap不足とcontainer physical memory超過を区別する。","distractors_ja":["driverのheapであり、executor containerのPython worker枠を増やさない。","joinのbroadcast判断サイズでありcontainer memory allowanceの設定ではない。","正解。条件にあるPython worker等の非JVM消費を含む追加枠を調整する。","shuffleのparallelism設定であり、非JVM枠そのものではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"tuning-yarn-python-worker-memory-overhead","images":[],"source_hash":"e5ed2ac9391cffc17aa04b660bb65763c5500fc746cc6dce8c79b04bbc502d3f"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-TUN-003","number":null,"stem":"A completed sort stage shows shuffle spill (memory) of 600 MB and shuffle spill (disk) of 90 MB in the Spark UI. All tasks succeeded and output validation passed. An engineer claims the difference proves that 510 MB of records was lost. Which interpretation of these metrics is correct?","options":["The metrics describe deserialized in-memory data and serialized disk data, so different sizes alone do not establish record loss.","The smaller disk value proves Spark dropped duplicate records during the sort.","The memory value measures the driver's heap and the disk value measures every executor's local disk capacity.","Spill to disk means the sort stage failed even if its task status is successful."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"spillメトリクスの読み方","concept":"tuning-spill-memory-disk-representation-metrics","family_id":"tuning-spill-memory-disk-representation-metrics","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A completed sort stage shows shuffle spill (memory) of 600 MB and shuffle spill (disk) of 90 MB in the Spark UI. All tasks succeeded and output validation passed. An engineer claims the difference proves that 510 MB of records was lost. Which interpretation of these metrics is correct?","options_ja":["The metrics describe deserialized in-memory data and serialized disk data, so different sizes alone do not establish record loss.","The smaller disk value proves Spark dropped duplicate records during the sort.","The memory value measures the driver's heap and the disk value measures every executor's local disk capacity.","Spill to disk means the sort stage failed even if its task status is successful."],"explanation_ja":"shuffle spill(memory)とspill(disk)は同じbyte表現ではない。前者はdeserialized form、後者はserialized formのサイズであり、差を欠落レコード量とは解釈できない。spillは性能調査の手掛かりだが、この成功stageのデータ欠落の証拠ではない。","distractors_ja":["正解。表現の異なるsizeの差だけで欠落を推定しない。","sortは重複を自動削除せず、このsize差は重複削除の証拠にもならない。","driver heapとディスク総容量の測定値ではない。","spill自体はディスクへ退避する仕組みであり、task失敗と同義ではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/web-ui.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/web-ui.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"tuning-spill-memory-disk-representation-metrics","images":[],"source_hash":"7a5f16f68f6b00d476a1c4c1883346415e194cd171f8c09c6e36c9e22e972845"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-TUN-004","number":null,"stem":"A DataFrame is repeatedly reused, so the team wants to retain SQL columnar caching. An executor runs out of memory while constructing a single in-memory columnar cache batch. The logical data and cluster size must remain unchanged. Which configuration change directly reduces the number of rows assembled in each cache batch?","options":["Increase spark.sql.files.maxRecordsPerFile.","Decrease spark.sql.inMemoryColumnarStorage.batchSize.","Increase spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold.","Increase spark.sql.inMemoryColumnarStorage.batchSize."],"correct":1,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"columnar cacheのバッチサイズ","concept":"tuning-columnar-cache-batch-row-size","family_id":"tuning-columnar-cache-batch-row-size","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A DataFrame is repeatedly reused, so the team wants to retain SQL columnar caching. An executor runs out of memory while constructing a single in-memory columnar cache batch. The logical data and cluster size must remain unchanged. Which configuration change directly reduces the number of rows assembled in each cache batch?","options_ja":["Increase spark.sql.files.maxRecordsPerFile.","Decrease spark.sql.inMemoryColumnarStorage.batchSize.","Increase spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold.","Increase spark.sql.inMemoryColumnarStorage.batchSize."],"explanation_ja":"inMemoryColumnarStorage.batchSizeはSQL columnar cacheのbatchサイズを制御する。大きいbatchは圧縮等に有利な場合があるがOOMのリスクも上がる。ここでは行数を直接減らす設定を問う。","distractors_ja":["出力fileの行数上限でありin-memory cacheに効かない。","正解。1つのcolumnar cache batchへまとめる行数を減らす。","broadcast join判断でありcache batchの行数ではない。","1 batchの行数を増やすため、求める変更と逆。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-performance-tuning.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-performance-tuning.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"tuning-columnar-cache-batch-row-size","images":[],"source_hash":"f41d2230bf2fbafa317cf62f8fee32711450c36fb664b8d73adb1d029f537535"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-TUN-005","number":null,"stem":"A downstream reader rejects any output data file containing more than 500000 records. A Spark batch writes Parquet with many reasonably balanced partitions, but some partitions contain more records than that limit. The team does not require an exact file count or byte size. Which setting directly caps records per output file without forcing all data into one partition?","options":["spark.sql.files.maxPartitionBytes=500000","spark.sql.files.openCostInBytes=500000","spark.sql.files.maxRecordsPerFile=500000","spark.sql.shuffle.partitions=500000"],"correct":2,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"出力fileの行数上限","concept":"tuning-output-max-records-per-file","family_id":"tuning-output-max-records-per-file","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A downstream reader rejects any output data file containing more than 500000 records. A Spark batch writes Parquet with many reasonably balanced partitions, but some partitions contain more records than that limit. The team does not require an exact file count or byte size. Which setting directly caps records per output file without forcing all data into one partition?","options_ja":["spark.sql.files.maxPartitionBytes=500000","spark.sql.files.openCostInBytes=500000","spark.sql.files.maxRecordsPerFile=500000","spark.sql.shuffle.partitions=500000"],"explanation_ja":"maxRecordsPerFileは1出力fileへ書く最大record数を指定する。同じtaskから複数fileへ分割される場合があり、正確なfile数やbyteサイズを保証する設定ではない。","distractors_ja":["file読取時にpartitionへまとめるbyte数であり、出力record数の指定ではない。","file読取時のopen cost見積りであり、出力fileのrecord上限ではない。","正解。要求されたrecord数を直接上限指定する。","shuffle partition数であり各fileのrecord数を上限保証しない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"tuning-output-max-records-per-file","images":[],"source_hash":"ad186e18bee3e3d0ece1174ff6f47f4a96f4a1cc025d550362d8bd2eab606343"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-TUN-006","number":null,"stem":"A Spark 3.5.6 batch rewrites a partitioned Parquet data source at /reports. Existing day partitions must remain unchanged except days present in replacement_df, which contains complete replacements for those days. This is a V1 file data source, not a Hive SerDe table or Delta table. Which writer configuration replaces only partitions receiving new rows?","options":["replacement_df.write.mode('overwrite').option('partitionOverwriteMode', 'dynamic').partitionBy('day').parquet('/reports')","replacement_df.write.mode('overwrite').option('partitionOverwriteMode', 'static').partitionBy('day').parquet('/reports')","replacement_df.write.mode('append').partitionBy('day').parquet('/reports')","replacement_df.write.mode('ignore').partitionBy('day').parquet('/reports')"],"correct":0,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"部分partitionの上書き","concept":"tuning-dynamic-partition-overwrite-preserve-untouched","family_id":"tuning-dynamic-partition-overwrite-preserve-untouched","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A Spark 3.5.6 batch rewrites a partitioned Parquet data source at /reports. Existing day partitions must remain unchanged except days present in replacement_df, which contains complete replacements for those days. This is a V1 file data source, not a Hive SerDe table or Delta table. Which writer configuration replaces only partitions receiving new rows?","options_ja":["replacement_df.write.mode('overwrite').option('partitionOverwriteMode', 'dynamic').partitionBy('day').parquet('/reports')","replacement_df.write.mode('overwrite').option('partitionOverwriteMode', 'static').partitionBy('day').parquet('/reports')","replacement_df.write.mode('append').partitionBy('day').parquet('/reports')","replacement_df.write.mode('ignore').partitionBy('day').parquet('/reports')"],"explanation_ja":"dynamic partition overwriteは実行時にデータを書いたpartitionだけを上書きする。入力が対象日の全置換データである条件が重要。Hive SerDe/Delta等に一般化せず、V1 Parquetのwriter条件を明記した。","distractors_ja":["正解。入力にあるdayの既存partitionを置き換え、それ以外を残す。","path全体のstatic overwriteは未入力日のpartition保持を満たさない。","既存対象日のデータを残して追加するため、置換にならず重複し得る。","既存pathがあるためwriteを行わず、対象日を更新できない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/configuration.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"tuning-dynamic-partition-overwrite-preserve-untouched","images":[],"source_hash":"c6dc2cdc72895db56b4927346950e153f273d3d3fd851122b938bee54b3277ac"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-TUN-007","number":null,"stem":"A Hive-metastore table has stale catalog size statistics after a data refresh. The optimizer uses an unrealistic size estimate when planning a join. Before forcing a join strategy, the engineer wants to recompute the table's size in bytes without scanning all row values. Which command directly collects that statistic in Spark SQL?","options":["REFRESH TABLE warehouse.dimension","DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED warehouse.dimension","ANALYZE TABLE warehouse.dimension COMPUTE STATISTICS NOSCAN","ANALYZE TABLE warehouse.dimension COMPUTE STATISTICS FOR ALL COLUMNS"],"correct":2,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"optimizer用サイズ統計","concept":"tuning-analyze-noscan-table-byte-statistics","family_id":"tuning-analyze-noscan-table-byte-statistics","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A Hive-metastore table has stale catalog size statistics after a data refresh. The optimizer uses an unrealistic size estimate when planning a join. Before forcing a join strategy, the engineer wants to recompute the table's size in bytes without scanning all row values. Which command directly collects that statistic in Spark SQL?","options_ja":["REFRESH TABLE warehouse.dimension","DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED warehouse.dimension","ANALYZE TABLE warehouse.dimension COMPUTE STATISTICS NOSCAN","ANALYZE TABLE warehouse.dimension COMPUTE STATISTICS FOR ALL COLUMNS"],"explanation_ja":"ANALYZE TABLE ... COMPUTE STATISTICS NOSCANはtableのsize in bytesを収集する。NOSCANは全rowをscanせずsizeを求める指定。統計を得る操作と既存統計を表示する操作を区別する。","distractors_ja":["data/metadata cacheのrefreshであり、この命令自体がtable size統計を収集する指定ではない。","既存metadataを表示するが、統計を再収集しない。","正解。行値を全scanせずbyte size統計を収集する。","column statistics収集はrow値のscanが必要で、NOSCAN要件を満たさない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-aux-analyze-table.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-aux-analyze-table.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"tuning-analyze-noscan-table-byte-statistics","images":[],"source_hash":"b9cb18366b61f7f15d774a50fee864cea1dceeb93b4040c97badccacf0073dcf"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-TUN-008","number":null,"stem":"A SQL write receives a highly uneven query result. The engineer adds a REBALANCE hint to avoid very small and very large output partitions, without requesting an exact file count. However, spark.sql.adaptive.enabled is false and the hint has no effect. Which change is required for Spark 3.5.6 to consider this best-effort hint?","options":["Set spark.sql.shuffle.partitions to 1 while leaving AQE disabled.","Enable spark.sql.adaptive.enabled for the query.","Replace REBALANCE with BROADCAST on the complete output.","Disable spark.sql.inMemoryColumnarStorage.compressed."],"correct":1,"domain":"Tuning","topic":"REBALANCEとAQEの前提","concept":"tuning-rebalance-hint-requires-aqe","family_id":"tuning-rebalance-hint-requires-aqe","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A SQL write receives a highly uneven query result. The engineer adds a REBALANCE hint to avoid very small and very large output partitions, without requesting an exact file count. However, spark.sql.adaptive.enabled is false and the hint has no effect. Which change is required for Spark 3.5.6 to consider this best-effort hint?","options_ja":["Set spark.sql.shuffle.partitions to 1 while leaving AQE disabled.","Enable spark.sql.adaptive.enabled for the query.","Replace REBALANCE with BROADCAST on the complete output.","Disable spark.sql.inMemoryColumnarStorage.compressed."],"explanation_ja":"REBALANCE hintはAQEが無効だと無視される。AQEを有効にすると、skewを分割する等のbest effortで出力partitionを調整する。file数やbyteサイズの厳密な保証ではない。","distractors_ja":["1 partitionへ集約しても、AQE無効時のhint無視は変わらない。","正解。REBALANCEが考慮されるための前提条件を満たす。","broadcastはjoin strategyのhintであり、query結果のwrite partition平準化ではない。","columnar cache圧縮の設定であり、REBALANCEの有効条件と無関係。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-hints.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/sql-ref-syntax-qry-select-hints.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"tuning-rebalance-hint-requires-aqe","images":[],"source_hash":"d17117a5ff2dd8ffd079b244a77c5505a2a4e80e33099c0131898a6d337c608f"}]},{"id":"AUTHORED-Structured-Streaming","title":"自作問題 · Structured Streaming","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-STR-001","number":null,"stem":"A query joins two streaming inputs, each with an event-time watermark. At a particular point, the independently computed input watermarks are 12:20 for one input and 12:05 for the other. The query uses Spark's default multiple-watermark policy. The team considers changing the policy to max for a newly deployed query. Which description correctly compares the two policies?","options":["The default global watermark is 12:20; max changes it to 12:05 and protects slower input.","The default global watermark is 12:05; max can advance it to 12:20 but risks discarding data from the slower input.","Both policies always use 12:20 because only the fastest input affects the global watermark.","Both policies keep separate watermarks indefinitely and never derive a global watermark."],"correct":1,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"複数入力watermarkの統合方針","concept":"multiple-watermarks-min-vs-max-tradeoff","family_id":"authored-multiple-watermarks-min-vs-max-tradeoff","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A query joins two streaming inputs, each with an event-time watermark. At a particular point, the independently computed input watermarks are 12:20 for one input and 12:05 for the other. The query uses Spark's default multiple-watermark policy. The team considers changing the policy to max for a newly deployed query. Which description correctly compares the two policies?","options_ja":["The default global watermark is 12:20; max changes it to 12:05 and protects slower input.","The default global watermark is 12:05; max can advance it to 12:20 but risks discarding data from the slower input.","Both policies always use 12:20 because only the fastest input affects the global watermark.","Both policies keep separate watermarks indefinitely and never derive a global watermark."],"explanation_ja":"複数watermarkの既定policyはminで、遅い入力を基準にglobal watermarkを進める。maxは速い入力の進捗を採用できる一方、遅い側のデータをより積極的にlateとして捨てる可能性がある。稼働済checkpointの途中変更とは区別し、新規queryの比較にした。","distractors_ja":["既定minとmaxの方向が逆。","正答。minは12:05、maxでは12:20に進み得て遅い入力に影響する。","既定minは遅い入力のwatermarkを使う。","複数入力からglobal watermarkを選ぶpolicyが存在する。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#policy-for-handling-multiple-watermarks"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#policy-for-handling-multiple-watermarks","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"multiple-watermarks-min-vs-max-tradeoff","images":[],"source_hash":"39a112fc2ad74cfe3744a41413d0e0b238c994eb2d010bf53bcbe4b81fec049e"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-STR-002","number":null,"stem":"A single micro-batch query uses foreachBatch to append results to an external database. It restarts from the same checkpoint after a failure, and one batch may be delivered to the callback again. The database supports transactions. The team needs to prevent duplicate committed output for the same batch. Which implementation addresses this requirement?","options":["Use the supplied batchId as a durable deduplication key and atomically commit the output together with its processed-batch marker.","Use outputMode(\"append\"); that setting alone makes every external callback exactly once.","Generate a new random identifier on each callback invocation and treat every identifier as a new batch.","Store processed batch IDs only in a local Python set that is cleared when the driver restarts."],"correct":0,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"foreachBatchの冪等な外部出力","concept":"foreachbatch-durable-batchid-deduplication","family_id":"authored-foreachbatch-durable-batchid-deduplication","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A single micro-batch query uses foreachBatch to append results to an external database. It restarts from the same checkpoint after a failure, and one batch may be delivered to the callback again. The database supports transactions. The team needs to prevent duplicate committed output for the same batch. Which implementation addresses this requirement?","options_ja":["Use the supplied batchId as a durable deduplication key and atomically commit the output together with its processed-batch marker.","Use outputMode(\"append\"); that setting alone makes every external callback exactly once.","Generate a new random identifier on each callback invocation and treat every identifier as a new batch.","Store processed batch IDs only in a local Python set that is cleared when the driver restarts."],"explanation_ja":"foreachBatchの既定保証はat-least-once。再試行されるbatchIdを永続的に識別し、外部DB内の出力と処理済markerを同一transactionで確定すれば再試行による二重commitを防げる。checkpointだけで任意外部処理までexactly-onceにはならない。","distractors_ja":["正答。永続batchIdと原子的な外部commitで再実行を冪等化する。","appendは出力modeでありcallbackの外部副作用を重複排除しない。","再試行を新規処理とみなすため重複を防げない。","driver再起動後に処理済情報が失われる。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#using-foreach-and-foreachbatch"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#using-foreach-and-foreachbatch","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"foreachbatch-durable-batchid-deduplication","images":[],"source_hash":"8abc783d1f77d4c5197f2289e6daf16423ae14edb35bd0749ae9ef769a470d5a"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-STR-003","number":null,"stem":"A command-line PySpark program defines a non-aggregated, stateless streaming DataFrame named events that supports append output to the console. It must start writing rows and keep the main program waiting while that query runs. The developer has only constructed writer = events.writeStream.format(\"console\"); no query has started yet. Which next step correctly starts execution and then waits for termination?","options":["query = writer.start(); query.awaitTermination()","query = writer; query.stop(); query.awaitTermination()","writer.awaitTermination(); writer.start()","events.collect(); writer.start()"],"correct":0,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"query起動と待機の順序","concept":"streamingquery-start-handle-awaittermination","family_id":"authored-streamingquery-start-handle-awaittermination","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A command-line PySpark program defines a non-aggregated, stateless streaming DataFrame named events that supports append output to the console. It must start writing rows and keep the main program waiting while that query runs. The developer has only constructed writer = events.writeStream.format(\"console\"); no query has started yet. Which next step correctly starts execution and then waits for termination?","options_ja":["query = writer.start(); query.awaitTermination()","query = writer; query.stop(); query.awaitTermination()","writer.awaitTermination(); writer.start()","events.collect(); writer.start()"],"explanation_ja":"DataStreamWriterは出力設定のbuilder。startで実行を起動しStreamingQuery handleを返す。awaitTerminationはその実行済queryの終了待ちであり、startの代わりではない。 本問は集約なし・append出力対応という前提で、output modeによる開始失敗は対象外。","distractors_ja":["正答。先にstartして得られたqueryで待機する。","writerをqueryへ代入してもqueryは起動せずstop/awaitTerminationも適用先が違う。","awaitTerminationは設定用writerの起動前待機APIではない。","streaming DataFrameへ通常collectをかけるのは有効なquery起動手順ではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#starting-streaming-queries","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#managing-streaming-queries"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#starting-streaming-queries","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"streamingquery-start-handle-awaittermination","images":[],"source_hash":"a7dfd85a4d7bfa6be8437d7e01de8023ea9591e05379ad72c038b089dab3f49b"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-STR-004","number":null,"stem":"A streaming DataFrame events contains new purchases, and a static DataFrame products contains product descriptions. The application must emit every purchase, including purchases whose product ID is absent from the static DataFrame. It does not need to emit static products that have no purchases. For Spark 3.5.6 Structured Streaming, which statement about events.join(products, \"product_id\", \"left_outer\") is correct?","options":["It requires a watermark on products before any unmatched purchase can be emitted.","It emits unmatched static products as well as unmatched purchases, making it equivalent to a full outer join.","It is a supported, non-stateful stream-static join that preserves unmatched rows from events with NULL product fields.","It is unsupported because every outer join involving a stream requires both inputs to be streaming."],"correct":2,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"stream-static結合の保存側","concept":"stream-static-left-outer-supported-stateless","family_id":"authored-stream-static-left-outer-supported-stateless","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A streaming DataFrame events contains new purchases, and a static DataFrame products contains product descriptions. The application must emit every purchase, including purchases whose product ID is absent from the static DataFrame. It does not need to emit static products that have no purchases. For Spark 3.5.6 Structured Streaming, which statement about events.join(products, \"product_id\", \"left_outer\") is correct?","options_ja":["It requires a watermark on products before any unmatched purchase can be emitted.","It emits unmatched static products as well as unmatched purchases, making it equivalent to a full outer join.","It is a supported, non-stateful stream-static join that preserves unmatched rows from events with NULL product fields.","It is unsupported because every outer join involving a stream requires both inputs to be streaming."],"explanation_ja":"streamを左、staticを右にしたleft outerは対応し、stream-static結合自体はstatefulではない。左側purchaseを残しstaticに一致しない説明列をNULLにする。将来の右stream到着を待つstream-stream outerのwatermark要件とは異なる。","distractors_ja":["static DataFrameにstream watermarkを設定して待つ必要はない。","left outerは一致しない右側static行だけを出すことはない。","正答。対応する非stateful lookupで左側stream行を保持する。","stream-staticのleft outerは明示的に対応する。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#join-operations"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#join-operations","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"stream-static-left-outer-supported-stateless","images":[],"source_hash":"092e82fd6d36beaa76ea0648d53e631a6193b434ca8c57808060ebfc33537ef1"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-STR-005","number":null,"stem":"An advertising query left-outer-joins an impressions stream to a clicks stream using only an ad ID. A click should match only if its event time is between the impression time and one hour later. Event time and micro-batch execution continue to advance on the inputs. The team needs Spark to eventually emit an impression with NULL click fields when no eligible click can still arrive. Which design provides the necessary event-time information?","options":["Apply an equality join on ad ID only and increase executor memory.","Sort each stream by its timestamp before the equality join, without using watermarks.","Add appropriate event-time watermarks and include the one-hour event-time range in the join condition.","Use outputMode(\"complete\") without adding any time constraints."],"correct":2,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"stream-stream外部結合の未一致確定","concept":"stream-stream-outer-time-bound-state-eviction","family_id":"authored-stream-stream-outer-time-bound-state-eviction","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"An advertising query left-outer-joins an impressions stream to a clicks stream using only an ad ID. A click should match only if its event time is between the impression time and one hour later. Event time and micro-batch execution continue to advance on the inputs. The team needs Spark to eventually emit an impression with NULL click fields when no eligible click can still arrive. Which design provides the necessary event-time information?","options_ja":["Apply an equality join on ad ID only and increase executor memory.","Sort each stream by its timestamp before the equality join, without using watermarks.","Add appropriate event-time watermarks and include the one-hour event-time range in the join condition.","Use outputMode(\"complete\") without adding any time constraints."],"explanation_ja":"stream-stream outerで未一致を確定するには、右側を中心とする適切なwatermarkと対応可能な時刻範囲条件が必要。時間上限がなければ将来ずっと一致が来る可能性が残る。両側watermarkは状態清掃にも使えるが、単なるsortやmemory増強は未一致確定の論理を与えない。 watermarkはmicro-batch終了時に進み、次batchでstate清掃やouter NULL出力に使われる。入力がidleになりbatchやwatermarkが進まない場合、NULL出力はさらに遅れる可能性がある。本問は両者が継続的に進む前提。","distractors_ja":["memoryを増やしても将来一致しないと確定する時間条件がない。","sortはevent-timeの遅延上限やstateの有効期限を定義しない。","正答。watermarkと時間範囲で将来の一致可能性を制限する。","output modeだけでは外部結合の未一致確定に必要な時間情報を与えない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#outer-joins-with-watermarking"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#outer-joins-with-watermarking","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"stream-stream-outer-time-bound-state-eviction","images":[],"source_hash":"f19a0787b1f6cada634f40bd05eb43670a1d778a78bea6ec5c046b5d5250aa7f"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-STR-006","number":null,"stem":"A developer can read a CSV directory successfully with a batch reader, but now wants to monitor that directory with spark.readStream. The input has known columns order_id INT and amount DOUBLE. The session retains the default spark.sql.streaming.schemaInference=false. To provide a consistent schema without enabling streaming schema inference, which reader is appropriate?","options":["spark.readStream.schema(\"order_id INT, amount DOUBLE\").option(\"header\", True).csv(path)","spark.readStream.option(\"header\", True).csv(path)","spark.readStream.option(\"inferSchema\", True).csv(path), with no schema and no session configuration change","spark.readStream.option(\"header\", True).option(\"maxFilesPerTrigger\", 1).csv(path)"],"correct":0,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"file streamの明示schema","concept":"file-stream-explicit-schema-default","family_id":"authored-file-stream-explicit-schema-default","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A developer can read a CSV directory successfully with a batch reader, but now wants to monitor that directory with spark.readStream. The input has known columns order_id INT and amount DOUBLE. The session retains the default spark.sql.streaming.schemaInference=false. To provide a consistent schema without enabling streaming schema inference, which reader is appropriate?","options_ja":["spark.readStream.schema(\"order_id INT, amount DOUBLE\").option(\"header\", True).csv(path)","spark.readStream.option(\"header\", True).csv(path)","spark.readStream.option(\"inferSchema\", True).csv(path), with no schema and no session configuration change","spark.readStream.option(\"header\", True).option(\"maxFilesPerTrigger\", 1).csv(path)"],"explanation_ja":"file-based streamは既定でschemaの明示が必要。headerは列名、maxFilesPerTriggerはinput量であり型schemaを与えない。streaming schema inferenceを有効化しない条件のためschemaを指定する選択肢だけが要件を満たす。","distractors_ja":["正答。既知のschemaを明示してstream readerに与える。","headerは型schemaを提供しない。","streaming.schemaInference=falseのまま明示schemaなしで読む解決にならない。","maxFilesPerTriggerはbatch当たりfile数制限で型定義ではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#schema-inference-and-partition-of-streaming-dataframesdatasets"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#schema-inference-and-partition-of-streaming-dataframesdatasets","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"file-stream-explicit-schema-default","images":[],"source_hash":"313e7c08039adbdadfa1341d96c29eb2b48f13d3691fa651679571c45d5876f6"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-STR-007","number":null,"stem":"A stateful aggregation has successfully run with spark.sql.shuffle.partitions=24 and a persistent checkpoint. The team stops the query and proposes restarting it from that same checkpoint with the setting changed to 48. The state and processed-source progress must be preserved. According to Spark 3.5.6's documented restrictions, which action is appropriate?","options":["Change outputMode to append; this makes the previous state partitioning irrelevant.","Keep the state-partition count compatible with the existing checkpoint; do not change this setting to 48 for that resumed stateful query.","Delete only the offset log files and retain the remaining checkpoint files so Spark can infer a new state partition count.","Change it to 48 because checkpoint recovery automatically repartitions all existing state without restrictions."],"correct":1,"domain":"Structured Streaming","topic":"stateful query再開時のpartition互換","concept":"stateful-checkpoint-shuffle-partitions-immutable","family_id":"authored-stateful-checkpoint-shuffle-partitions-immutable","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A stateful aggregation has successfully run with spark.sql.shuffle.partitions=24 and a persistent checkpoint. The team stops the query and proposes restarting it from that same checkpoint with the setting changed to 48. The state and processed-source progress must be preserved. According to Spark 3.5.6's documented restrictions, which action is appropriate?","options_ja":["Change outputMode to append; this makes the previous state partitioning irrelevant.","Keep the state-partition count compatible with the existing checkpoint; do not change this setting to 48 for that resumed stateful query.","Delete only the offset log files and retain the remaining checkpoint files so Spark can infer a new state partition count.","Change it to 48 because checkpoint recovery automatically repartitions all existing state without restrictions."],"explanation_ja":"stateはkeyのhashで物理partitionへ配置されるため、既存checkpointで再開するstateful queryのshuffle partition数は変更できない。新しい設定にはcheckpointを破棄し新queryとして始める必要があるが、問題は既存状態・進捗保持を必須としているため24と互換を保つ。","distractors_ja":["出力mode変更はstateの物理partition配置の互換制約を取り除かない。","正答。既存stateのpartition構成を維持する。","checkpoint内部の一部だけを削るのは整合性ある移行手順ではない。","既存stateの自動再partitionが無条件に保証されるわけではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#additional-information"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/structured-streaming-programming-guide.html#additional-information","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"stateful-checkpoint-shuffle-partitions-immutable","images":[],"source_hash":"c3cd5e4e7147511952895e4cf464db2fa1804b096810d23ba7e133a4d12d374f"}]},{"id":"AUTHORED-Spark-Connect","title":"自作問題 · Spark Connect","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-CON-001","number":null,"stem":"An analyst uses a Spark Connect client on a laptop to read a remote table containing several terabytes. The client builds a DataFrame expression that selects two columns and filters by a date. The analyst then requests a small result with `limit(10).collect()`. Which description of the request sent from the client to the Spark Connect server is correct?","options":["The client first downloads the complete remote table and uploads a filtered copy.","The client compiles the query directly into executor machine code and sends one binary per partition.","The client sends only the Python variable name; the driver reads the laptop process memory to obtain its contents.","The client encodes DataFrame operations as an unresolved logical plan; the server resolves and executes the plan."],"correct":3,"domain":"Spark Connect","topic":"クライアントから送る論理計画","concept":"connect-unresolved-plan-request","family_id":"authored-connect-unresolved-plan-request","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"An analyst uses a Spark Connect client on a laptop to read a remote table containing several terabytes. The client builds a DataFrame expression that selects two columns and filters by a date. The analyst then requests a small result with `limit(10).collect()`. Which description of the request sent from the client to the Spark Connect server is correct?","options_ja":["The client first downloads the complete remote table and uploads a filtered copy.","The client compiles the query directly into executor machine code and sends one binary per partition.","The client sends only the Python variable name; the driver reads the laptop process memory to obtain its contents.","The client encodes DataFrame operations as an unresolved logical plan; the server resolves and executes the plan."],"explanation_ja":"Spark ConnectのクライアントはDataFrame操作を未解決論理計画へ変換しProtocol BuffersでgRPC送信する。サーバー側で論理演算子へ変換して通常のSpark最適化・実行を行う。大規模table全体をclientへ移してからfilterする仕組みではない。","distractors_ja":["remote table全件を先にclientへdownloadする要件はない。","物理実行計画やexecutor機械語をclientが配布する設計ではない。","別process/別hostのPython変数を共有memoryのようには読み出さない。","正答。未解決論理計画をserverへ送り解決・実行する。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"connect-unresolved-plan-request","images":[],"source_hash":"be76587bbeea28e1c1210d543d8cd54846285c0c2fc48625c41dc33233f2cb5a"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-CON-002","number":null,"stem":"A developer is investigating network traffic between a Python Spark Connect client and a remote Spark server. The server has finished executing a DataFrame query, and the client is receiving its result rows. The developer needs to identify the standard transport and result representation, rather than the representation used to submit the query. Which description is correct for Spark Connect in Spark 3.5.6?","options":["Results are shared as direct references to executor JVM objects in the client process.","Results are streamed through gRPC as Apache Arrow-encoded row batches.","Results are returned as the same unresolved logical plans used to submit the query.","Results are always written to a single CSV file that the client must read through a shared filesystem."],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark Connect","topic":"結果転送の形式","concept":"connect-arrow-result-batches","family_id":"authored-connect-arrow-result-batches","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A developer is investigating network traffic between a Python Spark Connect client and a remote Spark server. The server has finished executing a DataFrame query, and the client is receiving its result rows. The developer needs to identify the standard transport and result representation, rather than the representation used to submit the query. Which description is correct for Spark Connect in Spark 3.5.6?","options_ja":["Results are shared as direct references to executor JVM objects in the client process.","Results are streamed through gRPC as Apache Arrow-encoded row batches.","Results are returned as the same unresolved logical plans used to submit the query.","Results are always written to a single CSV file that the client must read through a shared filesystem."],"explanation_ja":"Spark Connectはquery要求に未解決論理計画を用いる一方、結果はgRPC上でArrow形式のrow batchとしてstreamする。要求形式と結果形式を分ける判断。","distractors_ja":["clientはexecutorと同一JVMのobject参照を共有しない。","正答。gRPCとArrow row batchesが結果転送に使われる。","未解決論理計画は要求の表現で、計算結果行そのものではない。","共有filesystem上の単一CSVを必須とする設計ではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"connect-arrow-result-batches","images":[],"source_hash":"edcb40a4c4a42fdb85e710a858cf9e68fc16f3840a58cbe23444078bd4f0c2cf"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-CON-003","number":null,"stem":"Two teams use separate Spark Connect client processes to access the same Spark service. They need different versions of a Python library used only for client-side report formatting; this library is not called by any UDF or executor task. One client also performs memory-intensive local report formatting. Which benefit follows from the client-server separation in this situation?","options":["Both formatting libraries must be installed into the Spark driver Python environment with exactly the same version.","Client-side memory allocations are automatically moved into Spark executor storage memory.","Each team automatically receives a dedicated Spark cluster with independent executor resources.","The teams can isolate their client dependencies and local memory use in separate processes without placing those formatting libraries in the driver process."],"correct":3,"domain":"Spark Connect","topic":"クライアント依存ライブラリの分離","concept":"connect-client-process-dependency-isolation","family_id":"authored-connect-client-process-dependency-isolation","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"Two teams use separate Spark Connect client processes to access the same Spark service. They need different versions of a Python library used only for client-side report formatting; this library is not called by any UDF or executor task. One client also performs memory-intensive local report formatting. Which benefit follows from the client-server separation in this situation?","options_ja":["Both formatting libraries must be installed into the Spark driver Python environment with exactly the same version.","Client-side memory allocations are automatically moved into Spark executor storage memory.","Each team automatically receives a dedicated Spark cluster with independent executor resources.","The teams can isolate their client dependencies and local memory use in separate processes without placing those formatting libraries in the driver process."],"explanation_ja":"Connect clientを別process環境で動かすため、clientのみで使う依存やlocal memoryをdriver processから分離できる。server上で実行するPython UDFの依存配布は別問題なので、ここではclient formattingに限定した。","distractors_ja":["clientだけで使うlibraryをdriverと同版へ強制する必要はない。","local Python処理が自動的にexecutorへoffloadされるわけではない。","process分離が専用clusterの自動割当を意味するわけではない。","正答。client-only依存とlocal processの資源を分離できる。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"connect-client-process-dependency-isolation","images":[],"source_hash":"4eaa947002851f61c1ca92907c90b41266b8d6fb10323c604576424104cd79b2"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-CON-004","number":null,"stem":"A team plans to move an existing PySpark DataFrame application to Spark Connect. Most transformations are supported, but the application uses one less common DataFrame method. The team cannot assume that support in the traditional in-process PySpark API proves support in Spark Connect for the target release. What is the most reliable first check before choosing a replacement or running a migration test?","options":["Verify only that the method is supported in pandas, because Spark Connect delegates every method to pandas.","Consult the target release API reference for an explicit Spark Connect support indication on that method.","Check only whether the client machine has Java installed.","Assume all DataFrame methods are supported because the class name is the same."],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark Connect","topic":"移行時のAPI対応確認","concept":"connect-specific-api-version-support-marker","family_id":"authored-connect-specific-api-version-support-marker","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A team plans to move an existing PySpark DataFrame application to Spark Connect. Most transformations are supported, but the application uses one less common DataFrame method. The team cannot assume that support in the traditional in-process PySpark API proves support in Spark Connect for the target release. What is the most reliable first check before choosing a replacement or running a migration test?","options_ja":["Verify only that the method is supported in pandas, because Spark Connect delegates every method to pandas.","Consult the target release API reference for an explicit Spark Connect support indication on that method.","Check only whether the client machine has Java installed.","Assume all DataFrame methods are supported because the class name is the same."],"explanation_ja":"公式Connect概要は、対応APIにSupports Spark Connectの注記があるため対象versionのAPI referenceを確認するよう案内する。class全体の概論から個別methodへ無条件に一般化しない。","distractors_ja":["Spark Connectは全操作をpandasへ委譲する実装ではない。","正答。対象version・対象methodの明示対応を確認する。","client Java有無ではremote method対応を判断できない。","DataFrameの大部分の対応は、すべてのmethod対応の保証ではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"connect-specific-api-version-support-marker","images":[],"source_hash":"a2e18c45d6bf846e213291c0911d67e9de427c16e929b3adb2a5ade57b54e2e0"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-CON-005","number":null,"stem":"A platform team wants to upgrade its Spark Connect server while several deployed applications continue to use their existing client releases. The team expects client-server separation to reduce coordinated application deployments, but it still reviews compatibility before the rollout. Which condition is essential to the compatibility benefit described by Spark Connect's architecture?","options":["A client can use any future RPC without checking server support because gRPC removes all version constraints.","The server-side RPC definitions used by the existing clients must remain backward compatible.","The server must execute every application inside the client Python process.","Every client and the server must share one Python virtual environment."],"correct":1,"domain":"Spark Connect","topic":"サーバーとクライアントの独立更新","concept":"connect-server-upgrade-rpc-compatibility","family_id":"authored-connect-server-upgrade-rpc-compatibility","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A platform team wants to upgrade its Spark Connect server while several deployed applications continue to use their existing client releases. The team expects client-server separation to reduce coordinated application deployments, but it still reviews compatibility before the rollout. Which condition is essential to the compatibility benefit described by Spark Connect's architecture?","options_ja":["A client can use any future RPC without checking server support because gRPC removes all version constraints.","The server-side RPC definitions used by the existing clients must remain backward compatible.","The server must execute every application inside the client Python process.","Every client and the server must share one Python virtual environment."],"explanation_ja":"Connectの分離設計ではserverをclientアプリから独立更新しやすいが、既存clientが使うserver RPCの後方互換を前提とする。任意version組合せを無条件に保証するものではない。","distractors_ja":["gRPCはすべてのversion組合せの互換を自動保証しない。","正答。既存clientが利用するRPC定義の互換維持が条件。","実行をclient processへ移すことはserver独立更新の条件ではない。","別process環境を共有virtualenvへ統一する必要はない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"connect-server-upgrade-rpc-compatibility","images":[],"source_hash":"17b84e7858bbb498ec15f096f83aa23b6afb1cb375f67cbfd25fc750e2225084"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-CON-006","number":null,"stem":"A developer has installed the Spark Connect client package and configured `SparkSession.builder.remote(\"sc://localhost:15002\")`. The local network is functioning, but the connection is refused because no process is listening on port 15002. The developer assumed that configuring the remote address would also launch a Spark Connect server. What should the developer do first?","options":["Set SPARK_REMOTE to the same address; this environment variable starts a server automatically.","Replace sc:// with https:// and keep all server processes stopped.","Start a Spark Connect server process using the matching Spark distribution and Connect package, then connect the client to its listening address.","Create additional remote SparkSession objects until one starts listening on the port."],"correct":2,"domain":"Spark Connect","topic":"接続設定とサーバープロセス起動","concept":"connect-remote-does-not-launch-server","family_id":"authored-connect-remote-does-not-launch-server","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A developer has installed the Spark Connect client package and configured `SparkSession.builder.remote(\"sc://localhost:15002\")`. The local network is functioning, but the connection is refused because no process is listening on port 15002. The developer assumed that configuring the remote address would also launch a Spark Connect server. What should the developer do first?","options_ja":["Set SPARK_REMOTE to the same address; this environment variable starts a server automatically.","Replace sc:// with https:// and keep all server processes stopped.","Start a Spark Connect server process using the matching Spark distribution and Connect package, then connect the client to its listening address.","Create additional remote SparkSession objects until one starts listening on the port."],"explanation_ja":"remote builderやSPARK_REMOTEはclientが接続する宛先の指定。サーバー起動は別の手順で、Spark distributionのstart-connect-server.sh等と対応packageを使用する。既にserverが動作する場合のclient接続方法を問う原問とは起動責任を区別。","distractors_ja":["SPARK_REMOTEも宛先設定でありserver起動命令ではない。","接続URIのschemeを書き換えてもserver processは発生しない。","正答。listenするserver processを先に用意する。","remote sessionを増やすことはserver process起動の代替にならない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/spark-connect-overview.html","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"connect-remote-does-not-launch-server","images":[],"source_hash":"8bccd7df3339020f5e29010c16e90f1b506f5a2444913ff7e3ff3bad9a31a346"}]},{"id":"AUTHORED-Pandas-API","title":"自作問題 · Pandas API","minutes":0,"questions":[{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-PAN-001","number":null,"stem":"A scalar Pandas UDF should double each measurement while retaining one result for every input row, including rows whose measurement is NULL. The developer writes:\n\n```python\n@F.pandas_udf(\"double\")\ndef twice(s: pd.Series) -> pd.Series:\n    return s.dropna() * 2\n```\n\nAn input Arrow batch contains values [1.0, NULL, 3.0]. Pandas and PyArrow are correctly installed. Which change addresses the scalar UDF contract while preserving the required row count?","options":["Remove the return type so Spark accepts a shorter output Series.","Return s * 2, preserving the input Series length and its missing-value positions.","Keep dropna(); scalar Pandas UDFs can return any number of rows.","Return pd.Series([s.sum() * 2]); Spark automatically broadcasts one scalar result to every input row."],"correct":1,"domain":"Pandas API","topic":"scalar Pandas UDFの出力長","concept":"scalar-pandas-udf-preserve-batch-length","family_id":"authored-scalar-pandas-udf-preserve-batch-length","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A scalar Pandas UDF should double each measurement while retaining one result for every input row, including rows whose measurement is NULL. The developer writes:\n\n```python\n@F.pandas_udf(\"double\")\ndef twice(s: pd.Series) -> pd.Series:\n    return s.dropna() * 2\n```\n\nAn input Arrow batch contains values [1.0, NULL, 3.0]. Pandas and PyArrow are correctly installed. Which change addresses the scalar UDF contract while preserving the required row count?","options_ja":["Remove the return type so Spark accepts a shorter output Series.","Return s * 2, preserving the input Series length and its missing-value positions.","Keep dropna(); scalar Pandas UDFs can return any number of rows.","Return pd.Series([s.sum() * 2]); Spark automatically broadcasts one scalar result to every input row."],"explanation_ja":"scalar Series-to-Series Pandas UDFは入力batchと同じ長さのSeriesを返す必要がある。dropnaはNULL要素を削って長さを変える。s*2は欠損位置を保ち1入力行1出力行という契約を満たす。","distractors_ja":["戻り型宣言を消してもscalar UDFの長さ要件は変わらない。","正答。行数とNULL位置を保持して要素ごとの計算を行う。","scalar UDFは任意行数のmapInPandasとは契約が違う。","集約1値を返す関数ではなく、Sparkが自動broadcastして長さ補正する保証はない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/user_guide/sql/arrow_pandas.html#series-to-series"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/user_guide/sql/arrow_pandas.html#series-to-series","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"scalar-pandas-udf-preserve-batch-length","images":[],"source_hash":"3f532f87411bdf76015e7de1d33448c082b701ffa0d60e7d4c44d5a6299ee148"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-PAN-002","number":null,"stem":"A job uses df.groupBy(\"customer_id\").applyInPandas(...) for a custom per-customer calculation. One customer has millions of rows, while most have only a few. The job fails in the Python worker when processing that customer's group. Lowering spark.sql.execution.arrow.maxRecordsPerBatch does not solve the failure. Which explanation best identifies the relevant memory boundary in Spark 3.5.6?","options":["applyInPandas always collects the entire input DataFrame into the driver.","Each group is loaded into memory for the function, and maxRecordsPerBatch does not limit the number of rows in that group.","The configuration guarantees each group is divided into smaller independent function calls, so the failure must be unrelated to group size.","Every group is processed as one row at a time, so only the size of one row can affect worker memory."],"correct":1,"domain":"Pandas API","topic":"grouped Pandas処理のメモリ境界","concept":"applyinpandas-whole-group-not-arrow-batch","family_id":"authored-applyinpandas-whole-group-not-arrow-batch","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A job uses df.groupBy(\"customer_id\").applyInPandas(...) for a custom per-customer calculation. One customer has millions of rows, while most have only a few. The job fails in the Python worker when processing that customer's group. Lowering spark.sql.execution.arrow.maxRecordsPerBatch does not solve the failure. Which explanation best identifies the relevant memory boundary in Spark 3.5.6?","options_ja":["applyInPandas always collects the entire input DataFrame into the driver.","Each group is loaded into memory for the function, and maxRecordsPerBatch does not limit the number of rows in that group.","The configuration guarantees each group is divided into smaller independent function calls, so the failure must be unrelated to group size.","Every group is processed as one row at a time, so only the size of one row can affect worker memory."],"explanation_ja":"grouped applyInPandasはgroup全体を1つのpandas.DataFrameへ渡す。skewした巨大groupでworker memoryを超える可能性があり、maxRecordsPerBatchはこのgroup境界に適用されない。group分割が計算の意味を変えないか検討したうえで設計/分散集約を見直す。","distractors_ja":["全入力をdriverへ集める処理ではなく、問題はworker内groupの大きさ。","正答。関数の入力単位はgroup全体でbatch設定とは別。","その設定は巨大groupの自動分割を保証しない。","関数へ1行ずつ渡すAPIではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/user_guide/sql/arrow_pandas.html#grouped-map"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/user_guide/sql/arrow_pandas.html#grouped-map","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"applyinpandas-whole-group-not-arrow-batch","images":[],"source_hash":"67466b7f060e34faaf6ce06251911400b140256caf6a00cf20e72d951c9a12eb"},{"id":"AUTHORED-SPARK-20261025-PAN-003","number":null,"stem":"A developer disables spark.sql.execution.arrow.pyspark.enabled while diagnosing conversion of Spark DataFrames to local pandas DataFrames. The same application defines a valid scalar Pandas UDF and has compatible pandas and PyArrow installed on the required nodes. The developer concludes that the configuration also turns that Pandas UDF into a standard row-by-row Python UDF. Which statement is correct?","options":["The configuration controls Arrow optimization for toPandas/createDataFrame conversions; it does not convert an explicitly defined Pandas UDF into an ordinary Python UDF.","The setting removes the declared Spark return type from every Pandas UDF.","All Pandas UDF calls must fail when this conversion optimization is disabled, even with dependencies correctly installed.","Every Pandas UDF automatically becomes a standard Python UDF whenever this setting is false."],"correct":0,"domain":"Pandas API","topic":"Arrow変換設定とPandas UDFの区別","concept":"arrow-conversion-config-not-pandas-udf-switch","family_id":"authored-arrow-conversion-config-not-pandas-udf-switch","source_kind":"authored","difficulty":"exam","stem_ja":"A developer disables spark.sql.execution.arrow.pyspark.enabled while diagnosing conversion of Spark DataFrames to local pandas DataFrames. The same application defines a valid scalar Pandas UDF and has compatible pandas and PyArrow installed on the required nodes. The developer concludes that the configuration also turns that Pandas UDF into a standard row-by-row Python UDF. Which statement is correct?","options_ja":["The configuration controls Arrow optimization for toPandas/createDataFrame conversions; it does not convert an explicitly defined Pandas UDF into an ordinary Python UDF.","The setting removes the declared Spark return type from every Pandas UDF.","All Pandas UDF calls must fail when this conversion optimization is disabled, even with dependencies correctly installed.","Every Pandas UDF automatically becomes a standard Python UDF whenever this setting is false."],"explanation_ja":"spark.sql.execution.arrow.pyspark.enabledは主にtoPandasおよびpandasからcreateDataFrameする変換のArrow最適化を制御する。pandas_udfとして定義された関数のAPI種別を通常udfへ変換するスイッチではない。Pandas UDFに必要な依存や型・長さの契約は別途満たす。","distractors_ja":["正答。変換最適化設定と明示定義したPandas UDFの契約を区別する。","Spark return typeを消す設定ではない。","その設定だけを理由にPandas UDF全件が失敗するわけではない。","falseによってpandas_udfが通常udfへ書き換わるわけではない。"],"references":["https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/user_guide/sql/arrow_pandas.html#enabling-for-conversion-tofrom-pandas","https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/user_guide/sql/arrow_pandas.html#pandas-udfs-aka-vectorized-udfs"],"source_url":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.6/api/python/user_guide/sql/arrow_pandas.html#enabling-for-conversion-tofrom-pandas","validation":"公式文書と選択肢の整合性を確認。実行未検証。","revision":null,"content_revision":"20261025-abcd-r1","grading_hold":null,"decision_id":"arrow-conversion-config-not-pandas-udf-switch","images":[],"source_hash":"05cb64565711d968a40ebf2a093e2390f7d101c072ac4b2009fac21e36b8a8da"}]}],"variants":[],"additional":[],"drills":[],"generic":true,"edition":2};
